window.ZGCLAB_DETAILS["news-40"]=[{"id":"news-2262","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"消息人士称多个CISA部门在停摆裁员中被针对","title_en":"Multiple CISA divisions targeted in shutdown layoffs, people familiar say - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"据多名知情人士透露，美国网络安全和基础设施安全局（CISA）的多个部门在周五晚间收到的裁员通知中受到影响，包括利益相关方参与司和基础设施安全司，综合运营司据信也受波及。美国国土安全部发言人表示，此次裁员旨在让CISA“回归使命”。一份法庭文件显示，国土安全部共裁减176名员工，但CISA具体受影响人数尚不清楚。","summary_en":"Several divisions within the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency were affected by reduction-in-force notices issued Friday evening, including the Stakeholder Engagement Division and Infrastructure Security Division, with the Integrated Operations Division also believed impacted, multiple people familiar said. A Department of Homeland Security spokesperson said the RIFs aim to get CISA 'back on mission.' A court filing says 176 DHS employees were cut, though the exact CISA number is unclear.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:39:35.652381+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/cybersecurity/2025/10/multiple-cisa-divisions-targeted-shutdown-layoffs-people-familiar-say/408773","content":"Multiple CISA divisions targeted in shutdown layoffs, people familiar say\nSOPA Images/Getty\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nOctober 13, 2025\nCISA units including its Stakeholder Engagement division are believed to have been hit. A DHS spokesperson said that the RIFs are meant to help get CISA “back on mission.”\nCyber Threats\nWhite House\nSeveral divisions in the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency were affected in termination orders issued to the federal workforce on Friday evening, multiple people familiar told Nextgov/FCW.Staff within the Stakeholder Engagement Division, as well as the cyber-defense agency’s Infrastructure Security Division, were targeted with reduction-in-force notices, or RIFs, said the people. OMB Director Russ Vought announced the actions on Friday in line with Trump administration promises to enact layoffs during the ongoing government shutdown.The Integrated Operations Division is also believed to have been impacted, one of the people said. All sources in this story spoke on the condition of anonymity due to fear of reprisal from the Trump administration.A DHS spokesperson said that the RIFs are meant to help get CISA “back on mission.” The agency has been targeted for substantive reductions since Trump reentered the Oval Office in January.CISA’s Stakeholder Engagement Division oversees the agency’s national and international partnership work. The Infrastructure Security division is widely considered a core unit of the agency that helps protect against risks to infrastructure like power grids and water treatment plants.That said, the infrastructure division hosts some subbranches that the Trump administration considers to be defunct. The Chemical Security subdivision, namely, is expected to phase out a number of initiatives and programs, per DHS budget justification documents released in May.Employees in the Chemical Security office are believed to have been targeted Friday, though many staff there also took deferred resignation program offers earlier this year, according to another person with knowledge of the matter. DRPs, which have been issued multiple times to feds since January, allow people to leave government service early while getting paid for a set period of time.The exact number of cybersecurity agency staff terminated Friday is not clear. A court filing says that 176 employees were cut in DHS, where CISA is housed. The administration’s filing suggested that further cuts remain a possibility across the federal enterprise.Cybersecurity has been historically a bipartisan darling of Washington, but CISA, deemed the nation’s core civilian cyberdefense agency, has become a recent subject of political scuffles due to its prior work combatting mis- and disinformation.The agency has faced scrutiny from the Trump administration for some time. Top officials have aimed to “refocus” its mission amidst GOP accusations that the agency engaged in censorship of Americans’ free speech. Those claims stem from CISA’s earlier collaboration with social media platforms to remove false information online concerning the COVID-19 pandemic, elections and other divisive subjects around 2020.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nSenator makes new attempt to extend cyber info-sharing law by 10 years\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"据知情人士称，CISA多个部门成为政府停摆裁员的目标\nSOPA Images/Getty\n获取最新联邦技术新闻，直接发送到您的收件箱。\n电子邮件注册新闻通讯保持联系精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\n人工智能应用立即阅读DOGE实施一年后的效率立即阅读洞察与报告\n了解Atlassian Government Cloud的投资回报率由Atlassian呈现\n立即下载\n未来五年的欺诈：我们最好现在就做好准备由Socure呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2025年10月13日\n据悉，包括利益相关者参与部门在内的CISA部门受到了影响。国土安全部发言人表示，这些裁员旨在帮助CISA“回归使命”。\n网络威胁\n白宫\n多名知情人士告诉Nextgov/FCW，网络安全与基础设施安全局（CISA）的几个部门受到了周五晚间向联邦雇员发布的解雇令影响。这些人士称，利益相关者参与部门以及这家网络防御机构的基础设施安全部门的员工，都收到了裁员通知，即RIF。管理和预算办公室主任Russ Vought于周五宣布了这些措施，这与特朗普政府在政府持续停摆期间实施裁员的承诺一致。其中一名人士称，综合行动部门也可能受到了影响。由于担心遭到特朗普政府报复，本报道中的所有消息人士都要求匿名。国土安全部发言人表示，这些RIF旨在帮助CISA“回归使命”。自特朗普于1月重新入主白宫以来，该机构一直被列为实质性削减的目标。CISA的利益相关者参与部门负责该机构的国内和国际伙伴关系工作。基础设施安全部门被广泛认为是该机构的核心部门，负责帮助防范电网和水处理厂等基础设施面临的风险。不过，基础设施部门下设的一些分支机构被特朗普政府视为已经失效。根据国土安全部5月发布的预算论证文件，化学品安全分支预计将逐步停止若干倡议和项目。据另一名了解此事的人士称，化学品安全办公室的员工据信也在周五成为目标，不过那里的许多员工今年早些时候也接受了延期离职计划的提议。自1月以来，政府已多次向联邦雇员提供DRP，允许他们提前离开政府服务，同时在一段固定期限内继续领取薪酬。周五被解雇的网络安全机构员工确切人数尚不清楚。一份法院文件称，国土安全部有176名员工被裁减，CISA隶属于该部。政府提交的文件暗示，整个联邦体系仍有可能进一步裁员。网络安全历来是华盛顿两党的宠儿，但被视为美国核心民用网络防御机构的CISA，因其此前打击错误信息和虚假信息的工作，近期成为政治争执的焦点。特朗普政府一段时间以来一直在审查该机构。高层官员一直试图“重新聚焦”其使命，此前共和党人士指责该机构审查美国人的言论自由。这些指控源于CISA早先与社交媒体\n平台删除有关2020年左右新冠疫情、选举和其他分裂性议题的虚假信息。\n分享此内容：\n下一篇故事：\n参议员再次尝试将网络信息共享法延长10年\n本周科技法案：根据第702条监控人工智能的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\nGenAI.mil记录了近170万用户，并计划新增模型\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤甲骨文公司负责人\n白宫强制要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助商内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作从劳动力开始\n本周科技法案：根据第702条监控人工智能的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\nGenAI.mil记录了近170万用户，并计划新增模型\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤甲骨文公司负责人\n白宫强制要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助商内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作从劳动力开始","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2025-10-13T15:27:29+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1747","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"拜登时期网络负责人出任RSAC首席执行官后CISA停止参会","title_en":"CISA to cease participation at RSAC conference after Biden-era cyber leader named CEO - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"美国网络安全和基础设施安全局（CISA）宣布将不参加3月举行的RSAC网络安全大会，此前约一周，拜登时期的CISA局长珍·伊斯特利被任命为该大会首席执行官。CISA发言人表示，该决定是基于对利益相关方参与的定期审查。此举凸显特朗普政府严格管控现任官员参与与前高级领导人相关行业活动的立场。","summary_en":"The Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency will not participate in the March RSAC cybersecurity conference, about a week after Biden-era CISA Director Jen Easterly was named CEO of the event. An agency spokesperson said the decision followed a regular review of stakeholder engagements. The move underscores the Trump administration's effort to tightly police how current officials engage with industry events tied to former senior leaders.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:22:36.804682+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/cybersecurity/2026/01/cisa-cease-participation-rsac-conference-after-biden-era-cyber-leader-named-ceo/410921","content":"CISA to cease participation at RSAC conference after Biden-era cyber leader named CEO\nThomas Fuller/SOPA Images/LightRocket via Getty Images\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nJanuary 23, 2026\nThe decision, which has been in motion over the last week, highlights the Trump administration’s push to strictly control how current officials participate in industry events linked to former senior leaders now under White House scrutiny.\nCISA\nLeadership\nIndustry\nThe Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency will not partake in the RSAC cybersecurity conference in March, a decision that comes around a week after a top Biden-era cyber leader was named CEO of the event.Jen Easterly, the former CISA director under President Joe Biden, was announced as the CEO of RSAC Conference last Thursday. Hours after the announcement, several top Trump administration cyber officials discussed plans to cancel their attendance, Nextgov/FCW first reported.“CISA has reviewed and determined that we will not participate in the RSA Conference since we regularly review all stakeholder engagements, to ensure maximum impact and good stewardship of taxpayer dollars,” agency spokesperson Marci McCarthy said in a Friday statement.“Since the beginning of this administration, CISA has made significant progress in returning to our statutory, core mission and focusing on President [Donald] Trump’s policies for maximum security for all Americans,” she said.A conference spokesperson did not immediately return a request for comment.RSAC, which takes place annually in San Francisco, is one of the world’s largest cybersecurity forums, and has long been hailed as a convening hub for cyber officials and private sector firms vying for business with the government. It also brings together many international cyber officials who frequently meet behind the scenes with American counterparts to discuss overseas matters.Easterly became a target of Trump ally and far-right activist Laura Loomer last year, when Loomer flagged Easterly’s plans to return to West Point as the Robert F. McDermott Distinguished Chair in its Department of Social Sciences. The decision was soon reversed by the Army.CISA, the nation’s main civilian cyberdefense office, has undergone major workforce shifts since early last year, as the Department of Homeland Security — its parent agency — has sought to refocus the agency back to its “core mission” amid past GOP misgivings about its activities during the Biden administration, namely that the agency engaged in censorship of Americans’ free speech.The move underscores the Trump administration’s effort to tightly police how current officials engage with industry events tied to former senior leaders, especially those in the crosshairs of the current White House.In an interview with WIRED about her appointment as CEO, Easterly said that cybersecurity “is not a political endeavor, RSAC is certainly not a political organization, and I am not a political person. I am a lifelong independent.”The move is sure to change scheduling plans for public panels and other behind-the-scenes engagements at the conference site. As of Friday evening, Chris Butera, CISA’s acting deputy head of cybersecurity, and Clayton Romans, the associate director of the CISA-led Joint Cyber Defense Collaborative, were listed as attendees on the conference page.This week, Democrats on the House Homeland Security Committee pressed CISA’s acting director with questions on workforce reductions and internal developments inside the cyberdefense office in a hearing that examined the agency’s direction under the second Trump administration.FBI and NSA officials were listed in earlier queries of the RSAC conference agenda, but are no longer seen on the schedule. A former FBI official, speaking on the condition of anonymity, said the bureau is also pulling participation from the conference. An NSA spokesperson declined to comment and referred Nextgov/FCW to the White House.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nDemocrats press CISA’s acting chief over major staffing cuts\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"CISA将在拜登时代的网络安全领导人被任命为首席执行官后停止参加RSAC大会\nThomas Fuller/SOPA Images/LightRocket via Getty Images\n获取最新联邦技术新闻，直接发送到您的收件箱。\n电子邮件注册新闻通讯保持联系精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\n人工智能应用立即阅读DOGE一年后的效率立即阅读洞察与报告\n发现Atlassian Government Cloud的投资回报率由Atlassian呈现\n立即下载\n未来五年的欺诈：我们现在最好做好准备由Socure呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2026年1月23日\n这一决定在过去一周内逐步酝酿，凸显了特朗普政府推动严格控制现任官员如何参加与目前受到白宫审查的前高级领导人有关联的行业活动。\nCISA\n领导层\n行业\n网络安全与基础设施安全局将不参加3月举行的RSAC网络安全大会。这一决定是在拜登时代的一名顶级网络安全领导人被任命为该活动首席执行官约一周后作出的。去年周四，前总统乔·拜登任内的CISA局长Jen Easterly被宣布为RSAC大会首席执行官。消息公布数小时后，特朗普政府的几名高级网络官员讨论了取消出席的计划，这是Nextgov/FCW率先报道的。该机构发言人Marci McCarthy在周五的一份声明中说：“CISA经过审查并决定不参加RSA大会，因为我们会定期审查所有利益相关方参与活动，以确保纳税人资金得到最大程度的有效利用和妥善管理。”她说：“自本届政府上任以来，CISA在回归法定核心使命以及聚焦总统[唐纳德]·特朗普的政策、为所有美国人实现最大安全保障方面取得了重大进展。”大会发言人没有立即回应置评请求。RSAC每年在旧金山举行，是全球最大的网络安全论坛之一，长期以来一直被誉为网络官员和争取政府业务的私营部门企业的聚会中心。它还汇聚了许多国际网络官员，他们经常与美国同行在幕后会面，讨论海外事务。去年，当极右翼活动人士、特朗普盟友劳拉·卢默指出Easterly计划重返西点军校、担任其社会科学系Robert F. McDermott杰出讲席教授时，Easterly成为特朗普盟友和极右翼活动人士劳拉·卢默的攻击目标。美国陆军很快推翻了这一决定。CISA是美国主要的民用网络防御机构，自去年年初以来经历了大规模人员变动，因为其上级机构国土安全部一直在寻求让该机构回归“核心使命”，这是在共和党过去对其拜登政府时期活动感到不满的背景下进行的，具体而言，共和党认为该机构参与了审查美国人的言论自由。此举凸显了特朗普政府努力严密监管现任官员如何参与与前高级领导人有关联的行业活动，尤其是那些受到现任白宫打击的人。在接受《连线》杂志采访、谈及获任首席执行官一事时，Easterly说，网络安全“不是政治事务，RSAC当然也不是政治组织，而我不是政治人物。我一生都是独立人士。”这一举动势必会改变日程安排\n关于在会议地点举行公开小组讨论及其他幕后活动的计划。截至周五晚，CISA代理网络安全负责人克里斯·布特拉（Chris Butera）以及由CISA牵头的联合网络防御协作组织副主任克莱顿·罗曼斯（Clayton Romans）均被列为会议页面上的与会者。本周，众议院国土安全委员会的民主党议员在一场审视该机构在特朗普第二届政府领导下发展方向的听证会上，就网络防御办公室的人员削减和内部进展向CISA代理局长提出质询。\n\n在此前查询RSAC会议议程时，FBI和NSA官员曾被列出，但如今已不再出现在日程中。一名前FBI官员以匿名为条件表示，该局也正在撤回参加会议的安排。一名NSA发言人拒绝置评，并将Nextgov/FCW转交白宫。\n\n分享：\n下一篇：\n民主党议员就大规模裁员向CISA代理负责人施压\n本周科技法案：监测人工智能在第702条下的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\nGenAI.mil用户数接近170万，计划新增模型\n众议院小组委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤甲骨文高管\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作从员工队伍开始\n本周科技法案：监测人工智能在第702条下的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\nGenAI.mil用户数接近170万，计划新增模型\n众议院小组委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤甲骨文高管\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作从员工队伍开始","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2026-01-23T22:56:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1399","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"2025年FBI依据FISA 702条款查询美国人数据的次数上升35%","title_en":"FBI queries of Americans’ data under FISA 702 rose 35% in 2025 - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"Nextgov/FCW报道，根据FBI致国会的信函，2025年FBI依据《外国情报监视法》第702条查询美国人数据的次数从2024年12月的5518次增至2025年11月的7413次，上升约35%。该权力允许机构在无搜查令情况下获取境外外国人的通信，可能附带收集美国人的通信。第702条将于4月到期，除非国会续期。白宫正推动干净延期。民权组织长期呼吁改革。","summary_en":"Nextgov/FCW reports that, according to an FBI letter to Congress, FBI searches of U.S. person data collected under Section 702 of the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act rose about 35%, from 5,518 in December 2024 to 7,413 in November 2025. The authority lets agencies access foreigners' overseas communications without a warrant, potentially sweeping up Americans' communications. Section 702 expires in April unless Congress renews it. The White House is pushing for a clean extension. Civil liberties groups have long sought reforms.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-18T15:18:35.085017+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/cybersecurity/2026/03/fbi-queries-americans-data-under-fisa-702-rose-35-2025/412103","content":"FBI queries of Americans’ data under FISA 702 rose 35% in 2025\nJ. David Ake/Getty Images\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nAccelerating Discovery in the AI EraPresented By Google Public Sector\nDownload Now\nCDW and Google Public Sector deliver a unified, mission-focused technology ecosystemPresented By Google Public Sector\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nMarch 12, 2026\nThe controversial spying power, which allows agencies to access foreigners’ overseas communications without a warrant, will expire in April unless Congress renews it. The White House is pushing for a clean extension.\nFBI\nSurveillance\nFBI searches of U.S. person data collected using a controversial spying authority rose some 35% in 2025, according to an FBI letter to Congress that was obtained by Nextgov/FCW.The bureau’s searches of Americans’ data, collected under Section 702 of the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act, rose from 5,518 in December 2024 to 7,413 in November 2025, according to the March 11 letter signed by Ted Groves, the acting assistant director of the FBI’s Office of Congressional Affairs.The letter was sent to Sens. Chuck Grassley, R-Iowa, and Dick Durbin, D-Ill., the top lawmakers on the Senate Judiciary Committee.The 702 authority, which permits the NSA, FBI and other agencies to access foreigners’ communications overseas without a court warrant, can incidentally sweep up communications of Americans talking to targeted persons, raising major civil liberties concerns.The number of U.S. person communications gathered under 702 is often a key statistic cited by civil society organizations that have long pushed for major reforms to the law.In 2024, only 38% of queries “returned either content or non-content 702-acquired information,” the letter says, adding that the figure dropped to 28% in 2025.“Content” refers to the substance of the communications, such as the actual text of emails, chat messages or recorded phone calls. Conversely, “non-content” information is metadata about communications, including details like the email addresses or phone numbers used, IP addresses or the time a message was transmitted.Collected 702 communications are stored in classified databases, where analysts query them for foreign intelligence. Search terms — known as “selectors” — can include names, phone numbers or email addresses of targeted individuals. Analysts may query stored U.S. person data when they believe doing so is reasonably likely to return useful information for investigations.The letter was reported earlier by The Record, the news unit of cyber threat intelligence firm Recorded Future.The FBI declined to comment.Section 702 is due to expire in April unless Congress acts to renew it.The Trump administration is pushing for a clean reauthorization of the law. FBI Director Kash Patel and CIA Director John Ratcliffe met privately with Senate Republicans Wednesday to push for an extension, as House Speaker Mike Johnson, R-La., said he intends to try passing a renewal the week before the authority lapses.Ratcliffe has previously told Congress that the Trump administration attributes many of its national security achievements to Section 702, according to a person familiar with the matter who spoke on the condition of anonymity to share details of private discussions about the statute.The increase outlined in the FBI letter will likely disappoint civil liberties groups, which have long pushed for a warrant requirement for searches of U.S. person data. National security officials have historically and successfully pushed back against a warrant measure in prior reauthorization cycles, though it came close to fruition during the 2024 reauthorization debate, when a House amendment failed in a 212–212 vote.The FBI has acknowledged its improper use of Section 702, specifically admitting to searching for information on individuals involved in the January 6, 2021, U.S. Capitol riot, as well as people arrested during 2020 racial justice protests following the police killing of George Floyd.The law, enacted in 2008, codified parts of the once-secret Stellarwind surveillance program created under the Bush administration after the Sept. 11, 2001, attacks. In 2013, former NSA contractor Edward Snowden disclosed documents detailing how the authority was used, fueling a global debate over privacy and mass surveillance.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nCISA launches investigation into Stryker cyberattack\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nAI is ready for federal health’s hardest problems\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nAI is ready for federal health’s hardest problems","content_cn":"2025年，根据《外国情报监视法》第702条对美国人数据的FBI查询增加了35%\nJ. David Ake/Getty Images\n将最新联邦技术新闻发送到您的收件箱。\n电子邮件注册新闻通讯保持联系精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\n人工智能应用立即阅读DOGE实施一年后的效率立即阅读洞察与报告\n在人工智能时代加速发现\n由Google公共部门提供\n立即下载\nCDW与Google公共部门提供统一、以任务为导向的技术生态系统\n由Google公共部门提供\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2026年3月12日\n这项颇具争议的监视权力允许政府机构在没有搜查令的情况下访问外国人在海外的通信，除非国会续期，否则将于4月到期。白宫正在推动无附加条件的延长。\nFBI\n监视\n根据Nextgov/FCW获得的一封FBI致国会信函，FBI对利用一项颇具争议的监视权限收集的美国人数据进行的搜索，在2025年增加了约35%。根据3月11日由FBI国会事务办公室代理助理主任Ted Groves签署的信函，该局对依据《外国情报监视法》第702条收集的美国人数据进行的搜索，从2024年12月的5,518次增加到2025年11月的7,413次。该信函发送给参议院司法委员会两位主要议员——艾奥瓦州共和党参议员Chuck Grassley和伊利诺伊州民主党参议员Dick Durbin。第702条授权允许国家安全局、FBI及其他机构在没有法院搜查令的情况下访问外国人在海外的通信；这一过程可能附带收集与目标人物交谈的美国人的通信，引发重大公民自由担忧。在第702条下收集的美国人通信数量，经常被长期推动对该法律进行重大改革的公民社会组织引用为关键统计数据。信函称，2024年只有38%的查询“返回了通过第702条获取的内容或非内容信息”，并补充说，这一比例在2025年降至28%。“内容”指通信的实质，例如电子邮件、聊天消息或录音电话的实际文字。相反，“非内容”信息是通信的元数据，包括所使用的电子邮件地址或电话号码、IP地址，或消息传输时间等细节。收集的第702条通信存储在机密数据库中，分析人员在其中查询这些通信以获取外国情报。搜索词——称为“选择器”——可以包括目标个人的姓名、电话号码或电子邮件地址。当分析人员认为这样做很可能为调查返回有用信息时，他们可以查询存储的美国人数据。该信函此前由网络威胁情报公司Recorded Future旗下新闻部门The Record报道。FBI拒绝置评。第702条将于4月到期，除非国会采取行动续期。特朗普政府正在推动无附加条件地重新授权该法律。据一位了解相关情况、并在接受采访时表示此事的人士称，FBI局长Kash Patel和CIA局长John Ratcliffe周三与参议院共和党人私下会面，推动延长该授权；与此同时，众议院议长、路易斯安那州共和党人Mike Johnson表示，他打算在该授权失效前一周尝试通过续期法案。此前，Ratcliffe曾告诉国会，特朗普政府将其许多国家安全成就归功于第702条，据一位了解此事、并在接受采访时表示此事的人士所说。\n以匿名为条件分享有关该法规私下讨论的细节。FBI信中概述的增加幅度可能会令公民自由团体失望，这些团体长期以来一直推动对搜索美国人士数据设立令状要求。国家安全官员在此前的重新授权周期中，历来成功地反对令状措施，不过在2024年的重新授权辩论期间，该措施一度接近实现；当时众议院的一项修正案以212票对212票未获通过。FBI已承认其不当使用第702条，具体承认曾搜索涉及2021年1月6日美国国会大厦骚乱人员的信息，以及在警察杀害乔治·弗洛伊德后、2020年种族正义抗议期间被捕人员的信息。\n该法律于2008年颁布，将布什政府在2001年9月11日袭击事件后创建的、曾属机密的“星风”监视项目部分内容编入法律。2013年，前美国国家安全局承包商爱德华·斯诺登披露了详细说明该权限如何被使用的文件，引发了全球范围内关于隐私和大规模监控的辩论。\n分享此文：\n下一篇：\nCISA启动对斯崔克网络攻击的调查\nGenAI.mil记录的用户接近170万，计划新增模型\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改造成本不断膨胀传唤甲骨文高管\nGSA网页设计系统负责人由财政部人工智能工程师接替\n白宫要求各机构为面向公众的网站使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\n人工智能已准备好应对联邦医疗领域最棘手的问题\nGenAI.mil记录的用户接近170万，计划新增模型\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改造成本不断膨胀传唤甲骨文高管\nGSA网页设计系统负责人由财政部人工智能工程师接替\n白宫要求各机构为面向公众的网站使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\n人工智能已准备好应对联邦医疗领域最棘手的问题","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2026-03-12T22:21:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1283","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"亲伊朗黑客声称入侵FBI局长电子邮箱","title_en":"Pro-Iran hackers claim breach of FBI director’s email - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"亲伊朗黑客组织Handala声称入侵美国联邦调查局局长卡什·帕特尔的个人邮箱，并在网上散布据称来自该邮箱的图片和文件，其中许多为帕特尔任局长前的私人内容。一名知情人士称泄露内容看似真实。FBI回应称已知悉恶意行为者针对局长个人邮箱信息，已采取缓解措施，并称相关信息属历史性内容、不涉及政府信息。Handala称此举是对FBI查封其域名并悬赏的回应。","summary_en":"The pro-Iran hacking group Handala claimed it accessed FBI Director Kash Patel's personal email and posted purported contents online, many showing Patel in a personal capacity before becoming director. A person familiar with the matter said the leaks appear authentic. The FBI said it is aware of malicious actors targeting the director's personal email information, has taken all necessary steps to mitigate risks, and described the information as historical and involving no government information. Handala said it acted after the FBI seized its domains and announced a reward.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:47:42.111205+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/cybersecurity/2026/03/pro-iran-hackers-claim-breach-fbi-directors-email/412440","content":"Pro-Iran hackers claim breach of FBI director’s email\nFBI Director Kash Patel testifies during a House Select Committee on Intelligence hearing on the 2026 Annual Worldwide Threats Assessment at the U.S. Capitol in Washington, DC on March 19, 2026.\nNathan Posner/Anadolu via Getty Images\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nMarch 27, 2026\nThe leaks appear to be authentic, according to a person familiar with the matter.\nFBI\nLeadership\nCyber Threats\nInternational\nA pro-Iran hacker group claimed to have accessed FBI Director Kash Patel’s personal email and posted purported contents from the inbox online.Handala, which claimed responsibility in recent weeks for hacks against Stryker and Lockheed Martin in response to the Iran war, circulated images and documents online that they claimed to be from Patel’s email account. Many images include pictures of Patel in a personal capacity before becoming FBI director.The leaks appear to be authentic, according to a person familiar with the matter who requested anonymity because they weren’t authorized to publicly discuss details of the breach.The incident was first reported by Reuters. “The FBI is aware of malicious actors targeting Director Patel’s personal email information, and we have taken all necessary steps to mitigate potential risks associated with this activity. The information in question is historical in nature and involves no government information,” the bureau said in a statement after this story published. Handala said it carried out the intrusion after the FBI last week said it seized domains used by the group.“Today, once again, the world witnessed the collapse of America’s so-called security legends,” the group wrote on its website. “While the FBI proudly seized our domains and immediately announced a $10 million reward for the heads of Handala Hack members, we decided to respond to this ridiculous show in a way that will be remembered forever.”The breach is likely legitimate, according to a former U.S. official who said that administration officials’ personal email accounts are a frequent target of Iranian hackers.It would not be the first time that Iran-aligned hackers executed a “hack and leak” operation against U.S. targets. In 2024, the Trump campaign was accessed in an Iranian hack that exposed vetting documents for Vice President JD Vance.Editor’s note: This story has been updated to include remarks from a former U.S. official and the FBI.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nLawmakers question VPN impact on Americans' FISA surveillance protections\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"亲伊朗黑客声称入侵FBI局长电子邮件\nFBI局长Kash Patel于2026年3月19日在美国华盛顿特区国会大厦举行的众议院情报特别委员会关于《2026年度全球威胁评估》的听证会上作证。\nNathan Posner/Anadolu via Getty Images\n将最新联邦技术新闻发送至您的收件箱。\n电子邮件 注册新闻通讯 保持联系 精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\nAI应用 立即阅读 DOGE实施一年后的效率 立即阅读 洞察与报告\n探索Atlassian政府云的投资回报率，由Atlassian呈现\n立即下载\n未来五年的欺诈：我们最好现在就做好准备，由Socure呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2026年3月27日\n据一名知情人士称，这些泄露内容似乎是真实的。\nFBI\n领导层\n网络威胁\n国际\n一个亲伊朗黑客组织声称已访问FBI局长Kash Patel的个人电子邮件，并在网上发布了据称来自其收件箱的内容。Handala声称近期因伊朗战争而对Stryker和Lockheed Martin实施黑客攻击，并在网上传播图片和文件，称其来自Patel的电子邮件账户。许多图片包含Patel在成为FBI局长前以私人身份拍摄的照片。\n一名了解此事的人士表示，这些泄露内容似乎是真实的；该人士因无权公开讨论此次入侵的细节而要求匿名。\n路透社最先报道了这一事件。本报道发布后，该局在一份声明中表示：“FBI已知悉恶意行为者正瞄准Patel局长的个人电子邮件信息，我们已采取一切必要措施，以减轻与该活动相关的潜在风险。有关信息具有历史性质，且不涉及任何政府信息。”\nHandala称，在FBI上周表示查封了该组织使用的域名后，该组织实施了此次入侵。\n该组织在其网站上写道：“今天，世界再次见证了美国所谓安全神话的崩塌。FBI自豪地查封了我们的域名，并立即为Handala Hack成员的人头悬赏1,000万美元；我们决定以一种将被永远铭记的方式回应这场荒谬的表演。”\n一名前美国官员表示，此次入侵很可能属实；政府官员的个人电子邮件账户是伊朗黑客经常瞄准的目标。\n这不会是伊朗关联黑客首次对美国目标实施“黑客入侵并泄露”行动。2024年，Trump竞选团队遭到伊朗黑客入侵，导致有关副总统JD Vance的审查文件被曝光。\n编辑注：本文已更新，纳入一名前美国官员和FBI的评论。\n分享：\n下一篇报道：\n议员质疑VPN对美国人FISA监控保护的影响\n本周科技法案：监督AI在第702条下的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多内容\nGenAI.mil用户接近170万，计划新增模型\n独家\n联邦首席信息官称，Trump政府希望AI能缓解人员流失\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤Oracle负责人\nGSA网页设计系统负责人由财政部AI工程师接替\n赞助内容\n联邦AI准备始于劳动力\n本周科技法案：监督AI在第702条下的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多内容\nGenAI.mil用户接近170万，计划新增模型\n独家\n联邦首席信息官称，Trump政府希望AI能缓解人员流失\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤Oracle负责人\nGSA网页设计系统负责人由财政部AI工程师接替\n赞助内容\n联邦AI准备始于劳动力","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2026-03-27T15:51:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1021","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"CISA推出CI Fortify以在冲突期间保护关键基础设施","title_en":"CISA unveils CI Fortify to help secure critical infrastructure during conflicts - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"CISA于周二发布CI Fortify项目，旨在帮助关键基础设施所有者与运营者在 geopolitical 冲突期间抵御黑客并维持连续性。指导建议运营者假设冲突场景下第三方连接（电信、互联网、供应商等）将不可靠，且威胁行为者可能已进入运营技术网络，重点应放在隔离与恢复规划。代理局长Nick Andersen鼓励各组织审阅并落实建议。美国官员多年来评估中国正渗透非军事关键基础设施，以备在涉台重大冲突中实施破坏。","summary_en":"CISA released its CI Fortify project on Tuesday to help critical infrastructure owners and operators defend against hackers and maintain continuity during geopolitical conflict. Guidance says operators should assume third-party connections such as telecommunications, internet, vendors and service providers will be unreliable, and that threat actors may have some access to operational technology networks, focusing on isolation and recovery planning. Acting Director Nick Andersen encouraged organizations to review and implement the recommendations. U.S. officials have long assessed that China is burrowing into non-military critical infrastructure to enable sabotage in a major conflict involving Taiwan.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:02:35.982431+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/cybersecurity/2026/05/cisa-unveils-ci-fortify-help-secure-critical-infrastructure-during-conflicts/413333","content":"CISA unveils CI Fortify to help secure critical infrastructure during conflicts\nMF3d/Getty Images\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nMay 5, 2026\nFor years, U.S. officials have assessed that China is infiltrating non-military critical infrastructure to enable sabotage during a major conflict involving Taiwan.\nCISA\nCritical Infrastructure\nCyber Defense\nThe Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency announced the release of its CI Fortify project on Tuesday, aiming to help critical infrastructure owners and operators defend themselves against hackers and maintain continuity during a geopolitical conflict.“For planning purposes, operators should assume that in a conflict scenario third-party connections — such as telecommunications, internet, vendors, service providers, and upstream dependencies — will be unreliable and that threat actors will have some access to the [operational technology] network,” a webpage describing the initiative says.Per guidance, CISA wants critical infrastructure providers to focus on isolation and recovery planning objectives.“We strongly encourage organizations to review this guidance, implement the recommended actions and collaborate with CISA to strengthen CI defenses against opportunistic threat actors,” agency acting director Nick Andersen said in a prepared statement.Critical infrastructure — like water treatment plants, financial institutions and electric grids — are a regular target for foreign hackers. U.S. officials have assessed for years that China is burrowing into non-military critical infrastructure networks, preparing to sabotage them should the U.S. enter into a major conflict with the nation, especially involving Chinese interests in Taiwan.Hackers linked to China, Russia, Iran, North Korea and ransomware groups will continue to pose critical threats to U.S. networks and critical infrastructure, U.S. intelligence agencies assessed this year.Amid the U.S.-Israel war against Iran, Tehran-backed hackers exploited and disrupted operational technology control systems embedded in multiple U.S. critical infrastructure sectors, targeting equipment manufactured by Rockwell Automation, according to a government advisory issued last month.Last year, Australia, a Five Eyes partner, launched its own CI Fortify program.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nOperational technology providers are feeling ‘annoyance’ at exclusion from Anthropic’s Mythos rollout, sources say\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"CISA推出CI Fortify，以帮助在冲突期间保护关键基础设施\nMF3d/Getty Images\n将最新联邦科技新闻发送至您的收件箱。\nemail注册新闻简报保持联系精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\nAI应用立即阅读DOGE一年后的效率立即阅读洞察与报告\n探索Atlassian Government Cloud的投资回报率，由Atlassian呈现\n立即下载\n未来五年的欺诈：我们最好现在就做好准备，由Socure呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2026年5月5日\n多年来，美国官员一直评估认为，中国正在渗透非军事关键基础设施，以便在涉及台湾的重大冲突期间实施破坏。\nCISA\n关键基础设施\n网络防御\n网络安全和基础设施安全局周二宣布推出其CI Fortify项目，旨在帮助关键基础设施所有者和运营者抵御黑客攻击，并在地缘政治冲突期间维持连续运营。介绍该倡议的网页称：“出于规划目的，运营者应假定，在冲突情景中，第三方连接——如电信、互联网、供应商、服务提供商和上游依赖关系——将不可靠，且威胁行为者将能够在一定程度上访问[运营技术]网络。”\n根据指导意见，CISA希望关键基础设施提供者聚焦于隔离和恢复规划目标。\n该机构代理局长Nick Andersen在一份准备好的声明中表示：“我们强烈鼓励各组织审查本指导意见，实施建议行动，并与CISA合作，以加强CI防御能力，抵御机会主义威胁行为者。”\n关键基础设施——如水处理厂、金融机构和电网——经常成为外国黑客的目标。多年来，美国官员一直评估认为，中国正深入渗透非军事关键基础设施网络，为在美国与该国发生重大冲突时破坏这些网络做准备，尤其是在涉及中国在台湾利益的情况下。\n美国情报机构今年评估认为，与中国、俄罗斯、伊朗、朝鲜有关联的黑客以及勒索软件团伙，将继续对美国网络和关键基础设施构成重大威胁。\n根据上月发布的一份政府公告，在美以对伊朗的战争期间，德黑兰支持的黑客利用并扰乱了嵌入美国多个关键基础设施行业的运营技术控制系统，目标是由Rockwell Automation制造的设备。\n去年，五眼联盟伙伴澳大利亚推出了自己的CI Fortify项目。\n分享此内容：\n下一篇报道：\n消息人士称，运营技术提供商因被排除在Anthropic的Mythos推出之外而感到“恼火”\n本周科技法案：监督第702条下AI的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；等等\n独家\n联邦首席信息官称，特朗普政府希望AI能够缓解人员流失\nGenAI.mil用户接近170万，计划新增模型\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本飙升传唤Oracle负责人\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\n联邦AI准备始于劳动力\n本周科技法案：监督第702条下AI的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；等等\n独家\n联邦首席信息官称，特朗普政府希望AI能够缓解人员流失\nGenAI.mil用户接近170万，计划新增模型\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本飙升传唤Oracle负责人\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\n联邦AI准备始于劳动力","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2026-05-05T16:26:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-668","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Bing News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"消息人士称黑客入侵国土安全部信息共享网络","title_en":"Hackers breached DHS information-sharing network, people familiar say - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"据两名知情人士透露，不明威胁行为者近期入侵了美国国土安全部的国土安全信息网络（HSIN），可能暴露联邦、州、地方及行业伙伴间共享的敏感非机密数据。入侵据信发生在5月底至6月初，黑客针对HSIN服务器及SharePoint协作系统。情报与分析办公室已进行损害评估。DHS发言人表示已隔离受影响系统并展开取证调查，称机密网络未受影响。","summary_en":"According to two people familiar with the matter, an unknown threat actor accessed the Homeland Security Information Network (HSIN), a key DHS information-sharing database, potentially exposing sensitive unclassified data exchanged among federal, state, local and industry partners. The intrusion is believed to have occurred between late May and early June, targeting HSIN servers and a SharePoint collaboration system. DHS's Office of Intelligence and Analysis conducted a damage assessment. A DHS spokesperson said the department isolated affected systems and launched a forensic investigation, stating no classified networks were impacted.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-18T05:48:54.562464+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/cybersecurity/2026/06/hackers-breached-dhs-information-sharing-network-people-familiar-say/414534","content":"Hackers breached DHS information-sharing network, people familiar say\nWin McNamee/Getty Images\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nAccelerating Discovery in the AI EraPresented By Google Public Sector\nDownload Now\nCDW and Google Public Sector deliver a unified, mission-focused technology ecosystemPresented By Google Public Sector\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nJune 30, 2026\nThe Homeland Security Information Network is used by government, international and private sector partners to share sensitive but unclassified information.\nDHS\nCyber Threats\nData Breaches\nA key Department of Homeland Security information-sharing database was accessed by an unknown threat actor in recent weeks, potentially exposing sensitive data exchanged between federal, state, local and industry partners, according to two people familiar with the matter.DHS investigators are probing the intrusion of the Homeland Security Information Network, said both people, who spoke on the condition of anonymity because the incident is sensitive. The hackers’ affiliation and whether any documentation was pilfered from the system are both unclear.The department’s Office of Intelligence and Analysis has conducted a damage assessment of the intrusion, which is believed to have occurred sometime between late May and early June, said one of the people. The hackers targeted HSIN servers and a SharePoint system used for collaboration efforts, the person added.Approved users lean on the network to securely access data, exchange requests with partner agencies, manage operations, coordinate safety and security for planned events, respond to incidents and share mission-critical information needed to protect their communities, according to its website. HSIN carries unclassified but sensitive information shared among federal, state, local, territorial, tribal, international and private-sector partners.The intrusion comes as the U.S. is overseeing security for World Cup games across the country, placing added scrutiny on the systems federal, state and local officials use to coordinate major events. A breach of the platform could raise concerns about whether hackers gained insight into security planning, interagency coordination or response procedures surrounding one of the most visible international events hosted predominately in the United States.The platform supports real-time communication, document sharing, alerts, web conferencing and incident management. It’s also used to exchange information about persons of interest and potential threats to help agencies maintain situational awareness during emergencies and events.“The Department of Homeland Security is aware of a recent cyber incident involving a specific, unclassified legacy information sharing environment. We immediately took action to isolate the affected systems, mitigate the vulnerability, and launch a comprehensive forensic investigation,” a department spokesperson said after this story published. “There is no indication that classified networks were impacted, and the system remains operational for our partners. As this is an ongoing investigation, we cannot provide further operational details at this time.\"The development would not be the first time HSIN has faced security problems. In 2023, an access misconfiguration linked to a contractor’s coding error caused restricted HSIN data to be exposed to unapproved users inside the platform, according to a memo obtained by Nextgov/FCW. The error let information intended for a limited set of authorized users be made available more broadly across HSIN, including sensitive U.S. person data and other personally identifying information. The full consequences of that misconfiguration are still unclear, according to a third person. Wired previously reported that incident.Nation-state groups and criminal hackers routinely target U.S. systems to collect intelligence, steal sensitive information, disrupt operations or gain footholds inside government networks. In February, a suspected China-linked breach of an FBI surveillance system likely revealed phone numbers of targets being monitored by the bureau, Nextgov/FCW previously reported.Editor's note: This article has been updated to include comment from DHS.\nTo securely contact the reporter for this story, he can be reached on Signal at username djd.99\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nSecret Service phone security lapses put US officials at risk, watchdog says\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nOPM memo pushes agencies to speed up hiring with AI\nsponsor content\nAI is ready for federal health’s hardest problems\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nOPM memo pushes agencies to speed up hiring with AI\nsponsor content\nAI is ready for federal health’s hardest problems","content_cn":"黑客攻破国土安全部信息共享网络，知情人士称\nWin McNamee/Getty Images\n将最新联邦技术新闻发送到您的收件箱。\n电子邮件注册新闻通讯保持联系精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\n人工智能应用立即阅读DOGE一年后的效率立即阅读洞察与报告\n在人工智能时代加速发现由 Google Public Sector 提供\n立即下载\nCDW 与 Google Public Sector 提供统一的、以任务为导向的技术生态系统由 Google Public Sector 提供\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW 网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2026年6月30日\n国土安全信息网络供政府、国际组织和私营部门合作伙伴共享敏感但未分类的信息。\nDHS\n网络威胁\n数据泄露\n据两名知情人士透露，国土安全部一个关键信息共享数据库在最近几周被不明威胁行为者访问，联邦、州、地方及行业合作伙伴之间交换的敏感数据可能因此暴露。两名人士均表示，国土安全部调查人员正在调查国土安全信息网络遭入侵一事；由于事件敏感，他们以匿名方式接受采访。黑客的所属关系以及系统中的任何文件是否被窃取，目前都不清楚。其中一名人士表示，国土安全部情报与分析办公室已对这次入侵展开损害评估，据认为入侵发生在5月底至6月初之间。该人士补充说，黑客的目标是 HSIN 服务器和一个用于协作工作的 SharePoint 系统。根据其网站介绍，经批准的用户依靠该网络安全访问数据、向合作机构提出请求、管理行动、协调计划活动的安全与安保工作、应对事件，并共享保护其社区所需的关键任务信息。HSIN 承载联邦、州、地方、领地、部落、国际及私营部门合作伙伴之间共享的未分类但敏感的信息。\n此次入侵发生之际，美国正在统筹全国各地世界杯比赛的安保工作，这使联邦、州和地方官员用于协调重大活动的系统受到更多审视。该平台遭入侵可能引发人们对黑客是否获取了有关安保规划、机构间协调或应对程序的洞见的担忧，而这些内容涉及一项主要在美国举办、最受全球关注的国际赛事。\n该平台支持实时通信、文件共享、警报、网络会议和事件管理。它还用于交换有关关注人员及潜在威胁的信息，帮助机构在紧急情况和活动期间保持态势感知。\n“国土安全部知悉一起近期网络事件，涉及一个特定的、未分类的旧信息共享环境。我们立即采取行动，隔离受影响的系统，缓解漏洞，并启动全面的取证调查。”该部门发言人在本报道发布后表示。“没有迹象表明机密网络受到影响，该系统仍在为我们的合作伙伴运行。由于调查仍在进行中，我们目前无法提供更多行动细节。”\n这并不是 HSIN 第一次面临\n安全问题。根据 Nextgov/FCW 获得的一份备忘录，2023 年，一起与承包商编码错误有关的访问配置错误，导致受限的 HSIN 数据在平台内暴露给未经批准的用户。该错误使原本只应提供给一小部分获授权用户的信息，在 HSIN 内被更广泛地访问，其中包括敏感的美国人员数据和其他个人身份信息。根据第三位知情人士的说法，这一配置错误造成的全部后果仍不清楚。Wired 此前曾报道过该事件。\n\n国家级组织和犯罪黑客经常以美国系统为目标，以收集情报、窃取敏感信息、扰乱行动或在政府网络内部取得立足点。Nextgov/FCW 此前报道，2 月份，一起疑似与中国有关的 FBI 监控系统入侵事件，很可能暴露了该局正在监控的目标对象的电话号码。\n\n编辑注：本文已更新，加入了国土安全部的评论。\n\n如需安全地联系本报道记者，可通过 Signal 联系，用户名为 djd.99\n\n分享本文：\n\n下一篇报道：\n\n监察机构称，特勤局电话安全漏洞使美国官员面临风险\n\n独家\n\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官称\n\nGenAI.mil 记录了近 170 万用户，并计划新增模型\n\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本不断膨胀问题，传唤甲骨文公司负责人\n\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用 Login.gov\n\n人事管理局备忘录推动各机构利用人工智能加快招聘\n\n赞助内容\n\n人工智能已准备好应对联邦医疗领域最棘手的问题\n\n独家\n\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官称\n\nGenAI.mil 记录了近 170 万用户，并计划新增模型\n\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本不断膨胀问题，传唤甲骨文公司负责人\n\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用 Login.gov\n\n人事管理局备忘录推动各机构利用人工智能加快招聘\n\n赞助内容\n\n人工智能已准备好应对联邦医疗领域最棘手的问题","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2026-06-30T15:11:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-639","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"CISA预计9月前敲定关键网络事件报告规则","title_en":"CISA expects to finalize key cyber reporting rule by September - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"根据上周公布的一份监管文件，CISA预计将于9月敲定《关键基础设施网络事件报告法》（CIRCIA）的最终规则，要求关键基础设施实体在72小时内报告重大网络事件、24小时内报告勒索软件付款。该法2022年通过，最终规则多次推迟，CISA于2024年4月发布首个程序性通知，并错过了2025年10月的法定截止期限。上月，因国土安全部资金中断而推迟的利益相关方会议重新召开。","summary_en":"CISA expects to finalize the Cyber Incident Reporting for Critical Infrastructure Act rule in September, requiring critical infrastructure entities to report major incidents within 72 hours and ransomware payments within 24 hours, according to a regulation document. CIRCIA passed in 2022, but the final rule has been repeatedly delayed; CISA issued its first procedural notice in April 2024 and missed the statutory October 2025 deadline. Additional stakeholder town halls were held last month after a DHS funding lapse delayed meetings.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T10:51:33.245663+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/cybersecurity/2026/07/cisa-expects-finalize-key-cyber-reporting-rule-september/414607","content":"CISA expects to finalize key cyber reporting rule by September\nYuichiro Chino/Getty Images\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nJuly 6, 2026\nLast month, CISA held additional stakeholder town halls on the forthcoming CIRCIA final rule after a now-resolved DHS funding lapse this spring delayed the meetings.\nCISA\nIndustry\nRegulations\nThe Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency expects to finalize a bedrock cybersecurity incident reporting rule in September, requiring critical infrastructure providers to report major hacks directly to the cyberdefense agency, according to a regulation document published last week. The Cyber Incident Reporting for Critical Infrastructure Act requires critical infrastructure entities to report substantial incidents to CISA within 72 hours and ransomware payments within 24 hours. CIRCIA’s underlying measure in Congress first passed in 2022, though the final rule has been delayed for some time. CISA published the first procedural notice for the rule in April 2024 and missed the statutory October 2025 final-rule deadline. Last month, the agency held additional stakeholder town halls to further discuss the directive after a now-resolved shutdown of the Department of Homeland Security in the spring delayed scheduling of those meetings.CIRCIA is significant because CISA has historically relied on voluntary cooperation from critical infrastructure operators, and the law would move a major piece of that relationship into a mandatory reporting regime. The directive grew out of post-SolarWinds and Colonial Pipeline concerns that voluntary reporting left the government blind to major cyber incidents. Russia’s invasion of Ukraine added urgency by raising fears that geopolitical conflict could spill into attacks on U.S. critical infrastructure.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nHackers breached DHS information-sharing network, people familiar say\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"CISA 预计将在 9 月前敲定关键网络事件报告规则\nYuichiro Chino/Getty Images\n将最新的联邦技术新闻发送到你的收件箱。\n电子邮件\n注册新闻简报\n保持联系\n精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\n人工智能应用\n立即阅读\nDOGE 一年后的效率\n立即阅读\n洞察与报告\n了解 Atlassian 政府云的投资回报\n由 Atlassian 提供\n立即下载\n欺诈的未来五年：我们现在必须做好准备\n由 Socure 提供\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW 网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2026 年 7 月 6 日\n上个月，在今年春季一场现已解决的国土安全部资金中断导致会议延期后，CISA 又举行了多场利益相关者市政厅会议，讨论即将出台的 CIRCIA 最终规则。\nCISA\n行业\n监管\n据上周发布的一份法规文件称，网络安全与基础设施安全局预计将在 9 月敲定一项基础性网络安全事件报告规则，要求关键基础设施提供商直接向网络防御机构报告重大黑客攻击。《关键基础设施网络事件报告法》要求关键基础设施实体在 72 小时内向 CISA 报告重大事件，并在 24 小时内报告勒索软件付款。CIRCIA 的基础法案于 2022 年在国会首次通过，但最终规则已经延迟了一段时间。CISA 于 2024 年 4 月发布了该规则的第一份程序性通知，并错过了法定的 2025 年 10 月最终规则期限。上个月，在春季一场现已解决的国土安全部停摆导致这些会议的日程安排延迟后，该机构又举行了多场利益相关者市政厅会议，进一步讨论这项指令。CIRCIA 之所以重要，是因为 CISA 历来依赖关键基础设施运营商的自愿合作，而这项法律将这种关系中的一个重要部分转变为强制报告制度。这项指令源于 SolarWinds 和 Colonial Pipeline 事件后的担忧：自愿报告使政府无法掌握重大网络事件。俄罗斯入侵乌克兰进一步增加了紧迫性，因为人们担心地缘政治冲突可能蔓延为针对美国关键基础设施的攻击。\n分享：\n下一篇：\n知情人士称，黑客入侵了国土安全部信息共享网络\n本周科技法案：监控人工智能在第 702 条下的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官称\nGenAI.mil 的用户已接近 170 万，计划新增模型\n众议院委员会传唤甲骨文负责人，因退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本不断膨胀\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用 Login.gov\n赞助内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作始于劳动力\n本周科技法案：监控人工智能在第 702 条下的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官称\nGenAI.mil 的用户已接近 170 万，计划新增模型\n众议院委员会传唤甲骨文负责人，因退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本不断膨胀\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用 Login.gov\n赞助内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作始于劳动力","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2026-07-06T21:13:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-72","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"ShinyHunters声称窃取FBI数据并要求该局撤回网络警告","title_en":"ShinyHunters claims FBI data theft, demands bureau retract cyber warning - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"网络犯罪组织ShinyHunters声称窃取美国联邦调查局员工和求职者的敏感信息，并要求该局一周内撤回5月15日关于其使用骚扰、威胁乃至“报假警”手段的公开警告。该组织称通过Oracle PeopleSoft未知漏洞进入AWS GovCloud服务器，窃取约2至3TB数据。Nextgov/FCW收到疑似含近5000名FBI员工姓名、住址、电话及亲属信息的文件，并核实部分人员确在FBI任职，但数据集未全部验证。FBI称已知悉并正在调查。","summary_en":"The ShinyHunters cybercriminal group claims it stole sensitive data on FBI employees and job applicants and demanded the bureau retract a May 15 public warning about its harassment and swatting tactics within a week. The group said it exploited a previously unknown Oracle PeopleSoft vulnerability to access AWS GovCloud servers and took two to three terabytes of data. Nextgov/FCW received a file appearing to contain personal information on nearly 5,000 FBI employees, including names, home addresses, phone numbers and relatives' details, and confirmed some listed people work at the FBI, though the full dataset was not verified. The FBI said it is investigating.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T19:54:10.203679+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/cybersecurity/2026/09/shinyhunters-claims-fbi-data-theft-demands-bureau-retract-cyber-warning/416144","content":"ShinyHunters claims FBI data theft, demands bureau retract cyber warning\nAnna Moneymaker/Getty Images\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nSeptember 22, 2026\nThe hacking group says it obtained sensitive employee and applicant records. The full scope of the claimed breach remains unclear.\nCyber Threats\nFBI\nThe ShinyHunters cybercriminal group claims it has stolen sensitive information about FBI employees and job applicants and is demanding that the bureau retract a public warning about its tactics within a week.The demand, addressed to FBI Director Kash Patel and Cyber Division Assistant Director Brett Leatherman, seeks to pressure the agency into changing its public account of the group’s activities. The group claims the effort was not financially motivated.In a statement attributed to the group, the hackers claimed access to several FBI services, including human resources systems and a service identified as Medlink. “We have compromised the FBI. We hold very sensitive data on almost ALL FBI Agents and individuals who filed an application with the FBI for a job,” the statement says.An FBI jobs page also displayed a “Scheduled Maintenance Underway” notice Tuesday, saying the site was temporarily unavailable. The notice did not identify a security incident or explain whether the outage was related to the hackers’ claims. An earlier version of the webpage appears to show a seizure notice posted by the group.The language ShinyHunters wants removed appears in a May 15 FBI public service announcement issued after an attack disrupted an online learning management system used by educational institutions. In the announcement, the FBI warned that ShinyHunters uses harassment to pressure victims, including threatening communications to victims and family members and, in some cases, swatting, the practice of calling in false emergency reports intended to trigger an armed police response at someone’s home. The alert also warned that attackers may exaggerate their access to personal information or falsely claim to possess compromising photographs or videos.ShinyHunters denied those practices in its statement and gave the bureau one week to correct or remove the warning. “The FBI is aware of claims regarding unauthorized activity affecting FBIjobs.gov and is currently investigating,” the bureau said in a statement after this story was published. Nextgov/FCW has also reached out to the FBI Agents Association, which advocates on behalf of active and retired FBI special agents.If the claimed employee records are authentic, their exposure could give criminals or foreign intelligence services information useful for identifying, contacting or intimidating FBI personnel and their families.The claims come as the FBI works to pursue a more coordinated campaign focused on dismantling hackers’ infrastructure and arresting cybercrime operatives. Its new cyber strategy released this month in part emphasizes disrupting criminal hackers even when those responsible remain beyond the immediate reach of U.S. law enforcement.A ShinyHunters representative sent Nextgov/FCW a text file appearing to contain sensitive personal information on nearly 5,000 FBI employees, including their names, home addresses, phone numbers, and data about their spouses and siblings. Nextgov/FCW queried some of the listed peoples’ names online and found that they are employed with the FBI. The job postings in the data included employees designated as intelligence analysts, attorneys, student trainees and special agents, among other roles. The entire data set was not verified.A representative told 404 Media, which earlier reported the incident Tuesday, that the hackers gained access Monday night through what they described as a previously unknown vulnerability in Oracle’s PeopleSoft software, then accessed servers in Amazon Web Services’ GovCloud environment. The representative claimed the group took between two and three terabytes of data.The account of the intrusion has not been independently verified. Nextgov/FCW has asked Oracle and AWS for comment.Cybersecurity specialists should focus on the group’s claims regarding an exploit in the PeopleSoft platform because it could be used more broadly to breach other systems, said Dan Calderone, the chief technology officer at cybersecurity and AI firm Suzu Labs.“They also say this isn't financially motivated, but I’d take that with a grain of salt. I have a hard time believing terabytes of FBI personnel data just sit on a shelf,” Calderone said. “Foreign intelligence services would love to have it, and having the FBI on their resume makes every future extortion demand more believable, and if the PeopleSoft zero-day is real, the exploit may be worth more than the data.” On top of that, FBI agents and their spouses “could have their home addresses posted publicly within a week if this threat is followed through,” he added.Editor’s Note: This story was updated to include a statement from the FBI, remarks from Dan Calderone and additional details about the data allegedly accessed by ShinyHunters.\nDavid DiMolfetta can be reached on Signal via username djd.99\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nAI agents are getting better at cybersecurity. That cuts both ways.\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"ShinyHunters声称窃取了联邦调查局数据，要求该局撤回网络警告\nAnna Moneymaker/Getty Images\n将最新的联邦技术新闻发送到您的收件箱。\n电子邮件注册新闻通讯保持联系精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\n人工智能应用立即阅读DOGE实施一年后的效率立即阅读洞察与报告\n发现Atlassian政府云的投资回报率由Atlassian呈现\n立即下载\n欺诈的未来五年：我们现在最好做好准备由Socure呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2026年9月22日\n该黑客组织称其获取了敏感的员工和求职者记录。所声称的入侵事件的全部范围仍不清楚。\n网络威胁\n联邦调查局\n网络犯罪组织ShinyHunters声称窃取了联邦调查局员工和求职者的敏感信息，并要求该局在一周内撤回一份关于其手段的公开警告。这一要求发给联邦调查局局长Kash Patel和网络部门助理局长Brett Leatherman，旨在向该机构施压，要求其改变对该组织活动的公开说法。该组织称，这次行动并非出于经济动机。在一份据称由该组织发表的声明中，黑客声称已访问联邦调查局的多项服务，包括人力资源系统以及一项名为Medlink的服务。声明称：“我们已经攻破了联邦调查局。我们掌握了几乎所有联邦调查局特工以及向联邦调查局提交求职申请人员的高度敏感数据。”联邦调查局招聘页面周二还显示“正在进行计划维护”的通知，称该网站暂时无法访问。通知没有指明发生了安全事件，也没有说明此次中断是否与黑客的说法有关。该网页的早期版本似乎显示了该组织发布的扣押通知。ShinyHunters希望删除的措辞出现在联邦调查局5月15日发布的一份公共服务公告中。该公告是在一次攻击导致教育机构使用的在线学习管理系统中断后发布的。在公告中，联邦调查局警告称，ShinyHunters利用骚扰向受害者施压，包括向受害者及其家人发送威胁信息，并且在某些情况下实施“swatting”，即拨打虚假的紧急报警电话，意图引发武装警察到某人家中响应。该警报还警告称，攻击者可能夸大其获取个人信息的程度，或虚假声称拥有具有不利影响的照片或视频。ShinyHunters在声明中否认这些做法，并给该局一周时间纠正或删除这份警告。该局在本文发布后的一份声明中说：“联邦调查局已知悉有关未经授权活动影响FBIjobs.gov的说法，目前正在调查。”Nextgov/FCW还联系了联邦调查局特工协会，该协会代表现役和退休的联邦调查局特工进行倡导。如果所声称的员工记录真实，其泄露可能会向犯罪分子或外国情报机构提供有助于识别、联系或恐吓联邦调查局人员及其家人的信息。这些说法出现之际，联邦调查局正努力推动一项更加协调的行动，重点是瓦解黑客基础设施并逮捕网络犯罪分子。该局本月发布的新网络战略部分强调，即使相关人员仍在美国执法部门当前无法触及的范围之外，也要扰乱犯罪黑客的活动。一\nShinyHunters 的一名代表向 Nextgov/FCW 发送了一份文本文件，其中似乎包含近 5,000 名联邦调查局（FBI）员工的敏感个人信息，包括他们的姓名、家庭住址、电话号码，以及其配偶和兄弟姐妹的信息。Nextgov/FCW 在网上查询了其中一些人员的姓名，发现他们确实受雇于 FBI。数据中的职位包括情报分析师、律师、学生实习生和特工等。整套数据尚未得到核实。\n\n这名代表告诉 404 Media（该媒体周二早些时候率先报道了此事），黑客于周一晚间通过他们所称的 Oracle PeopleSoft 软件中一个此前未知的漏洞获得访问权限，随后进入亚马逊网络服务（AWS）的 GovCloud 环境中的服务器。该代表声称，该组织窃取了两至三太字节的数据。\n\n这起入侵事件的说法尚未得到独立核实。Nextgov/FCW 已向 Oracle 和 AWS 询问置评。\n\n网络安全专家应关注该组织关于 PeopleSoft 平台漏洞利用的说法，因为这一漏洞可能被更广泛地用于入侵其他系统。网络安全和人工智能公司 Suzu Labs 的首席技术官 Dan Calderone 表示。\n\n“他们还说这不是出于经济动机，但我对此会持保留态度。我很难相信数太字节的 FBI 人员数据只是被搁置在那里，”Calderone 说。“外国情报机构会非常希望得到这些数据，而把 FBI 写进他们的履历，会让今后的每一次勒索要求都更可信；如果 PeopleSoft 零日漏洞确实存在，那么这个漏洞的价值可能高于这些数据。”\n\n他补充说，除此之外，FBI 特工及其配偶“如果这一威胁被付诸实施，可能会在一周内被公开发布家庭住址”。\n\n编者按：本文已更新，加入了 FBI 的声明、Dan Calderone 的评论，以及有关 ShinyHunters 据称访问的数据的更多细节。\n\n可通过 Signal 用户名 djd.99 联系 David DiMolfetta\n\n分享本文：\n\n下一篇：\n\n人工智能代理在网络安全方面正变得越来越强。这是一把双刃剑。\n\n本周科技法案：监控人工智能在第 702 条下的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌读取器；以及更多\n\nGenAI.mil 记录了近 170 万用户，并计划新增模型\n\n独家\n\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\n\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤 Oracle 高管\n\nGSA 网站设计系统负责人由财政部人工智能工程师接替\n\n赞助商内容\n\n联邦人工智能准备工作从劳动力开始\n\n本周科技法案：监控人工智能在第 702 条下的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌读取器；以及更多\n\nGenAI.mil 记录了近 170 万用户，并计划新增模型\n\n独家\n\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\n\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤 Oracle 高管\n\nGSA 网站设计系统负责人由财政部人工智能工程师接替\n\n赞助商内容\n\n联邦人工智能准备工作从劳动力开始","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2026-09-22T19:09:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3000","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"国土安全部在新使命重点下取消科技局拨款","title_en":"DHS cancels S&T grants under a new mission focus for the directorate - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"美国国土安全部暂停其科学与技术局所有拨款及合作协议，以重新调整使命重点。备忘录要求终止所有现有及待批拨款，包括九个卓越中心合作协议，理由是与当前战略优先事项可能不一致。其他被终止项目涉及科学领导奖、社会与行为科学拨款及少数族裔服务机构合作协议，金额约1760万美元。新备忘录列出四项优先使命领域及研究重点，工作人员预计将面临大规模裁员。","summary_en":"The Department of Homeland Security halted all funding for grants and cooperative agreements in its Science and Technology Directorate as part of a mission realignment. A memo requested termination of all current and pending grants, including nine Centers of Excellence agreements, citing potential misalignment with strategic priorities. Other terminated programs included Scientific Leadership Awards, social and behavioral science grants and Minority Serving Institutions agreements, totaling about $17.6 million. A second memo outlined four priority mission areas and research priorities, while staff anticipate significant personnel cuts.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:54:13.343539+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/digital-government/2025/04/dhs-cancels-st-grants-under-new-mission-focus-directorate/404378","content":"DHS cancels S&T grants under a new mission focus for the directorate\nSamuel Corum/Anadolu Agency/Getty Images\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nAlexandra Kelley,Staff Correspondent, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nAlexandra Kelley\n|\nApril 8, 2025\nThe Department of Homeland Security’s national security research subdivision, the Science and Technology Directorate, will stop current grants and refocus its mission priorities.\nDHS\nThe Department of Homeland Security halted all funding to grants and cooperative agreements housed in its Science and Technology Directorate, part of a larger transformation effort to realign the directorate’s mission areas of focus, according to two sources familiar with the decision. In a memo sent to S&T staff last week and obtained by Nextgov/FCW, titled “Termination of the Centers of Excellence,” DHS leadership called for cutting all S&T financial assistance. S&T’s Centers of Excellence program is a collaborative research effort composed of nine centers that pairs government resources with colleges and universities. The letter was signed by DHS Acting Undersecretary Julie Brewer and sent to DHS Secretary Kristi Noem. As justification for terminating the COEs, the memo cited a risk of “potential misalignment with current strategic priorities” and conflict with new departmental guidance. “The purpose of this memorandum is to request your approval to terminate all current and pending grants and cooperative agreements administered by the Science and Technology Directorate, including all Centers of Excellence cooperative agreements and any other S&T grants managed through the DHS grants and Financial Assistance Division,” the memo said.That request for approval was granted, the two sources familiar told Nextgov/FCW.The COE program focused on topics like agency operational efficiency, cross-boarder threat screenings, critical infrastructure resiliency and counterterrorism analysis and education. Along with the COEs, other S&T programming — such as the Scientific Leadership Awards; social and behavioral science grants; and cooperative agreements with Minority Serving Institutions — were also terminated, the first person familiar confirmed. The total dollar amount of grant funding cut for the latter three programs comes at approximately $17.6 million.While ongoing funding for select S&T programming has been halted, the Trump administration identified new national security priorities the directorate will focus on, according to a second memo obtained by Nextgov/FCW and dated March 4 that was also sent by Brewer.The four priority mission areas outlined for S&T were listed as: Secure and Manage Air, Land, Maritime Border; Enforce U.S. Immigration Laws; Counter Transnational Criminal Organizations and Other Illicit Actors; and Expedite Lawful Trade and Travel.The memo also established a second series of research priorities for S&T. The first is listed as Threat Characterization, Detection, and Mitigation, specifying four threat subgroups: chemical agents, biological agents, explosive materials and artificial intelligence. The remaining secondary research priorities were Cybersecurity, Emergency Response and Public Safety, and Critical Infrastructure. The memo said that these mission areas align with several of President Donald Trump’s recent executive orders.Some components of S&T’s original operations will remain. The memo noted that S&T should continue to partner with other DHS components; first responders; and state, local and tribal entities to aid in research and development efforts.Following news of the halt in grant funding and programming, the first person familiar went on to say that S&T staff are anticipating receiving reduction-in-force notices. “We're waiting this week to see how deep our cuts are going to be for personnel. And I expect at an organization that has approximately 500 feds, I think the low end of what we're going to lose is 50% — that’s speculation on my part,” they said. Changes within S&T and DHS follow the large-scale reductions in force across the government ordered by the Trump administration with the intention of improving overall government efficiency. DHS in particular has seen several changes in its leadership team over the past few weeks.DHS did not respond to a request for comment by the time of publication.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nSSA reorg plan contemplates field office closures, contradicting public statements\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"DHS取消S&T拨款，因该局确立新的任务重点\nSamuel Corum/Anadolu Agency/Getty Images\n将最新的联邦技术新闻发送到您的收件箱。\n电子邮件注册新闻通讯保持联系精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\n人工智能应用立即阅读DOGE实施一年后的效率立即阅读洞察与报告\n发现Atlassian Government Cloud的投资回报率由Atlassian呈现\n立即下载\n未来五年的欺诈：我们最好现在就做好准备由Socure呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nAlexandra Kelley，Nextgov/FCW特约记者\n作者\nAlexandra Kelley\n|\n2025年4月8日\n国土安全部负责国家安全研究的下属部门科学与技术局将停止现有拨款，并重新确定其任务重点。\nDHS\n据两名熟悉该决定的消息人士称，国土安全部停止了其科学与技术局下属所有拨款和合作协议的资金，这是重新调整该局重点任务领域的一项更大转型工作的一部分。Nextgov/FCW获得的一份上周发给S&T员工的备忘录题为“终止卓越中心”，国土安全部领导层要求削减所有S&T财政援助。S&T的卓越中心项目是一项协作研究工作，由九个中心组成，将政府资源与学院和大学配对。该信由国土安全部代理副部长Julie Brewer签署，并发送给国土安全部部长Kristi Noem。作为终止COE的理由，备忘录援引了“可能与当前战略重点不一致”的风险，以及与部门新指引的冲突。备忘录称：“本备忘录旨在请求您批准终止由科学与技术局管理的所有当前和待定拨款及合作协议，包括所有卓越中心合作协议，以及通过DHS拨款和财政援助司管理的任何其他S&T拨款。”熟悉情况的两名消息人士告诉Nextgov/FCW，该批准请求已获批准。COE项目关注的主题包括机构运营效率、跨境威胁筛查、关键基础设施韧性以及反恐分析和教育。第一位熟悉情况的人士确认，除COE外，其他S&T项目——例如科学领导力奖、社会与行为科学拨款，以及与少数族裔服务机构签订的合作协议——也被终止。后面三个项目被削减的拨款总额约为1760万美元。尽管部分S&T项目的持续资金已被叫停，但据Nextgov/FCW获得的第二份备忘录称，该局将聚焦于特朗普政府确定的新国家安全重点；该备忘录日期为3月4日，同样由Brewer发出。备忘录列出的S&T四个优先任务领域是：确保并管理空中、陆地和海上边境；执行美国移民法；打击跨国犯罪组织及其他非法行为者；加快合法贸易和旅行。备忘录还为S&T确立了第二系列研究重点。第一项列为威胁特征识别、检测与缓解，具体说明了四类威胁：化学制剂、生物制剂、爆炸材料和人工智能。其余次级研究重点\n分别是网络安全、应急响应与公共安全，以及关键基础设施。备忘录称，这些任务领域与唐纳德·特朗普总统近期发布的几项行政命令相一致。S&T 原有运营中的一些部分将会保留。备忘录指出，S&T 应继续与国土安全部其他组成部门、第一响应人员以及州、地方和部落实体合作，以协助研究与开发工作。在有关暂停拨款和项目的消息传出后，第一位知情人士继续表示，S&T 员工预计将收到裁员通知。“我们正在等待本周的消息，看看人员削减会有多严重。我预计，在一个约有500名联邦雇员的组织中，我们损失人数的低端估计是50%——这是我的推测，”他们说。S&T 和国土安全部内部的变化，发生在特朗普政府下令在全政府范围内大规模裁员之后，目的是提高政府整体效率。尤其是国土安全部，过去几周其领导团队已经发生了多项变化。截至发布时，国土安全部未回应置评请求。\n分享此内容：\n下一篇故事：\nSSA 重组计划考虑关闭外地办事处，与公开声明相矛盾\n本周科技法案：监控第702条下人工智能的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌读取器；以及更多\nGenAI.mil 用户数接近170万，计划增加新模型\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员短缺，联邦首席信息官表示\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤甲骨文高管\nGSA 网页设计系统负责人由财政部人工智能工程师接替\n赞助内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作从劳动力开始\n本周科技法案：监控第702条下人工智能的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌读取器；以及更多\nGenAI.mil 用户数接近170万，计划增加新模型\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员短缺，联邦首席信息官表示\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤甲骨文高管\nGSA 网页设计系统负责人由财政部人工智能工程师接替\n赞助内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作从劳动力开始","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2025-04-08T14:26:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2928","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"参议员私下游说保留国土安全部卓越中心","title_en":"Senators privately advocated to save DHS Centers of Excellence - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"美国国土安全部两个卓越中心收到终止暂停通知，此前阿拉斯加州和内布拉斯加州参议员与国土安全部长诺姆私下沟通。内布拉斯加大学奥马哈分校的NCITE和阿拉斯加大学安克雷奇分校的ADAC-ARCTIC原定终止被暂停。参议员沙利文和里基茨分别强调这些中心在北极战略和反恐研究中的重要性。两所大学确认4月8日收到终止通知，次日获知暂停，目前等待进一步指示。","summary_en":"Two Department of Homeland Security Centers of Excellence received notices that their terminations were paused after senators from Alaska and Nebraska privately spoke with DHS Secretary Kristi Noem. NCITE at the University of Nebraska at Omaha and ADAC-ARCTIC at the University of Alaska Anchorage had their planned terminations paused. Senators Sullivan and Ricketts emphasized the centers' importance for Arctic strategy and counterterrorism research. Both universities confirmed receiving termination notices on April 8 and learning of the pause the next day, while awaiting further guidance.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:49:43.296575+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/emerging-tech/2025/04/senators-privately-advocated-save-dhs-centers-excellence/404872","content":"Senators privately advocated to save DHS Centers of Excellence\nJ. David Ake/Getty Images\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nAlexandra Kelley,Staff Correspondent, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nAlexandra Kelley\n|\nApril 25, 2025\nLawmakers representing Alaska and Nebraska held private conversations with DHS Secretary Kristi Noem to pause funding terminations for their states’ Centers of Excellence.\nHomeland Security\nCongress\nTwo of the Department of Homeland Security’s Centers of Excellence have received notices that their scheduled terminations have been paused, seemingly the result of private advocacy by the states’ senators with DHS Secretary Kristi Noem. Following the cancellation of the agency’s Science and Technology Directorate’s Centers of Excellence program, which set up partnerships with universities to study a diverse body of national security threats, two of the centers have their planned terminations paused: NCITE at the University of Nebraska at Omaha and ADAC-ARCTIC at the University of Alaska Anchorage. Each center focuses on developing technology solutions to the nation’s most pressing national security issues. In ADAC-ARCITC’s case, recent projects have included expanding drone operations in the Bering Strait, supporting professional residencies dedicated to Indigenous and Native peoples and enabling broader Arctic education. Senators from both states confirmed that they held private conversations with DHS Secretary Kristi Noem to halt termination efforts for their individual Centers of Excellence. Senator Dan Sullivan, R-Ark., told Nextgov/FCW that he has had “a number of” conversations with Noem about the strategic importance of a DHS S&T center’s presence in the Arctic. “After Senator Sullivan heard that funding had been cut for the Center, he reminded Secretary Noem of these conversations,” Amanda Coyne, the communications director for Sullivan told Nextgov/FCW. “Senator Sullivan is grateful that the secretary took immediate action to restore the funds.”The center, housed in the University of Alaska Anchorage, confirmed to Nextgov/FCW that termination of federal funding has been paused as of early April. “The University of Alaska Anchorage (UAA) received notice of termination and stop work on April 8, 2025,” said a spokesperson at the University of Alaska Anchorage. “On April 9, 2025, UAA received notice that award termination is paused. UAA has not received any further guidance from DHS at this time.”In addition, the National Counterterrorism Innovation, Technology, and Education Center, housed within University of Nebraska at Omaha had its funding termination reversed following discussions between Sen. Pete Ricketts, R-Neb. and Noem.Ricketts told Nextgov/FCW that a main focus of the NCITE is its research and analysis into cartel activity, how to secure U.S. borders and how to counter related terrorism.Featuring a consortium of researchers across the U.S. and Europe, NCITE in Nebraska acts as the nerve center for studying modern terrorism threats and what technologies can mitigate them. Some of the recent work to emerge from the NCITE consortium are documents discussing terrorism’s intersection with AI and the metaverse, how terrorists can use drones to cause harm, and how AI could potentially trick critical infrastructure operators.  “I encourage the administration to give their [NCITE’s] work a hard look,” he said.During a town hall in Scottsbluff, Nebraska on Friday, Ricketts said that he contacted Noem, pitching NCITE as a research hub in line with Trump administration priorities on securing the U.S.-Mexico border.“[Noem] said ‘I will keep [NCITE] funded through the fiscal year,’” Ricketts said. Officials from NCITE, as well, confirmed that it is awaiting further information following the pause in funding termination. “On April 8, the University of Nebraska at Omaha received a notification of award termination from DHS to end all work on its cooperative agreement,” the university told Nextgov/FCW. “A day later, DHS notified the university that the award termination was paused. NCITE and UNO have not received further updates from DHS.”DHS did not respond to a request for comment.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nCelebrating Citizen Science Month with new government-sponsored projects\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"参议员私下倡议挽救国土安全部卓越中心\nJ. David Ake/Getty Images\n将最新联邦技术新闻发送到您的收件箱。\n电子邮件 注册新闻通讯 保持联系 精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\n人工智能应用 立即阅读\nDOGE成立一年后的效率 立即阅读\n洞察与报告\n了解Atlassian政府云的投资回报率\nAtlassian呈现\n立即下载\n欺诈的未来五年：我们现在最好做好准备\nSocure呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nAlexandra Kelley，Nextgov/FCW特约记者\n作者\nAlexandra Kelley\n|\n2025年4月25日\n代表阿拉斯加州和内布拉斯加州的议员与国土安全部长Kristi Noem私下交谈，要求暂停终止两州卓越中心的资金。\n国土安全\n国会\n国土安全部的两个卓越中心已收到通知，其预定终止已被暂停，这似乎是两州参议员与国土安全部长Kristi Noem私下游说的结果。在该机构科学与技术局的卓越中心项目被取消后——该项目与大学建立合作关系，研究各种国家安全威胁——其中两个中心的计划终止被暂停：内布拉斯加大学奥马哈分校的NCITE，以及阿拉斯加大学安克雷奇分校的ADAC-ARCTIC。每个中心都专注于为国家最紧迫的国家安全问题开发技术解决方案。就ADAC-ARCITC而言，近期项目包括扩大白令海峡的无人机行动、支持专门面向原住民和土著民族的专业驻留项目，以及推动更广泛的北极教育。两州的参议员均确认，他们曾与国土安全部长Kristi Noem私下交谈，以叫停针对各自卓越中心的终止行动。共和党阿拉斯加州参议员Dan Sullivan告诉Nextgov/FCW，他曾与Noem进行“多次”交谈，讨论国土安全部科学与技术局中心在北极存在的战略重要性。Sullivan的通信主任Amanda Coyne告诉Nextgov/FCW：“Sullivan参议员听说该中心的资金被削减后，提醒了Noem部长他们的这些谈话。Sullivan参议员感谢部长立即采取行动恢复资金。”该中心设在阿拉斯加大学安克雷奇分校，该校向Nextgov/FCW确认，联邦资金的终止已于4月初暂停。阿拉斯加大学安克雷奇分校的一位发言人说：“阿拉斯加大学安克雷奇分校（UAA）于2025年4月8日收到终止和停止工作的通知。2025年4月9日，UAA收到通知，称该拨款终止已暂停。截至目前，UAA尚未收到国土安全部的进一步指示。”此外，设在内布拉斯加大学奥马哈分校内的国家反恐创新、技术与教育中心，在共和党内布拉斯加州参议员Pete Ricketts与Noem讨论后，其资金终止决定被撤销。Ricketts告诉Nextgov/FCW，NCITE的一个主要重点是研究和分析卡特尔活动、如何确保美国边境安全，以及如何应对相关恐怖主义。NCITE汇集了来自美国和欧洲各地的研究人员，在内布拉斯加州充当研究现代恐怖主义威胁以及哪些技术能够缓解这些威胁的神经中枢。NCITE联盟近期开展的一些工作包括文件\n讨论恐怖主义与人工智能和元宇宙的交集、恐怖分子如何利用无人机造成伤害，以及人工智能可能如何欺骗关键基础设施运营商。“我鼓励政府认真审视他们（NCITE）的工作，”他说。在周五于内布拉斯加州斯科茨布拉夫举行的市民大会上，Ricketts表示，他联系了Noem，提议将NCITE打造为一个研究中心，这与特朗普政府关于确保美国—墨西哥边境安全的优先事项一致。“[Noem]说：‘我会让[NCITE]获得本财年的资金支持，’”Ricketts说。NCITE的官员也证实，在暂停终止拨款后，该机构正在等待进一步信息。“4月8日，内布拉斯加大学奥马哈分校收到国土安全部（DHS）发出的终止拨款通知，要求结束其合作协议下的所有工作，”该大学向Nextgov/FCW表示。“一天后，DHS通知该大学，拨款终止程序已暂停。NCITE和UNO尚未收到DHS的进一步更新。”DHS未回应置评请求。\n分享此文：\n下一篇故事：\n用新的政府资助项目庆祝公民科学月\n本周科技法案：依据第702条监督人工智能的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多\nGenAI.mil记录了近170万名用户，并计划新增模型\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\n众议院委员会传唤甲骨文高管，要求其说明退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本不断膨胀的问题\n总务管理局网页设计系统负责人由财政部人工智能工程师接任\n赞助商内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作从劳动力队伍开始\n本周科技法案：依据第702条监督人工智能的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多\nGenAI.mil记录了近170万名用户，并计划新增模型\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\n众议院委员会传唤甲骨文高管，要求其说明退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本不断膨胀的问题\n总务管理局网页设计系统负责人由财政部人工智能工程师接任\n赞助商内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作从劳动力队伍开始","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2025-04-25T22:46:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3063","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"国土安全部任命安托万·麦科德为首席信息官","title_en":"DHS taps Antoine McCord for top tech post - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"美国国土安全部任命安托万·麦科德为新的首席信息官，负责监管该部门约110亿美元的信息技术预算。麦科德是前海军陆战队员，拥有情报界和私营部门经验，据消息人士称他对人工智能在机构中的应用充满热情。他上任之际正值政府效率部（DOGE）相关人员进驻该部门，其技术工作可能对特朗普政府的移民政策目标至关重要。","summary_en":"The Department of Homeland Security has named Antoine McCord as its new chief information officer, overseeing roughly $11 billion in IT spending. A former Marine with intelligence community and private sector experience, McCord is reportedly enthusiastic about AI use at the agency. He arrived alongside affiliates of the Department of Government Efficiency, and DHS tech may be central to the Trump administration's immigration policy objectives.","tag":"technology","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:59:32.520856+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/people/2025/03/dhs-taps-antoine-mccord-top-tech-post/403727","content":"DHS taps Antoine McCord for top tech post\nbpperry/Getty Images\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nNatalie Alms and David DiMolfetta\nBy\nNatalie Alms and David DiMolfetta\n|\nMarch 13, 2025\nMcCord is a former Marine and is excited about the use of AI in the agency, people familiar say.\nDHS\nCIOs or CXOs\nDOGE\nThe Department of Homeland Security has a new chief information officer. Antoine McCord started in the role overseeing all department tech at the agency Monday, according to two sources familiar with the matter. Nextgov/FCW also obtained an image showing McCord has a DHS email in the department’s internal directory.McCord landed in the department at the start of the week with affiliates of the government-slashing Department of Government Efficiency, according to one source familiar, who noted that the new CIO is excited about the use of artificial intelligence. He is a Marine with intelligence community and private sector experience, according to another person familiar who said that an email announcing his hire was sent out Thursday. In this role, McCord will be overseeing an IT budget of approximately $11 billion.Given that the department houses components like Customs and Border Protection, its tech may be central to the Trump administration’s policy objectives around immigration. Earlier this week, DHS announced that it was repurposing a Biden-era app to add a “self-deport” feature.McCord didn’t return a request for comment when emailed. DHS also did not respond to a request for comment.The DHS CIO role is an appointment. Google alum Eric Hysen — who was a founding member of what was formerly the U.S. Digital Service and has since been transformed into the DOGE — was the CIO for the department during the Biden administration. McCord is the latest confirmed agency CIO in the Trump administration, which is pushing to politicize these roles. The Energy Department just installed a new CIO who had formerly supported DOGE. This story is breaking and may be updated.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nEnergy appoints Twitter, Google and DOGE alum as new CIO\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"国土安全部任命 Antoine McCord 担任最高技术职位\nbpperry/Getty Images\n将最新联邦技术新闻发送到您的收件箱。\n电子邮箱注册新闻通讯保持联系精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\nAI 应用立即阅读DOGE 一年后的效率立即阅读洞察与报告\n探索 Atlassian Government Cloud 的投资回报率，Atlassian 呈现\n立即下载\n未来五年的欺诈：我们现在最好做好准备，Socure 呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nNatalie Alms 和 David DiMolfetta\n作者\nNatalie Alms 和 David DiMolfetta\n|\n2025年3月13日\n知情人士称，McCord 曾是海军陆战队员，并对该机构使用 AI 感到兴奋。\nDHS\nCIO 或 CXO\nDOGE\n据两位知情消息人士称，国土安全部有了一位新的首席信息官。Antoine McCord 于周一开始担任该职位，负责监督该部门在该机构的所有技术工作。Nextgov/FCW 还获得了一张图片，显示 McCord 在该部门内部通讯录中拥有 DHS 电子邮箱地址。\n一位知情消息人士称，McCord 于本周初与大幅削减政府开支的政府效率部附属人员一同进入该部门，并指出这位新 CIO 对使用人工智能感到兴奋。另一位知情人士称，他是一名海军陆战队员，拥有情报界和私营部门经验；该人士表示，一封宣布其聘任的电子邮件于周四发出。\n在这一职位上，McCord 将监督约 110 亿美元的 IT 预算。\n鉴于该部门下辖海关与边境保护局等组成机构，其技术可能对特朗普政府围绕移民问题的政策目标至关重要。本周早些时候，DHS 宣布正重新利用一款拜登时期的应用程序，以增加“自行遣返”功能。\n通过电子邮件联系时，McCord 未回应置评请求。DHS 同样未回应置评请求。\nDHS CIO 一职为任命职位。曾任 Google 员工的 Eric Hysen——他曾是原美国数字服务局的创始成员，该机构后来转型为 DOGE——在拜登政府期间担任该部门 CIO。\nMcCord 是特朗普政府最新一位获得确认的机构 CIO；该政府正推动使这些职位政治化。能源部刚刚任命了一位新 CIO，此人此前曾支持 DOGE。\n这是一起突发新闻，可能会更新。\n分享：\n下一篇报道：\n能源部任命曾任 Twitter、Google 和 DOGE 人员的新 CIO\n本周科技法案：监督第 702 条下 AI 的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌读取器；以及更多\nGenAI.mil 用户接近 170 万，计划新增模型\n独家\n联邦 CIO 称，特朗普政府希望 AI 能缓解人员流失\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本飙升传唤 Oracle 高管\nGSA 网站设计系统负责人由财政部 AI 工程师接替\n赞助内容\n联邦 AI 准备始于劳动力\n本周科技法案：监督第 702 条下 AI 的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌读取器；以及更多\nGenAI.mil 用户接近 170 万，计划新增模型\n独家\n联邦 CIO 称，特朗普政府希望 AI 能缓解人员流失\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本飙升传唤 Oracle 高管\nGSA 网站设计系统负责人由财政部 AI 工程师接替\n赞助内容\n联邦 AI 准备始于劳动力","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2025-03-13T18:32:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3015","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"凯伦·埃文斯在出任CISA职务数周后获提名任国土安全部副部长","title_en":"Karen Evans tapped for DHS undersecretary weeks after assuming CISA role - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"网络安全和基础设施安全局网络安全执行助理主任凯伦·埃文斯被提名出任国土安全部管理事务副部长，距其担任CISA职务仅数周。埃文斯曾在特朗普第一任期担任能源部网络安全、能源安全和应急响应助理部长，也曾任国土安全部首席信息官，并领导过美国网络挑战项目，在布什政府时期负责管理白宫预算办公室的电子政务与信息技术事务。","summary_en":"Karen Evans, executive assistant director for cybersecurity at CISA, was nominated to serve as Under Secretary for Management at DHS, just weeks after assuming her CISA role. A former Energy Department cyber official in Trump's first term, Evans previously served as DHS CIO and led the U.S. Cyber Challenge. During the George W. Bush administration, she oversaw OMB's electronic government and IT office and its $71 billion governmentwide IT portfolio.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:55:25.668931+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/people/2025/03/karen-evans-tapped-dhs-undersecretary-weeks-after-assuming-cisa-role/404131","content":"Karen Evans tapped for DHS undersecretary weeks after assuming CISA role\nKaren Evans speaks during her swearing in as assistant secretary of Energy cybersecurity on October 12, 2018. Evans was recently nominated to serve as the deputy under secretary for management at DHS.\nDONICA PAYNE/Department of Energy\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nMarch 28, 2025\nEvans had been selected as both a senior advisor and then executive assistant director for cybersecurity at CISA prior to her DHS nomination.\nLeadership\nDHS\nKaren Evans, the executive assistant director for cybersecurity at the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, was nominated Thursday to serve as Under Secretary for Management at DHS.The news comes just weeks after Evans, a former Energy Department cyber official in President Donald Trump’s first term, assumed the CISA role. Evans served as the first assistant secretary for cybersecurity, energy security and emergency response at Energy as an appointee in Trump’s first term. She also previously served as the CIO for the Department of Homeland Security. Prior to her selection for the executive assistant director role, Evans was serving as a senior advisor in CISA’s cybersecurity division.Prior to joining CISA, Evans most recently worked as managing director of the Cyber Readiness Institute, which, according to its website, seeks to “empower small and medium-sized enterprises with free tools and resources to help them become more secure and resilient.”Evans led the U.S. Cyber Challenge, a public-private initiative aimed at strengthening the cyber workforce, before taking up her position at Energy under the first Trump administration. She also contributed to Trump’s first transition team, handling government technology management. During the George W. Bush administration, she was administrator for the Office of Electronic Government and Information Technology in the Office of Management and Budget and oversaw its $71 billion governmentwide IT spending portfolio.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nTrump order aims to outlaw most government unions on ‘national security’ grounds\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"凯伦·埃文斯在担任 CISA 职务数周后获提名出任 DHS 次长\n凯伦·埃文斯于2018年10月12日在其能源部网络安全助理部长宣誓就职仪式上发言。埃文斯近日获提名担任 DHS 管理副次长。\nDONICA PAYNE/能源部\n将最新联邦技术新闻发送至您的收件箱。\n电子邮件 注册新闻通讯 保持联系 精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\nAI 应用 立即阅读 效率：DOGE 一年后 立即阅读 洞察与报告\n探索 Atlassian 政府云的投资回报率 由 Atlassian 呈现\n立即下载\n未来五年的欺诈：我们最好现在就做好准备 由 Socure 呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW 网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2025年3月28日\n埃文斯在获 DHS 提名之前，曾先后被选任为 CISA 网络安全部门的高级顾问和网络安全执行助理主任。\n领导层\nDHS\n网络安全和基础设施安全局（CISA）网络安全执行助理主任凯伦·埃文斯于周四获提名担任 DHS 管理次长。此消息传出仅数周前，曾在唐纳德·特朗普总统第一任期担任能源部网络安全官员的埃文斯刚刚出任 CISA 职务。埃文斯在特朗普第一任期内担任政治任命的首任能源部网络安全、能源安全与应急响应助理部长。她此前还担任过国土安全部 CIO。在获选担任执行助理主任之前，埃文斯曾任 CISA 网络安全部门高级顾问。\n加入 CISA 前，埃文斯最近担任网络准备研究所的常务董事。该研究所网站称，其目标是“通过提供免费工具和资源，赋能中小型企业，帮助它们变得更加安全、更具韧性。”\n在特朗普第一届政府期间赴能源部任职前，埃文斯曾领导美国网络挑战赛，这是一项旨在加强网络安全人才队伍的公私合作倡议。她还参与了特朗普第一届政府的过渡团队，负责政府技术管理工作。在乔治·W·布什政府期间，她担任管理和预算办公室电子政务和信息技术办公室主任，并监管规模达710亿美元的全政府 IT 支出组合。\n分享此文：\n下一篇：\n特朗普行政令拟以“国家安全”为由取缔大多数政府工会\n本周科技法案：监督第702条下 AI 的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌识别器；以及更多\nGenAI.mil 用户数近170万，计划新增模型\n独家\n联邦 CIO 称，特朗普政府希望 AI 能缓解人员流失\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本飙升传唤甲骨文负责人\nGSA 网站设计系统主管被财政部 AI 工程师取代\n赞助内容\n联邦 AI 准备工作始于劳动力队伍\n本周科技法案：监督第702条下 AI 的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌识别器；以及更多\nGenAI.mil 用户数近170万，计划新增模型\n独家\n联邦 CIO 称，特朗普政府希望 AI 能缓解人员流失\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本飙升传唤甲骨文负责人\nGSA 网站设计系统主管被财政部 AI 工程师取代\n赞助内容\n联邦 AI 准备工作始于劳动力队伍","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2025-03-28T19:15:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2768","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"FBI的布雷特·莱瑟曼将领导该局网络部门","title_en":"FBI’s Brett Leatherman to take lead of bureau’s cyber division - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"长期任职FBI的布雷特·莱瑟曼宣布，经局长卡什·帕特尔提拔，他将出任FBI网络部门助理局长，接替上月退休并加入微软的布莱恩·沃恩德兰。莱瑟曼此前担任网络行动副主管，长期对外介绍FBI针对国家黑客和网络犯罪分子的行动。他在采访中称，中国政府黑客组织和勒索软件团伙仍令他担忧，并对公私合作与盟友关系的改善表示乐观。","summary_en":"Longtime FBI official Brett Leatherman announced he will become assistant director of the bureau's Cyber Division, elevated by FBI Director Kash Patel and succeeding Brian Vorndran, who retired to join Microsoft. Previously deputy director for Cyber Operations, Leatherman has been a consistent public voice on FBI cyber takedowns targeting nation-state hackers and cybercriminals. He said Chinese government hacking groups and ransomware gangs still keep him up at night, while expressing optimism about public-private and international partnerships.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:17:48.916958+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/people/2025/06/fbis-brett-leatherman-take-lead-bureaus-cyber-division/405926","content":"FBI’s Brett Leatherman to take lead of bureau’s cyber division\nBrett Leatherman, previously the FBI's deputy director for cyber operations, will now serve as head of its Cyber Division.\nCourtesy: FBI\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nJune 9, 2025\nLeatherman said in a recent LinkedIn post that FBI Director Kash Patel elevated him to the role.\nFBI\nLeadership\nBrett Leatherman, a longtime FBI official who was most recently deputy director for Cyber Operations, will take over as assistant director of the bureau’s Cyber Division, he announced in a LinkedIn post Sunday.Leatherman said FBI Director Kash Patel elevated him to the position, taking over from Brian Vorndran, who retired from government service last month to join Microsoft as a deputy CISO.Leatherman has been a consistent voice detailing various FBI cyber takedown operations and other moves from agents to deter nation-state hackers and cybercriminals. In an interview with Nextgov/FCW at the RSAC Conference earlier this year, Leatherman said that Chinese government hacking groups and ransomware gangs continue to keep him up at night. He also said he was feeling better about improvements between public-private partnerships.“I’m feeling better about resilience overall in private industry,” he said. “We’re not there yet, but the relationships that we have built with the private sector … to move the needle when it comes to deterrence through defense.” He added that international partnerships have continued to improve.Leatherman has served for over 20 years at the FBI. He told Nextgov/FCW in a magazine interview earlier this year that he joined after the September 11 terrorist attacks.“At the time, the FBI recognized there was a significant need to shift their hiring practices to really focus on people with cyber skill sets, because it wasn’t just about cybercrime, but also terrorism, counter intelligence and criminal investigations. We needed agents who had more of a technical discipline to help with that,” he said.“Our charge is simple but not easy: make malicious cyber activity unsustainable. That means disrupting criminal and nation-state actors, sharing intelligence that helps victims defend and recover, and shaping the broader operating environment through persistent engagement,” Leatherman said in his Sunday LinkedIn post.Cynthia Kaiser, another Cyber Division official, recently departed to join ransomware-focused firm Halcyon.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nTrump is planning to slash 107,000 federal jobs next year. See where\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"FBI 的布雷特·莱瑟曼将领导该局网络司\n布雷特·莱瑟曼此前担任 FBI 网络行动副主任，现将出任其网络司负责人。\n图片来源：FBI\n将最新联邦科技新闻发送至您的收件箱。\n电子邮件 注册新闻通讯 保持联系 精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\nAI 应用 立即阅读 效率：DOGE 实施一年后 立即阅读 洞察与报告\n了解 Atlassian Government Cloud 的投资回报率 由 Atlassian 呈现\n立即下载\n未来五年的欺诈：我们现在最好做好准备 由 Socure 呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW 网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2025年6月9日\n莱瑟曼在最近的一篇 LinkedIn 帖子中表示，FBI 局长卡什·帕特尔将他提拔至这一职位。\nFBI\n领导层\n布雷特·莱瑟曼是一位长期任职于 FBI 的官员，最近担任网络行动副主任；他周日在一篇 LinkedIn 帖子中宣布，将接任该局网络司助理局长。\n莱瑟曼表示，FBI 局长卡什·帕特尔将他提拔至这一职位，接替布莱恩·沃恩德兰。沃恩德兰上月从政府部门退休，加入微软担任副首席信息安全官。\n莱瑟曼一直积极介绍 FBI 开展的各类网络打击行动，以及特工为威慑民族国家黑客和网络犯罪分子而采取的其他措施。今年早些时候，在 RSAC 大会期间接受 Nextgov/FCW 采访时，莱瑟曼表示，中国政府黑客组织和勒索软件团伙仍令他夜不能寐。他还表示，他对公私合作关系的改善感觉更好。\n“我对私营行业整体的韧性感觉更好了，”他说，“我们还没有达到目标，但我们与私营部门建立的关系……正在通过防御推动威慑取得进展。”他补充说，国际伙伴关系也在持续改善。\n莱瑟曼已在 FBI 任职超过20年。他今年早些时候在接受 Nextgov/FCW 杂志采访时表示，自己是在9·11恐怖袭击后加入 FBI 的。\n“当时，FBI 认识到，迫切需要调整招聘做法，真正聚焦于具备网络技能的人才，因为这不仅关乎网络犯罪，也关乎恐怖主义、反情报和刑事调查。我们需要具有更多技术专长的特工来协助开展这些工作，”他说。\n莱瑟曼在周日的 LinkedIn 帖子中表示：“我们的任务很简单，但并不容易：让恶意网络活动无法持续。这意味着破坏犯罪分子和民族国家行为体的活动，分享帮助受害者防御和恢复的情报，并通过持续参与塑造更广泛的运营环境。”\n网络司另一位官员辛西娅·凯泽最近离职，加入了专注于勒索软件的公司 Halcyon。\n分享：\n下一篇报道：\n特朗普计划明年削减107,000个联邦职位。看看涉及哪些领域\n本周科技法案：监督第702条下 AI 的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌识读器；以及更多\nGenAI.mil 用户数接近170万，计划新增模型\n独家\n联邦首席信息官称，特朗普政府希望 AI 能缓解人员流失\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录系统改革成本飙升传唤甲骨文高管\nGSA 网页设计系统负责人由财政部 AI 工程师接任\n赞助内容\n联邦 AI 准备工作始于劳动力队伍\n本周科技法案：监督第702条下 AI 的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌识读器；以及更多\nGenAI.mil 用户数接近170万，计划新增模型\n独家\n联邦首席信息官称，特朗普政府希望 AI 能缓解人员流失\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录系统改革成本飙升传唤甲骨文高管\nGSA 网页设计系统负责人由财政部 AI 工程师接任\n赞助内容\n联邦 AI 准备工作始于劳动力队伍","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2025-06-09T18:09:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1845","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"特朗普再次提名普兰基领导CISA","title_en":"Trump renominates Plankey to lead CISA - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"白宫周二表示，美国总统特朗普再次提名肖恩·普兰基出任网络安全和基础设施安全局局长，此前其提名在上年底未获参议院全体通过。CISA隶属于美国国土安全部，负责防御联邦网络和全国关键基础设施，已有一年没有常任局长，并在特朗普政府削减开支努力中流失约三分之一人员。参议员蒂利斯表示，在国土安全部长诺姆承诺出席参议院司法委员会作证前，将阻止所有国土安全部提名人选。","summary_en":"President Donald Trump re-nominated Sean Plankey to lead the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, the White House said Tuesday, after the nomination failed to clear the full Senate at the end of last year. CISA, located within the Department of Homeland Security, defends federal networks and critical infrastructure. It has been without a permanent leader for a year and has lost about a third of its workforce under Trump administration spending-cut efforts. Sen. Thom Tillis said he would block all DHS nominees until Secretary Kristi Noem commits to testifying.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:24:11.998864+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/people/2026/01/trump-renominates-plankey-lead-cisa/410664","content":"Trump renominates Plankey to lead CISA\nSean Plankey, nominee to be director of the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, testifies during his Senate Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs Committee confirmation hearing in Dirksen building on Thursday, July 24, 2025.\nTom Williams/CQ-Roll Call, Inc via Getty Images\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nJanuary 13, 2026\nThe cyberdefense agency has been without a permanent director for the past year.\nLeadership\nCISA\nWhite House\nPresident Donald Trump re-nominated Sean Plankey to lead the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, the White House said Tuesday, after the nomination failed to clear the full Senate at the end of last year.Plankey’s name was listed alongside dozens of other nominees that Trump had sent to the Senate.CISA is located within the Department of Homeland Security and is responsible for defending federal networks and critical infrastructure across the country. It’s been without a permanent leader for a year, and the office has lost around a third of its workforce under Trump 2.0 efforts to eliminate government spending waste.The timing for Plankey’s confirmation isn’t fully clear. Sen. Thom Tillis, R-N.C., said last week that he’d block all DHS nominees until Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem commits to testifying before the Senate Judiciary Committee.CISA’s current acting director, Madhu Gottumukkala, is also being scrutinized by congressional Democrats after a Politico report last month showed he failed a polygraph exam that may have been wrongfully administered.Plankey, a former Energy Department cybersecurity official in Trump’s first term, had faced multiple holds to his nomination in the Senate throughout last year, some tied to broader political disputes rather than cybersecurity policy itself.Sen. Rick Scott, R-Fla., placed a hold on Plankey’s nomination last year, which involved DHS scaling down a Coast Guard cutter contract with Florida-based Eastern Shipbuilding Group, three people familiar with the matter previously told Nextgov/FCW. Plankey is currently a senior adviser in the Coast Guard.Whoever ultimately leads CISA will do so in a midterm election year. Election matters fall under the agency’s statutory mission to protect critical infrastructure, which includes systems such as election management software and vote tabulation machines, though CISA has faced sustained GOP scrutiny in recent years over its past work monitoring and flagging online misinformation in coordination with social media companies.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nNSA alum returns to agency to serve as deputy director\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"特朗普再次提名 Plankey 领导 CISA\nSean Plankey，这位获提名担任网络安全与基础设施安全局局长的人，于2025年7月24日星期四在 Dirksen 大楼举行的参议院国土安全与政府事务委员会确认听证会上作证。\nTom Williams/CQ-Roll Call, Inc via Getty Images\n获取最新联邦技术新闻，直接发送到您的收件箱。\n电子邮件注册新闻通讯保持联系精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\nAI 应用立即阅读DOGE 一年后的效率立即阅读洞察与报告\n发现 Atlassian Government Cloud 的投资回报率由 Atlassian 呈现\n立即下载\n未来五年的欺诈：我们现在就要做好准备由 Socure 呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW 网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2026年1月13日\n该网络防御机构过去一年一直没有正式局长。\n领导层\nCISA\n白宫\n白宫周二表示，特朗普总统再次提名 Sean Plankey 领导网络安全与基础设施安全局，此前该提名在去年年底未能通过参议院全院表决。Plankey 的名字与特朗普提交给参议院的数十名其他被提名人一起列出。CISA 隶属于国土安全部，负责保卫全国联邦网络和关键基础设施。在特朗普第二任期旨在消除政府支出浪费的行动下，该机构已一年没有正式领导人，办公室员工也减少了约三分之一。Plankey 的确认时间尚不完全明确。北卡罗来纳州共和党参议员 Thom Tillis 上周表示，在国土安全部长 Kristi Noem 承诺在参议院司法委员会作证之前，他将阻止所有国土安全部被提名人。CISA 现任代理局长 Madhu Gottumukkala 也受到国会民主党人的审查，此前 Politico 上月报道称，他未通过测谎考试，而该考试可能存在不当实施的问题。Plankey 曾在特朗普第一任期担任能源部网络安全官员，去年在参议院内曾多次遭到对其提名的搁置，其中一些与更广泛的政治争议有关，而非网络安全政策本身。佛罗里达州共和党参议员 Rick Scott 去年搁置了 Plankey 的提名；此前三名知情人士告诉 Nextgov/FCW，这涉及国土安全部缩减与佛罗里达州 Eastern Shipbuilding Group 签订的一份海岸警卫队舰艇合同。Plankey 目前是海岸警卫队的高级顾问。最终领导 CISA 的人将在中期选举年履职。选举事务属于该机构保护关键基础设施的法定使命，其中包括选举管理软件和选票计票机等系统，不过近年来，CISA 过去与社交媒体公司合作监测和标记网络错误信息的工作持续受到共和党人的审查。\n分享本文：\n下一篇：\nNSA 校友重返该机构担任副局长\n本周科技法案：监测人工智能在第702条下的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\nGenAI.mil 用户数量已接近170万，并计划新增模型\n众议院委员会传唤 Oracle 高管，调查退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本不断膨胀的问题\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的\n网站\n赞助商内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作始于劳动力\n本周科技法案：监控第702条下的人工智能使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\nGenAI.mil记录的用户数接近170万，并计划增加新的模型\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本不断膨胀一事传唤甲骨文公司高管\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助商内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作始于劳动力","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2026-01-13T22:48:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1508","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"CISA代理局长调任国土安全部新职","title_en":"CISA acting director moved to new DHS role - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"据国土安全部官员向Nextgov/FCW证实，网络安全和基础设施安全局（CISA）代理局长Madhu Gottumukkala将调任国土安全部战略实施主任，CISA网络安全执行助理局长Nick Andersen将接任代理局长。报道提及Gottumukkala近期受到多家媒体负面报道，包括测谎未通过、将敏感文件上传至ChatGPT等。CISA目前仍无正式局长，特朗普政府已重新提名Sean Plankey。","summary_en":"A DHS official confirmed to Nextgov/FCW that Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency acting director Madhu Gottumukkala will move to a new role as director of strategic implementation at DHS, with Nick Andersen, CISA's executive assistant director for cybersecurity, becoming acting director. The report notes recent unflattering media coverage of Gottumukkala, including a failed polygraph and uploading sensitive files to ChatGPT. CISA still lacks a permanent director; the administration renominated Sean Plankey.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T15:27:57.711370+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/people/2026/02/cisa-acting-director-moved-new-dhs-role/411737","content":"CISA acting director moved to new DHS role\nMadhu Gottumukkala, acting director of the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, testifies during the DHS oversight hearing in the Cannon House office building on January 21, 2026\nHeather Diehl/Getty Images)\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nAccelerating Discovery in the AI EraPresented By Google Public Sector\nDownload Now\nCDW and Google Public Sector deliver a unified, mission-focused technology ecosystemPresented By Google Public Sector\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nFebruary 26, 2026\nMadhu Gottumukkala will be moved into a strategic implementation role in the Department of Homeland Security. Nick Andersen will take his place.\nCISA\nLeadership\nCybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency acting director Madhu Gottumukkala is expected to depart the agency for a new role amid news of several leadership shakeups and incidents during his tenure, a DHS official confirmed to Nextgov/FCW. Gottumukkala will now serve as director of strategic implementation at DHS, which houses the cyberdefense agency. Nick Andersen, who has served as the executive assistant director for cybersecurity at CISA, will now be the agency’s acting director, added the official.The DHS official spoke on the condition of anonymity to discuss the new roles. Andersen did not immediately return a request for comment. ABC News first reported on the personnel moves.Gottumukkala has recently been the subject of several unflattering Politico reports, including one describing a failed counterintelligence polygraph exam, another noting that he uploaded sensitive files into ChatGPT and a third describing how he was involved in an initial reassignment order for CISA Chief Information Officer Bob Costello in January.Costello was again handed reassignment orders recently, per Thursday reporting.The new roles come as CISA has lost much of its workforce to a slew of efforts the Trump administration undertook in the last year to reduce purported government bloat and spending waste. Former officials and other observers have pushed back on some of those cuts.The cyberdefense agency still does not have a permanent director. The Trump administration renominated Sean Plankey for that role earlier this year after his initial nomination lapsed at the close of 2025.A senior DHS official said Gottumukkala “has done a remarkable job in a thankless task of helping reform CISA back to its core statutory mission” and that “he will continue his work to save taxpayer money” in his new role. The senior official also said the Trump administration is looking forward to Plankey’s confirmation.Editor’s Note: This story has been updated to add comments from a senior DHS official.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nWyden to block confirmation for NSA director, citing lack of experience\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nAI is ready for federal health’s hardest problems\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nAI is ready for federal health’s hardest problems","content_cn":"CISA代理局长调任国土安全部新职务\n网络安全与基础设施安全局代理局长Madhu Gottumukkala于2026年1月21日在坎农大厦举行的国土安全部监督听证会上作证\nHeather Diehl/Getty Images）\n获取最新联邦技术新闻，发送至您的收件箱。\n电子邮件注册新闻通讯保持联系精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\n人工智能应用立即阅读DOGE实施一年后的效率立即阅读洞察与报告\n人工智能时代的加速发现由Google公共部门提供\n立即下载\nCDW与Google公共部门提供统一、使命导向的技术生态系统由Google公共部门提供\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2026年2月26日\nMadhu Gottumukkala将调任国土安全部战略实施职务。Nick Andersen将接替他。\nCISA\n领导层\n一名国土安全部官员向Nextgov/FCW证实，网络安全与基础设施安全局代理局长Madhu Gottumukkala预计将离开该机构，出任新职务；在其任期内，该机构经历了数起领导层变动和事件。Gottumukkala现在将担任国土安全部战略实施主任；该部负责网络防御机构。该官员补充说，曾任CISA网络安全执行助理局长的Nick Andersen将担任该机构代理局长。国土安全部官员以匿名为条件谈论新职务。Andersen未立即回应置评请求。ABC新闻最先报道了这些人事变动。最近，Gottumukkala成为Politico多篇负面报道的对象，其中一篇称其未通过反情报测谎考试，另一篇指出他将敏感文件上传到ChatGPT，还有一篇描述他参与了1月份对CISA首席信息官Bob Costello的初次调任命令。据周四报道，Costello最近再次收到调任命令。这些新职务公布之际，CISA的大量员工因特朗普政府去年开展的一系列削减所谓政府臃肿和支出浪费的行动而离职。前官员和其他观察人士对其中一些削减提出了反对意见。该网络防御机构仍没有正式局长。今年早些时候，特朗普政府重新提名Sean Plankey担任该职务，此前他的首次提名在2025年底到期。一名国土安全部高级官员表示，Gottumukkala“在一项吃力不讨好的任务中帮助改革CISA，使其回归法定核心使命方面做得非常出色”，并且“他将在新职务上继续为节省纳税人资金而努力”。这名高级官员还表示，特朗普政府期待Plankey获得确认。\n编者按：本文已更新，加入国土安全部高级官员的评论。\n分享：\n下一篇：\nWyden将阻止确认NSA局长，理由是缺乏经验\nGenAI.mil记录近170万用户，计划增加新模型\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官称\n众议院小组委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本膨胀传唤甲骨文负责人\nGSA网页设计系统负责人被财政部人工智能工程师替换\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\n人工智能已准备好应对联邦医疗领域最棘手的问题\nGenAI.mil记录近170万用户，计划增加新模型\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官称\n众议院小组委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本膨胀传唤甲骨文负责人\nGSA网页设计系统负责人被财政部人工智能工程师替换\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\n人工智能已准备好应对联邦医疗领域最棘手的问题","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2026-02-27T00:53:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1512","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"CISA首席信息官收到调职令后预计将离开该机构","title_en":"CISA CIO expected to leave agency after receiving transfer orders - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"据三名知情人士透露，美国网络安全和基础设施安全局（CISA）首席信息官鲍勃·科斯特洛已收到调职令，预计将很快离开这个隶属国土安全部的网络机构。科斯特洛手中有多个下一份工作的选择，尚不清楚是转往其他政府机构还是进入业界。上月CISA代理局长曾试图将其调走，但被其他政治任命官员阻止。CISA未回应置评请求。","summary_en":"According to three people familiar with the matter, Bob Costello, chief information officer of the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA), has been handed reassignment orders and is expected to soon leave the DHS cyber agency. Costello has multiple offers for his next job, though it is unclear whether they involve another government agency or industry. Last month, CISA's acting director reportedly took steps to transfer him, but other political appointees blocked it. CISA did not respond to a request for comment.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:13:05.198794+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/people/2026/02/cisa-cio-expected-leave-agency-after-receiving-transfer-orders/411718","content":"CISA CIO expected to leave agency after receiving transfer orders\nThomas Fuller/SOPA Images/LightRocket via Getty Images\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nFebruary 26, 2026\nLast month, the cyber agency’s acting director reportedly took steps to transfer Costello, but other political appointees blocked it.\nCISA\nLeadership\nThe Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency’s chief information officer has been handed reassignment orders and is expected to soon transfer out of the DHS cyber agency, according to three people familiar with the matter.Bob Costello, the chief information officer, has multiple offers on the table for his next job, though it’s not clear if those offers include opportunities to go to another government agency or leave for industry, the one of the three people said.All sources spoke on the condition of anonymity to communicate details of the transfer.Last month, CISA’s acting director Madhu Gottumukkala reportedly took steps to transfer Costello, but other political appointees blocked it.Costello has been widely viewed as a well-liked and talented CIO in the agency, appearing at government and industry events. The motivations for the transfer orders are not clear.Costello did not respond to a request for comment. Nextgov/FCW has also asked CISA for comment.Cybersecurity Dive first reported the transfer and said Costello was offered reassignment to FEMA.This story is breaking and may be updated.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nDOJ elevates deputy CIO to top IT role\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"据报道，CISA首席信息官在收到调任命令后预计将离开该机构\nThomas Fuller/SOPA Images/LightRocket via Getty Images\n获取最新联邦技术新闻，直接发送到您的收件箱。\n电子邮件注册新闻简报保持联系精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\n人工智能应用立即阅读\nDOGE一年之后的效率立即阅读\n洞察与报告\n发现Atlassian Government Cloud的投资回报率\n由Atlassian呈现\n立即下载\n未来五年的欺诈：我们最好现在就做好准备\n由Socure呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2026年2月26日\n据报道，上个月，这家网络机构的代理局长采取措施调任Costello，但其他政治任命官员阻止了这一行动。\nCISA\n领导层\n据三名知情人士透露，网络安全与基础设施安全局（CISA）的首席信息官已收到调任命令，预计很快将调离国土安全部旗下的网络机构。据其中一人称，首席信息官Bob Costello手头有多份下一份工作的邀请，但目前尚不清楚这些邀请是否包括前往另一家政府机构的机会，或是离开政府转入企业界。所有消息人士都要求匿名，以便透露调任详情。据报道，上个月，CISA代理局长Madhu Gottumukkala采取措施调任Costello，但其他政治任命官员阻止了这一行动。Costello在该机构内一直被广泛认为是一位受欢迎且有才华的首席信息官，曾出席政府和行业活动。目前尚不清楚发出调任命令的动机。Costello未回应置评请求。Nextgov/FCW也已向CISA寻求评论。Cybersecurity Dive首先报道了此次调任，并称Costello获提供调任至联邦紧急事务管理局（FEMA）的职位。本报道正在更新中，后续可能会有变化。\n分享此文：\n下一篇：\n司法部提拔副首席信息官担任最高IT职位\n本周科技法案：监测人工智能在《702条款》下的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官称\nGenAI.mil用户数已近170万，并计划新增模型\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部电子健康记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤甲骨文高管\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作从劳动力队伍开始\n本周科技法案：监测人工智能在《702条款》下的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官称\nGenAI.mil用户数已近170万，并计划新增模型\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部电子健康记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤甲骨文高管\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作从劳动力队伍开始","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2026-02-26T18:18:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1476","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"CISA在近期领导层变动后任命新的代理网络主管","title_en":"CISA appoints new acting cyber chief after recent leadership shakeups - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"克里斯·布特拉将出任美国网络安全和基础设施安全局网络安全部门的代理执行助理主任，此前该职位担任者尼克·安德森接掌CISA领导职务。上周，前CISA代理局长马杜·戈图穆卡拉被调往国土安全部另一岗位。布特拉过去两年多次在过渡期担任CISA技术与领导职务。与此同时，CISA网络部门代理副主任谢莉·哈特苏克预计将在近日离职，该机构仍在持续流失人才。","summary_en":"Chris Butera will serve as the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency's acting executive assistant director for its cybersecurity division, after prior occupant Nick Andersen stepped into the top leadership role at the agency. Last week, former CISA acting director Madhu Gottumukkala was moved to another role in DHS. Butera has cycled through CISA technical and leadership roles during transitions over the past two years. Meanwhile, Shelly Hartsook, acting associate director in CISA's cyber division, is expected to leave soon as the agency continues losing talent.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:12:36.507524+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/people/2026/03/cisa-appoints-new-acting-cyber-chief-after-recent-leadership-shakeups/411833","content":"CISA appoints new acting cyber chief after recent leadership shakeups\nChris Butera was recently named acting executive assistant director for CISA's cyber division.\nCISA\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nMarch 3, 2026\nChris Butera is now serving as acting executive assistant director for CISA’s cybersecurity division, after its former occupant, Nick Andersen, stepped into the top leadership role at the cyber agency.\nCISA\nLeadership\nChris Butera will serve as the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency’s acting executive assistant director for the agency’s cybersecurity division, following leadership changes put into place at the agency last week.Prior CISA acting director Madhu Gottumukkala was moved to another role in DHS, while Nick Andersen — the previous executive assistant director for the cyber division — took his place helming the agency.“As CISA’s senior career cybersecurity official, he is responsible for leading the agency’s mission to address the nation’s most significant cyber threats and vulnerabilities and to increase the security and resilience of U.S. critical infrastructure,” an agency bio page for Butera says.Butera has cycled through CISA technical and leadership roles over the past two years, often during transitions. He served as the Cybersecurity Division’s senior technical director before becoming acting executive assistant director in June 2025, leading the agency’s cyber mission until September, when Andersen was named to the permanent post.The change comes shortly after Gottumukkala became the subject of several unflattering media reports that led up to his reassignment elsewhere in DHS.The new roles come as CISA has lost much of its workforce to a slew of efforts the Trump administration undertook in the last year to reduce purported government bloat and spending waste. Former officials and other observers have pushed back on some of those cuts.The agency continues to lose talent. Shelly Hartsook, acting associate director in CISA’s cyber division, is expected to leave in the coming days, a person familiar with the matter said. The planned departure was first reported by Federal News Network.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nCISA acting director moved to new DHS role\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"CISA任命新的代理网络安全负责人，此前该机构近期经历领导层变动\n克里斯·布特拉最近被任命为CISA网络部门的代理执行助理局长。\nCISA\n将最新的联邦技术新闻发送到您的收件箱。\n电子邮件 注册新闻通讯 保持联系 精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\n人工智能应用 立即阅读\nDOGE实施一年后的效率 立即阅读\n洞察与报告\n发现Atlassian Government Cloud的投资回报率\n由Atlassian呈现\n立即下载\n未来五年的欺诈：我们现在最好做好准备\n由Socure呈现\n立即下载\n作者\n大卫·迪莫尔费塔，Nextgov/FCW网络安全记者\n作者\n大卫·迪莫尔费塔\n|\n2026年3月3日\n在前任负责人尼克·安德ersen转任该网络安全机构最高领导职务后，克里斯·布特拉现任CISA网络安全部门的代理执行助理局长。\nCISA\n领导层\n克里斯·布特拉将担任网络安全与基础设施安全局（CISA）网络安全部门的代理执行助理局长，此前该机构上周实施了领导层调整。CISA前代理局长马杜·戈图穆卡拉被调往国土安全部的另一职位，而此前担任网络部门执行助理局长的尼克·安德ersen接替了他，领导该机构。CISA关于布特拉的机构简介页面称：“作为CISA资深的职业网络安全官员，他负责领导该机构履行应对国家最重大的网络威胁和漏洞的使命，并提高美国关键基础设施的安全性和韧性。”过去两年，布特拉在CISA的技术和领导岗位之间轮换，常常发生在过渡时期。他曾任网络安全部门高级技术主管，随后于2025年6月成为代理执行助理局长，领导该机构的网络任务，直到9月安德ersen被任命担任正式职务。这一变动发生前不久，戈图穆卡拉成为多篇负面媒体报道的对象，最终被调往国土安全部其他部门。新的人事安排出台之际，CISA的大量人员已因特朗普政府过去一年为削减所谓政府臃肿和支出浪费而采取的一系列措施离职。前任官员和其他观察人士对其中一些削减措施提出了反对意见。该机构仍在流失人才。一名知情人士称，CISA网络部门代理副局长谢莉·哈茨库克预计将在未来几天离职。联邦新闻网络首先报道了这一计划中的离职。\n分享：\n下一篇：\nCISA代理局长转任国土安全部新职务\n本周科技法案：依据第702条监控人工智能的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\nGenAI.mil记录了近170万用户，计划新增模型\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤甲骨文高管\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作从劳动力开始\n本周科技法案：依据第702条监控人工智能的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\nGenAI.mil记录了近170万用户，计划新增模型\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤甲骨文高管\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作从劳动力开始","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2026-03-03T17:15:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1035","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"IBM安全高管成为CISA局长潜在人选","title_en":"IBM security executive emerges as possible contender to lead CISA - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"据五名知情人士透露，IBM安全服务负责人汤姆·帕克成为领导美国网络安全和基础设施安全局（CISA）的潜在人选。帕克拥有约二十年网络安全行业经验，但无政府工作经历。消息人士称，特朗普政府目前倾向选择他，国土安全部长马威·穆林也偏好仅具私营部门经验的CISA局长人选。CISA自去年以来一直无正式局长，前候选人肖恩·普兰基上月退出。国土安全部发言人表示目前没有人事公告。","summary_en":"Tom Parker, a security services lead at IBM with about two decades of cybersecurity industry experience, has emerged as a potential contender to lead the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA), according to five people familiar with the matter. Parker has no prior government experience, and one source said he is currently the Trump administration's preferred choice. Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin has favored a CISA director with only private-sector experience, another person said. CISA has lacked a permanent director since last year; previous nominee Sean Plankey withdrew last month. A DHS spokesperson said the agency has no personnel announcements at this time.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:03:18.750026+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/people/2026/05/ibm-security-executive-emerges-possible-contender-lead-cisa/413291","content":"IBM security executive emerges as possible contender to lead CISA\nThomas Fuller/SOPA Images/LightRocket via Getty Images\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nMay 3, 2026\nTom Parker doesn’t have prior government experience, characteristics the Trump administration may be seeking in its next pick to lead the cyber agency, a person familiar said.\nCISA\nCyber Threats\nTom Parker, a security services lead at IBM with some two decades of experience in the cybersecurity industry, has emerged as a potential contender to lead the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency after the most recent nominee withdrew himself from consideration for the role, according to five people familiar with the matter. Parker does not have prior government experience. As of now, he is the preferred choice for the Trump administration, one of the people said. Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin has been favoring a CISA director with only private sector experience, another one of the people said.All sources spoke on the condition of anonymity because they weren’t authorized to publicly communicate details concerning the administration’s thinking. Some of the people cautioned that the process is fluid and that the White House may go in a different direction. Parker did not respond to a request for comment. A DHS spokesperson said the agency “has no personnel announcements to make at this time.\"Nextgov/FCW has also asked the White House comment.Parker has held a number of roles in industry throughout his career. He founded cyber solutions firm Hubble, which was acquired in 2024. He was also inducted as a technology pioneer at the World Economic Forum, and he has for years been a speaker and panelist at Black Hat, an annual cybersecurity conference held in Las Vegas.CISA has been without a permanent director since President Donald Trump retook office last year. For the last year, Sean Plankey had been nominated to lead the cyberdefense agency, but withdrew late last month after being caught up in issues concerning Coast Guard cutter contracts with a GOP senator. Nick Andersen has been leading the agency in an acting capacity since its previous acting leader, Madhu Gottumukkala, left in February following a series of leadership incidents during his tenure.The agency has lost around a third of its workforce in the last year amid Trump administration efforts to shrink the size of the federal government and address other long-prevailing GOP concerns about the cyber agency’s activities. Parker has previously opined on the federal cyber reductions.“I think because an agency changes shape and/or the mission moves to other agencies doesn’t mean that the government doesn’t see the mission as being more or less important anymore. I think it’s important to not assume that that’s the case,” he said in a Dark Reading podcast recording last year.CISA's parent agency, DHS, has just emerged from a record funding lapse. A major trade association for government contractors warned last week that it will take time for federal operations to return to normal and for some contractors to get financial relief from missed reimbursements due to the shutdown.Editor's note: This article has been updated to include comment from DHS.\nIf you have a tip you'd like to share, David can be securely contacted at djd.99 on Signal.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nThe 2026 Federal 100\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"IBM安全主管成为领导CISA的潜在人选\nThomas Fuller/SOPA Images/LightRocket via Getty Images\n获取最新联邦技术新闻，直接发送到您的收件箱。\n电子邮件注册订阅新闻通讯保持联系精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\nAI应用立即阅读DOGE实施一年后的效率立即阅读洞察与报告\n发现Atlassian政府云的投资回报率由Atlassian呈现\n立即下载\n欺诈的未来五年：我们最好现在就做好准备由Socure呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2026年5月3日\n一位知情人士称，汤姆·帕克此前没有政府工作经验，而这可能正是特朗普政府在下一任网络机构负责人候选人身上寻求的特征。\nCISA\n网络威胁\n据五位知情人士透露，IBM安全服务负责人汤姆·帕克在网络安全行业拥有约20年的经验，在最近一名提名人退出该职位的考虑后，他已成为领导网络安全与基础设施安全局（CISA）的潜在人选。帕克此前没有政府工作经验。其中一人表示，目前他是特朗普政府的首选。另一位知情人士称，国土安全部长Markwayne Mullin一直倾向于选择一名只有私营部门经验的CISA局长。所有消息人士都以匿名为条件接受采访，因为他们无权公开谈论有关政府考虑的细节。一些人士提醒说，该流程仍在变化，白宫可能会转向不同方向。帕克没有回应置评请求。国土安全部发言人表示，该机构“目前没有人员任命公告。”Nextgov/FCW也已向白宫请求置评。帕克职业生涯中曾在业界担任多个职务。他创办了网络解决方案公司Hubble，该公司于2024年被收购。他还入选世界经济论坛的技术先驱，并多年来一直担任在拉斯维加斯举行的年度网络安全会议Black Hat的演讲者和小组讨论嘉宾。自唐纳德·特朗普总统去年重新上任以来，CISA一直没有常任局长。在过去一年中，Sean Plankey曾被提名领导这一网络防御机构，但上月底，在因涉及海岸警卫队快艇合同、牵涉一名共和党参议员的问题中受到牵连后，他退出了提名。自上一任代理负责人Madhu Gottumukkala在任期间发生一系列领导层事件并于2月离职后，Nick Andersen一直以代理身份领导该机构。在过去一年中，该机构的员工减少了约三分之一，这发生在特朗普政府努力缩减联邦政府规模、并应对共和党长期以来对该网络机构活动的其他担忧之际。帕克此前曾就联邦网络安全力量削减发表看法。“我认为，一个机构改变了形态，和/或使命转移到其他机构，并不意味着政府不再认为这一使命或多或少重要。我认为，重要的是不要假定情况就是这样，”他在去年一次Dark Reading播客录音中说。CISA的上级机构国土安全部刚刚结束了一次创纪录的资金中断。政府承包商的一个主要行业协会上周警告说，这将需要一段时间，才能让联邦\n恢复正常运营，并让一些承包商能够因停摆期间错过的报销获得财务救济。编辑注：本文已更新，加入了国土安全部（DHS）的评论。\n如果你有想分享的线索，可以通过 Signal 安全联系 David，用户名是 djd.99。\n分享本文：\n下一篇：\n2026年联邦百强\n本周科技法案：根据第702条监控人工智能的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\nGenAI.mil 记录了近170万用户，并计划新增模型\n众议院委员会传唤甲骨文公司负责人，要求说明退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本不断膨胀的问题\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用 Login.gov\n赞助内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作从劳动力队伍开始\n本周科技法案：根据第702条监控人工智能的使用；防止滥用 Flock 车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员流失，联邦首席信息官表示\nGenAI.mil 记录了近170万用户，并计划新增模型\n众议院委员会传唤甲骨文公司负责人，要求说明退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本不断膨胀的问题\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用 Login.gov\n赞助内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作从劳动力队伍开始","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2026-05-03T23:31:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-914","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"瑞安·多纳吉重返CISA，出任首位首席运营官","title_en":"Ryan Donaghy returns to CISA as first chief operating officer - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"据Nextgov/FCW报道，去年秋天离开网络安全和基础设施安全局（CISA）转任运输安全管理局的瑞安·多纳吉本周重返CISA，担任该机构首位首席运营官。CISA在领英公告中称，多纳吉将作为高级领导层首席顾问，负责机构运营、业务职能、财务与采购管理、政策制定及跨部门工作，并确保相关努力符合局长优先事项及法律法规，同时加强与国土安全部的协调。报道称，CISA多个部门正因特朗普时期的裁员与重组而面临调整，白宫拟在2027财年将该机构预算净削减3.6亿美元。","summary_en":"Ryan Donaghy returned to the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency this week as its first chief operating officer after leaving last fall for the Transportation Security Administration, according to Nextgov/FCW. CISA said in a LinkedIn post that Donaghy will serve as principal advisor to senior leadership on agency operations, business functions, financial and acquisition management, policy development and interagency efforts, ensuring compliance with laws and strengthening coordination across programs and DHS. Several CISA units face restructuring amid Trump-era cuts, and the White House seeks a net $360 million budget reduction for fiscal 2027.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T10:59:10.629379+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/people/2026/05/ryan-donaghy-returns-cisa-first-chief-operating-officer/413724","content":"Ryan Donaghy returns to CISA as first chief operating officer\nThomas Fuller/SOPA Images/LightRocket via Getty Images\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nDiscover the ROI of Atlassian Government CloudPresented By Atlassian\nDownload Now\nThe Next Five Years of Fraud: We Better Get Ready NowPresented By Socure\nDownload Now\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta,Cybersecurity Reporter, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\nMay 22, 2026\nDonaghy previously served in acting leadership roles across two of the agency’s divisions and moved to the Transportation Security Administration in October.\nCISA\nLeadership\nAfter departing the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency last fall, Ryan Donaghy returned to the agency this week to serve as its first chief operating officer, according to a Thursday announcement.Donaghy, who held acting director roles in two of the cyberdefense agency’s divisions, had moved to the Transportation Security Administration in October of last year, Nextgov/FCW first reported.“As COO, Ryan will serve as the principal advisor to CISA’s Senior Leadership on agency operations, business functions, financial and acquisition management, policy development, and interagency efforts supporting our strategic goals,” the agency said in a LinkedIn post. “She will help ensure these efforts reflect the Director’s priorities and comply with applicable laws and regulations, while strengthening coordination across enterprise programs and DHS.”Donaghy has held several leadership assignments in the cyberdefense agency, including chief of metrics and analysis within the Office of Infrastructure Protection, senior data scientist in the National Risk Management Center and deputy chief for chemical security policy, rulemaking and engagement. Several of CISA’s units have been targeted for restructuring amid sweeping Trump-era cuts and reorganization efforts at the agency.CISA has faced staffing reductions, program reviews and broader shakeups as the administration moves to scale back parts of the federal cybersecurity and infrastructure security apparatus. The White House is seeking to reduce the agency’s budget by a net amount of $360 million for the 2027 fiscal year.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nHHS to start Schedule P/C conversions while withholding details on new RIFs\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce\nTech bills of the week: Monitoring AI’s use under Section 702; Preventing abuse of Flock plate readers; and more\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nFederal AI readiness starts with the workforce","content_cn":"瑞安·多纳吉回到CISA，担任首任首席运营官\nThomas Fuller/SOPA Images/LightRocket via Getty Images\n获取最新联邦技术新闻，直接发送到您的收件箱。\n电子邮件 注册新闻通讯 保持联系 精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\n人工智能应用 立即阅读\nDOGE实施一年后的效率 立即阅读\n洞察与报告\n发现Atlassian政府云的投资回报\n由Atlassian呈现\n立即下载\n欺诈的未来五年：我们最好现在就做好准备\n由Socure呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta，Nextgov/FCW网络安全记者\n作者\nDavid DiMolfetta\n|\n2026年5月22日\n多纳吉此前曾在该机构两个部门担任代理领导职务，并于10月转往运输安全管理局。\nCISA\n领导层\n据周四发布的一则公告，去年秋天离开网络安全与基础设施安全局后，瑞安·多纳吉本周回到该机构，担任其首任首席运营官。多纳吉曾在这家网络防御机构的两个部门担任代理局长，并于去年10月转往运输安全管理局，Nextgov/FCW最先报道了这一消息。该机构在领英帖子中说：“作为首席运营官，瑞安将担任CISA高级领导层在机构运营、业务职能、财务与采购管理、政策制定以及支持我们战略目标的机构间工作的首席顾问。她将帮助确保这些工作体现局长的优先事项并遵守适用的法律法规，同时加强企业项目与国土安全部之间的协调。”多纳吉在该网络防御机构担任过多个领导职务，包括基础设施保护办公室的指标与分析主管、国家风险管理中心的高级数据科学家，以及化学安全政策、规则制定与参与事务副主管。在特朗普时期的大规模削减和机构重组工作中，CISA的多个部门成为重组目标。随着政府着手缩减联邦网络安全和基础设施安全体系的部分规模，CISA面临人员削减、项目审查和更广泛的调整。白宫寻求在2027财年将该机构预算净削减3.6亿美元。\n分享本文：\n下一篇：\n卫生与公众服务部将开始进行P/C计划转换，同时对新的裁员细节保持缄默\n本周科技法案：监控第702条下人工智能的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员损失，联邦首席信息官称\nGenAI.mil记录了近170万用户，计划新增模型\n众议院小组委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤甲骨文高管\n白宫要求各机构为面向公众的网站使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作始于劳动力\n本周科技法案：监控第702条下人工智能的使用；防止滥用Flock车牌读取器；以及更多\n独家\n特朗普政府希望人工智能能够缓解人员损失，联邦首席信息官称\nGenAI.mil记录了近170万用户，计划新增模型\n众议院小组委员会就退伍军人事务部医疗记录改革成本不断膨胀传唤甲骨文高管\n白宫要求各机构为面向公众的网站使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\n联邦人工智能准备工作始于劳动力","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2026-05-22T16:03:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-734","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"FBI任命卡尔·罗伯特·舒曼为新任首席信息官","title_en":"FBI taps Karl Robert Schumann as new CIO - Nextgov/FCW","summary_cn":"据司法部全部门技术官员名单，长期任职FBI的卡尔·罗伯特·舒曼成为该局新任常任首席信息官，此前凯蒂·伍德担任代理CIO。舒曼在FBI工作逾20年，2005年以特工身份加入，逐步晋升，近期担任副助理局长，此前任企业审查中心部门主管。作为FBI最高IT官员，他将负责监督该局的数字现代化、网络安全工作和科技治理战略。加入FBI前，他曾在美空军服役20年。","summary_en":"Longtime FBI official Karl Robert Schumann is the agency's new permanent chief information officer, according to the Justice Department's list of top departmentwide tech officials. Katie Wood previously served as acting CIO. Schumann joined the FBI in 2005 as a special agent and rose through the ranks over two decades, most recently serving as deputy assistant director and before that as section chief of the Enterprise Vetting Center. As the agency's top IT official, he will oversee the FBI's digital modernization, cybersecurity efforts and tech governance strategy. He previously served 20 years in the U.S. Air Force.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T14:15:58.484228+00:00","url":"https://www.nextgov.com/people/2026/06/fbi-taps-karl-robert-schumann-new-cio/414235","content":"FBI taps Karl Robert Schumann as new CIO\nKevin Carter / Contributor\nGet the latest federal technology news delivered to your inbox.\nemailRegister for NewsletterStay ConnectedFeatured eBooks\nLaw Enforcement Technology\nRead Now\nAI ApplicationsRead NowEfficiency, After a Year of DOGERead NowInsights & Reports\nAccelerating Discovery in the AI EraPresented By Google Public Sector\nDownload Now\nCDW and Google Public Sector deliver a unified, mission-focused technology ecosystemPresented By Google Public Sector\nDownload Now\nBy\nEdward Graham,Managing Editor, Nextgov/FCW\nBy\nEdward Graham\n|\nJune 16, 2026\nSchumann has been at the FBI for more than 20 years, rising from special agent to the agency's IT head.\nFBI\nWorkforce\nLongtime FBI official Karl Robert Schumann is the agency’s new permanent chief information officer, according to the Justice Department’s list of top departmentwide tech officials.DOJ’s IT leadership directory was updated Monday to list Schumann as the agency’s new CIO. Katie Wood previously served as the FBI’s acting CIO prior to Schumann’s elevation. Schumann did not respond to a LinkedIn message for comment, although he revised his job experiences on the professional networking platform to list the CIO position and a new role as the FBI’s Assistant Director (Senior Executive Service).As the agency’s top IT official, Schumann will now be responsible for overseeing the FBI’s digital modernization approach, cybersecurity efforts and tech governance strategy. Schumann first joined the FBI in 2005 as a special agent and has risen through the agency's ranks over the next two decades. More recently, he served as the FBI’s deputy assistant director (SES) and before that was Section Chief (SES) of the Enterprise Vetting Center. Prior to joining the agency, he served 20 years as both an active duty officer and as a reserve officer with the U.S. Air Force.Editor's note: This article has been updated to reflect Schumann's length of time at the FBI.\nShare This:\nNEXT STORY:\nPush for new Cyber Force service branch narrowly fails in the Senate\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nAI is ready for federal health’s hardest problems\nGenAI.mil records almost 1.7M users, plans new model additions\nExclusive\nTrump administration hopes AI can mitigate staffing losses, federal CIO says\nHouse panel subpoenas Oracle heads over VA health record overhaul’s ballooning costs\nGSA’s web design system head replaced with Treasury AI engineer\nWhite House mandates agencies use Login.gov for public-facing websites\nsponsor content\nAI is ready for federal health’s hardest problems","content_cn":"联邦调查局任命Karl Robert Schumann为新任首席信息官\nKevin Carter / 撰稿人\n获取最新联邦技术新闻，发送至您的收件箱。\n电子邮件注册新闻通讯保持联系精选电子书\n执法技术\n立即阅读\nAI应用立即阅读DOGE实施一年后的效率立即阅读洞察与报告\n加速AI时代的发现，由Google Public Sector呈现\n立即下载\nCDW和Google Public Sector提供统一、任务导向的技术生态系统，由Google Public Sector呈现\n立即下载\n作者\nEdward Graham，Nextgov/FCW执行编辑\n作者\nEdward Graham\n|\n2026年6月16日\nSchumann已在联邦调查局任职20多年，从特别探员晋升为该机构的IT负责人。\n联邦调查局\n劳动力\n\n根据司法部的部门级高级技术官员名单，长期任职的联邦调查局官员Karl Robert Schumann成为该机构新的正式首席信息官。司法部的IT领导层名录于周一更新，将Schumann列为该机构的新任CIO。在Schumann升任前，Katie Wood曾担任联邦调查局代理CIO。Schumann未回复通过LinkedIn发送的置评消息，不过他在该职业社交平台上修改了工作经历，将CIO职位和联邦调查局助理局长（高级行政服务）的新职务列入其中。作为该机构最高IT官员，Schumann现在将负责监督联邦调查局的数字现代化方法、网络安全工作和技术治理战略。\n\nSchumann于2005年首次以特别探员身份加入联邦调查局，并在随后二十年间逐步晋升。最近，他担任联邦调查局副助理局长（SES），在此之前担任企业审查中心处长（SES）。\n\n在加入该机构之前，他曾在美国空军服役20年，既担任现役军官，也担任预备役军官。\n\n编者注：本文已更新，以反映Schumann在联邦调查局任职的时长。\n\n分享本文：\n\n下一篇报道：\n推动成立新的网络部队军种的倡议在参议院以微弱差距未获通过\nGenAI.mil记录近170万用户，计划新增模型\n独家\n联邦CIO称，特朗普政府希望AI能缓解人员流失\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本飙升传唤Oracle负责人\nGSA网页设计系统负责人由财政部AI工程师接替\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\nAI已准备好应对联邦医疗卫生领域最棘手的问题\nGenAI.mil记录近170万用户，计划新增模型\n独家\n联邦CIO称，特朗普政府希望AI能缓解人员流失\n众议院委员会就退伍军人事务部健康记录改革成本飙升传唤Oracle负责人\nGSA网页设计系统负责人由财政部AI工程师接替\n白宫要求各机构在面向公众的网站上使用Login.gov\n赞助内容\nAI已准备好应对联邦医疗卫生领域最棘手的问题","publish_source":"nextgov.com","publish_time":"2026-06-16T21:48:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-241","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"州长国土安全顾问委员会2026年夏季会议——全国州长协会","title_en":"Governors Homeland Security Advisors Council 2026 Summer Meeting - National Governors Association","summary_cn":"全国州长协会报道，州长国土安全顾问委员会（GHSAC）于2026年8月26日至27日在弗吉尼亚州阿灵顿举行夏季会议，来自40个州、领地和哥伦比亚特区的代表参加。会议围绕防范和缓解物理与网络威胁展开协作，议题包括反无人机系统项目、2026年世界杯及美国建国250周年活动安保经验、关键基础设施保护与前沿AI模型对网络安全的影响，以及信息共享和融合中心的作用。会议还与州长网络安全顾问联合开展议程。","summary_en":"The National Governors Association reports that the Governors Homeland Security Advisors Council (GHSAC) held its 2026 Summer Meeting on August 26-27 in Arlington, Virginia, with representatives from 40 states, territories and the District of Columbia. The meeting advanced governors' homeland security priorities and covered counter-UAS programs, lessons from the 2026 FIFA World Cup and America's 250th anniversary events, critical infrastructure protection and the potential impact of frontier AI models on cybersecurity, and information sharing through fusion centers. Joint programming with Governors' Cybersecurity Advisors addressed cybersecurity grants and AI governance.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-18T13:45:10.039789+00:00","url":"https://www.nga.org/meetings/ghsac-2026-summer-meeting","content":"Governors Homeland Security Advisors Council 2026 Summer Meeting\nSep. 9, 2026\n|\nMeetings & Webinars\nThe Governors Homeland Security Advisors Council (GHSAC) met August 26-27, 2026, in Arlington, VA. The GHSAC regularly brings together Governors’ homeland security advisors from states, commonwealths, territories and the District of Columbia. The mission of the GHSAC is to inform and shape national homeland security policy; keep Governors abreast of the current threat environment and the impacts of federal homeland security legislation, regulations, and policies; and promote best practices related to homeland security activities. The meeting serves to advance Governors’ homeland security priorities, share best practices and improve the nation’s overall homeland security enterprise.\nThe meeting included representatives from 40 states, territories and the District of Columbia. Virginia Governor Abigail Spanberger welcomed participants to Virginia and shared ways her administration has advanced homeland security within the state through a Unified Readiness Framework. The GHSAC also met with Congressman Matt Van Epps, Drug Enforcement Administrator Terrence Cole, and Acting Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Administrator Nick Andersen, among other key federal leaders.\nThe meeting provided an opportunity for GHSAC members to share and collaborate on approaches to prevent and mitigate a variety of physical and cyber threats. This includes:\nFostering discussions with members of the National Security Council, the Federal Bureau of Investigation, and the Department of War’s Joint Interagency Task Force-401 in continued partnership on state and territory Counter-Unmanned Aircraft System programs to protect against malicious threats and ensure secure airspace for legitimate air traffic.\nSharing lessons learned and best practices following the 2026 FIFA World Cup and America’s 250th anniversary events to advance continued efforts to ensure the safety of special events across the cyber and physical domains.\nEngaging with several leading AI companies, the Department of Energy Office of Cybersecurity, Energy Security, and Emergency Response, and state officials on the security and protection of critical infrastructure, including the potential impact of frontier AI models on cybersecurity.\nHighlighting the ongoing importance of information sharing, including the important role that fusion centers and DHS Intelligence and Analysis field staff play in those processes.\nThe meeting also incorporated joint programming with Governor’s Cybersecurity Advisors that included discussions on the positive impact the State and Local Cybersecurity Grant program has had in building out state and local cybersecurity capabilities, cross-sector approaches for addressing cybersecurity threats from both prevention and consequence management perspectives, as well as how states are approaching the introduction of AI into state systems from a policy and governance perspective.\nRecent Publications\nReimagining Rural Maternal and Child Health\nAug. 6, 2026\nBuilding Degree Apprenticeships in Louisiana: A State-Led Approach…\nAug. 6, 2026\nBuilding Degree Apprenticeships in North Carolina: A State-Led…\nAug. 6, 2026\nPutting State Dollars to Work: How Governors’ Signature…\nJun. 25, 2026\nExplore More","content_cn":"州长国土安全顾问委员会 2026 年夏季会议\n2026年9月9日\n|\n会议与网络研讨会\n州长国土安全顾问委员会（GHSAC）于2026年8月26日至27日在弗吉尼亚州阿灵顿举行会议。GHSAC 定期召集来自各州、联邦邦、领地和哥伦比亚特区的州长国土安全顾问。GHSAC 的使命是为国家国土安全政策提供信息并塑造其方向；使州长及时了解当前威胁环境以及联邦国土安全立法、法规和政策的影响；并推广与国土安全活动相关的最佳实践。该会议旨在推进州长的国土安全优先事项、分享最佳实践，并改善国家整体国土安全体系。\n来自40个州、领地和哥伦比亚特区的代表参加了会议。弗吉尼亚州州长阿比盖尔·斯潘伯格欢迎与会者来到弗吉尼亚州，并介绍了其政府如何通过统一战备框架推进该州的国土安全工作。GHSAC 还会见了联邦众议员马特·范·埃普斯、缉毒局局长特伦斯·科尔以及代理网络安全与基础设施安全局局长尼克·安德森，以及其他重要联邦领导人。\n此次会议为 GHSAC 成员提供了分享并协作制定方法的机会，以预防和减轻各种实体和网络威胁。这包括：\n与国家安全委员会、联邦调查局及战争部跨机构联合特遣部队-401的成员开展讨论，继续合作推进州和领地反无人机系统项目，以防范恶意威胁，并确保合法航空交通拥有安全空域。\n分享在2026年国际足联世界杯和美国建国250周年活动之后获得的经验教训和最佳实践，以推进持续努力，确保特别活动在网络和实体领域的安全。\n与多家领先的人工智能公司、能源部网络安全、能源安全和应急响应办公室以及州官员就关键基础设施的安全与保护进行交流，包括前沿人工智能模型对网络安全的潜在影响。\n强调信息共享持续的重要性，包括融合中心和国土安全部情报与分析外勤人员在这些流程中发挥的重要作用。\n会议还与州长网络安全顾问共同开展联合议程，其中包括讨论州和地方网络安全拨款计划在建设州和地方网络安全能力方面产生的积极影响、从预防和后果管理视角应对网络安全威胁的跨部门方法，以及各州如何从政策和治理角度推进将人工智能引入州系统。\n近期出版物\n重新构想农村孕产妇和儿童健康\n2026年8月6日\n在路易斯安那州建设学位学徒制：州主导的方法……\n2026年8月6日\n在北卡罗来纳州建设学位学徒制：州主导的……\n2026年8月6日\n让州资金发挥作用：州长标志性……如何……\n2026年6月25日\n探索更多","publish_source":"nga.org","publish_time":"2026-09-09T13:17:23+00:00"}},{"id":"news-462","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"国土安全部长马林与州长及全国州长协会共同推进州-联邦国土安全协调","title_en":"DHS Secretary Mullin Joins Governors and NGA to Advance State-Federal Homeland Security Coordination - National Governors Association","summary_cn":"全国州长协会发布新闻稿称，美国国土安全部长马克韦恩·马林出席在俄克拉何马城举行的全国州长协会夏季会议，与州长们就州-联邦协调、灾害救援、网络安全、移民和无人机威胁等议题进行炉边谈话。文中还介绍GHSAC每年多次召开会议以影响州和全国国土安全政策，并提及30多位州长曾致信国会呼吁加强联邦与州在反无人机方面的协调，以及全国州长协会主办两党州长委员会全体会议。","summary_en":"A National Governors Association press release says Department of Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin attended NGA's Summer Meeting in Oklahoma City for a fireside chat with governors moderated by Oklahoma Gov. Kevin Stitt, covering state-federal coordination, disaster relief, cybersecurity, immigration and drone threats. It notes the Governors Homeland Security Advisors Council convenes multiple times yearly to shape homeland security policy, that more than 30 governors urged Congress in September 2025 to strengthen federal-state counter-drone coordination, and that NGA hosted a plenary of the bipartisan Council of Governors.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-18T13:58:22.479493+00:00","url":"https://www.nga.org/news/press-releases/dhs-secretary-mullin-joins-governors-and-nga-to-advance-state-federal-homeland-security-coordination","content":"DHS Secretary Mullin Joins Governors and NGA to Advance State-Federal Homeland Security Coordination\nAug. 1, 2026\n|\nPress Releases\nOKLAHOMA CITY – Department of Homeland Security (DHS) Secretary Markwayne Mullin attended the National Governors Association (NGA) Summer Meeting in Oklahoma City, joining governors for a fireside chat moderated by Oklahoma Gov. Kevin Stitt. Governors engaged in a wide-ranging discussion with Secretary Mullin on several priority issues, including state-federal coordination, disaster relief, cybersecurity, immigration and drone threats.\nNGA’s Governors Homeland Security Advisors Council (GHSAC) convenes multiple times per year to inform and shape state and national homeland security policy; keep abreast of the current threat environment and the impacts of federal homeland security legislation, regulations, and policies; and promote best practices related to homeland security activities. The GHSAC gathered in Nashville in April, where the council met with Federal Bureau of Investigation Director Kash Patel, and has ongoing engagement with federal entities including the National Security Council and Department of Homeland Security.\nNGA also convenes a network of Governor’s Cybersecurity Advisors, bringing the nation’s cybersecurity advisors together to drive cyber policy developments, trends, and best practices and to work with federal and private partners to advance cybersecurity efforts within their states and territories.\nTo address the increasing threat malicious drones pose to the nation’s critical infrastructure and public safety, more than 30 governors wrote to congressional leadership in September 2025 calling on Congress to enact legislation that strengthens and clarifies the coordination between federal and state authorities. NGA Chair Oklahoma Gov. Kevin Stitt and Vice Chair Maryland Gov. Wes Moore praised congressional action in December 2025 to authorize trained and certified state and territorial law enforcement and correctional officers to address the threat from nefarious drones in coordination with key federal agencies.\nOn Thursday, NGA hosted a plenary meeting of the bipartisan Council of Governors, a 10-member federally established body instituted to work with federal officials to address issues relating to the National Guard; national security; homeland defense; and disaster preparedness, response, and recovery.\nTo learn more about how NGA supports governors’ coordination on homeland security priorities, see these resources:\n2026 GHSAC Spring Meeting (recap)\nGHSAC Executive Committee Annual Meeting with Key Congressional and Administration Staff\nGovernors Homeland Security Advisors Council\nCouncil of Governors Holds 32nd Plenary Meeting\nCouncil of Governors Letter on Unmanned Aircraft Systems\nCouncil of Governors Recommendations on FEMA Reform\nRecent Publications\nReimagining Rural Maternal and Child Health\nAug. 6, 2026\nBuilding Degree Apprenticeships in Louisiana: A State-Led Approach…\nAug. 6, 2026\nBuilding Degree Apprenticeships in North Carolina: A State-Led…\nAug. 6, 2026\nPutting State Dollars to Work: How Governors’ Signature…\nJun. 25, 2026\nExplore More","content_cn":"国土安全部部长穆林与各州州长及全国州长协会携手推进州与联邦国土安全协调\n2026年8月1日\n|\n新闻稿\n俄克拉何马城——国土安全部（DHS）部长马克韦恩·穆林参加了在俄克拉何马城举行的全国州长协会（NGA）夏季会议，并与各州州长共同参加了一场由俄克拉何马州州长凯文·斯蒂特主持的炉边谈话。州长们与穆林部长就多个优先议题展开了广泛讨论，包括州与联邦协调、灾害救助、网络安全、移民以及无人机威胁。\n全国州长协会州长国土安全顾问委员会（GHSAC）每年多次召开会议，以通报并塑造州和国家国土安全政策；及时掌握当前威胁环境以及联邦国土安全立法、法规和政策的影响；并推广与国土安全活动相关的最佳实践。GHSAC于4月在纳什维尔召开会议，期间委员会会见了联邦调查局局长卡什·帕特尔，并持续与包括国家安全委员会和国土安全部在内的联邦机构开展合作。\n全国州长协会还召集州长网络安全顾问网络，将全国各州的网络安全顾问汇聚起来，推动网络政策发展、趋势和最佳实践，并与联邦及私营部门合作伙伴协作，推进各州和领地的网络安全工作。\n为应对恶意无人机对国家关键基础设施和公共安全日益加剧的威胁，30多位州长于2025年9月致函国会领导层，呼吁国会通过立法，加强并明确联邦与州政府机构之间的协调。全国州长协会主席、俄克拉何马州州长凯文·斯蒂特和副主席、马里兰州州长韦斯·摩尔赞扬了国会于2025年12月采取的行动：授权经过培训和认证的州及领地执法人员和惩教人员，与相关联邦机构协调应对不法无人机带来的威胁。\n周四，全国州长协会主持召开了两党州长委员会全体会议。该委员会是一个由联邦设立、拥有10名成员的机构，旨在与联邦官员合作，处理与国民警卫队、国家安全、国土防御以及灾害准备、响应和恢复相关的问题。\n如需进一步了解全国州长协会如何支持州长协调国土安全重点工作，请参阅以下资源：\n2026年GHSAC春季会议（回顾）\nGHSAC执行委员会与国会及政府关键工作人员年度会议\n州长国土安全顾问委员会\n州长委员会举行第32次全体会议\n州长委员会关于无人机系统的信函\n州长委员会关于联邦紧急事务管理局改革的建议\n近期出版物\n重新构想农村孕产妇和儿童健康\n2026年8月6日\n在路易斯安那州建设学位学徒制：州主导的方法……\n2026年8月6日\n在北卡罗来纳州建设学位学徒制：州主导的……\n2026年8月6日\n让州政府资金发挥作用：州长标志性……\n2026年6月25日\n探索更多","publish_source":"nga.org","publish_time":"2026-08-01T11:29:01+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1259","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Bing News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"参议院今日就2026年停摆投票？DHS停摆最新进展","title_en":"Is Senate vote today on shutdown 2026? Update, DHS shutdown news","summary_cn":"截至2026年3月30日，美国国土安全部（DHS）部分停摆进入第44天，成为史上最长政府停摆。众议院以213比203否决参议院DHS拨款协议，并加入选民身份条款，参议院少数党领袖舒默称该案将“胎死腹中”。参议院进入两周复活节休会，最早4月13日复会，停摆可能延续至第58天。特朗普下令向TSA员工发薪，但机场安检等待时间仍长，ICE人员继续驻留机场。","summary_en":"As of March 30, 2026, the partial Department of Homeland Security (DHS) shutdown has reached day 44, becoming the longest government shutdown in U.S. history. The House voted 213-203 to reject the Senate's DHS funding deal and added a voter ID provision; Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer called the measure \"dead on arrival.\" The Senate is on a two-week Easter recess and will not return until April 13, potentially extending the shutdown to day 58. President Trump ordered TSA workers to be paid, but airport security wait times remain long and ICE agents stay at airports.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T05:49:58.432827+00:00","url":"https://www.northjersey.com/story/news/2026/03/30/senate-vote-today-on-shutdown-2026-dhs-shutdown-updates-news-tsa-shutdown-government-shutdown-2026/89385051007","content":"NEWSWhen is Senate vote on government shutdown? DHS funding updates today Lori ComstockUSA TODAY NETWORKMarch 30, 2026, 12:03 p.m. ETIs the government shutdown over? Far from it after an unprecedented move by House Republicans to snub a Senate vote on a DHS funding deal to end the shutdown, meaning the government shutdown 2026 continues today amid long TSA wait times as TSA workers start getting paid.Here's the latest government shutdown 2026 updates, when a DHS shutdown vote takes place, and if a DHS funding bill can pass amid unpredictably long TSA wait times at airports and a stalled Senate vote to end the shutdown. Are the Senate and House on spring recess, or will there be a vote to end the government shutdown?TSA workers are set to get paychecks at the order of President Donald Trump, but it remains unclear how the DHS shutdown standoff resolves, with the DHS shutdown end out of sight and Congress on a two-week recess through the Easter holiday. A House vote Friday to approve a DHS funding bill is back to the Senate for a vote — but it'll likely be \"dead on arrival,\" said Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer, a New York Democrat, meaning Democrats won't provide enough support to reach the 60 votes needed to pass.A Senate vote to end the government shutdown — passed by the Senate in the dead of night — would have funded the Coast Guard, the Transportation Security Administration (TSA), Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA), and other DHS agencies. But it didn't include Border Patrol and ICE funding, which has been a sore spot for Democrats, who have sought to rein in ICE over the shooting deaths of Alex Pretti and Renee Good by federal agents in Minnesota in January. Both ICE and BP are already fully funded via a cash infusion from Trump's \"One Big, Beautiful Bill.\"House Speaker Mike Johnson and House Republicans on Friday led a 213-203 vote that struck down the Senate's DHS funding deal, that also added a voter ID provision to their new measure, as part of the SAVE America Act. The House vote calls for fully funding DHS for eight weeks, plus providing border and immigration funds that the Senate deal left out. Three Democrats voted in favor: Reps. Henry Cuellar, of Texas; Don Davis, of North Carolina; and Marie Gluesenkamp Perez, of Washington. But there's no current plan for the Senate to return from its scheduled recess, a GOP aide told ABC News, and the ongoing Capitol Hill impasse means the partial shutdown could reach days, or weeks, longer. While TSA employees will start seeing paychecks today, the shutdown continues to upend security wait times at airports, with TSA callout rates soaring as ICE agents remaining at airports for the time being.When is the next vote to end the shutdown, and when will the \"TSA shutdown\" end? Here's the latest DHS shutdown 2026 news and updates today.Does Senate vote today? When does Senate vote again on government shutdown 2026?A Senate vote to end the shutdown and bring the DHS shutdown end in sight is unlikely, barring any breakthroughs or changes to the Senate calendar 2026. The Senate returned at 10:30 a.m. ET today, Monday, March 30, but it was just a \"pro forma\" session to hit the gavel without any senators on the floor.When is Senate vote on DHS funding? Next government shutdown voteThe Senate retuned at 10:30 a.m. ET today, Monday, March 30, but no Senate votes will take place, with the Senate on a two-week spring recess through the Easter holiday. The Senate returns April 2, April 6 and April 9 for \"pro forma\" sessions — a simple gavel hit — but no votes will be cast, and senators are not in Washington DC.When will the government shutdown end? Partial government shutdown updateThe Senate returns at 3 p.m. ET on Monday, April 13, to potentially vote on the government shutdown. A Senate vote to end the DHS shutdown comes following a House vote Friday to fully fund DHS agencies for eight weeks — a remarkable snub to a bipartisan DHS funding deal passed by the Senate just hours prior.A shutdown end appears out of sight with Congress out of town, and even when the Senate return for the next vote to end the shutdown, Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer said it'll be dead on arrival,\" meaning it won't hit the 60 votes needed to pass.The House measure includes funding for ICE and Border Patrol — which was left out of the Senate approved bill early Friday morning. Democrats have vowed to vote \"no\" on ICE and Border Patrol funding until ICE reforms are made following the fatal shootings of two Americans in Minneapolis by federal agents.Will the DHS shutdown end today?It's unlikely the DHS shutdown ends today, since the Senate is on a two-week recess and won't return to Capitol Hill until Monday, April 13. The House returns Tuesday, April 14. Barring any changes to the Senate calendar, the next Senate vote to end the shutdown won't happen until we reach day 58 of the government shutdown 2026.Are TSA agents getting paid? Is there a TSA shutdown?Yes. President Donald Trump ordered new DHS Secretary Markwayne Mullin to pay TSA agents amid the ongoing shutdown, with TSA employees expected to see paychecks starting today, Monday, March 30.The term TSA shutdown, which has been used across social media, refers to the partial government shutdown that has severely strained TSA agents. Technically, the DHS is shut down, with Congress continuing a bitter impasse over how to pass DHS funding in the FY 2026 budget.Border Patrol and ICE have been fully funded due to Trump's \"One Big Beautiful Bill Act\"; however, TSA has been hit hard, with callout rates soaring at major airports like JFK, Philadelphia, Atlanta, BWI, New Orleans, and Houston. Over 500 TSA agents facing little to no pay have quit, the White House said on X.Longest government shutdown in US historyFull and partial shutdowns included, we are currently in the longest government shutdown in history, reaching 44 days today, Monday, March 30. The 2026 shutdown started on Feb. 14, 2026.The last government shutdown in 2025 was 43 days, stretching from Oct. 1, 2025, to Nov. 12, 2025. Senate Democrats and Republicans, including President Donald Trump, failed to agree on a short-term deal to keep the government funded, only passing (temporary) funding bills through Jan. 30.Senate calendar 2026The U.S. Senate is currently on a two-week recess, lasting through the Easter holiday, according to the Senate calendar 2026. Congress — the Senate and the House — won't return until Monday, April 13, barring any changes to the legislative calendar.The Senate schedule shows lawmakers typically tend to formal business, or are in session, around 140 of 165 days annually. These are \"legislative\" days when the U.S. Senate convenes to debate bills, vote, or conduct floor business. Unlike traditional jobs, a legislative day can last longer than 24 hours or stretch across multiple calendar days, and senators are usually occupied year-round with other responsibilities. Here's the official 2026 Senate schedule.Lori Comstock is a New Jersey-based news reporter covering trending news with USA TODAY Network's Mid-Atlantic Connect Team. She covers news in the Northeast, including New Jersey, Pennsylvania, Delaware, Washington DC, Maryland, and Virginia. Reach her at LComstock@usatodayco.com.Featured Weekly Ad","content_cn":"新闻：参议院何时就政府停摆投票？今日国土安全部资金更新\nLori Comstock\nUSA TODAY NETWORK\n2026年3月30日，美国东部时间下午12:03\n\n政府停摆结束了吗？远未结束。众议院共和党人史无前例地拒绝支持参议院就一项旨在结束停摆的国土安全部资金协议进行的投票，这意味着2026年政府停摆今日仍在继续，尽管TSA工作人员开始领薪，TSA排队等候时间依然很长。\n\n以下是2026年政府停摆的最新动态、国土安全部停摆投票何时举行，以及在机场TSA等待时间长得难以预测、参议院结束停摆的投票陷入停滞之际，国土安全部资金法案能否通过。参议院和众议院是在春季休会，还是将举行投票结束政府停摆？\n\n根据总统唐纳德·特朗普的命令，TSA工作人员将获得工资支票，但国土安全部停摆僵局将如何解决仍不清楚，国土安全部停摆的结束遥遥无期，而国会将在复活节假期期间休会两周。众议院周五投票通过一项国土安全部资金法案后，该法案将回到参议院进行投票——但参议院少数党领袖、纽约州民主党人查克·舒默表示，它很可能会“胎死腹中”，这意味着民主党人不会提供足够支持，以达到通过所需的60票。\n\n参议院在深夜通过的一项结束政府停摆的投票，本会为海岸警卫队、运输安全管理局（TSA）、联邦紧急事务管理局（FEMA）及其他国土安全部机构提供资金。但该方案不包括边境巡逻队和ICE的资金，这一直是民主党人的痛点；在联邦特工今年1月于明尼苏达州枪杀Alex Pretti和Renee Good后，民主党人一直寻求约束ICE。ICE和BP均已通过特朗普的“One Big, Beautiful Bill”注入资金而获得全额拨款。\n\n众议院议长迈克·约翰逊和众议院共和党人周五以213票对203票否决了参议院的国土安全部资金协议，并在其新措施中加入了一项选民身份证条款，作为SAVE America Act的一部分。众议院的投票要求为国土安全部提供八周的全额资金，并提供参议院协议未涵盖的边境和移民资金。三名民主党人投了赞成票：得克萨斯州众议员Henry Cuellar、北卡罗来纳州众议员Don Davis和华盛顿州众议员Marie Gluesenkamp Perez。\n\n但一名共和党助理告诉ABC News，目前没有让参议院从既定休会中提前返回的计划，而国会山持续的僵局意味着部分停摆可能会再持续数天，或数周。尽管TSA员工今天将开始收到工资支票，停摆仍在扰乱机场安检等待时间，随着ICE特工暂时仍留在机场，TSA员工请假率飙升。\n\n下一次结束停摆的投票是什么时候，“TSA停摆”又将何时结束？以下是今日2026年国土安全部停摆的最新新闻和动态。\n\n参议院今天投票吗？参议院何时再次就2026年政府停摆投票？\n\n除非参议院议程2026出现任何突破或变化，否则参议院不太可能举行结束停摆、让国土安全部停摆结束在望的投票。参议院于今天，即3月30日星期一美国东部时间上午10:30复会，但那只是一次“形式会议”，敲响议事槌时议场内没有任何参议员。\n\n参议院何时就国土安全部资金投票？下一次政府停摆投票\n\n参议院于今天，即3月30日星期一美国东部时间上午10:30复会，但不会举行任何参议院投票，因为参议院将在复活节假期期间春季休会两周。参议院将于4月2日复会，\n4月6日和4月9日将举行“形式性”会议——简单敲一下法槌——但不会进行投票，参议员们也不在华盛顿特区。\n政府停摆何时结束？部分政府停摆最新消息\n参议院将于4月13日星期一下午3点（美国东部时间）复会，可能就政府停摆进行投票。此前众议院于周五投票决定为国土安全部机构提供八周的全额资金，这对数小时前刚获参议院通过的一项两党国土安全部拨款协议构成了显著冷落。\n随着国会离开华盛顿，停摆结束似乎遥遥无期；即便参议院为下一次结束停摆的投票复会，参议院少数党领袖查克·舒默表示，该法案将“一提交即告失败”，这意味着它无法获得通过所需的60票。\n众议院的措施包括为ICE和边境巡逻队提供资金——这部分内容被排除在周五凌晨获参议院批准的法案之外。两名美国人在明尼阿波利斯遭联邦特工致命枪击后，民主党人已承诺，在ICE改革落实前，将对ICE和边境巡逻队拨款投“反对票”。\n国土安全部停摆今天会结束吗？\n国土安全部停摆今天不太可能结束，因为参议院正处于为期两周的休会期，直到4月13日星期一才会重返国会山。众议院将于4月14日星期二复会。除非参议院日程发生变化，否则下一次结束停摆的参议院投票要到2026年政府停摆第58天才会举行。\nTSA工作人员会领薪吗？TSA是否停摆？\n会。总统唐纳德·特朗普已命令新任国土安全部部长马克韦恩·马林在持续停摆期间向TSA工作人员发薪，TSA员工预计从今天，即3月30日星期一开始收到工资支票。\n社交媒体上使用的“TSA停摆”一词，指的是给TSA工作人员造成严重压力的部分政府停摆。严格来说，是国土安全部停摆，国会仍在如何通过2026财年预算中的国土安全部拨款问题上陷入激烈僵局。\n由于特朗普的《一项大而美法案》，边境巡逻队和ICE已获得全额拨款；然而，TSA受到严重冲击，肯尼迪国际机场、费城、亚特兰大、BWI、新奥尔良和休斯敦等主要机场的请假率激增。白宫在X上表示，500多名面临几乎没有或完全没有工资的TSA工作人员已辞职。\n美国历史上最长的政府停摆\n包括全面和部分停摆在内，我们目前正经历历史上最长的政府停摆，截至今天，即3月30日星期一，已达44天。2026年停摆始于2026年2月14日。\n上一次2025年的政府停摆持续了43天，从2025年10月1日延续至2025年11月12日。包括总统唐纳德·特朗普在内的参议院民主党人与共和党人未能就维持政府拨款的短期协议达成一致，仅通过了延续至1月30日的（临时）拨款法案。\n2026年参议院日程\n根据2026年参议院日程，美国参议院目前正处于为期两周、持续至复活节假期的休会期。除非立法日程发生变化，国会——参议院和众议院——要到4月13日星期一才会复会。\n参议院日程显示，议员们通常每年约有165天中的140天处理正式事务，或处于开会状态。这些是美国参议院召开以辩论法案、投票或处理议场事务的“立法”日。与传统工作不同，一个立法日可能持续超过24小时，或跨越多个日历日，而参议员通常全年还忙于其他职责。以下是官方的2026年\n参议院日程。Lori Comstock 是一名驻新泽西州的新闻记者，为《今日美国》网络的中大西洋连接团队报道热门新闻。她报道美国东北部的新闻，包括新泽西州、宾夕法尼亚州、特拉华州、华盛顿特区、马里兰州和弗吉尼亚州。可通过 LComstock@usatodayco.com 联系她。精选每周广告","publish_source":"northjersey.com","publish_time":"2026-03-30T16:03:40+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3526","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"FBI登临与巴尔的摩大桥坍塌涉事公司有关的货船","title_en":"FBI visits cargo ship managed by same company involved in Baltimore bridge collapse : NPR","summary_cn":"美国联邦调查局探员与环保署刑事调查部门及海岸警卫队调查人员登临巴尔的摩的Maersk Saltoro号货船，该船由Synergy Marine公司管理，与今年3月撞塌弗朗西斯·斯科特·基大桥的Dali号同属一家管理公司。司法部此前已起诉船东与运营方，索赔逾1亿美元，FBI另就此展开刑事调查。","summary_en":"FBI agents, along with EPA Criminal Investigation Division and Coast Guard Investigative Services officials, boarded the Maersk Saltoro in Baltimore, managed by Synergy Marine, the same company that operated the Dali, which struck and collapsed the Francis Scott Key Bridge in March. The Justice Department has sued the ship's owner and operator for over $100 million, and the FBI has opened a criminal investigation.","tag":"risk","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:46:55.824791+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2024/09/21/nx-s1-5121915/fbi-baltimore-bridge-collapse-cargo-ship","content":"FBI visits cargo ship managed by same company involved in Baltimore bridge collapse FBI agents boarded a vessel managed by the same company as the cargo ship that caused the collapse of the Francis Scott Key Bridge in March.\nNational\nFBI agents board Baltimore ship linked to Singaporean company sued in bridge collapse\nSeptember 21, 20242:02 PM ET\nJuliana Kim\nTugboats maneuver the damaged container ship Dali through the Port of Baltimore and into the Seagirt Marine Terminal on May 20.\nChip Somodevilla/Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nChip Somodevilla/Getty Images\nThe FBI and other federal authorities have boarded a vessel in Baltimore managed by the same company as the cargo ship that caused the collapse of the Francis Scott Key Bridge back in March. Their visit comes just a few days after the Justice Department filed a lawsuit against the companies that owned and operated the deadly ship.\nNational\nU.S. sues Dali ship owner and operator for $100 million over Baltimore bridge collapse\nIn a statement, the FBI said its agents along with officials from the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency’s Criminal Investigation Division and the Coast Guard's Investigative Services arrived at the Maersk Saltoro vessel Saturday morning. The FBI said the group was \"conducting court authorized law enforcement activity\" and declined to provide more details. Records from IHS Shipping Data show the Maersk Saltoro is managed by Synergy Marine Private Ltd., the same company that operated the cargo ship, Dali. On March 26, the massive vessel lost power and crashed into the Baltimore bridge, killing six construction workers who had been repairing potholes in the overnight hours on the structure.\nSponsor Message\nOn Wednesday, the Justice Department announced it was suing Synergy Marine and the ship's owner, Grace Ocean Private Ltd., — both of which are Singapore-based corporations — for negligence and dangerous cost-cutting decisions that led to the bridge collapse. The catastrophe shut down the busy port for months and it also obliterated a segment of Interstate 695 carried by the bridge. “The ship’s owner and manager … sent an ill-prepared crew on an abjectly unseaworthy vessel to navigate the United States’ waterways,” the Justice Department said in a civil claim filed in a federal court in Maryland.\nLaw\nFBI agents are searching the ship that crashed into Baltimore's Key Bridge\nThe federal government is seeking more than $100 million in costs that the U.S. incurred in responding to the disaster. The federal claim does not cover the expenses of rebuilding the bridge. Since Maryland built and owned the bridge, the state will pursue its own compensation, according to the Justice Department. The FBI has also opened a criminal investigation into the fatal collapse, with a focus on the massive ship and whether the crew knew that the vessel was malfunctioning before they left the port, the Washington Post reported in April.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nbaltimore bridge\nBaltimore\nships\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"联邦调查局访问由涉巴尔的摩大桥坍塌同一家公司管理的货船\n联邦调查局特工登上一艘由与导致弗朗西斯·斯科特·基大桥坍塌的货船同一家公司管理的船只。\n\n国家\n联邦调查局特工登上与大桥坍塌案中被起诉的新加坡公司有关联的巴尔的摩船只\n2024年9月21日，美国东部时间下午2:02\nJuliana Kim\n\n5月20日，拖船操纵受损的集装箱船“达利号”驶过巴尔的摩港，进入海吉特海运码头。\nChip Somodevilla/Getty Images\n隐藏说明\n切换说明\nChip Somodevilla/Getty Images\n\n联邦调查局和其他联邦机构登上了巴尔的摩一艘船只。该船由与今年3月导致弗朗西斯·斯科特·基大桥坍塌的货船同一家公司管理。他们的到访发生在司法部起诉这艘致命船只的所有者和运营商公司几天后。\n\n国家\n美国就巴尔的摩大桥坍塌向“达利号”船主和运营商索赔1亿美元\n\n联邦调查局在一份声明中说，其特工以及美国环境保护局刑事调查部门的官员和海岸警卫队调查处人员，于周六上午抵达“马士基萨尔托罗号”。联邦调查局表示，这一行人在“开展经法院授权的执法活动”，并拒绝提供更多细节。IHS Shipping Data的记录显示，“马士基萨尔托罗号”由Synergy Marine Private Ltd.管理，该公司也是货船“达利号”的运营商。3月26日，这艘巨轮失去动力，撞上巴尔的摩大桥，造成6名建筑工人死亡；当时他们在夜间对桥上的坑洼进行维修。\n\n赞助商消息\n\n周三，司法部宣布起诉Synergy Marine和该船所有者Grace Ocean Private Ltd.——两家公司均在新加坡注册——指控其疏忽以及危险的削减成本决定导致大桥坍塌。这场灾难使繁忙的港口关闭数月，也摧毁了大桥承载的695号州际公路一段。司法部在马里兰州联邦法院提交的民事诉状中称：“该船的所有者和管理者……派遣一支准备不足的船员，驾驶一艘完全不适航的船只在美国水道上航行。”\n\n法律\n联邦调查局特工正在搜查撞上巴尔的摩基大桥的船只\n\n联邦政府要求赔偿美国为应对这场灾难而承担的超过1亿美元费用。根据司法部的说法，联邦索赔不包括重建大桥的费用。由于大桥由马里兰州建造并拥有，该州将自行追偿。联邦调查局还对这起致命坍塌展开了刑事调查，重点关注这艘巨轮，以及船员在离港前是否知道船只存在故障；《华盛顿邮报》4月报道称，调查聚焦于此。\n\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类提议或要求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\n\n巴尔的摩大桥\n巴尔的摩\n船只\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n连接\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻公共编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n你的隐私选择\n纯文字\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2024-09-21T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-3523","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"FBI数据显示2023年美国犯罪率下降","title_en":"Crime in the U.S. fell in 2023, FBI data show : NPR","summary_cn":"美国联邦调查局发布《2023年全国犯罪摘要》报告，显示2023年整体犯罪较上年下降，暴力犯罪估计下降约3%，谋杀和非过失杀人下降约11.6%，强奸下降约9.4%，财产犯罪下降2.4%，但机动车盗窃上升12.6%。报告基于各地执法机构提交的数据，覆盖逾3.15亿人。拜登总统就犯罪率下降发表声明。","summary_en":"The FBI released its Summary of Crime in the Nation 2023 report, showing overall crime fell in 2023. Violent crime dropped an estimated 3%, with murder and nonnegligent manslaughter down about 11.6% and rape down 9.4%. Property crime fell 2.4%, though motor vehicle theft rose 12.6%. The report is based on data from law enforcement agencies covering over 315 million people. President Biden issued a statement on the decline.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:46:45.801506+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2024/09/23/g-s1-24184/fbi-crime-data-2023","content":"Crime in the U.S. fell in 2023, FBI data show New data released by the FBI show violent crime and property crime both fell in 2023 compared to the previous year.\nLaw\nCrime in the U.S. fell in 2023, FBI data show\nUpdated September 23, 20241:51 PM ET\nOriginally published September 23, 202412:14 PM ET\nRyan Lucas\nNew FBI data show violent crime and property crime both fell in 2023 compared to the previous year.\nSamuel Corum/Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nSamuel Corum/Getty Images\nCrime overall in the U.S. fell in 2023 compared to a year earlier, with significant decreases in murder and rape, according to new FBI data released Monday. The statistics, published in the FBI’s Summary of Crime in the Nation 2023 report, show a continued downward trend in crime across the country after it surged during the coronavirus pandemic. Overall violent crime dropped by an estimated 3% in 2023 compared to the previous year, according to the FBI report. The largest decreases were seen in murder and nonnegligent manslaughter, which fell an estimated 11.6%. Rape also saw a sharp decrease, falling by an estimated 9.4%.\nSponsor Message\nProperty crime also was down nationwide, dropping 2.4% in 2023 compared to the previous year. Burglary and larceny-theft saw decreases of 7.6% and 4.4% respectively. Motor vehicle theft, however, saw a significant jump. It was up 12.6% in 2023 compared to 2022. In a statement, President Biden claimed credit for the decline, noting that when he took office \"our nation had just seen the highest increase in murders ever recorded under the previous Administration.\" Biden cited his administration's action is stopping the illegal flow of guns, increased public safety funding, his executive action on guns and the gun violence legislation he signed. \"The only way to continue this progress is by investing in what works,\" he said. \"That’s why I will continue to urge Congress to fund 100,000 additional police officers and a strong ATF, invest in community violence intervention programs, and make commonsense gun safety reforms, including a ban on assault weapons.\" The FBI’s annual report is based on data submitted by law enforcement agencies around the country. For the 2023 report, the FBI says it received data from agencies the cover more than 315 million people. It also includes every city with a population of more than 1 million.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFBI\ncrime\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"FBI数据显示，美国2023年犯罪率下降\nFBI公布的新数据显示，与前一年相比，2023年的暴力犯罪和财产犯罪均有所下降。\n法律\nFBI数据显示，美国2023年犯罪率下降\n更新于2024年9月23日下午1:51（美国东部时间）\n最初发布于2024年9月23日下午12:14（美国东部时间）\n瑞安·卢卡斯\nFBI的新数据显示，与前一年相比，2023年的暴力犯罪和财产犯罪均有所下降。\n塞缪尔·科勒姆/盖蒂图片社\n隐藏说明文字\n切换说明文字\n塞缪尔·科勒姆/盖蒂图片社\n根据FBI周一公布的新数据，2023年美国总体犯罪率较前一年下降，谋杀和强奸案件显著减少。这些统计数据发布于FBI《2023年全国犯罪摘要》报告中，显示出新冠疫情期间犯罪激增后，全国犯罪持续呈下降趋势。根据FBI报告，2023年总体暴力犯罪较前一年估计下降3%。降幅最大的是谋杀和非过失杀人，估计下降11.6%。强奸案件也大幅减少，估计下降9.4%。\n赞助商消息\n财产犯罪在全国范围内也有所下降，2023年较前一年减少2.4%。入室盗窃和盗窃罪分别下降7.6%和4.4%。然而，机动车盗窃显著增加，2023年较2022年上升12.6%。拜登总统在一份声明中将这一降幅归功于自己的政府，并指出，他上任时“我们的国家刚刚经历了历届政府任期内有记录以来谋杀案增幅最高的一次”。拜登列举了其政府在阻止枪支非法流入、增加公共安全资金、采取枪支相关行政行动以及签署枪支暴力立法方面的举措。他说：“继续取得这一进展的唯一途径，就是投资于有效的措施。这就是为什么我将继续敦促国会为增加10万名警察提供资金，并支持建立一支强大的烟酒枪械及爆炸物管理局（ATF），投资社区暴力干预项目，并推动常识性的枪支安全改革，包括禁止攻击性武器。”FBI年度报告基于全国各地执法机构提交的数据。FBI表示，在2023年报告中，它收到了覆盖超过3.15亿人口的机构数据。报告还包括所有人口超过100万的城市。\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类提议或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\nFBI\n犯罪\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系我们\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n媒体\n公众编辑\n更正\n文字记录\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多元化\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2024-09-23T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-3410","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"特朗普提名克里斯蒂·诺姆领导国土安全部","title_en":"Kristi Noem is nominated to lead DHS in Trump administration : NPR","summary_cn":"当选总统特朗普宣布将提名南达科他州州长克里斯蒂·诺姆出任美国国土安全部部长。诺姆是特朗普的忠实盟友，若获任命将主管移民事务，特朗普曾在大选中重点强调该议题并承诺大规模驱逐无证移民。特朗普称她将与“边境沙皇”汤姆·霍曼密切合作，确保国土安全。","summary_en":"President-elect Donald Trump announced he will nominate South Dakota Gov. Kristi Noem to lead the Department of Homeland Security. A loyal Trump ally, Noem would oversee immigration, an issue Trump campaigned on heavily, pledging mass deportations of undocumented immigrants. Trump said she will work closely with 'Border Czar' Tom Homan to secure the border and keep the homeland safe from adversaries.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T12:02:06.224547+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2024/11/12/g-s1-33820/trump-kristi-noem-homeland-security","content":"Kristi Noem is nominated to lead DHS in Trump administration The two-term governor of South Dakota has been a loyal Trump ally. In her new role, Noem would oversee immigration, an issue Trump campaigned heavily on.\nPolitics\nTrump will nominate South Dakota Gov. Kristi Noem for Homeland Security secretary\nNovember 12, 20248:10 PM ET\nBy\nJeongyoon Han\n,\nElena Moore\nSouth Dakota Gov. Kristi Noem speaks prior to remarks from then-Republican presidential candidate Donald Trump at a campaign rally March 16 in Vandalia, Ohio.\nJeff Dean/AP\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nJeff Dean/AP\nPresident-elect Donald Trump has announced he'll nominate South Dakota Gov. Kristi Noem to lead the Department of Homeland Security. It's a role that holds significant weight for Trump, who campaigned heavily on the issue of immigration, pledging to carry out mass deportations of undocumented immigrants. Said Trump in a statement: \"She will work closely with 'Border Czar' Tom Homan to secure the Border, and will guarantee that our American Homeland is secure from our adversaries.\" Homan is the former head of Immigration and Customs Enforcement. Trump loyalist Stephen Miller is expected to be deputy chief of staff and focus on immigration policy. Neither Miller nor Homan's positions require Senate confirmation.\nSponsor Message\nPolitics\nTrump to nominate Fox News host Pete Hegseth to be defense secretary\nNoem, a devout Christian who is the first woman elected to serve as governor of South Dakota, is in her second term. In his statement, Trump cited one of her notable moves leading her state: \"Kristi has been very strong on Border Security. She was the first Governor to send National Guard Soldiers to help Texas fight the Biden Border Crisis, and they were sent a total of eight times.\" CNN first reported she would be Trump's DHS pick. Noem switched over to politics in 2006 as a state representative after helping run her family farm as a small business owner. Four years later, she headed to Washington, D.C., when she won the state's at-large seat in the U.S. House of Representatives. Before ultimately picking Ohio Sen. JD Vance, Trump spoke highly of Noem as he was deliberating his running mate, saying, \"She's been there for us for a long time … She's loyal, she's great.\" But Noem drew criticism earlier this year for saying that Native tribes in South Dakota benefit from the drug cartel industry — a comment that led her to be banned from all nine reservations in the state. Additionally, Noem wrote about having met North Korean leader Kim Jong-un in her memoir, No Going Back, which she then retracted from the final copy. In that same memoir, she wrote about killing her family dog, which drew particular scorn.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nTrump's presidential transition\n2024 election\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"Kristi Noem 获提名在特朗普政府中领导 DHS\n这位南达科他州两届州长一直是特朗普的忠实盟友。在新职位上，Noem 将负责移民事务，这是特朗普在竞选中重点强调的议题。\n\n政治\n\n特朗普将提名南达科他州州长 Kristi Noem 担任国土安全部长\n\n2024年11月12日 晚上8:10（美国东部时间）\n\n作者\nJeongyoon Han，\nElena Moore\n\n南达科他州州长 Kristi Noem 于3月16日在俄亥俄州范达利亚的一场竞选集会上、时任共和党总统候选人 Donald Trump 发表讲话前发言。\n\nJeff Dean/AP\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nJeff Dean/AP\n\n当选总统 Donald Trump 宣布，他将提名南达科他州州长 Kristi Noem 领导国土安全部。这一职位对特朗普具有重大分量；他在竞选中大力强调移民议题，并承诺对无证移民实施大规模驱逐。特朗普在一份声明中表示：“她将与‘边境沙皇’Tom Homan 密切合作，以保障边境安全，并将确保我们的美国本土免受对手威胁。”Homan 曾任移民与海关执法局局长。预计特朗普的忠实支持者 Stephen Miller 将担任副幕僚长，并重点负责移民政策。Miller 和 Homan 的职位均无需参议院确认。\n\n赞助商消息\n\n政治\n特朗普将提名福克斯新闻主持人 Pete Hegseth 担任国防部长\n\nNoem 是一名虔诚的基督徒，也是首位当选担任南达科他州州长的女性，目前正处于第二个任期。特朗普在声明中列举了她领导该州的一项显著举措：“Kristi 在边境安全方面非常强硬。她是第一位派遣国民警卫队士兵协助得克萨斯州应对拜登边境危机的州长，他们总共被派遣了八次。”CNN 最先报道称她将成为特朗普的 DHS 人选。Noem 在作为小企业主帮助经营家族农场后，于2006年以州众议员身份转入政界。四年后，她赢得美国众议院该州全州单一选区席位，前往华盛顿哥伦比亚特区。在最终选择俄亥俄州参议员 JD Vance 之前，特朗普在考虑竞选搭档时高度评价 Noem，称：“她长期以来一直支持我们……她忠诚，她很出色。”但 Noem 在今年早些时候因称南达科他州原住民部落受益于贩毒集团产业而受到批评——这一言论导致她被该州全部九个保留地禁止进入。此外，Noem 在其回忆录《No Going Back》中写到曾与朝鲜领导人 Kim Jong-un 会面，之后又从最终版本中撤回了这段内容。在同一本回忆录中，她写到了杀死自家的狗，此事尤其招致谴责。\n\nNPR 不为报道或采访提供或接受资金。\n\n任何提出此类提议或要求的人均非 NPR 员工，也不代表 NPR。\n\n特朗普的总统过渡\n2024年选举\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n\n连接\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n媒体\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字记录\n联系与帮助\n\n关于 NPR\n概述\n多样性\nNPR 网络\n无障碍\n伦理\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助 NPR\nNPR 招聘\nNPR 商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为 NPR 赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2024-11-12T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-3311","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"Up First简报：克里斯托弗·雷；路易吉·曼吉奥内；医疗保健；民主党全国委员会","title_en":"Up First briefing: Christopher Wray; Luigi Mangione; Health care; DNC : NPR","summary_cn":"NPR《Up First》简报头条报道FBI局长克里斯托弗·雷宣布将在拜登政府任期结束时辞职，此前当选总统特朗普已提名忠实盟友卡什·帕特尔接替。雷表示辞职是为避免FBI被拖入政治纷争，并强调FBI工作至关重要。简报还提及联合健康保险公司CEO被杀案引发对美国预期寿命和医疗体系的关注，以及民主党全国委员会领导层改选等话题。","summary_en":"NPR's Up First newsletter leads with FBI Director Christopher Wray announcing he will resign at the end of the Biden administration, after President-elect Trump named loyalist Kash Patel to replace him. Wray said stepping down avoids dragging the FBI deeper into the fray and stressed the bureau's critical work. The briefing also covers the UnitedHealthcare CEO killing drawing attention to U.S. life expectancy and health care, and the Democratic National Committee seeking new leadership.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:34:01.252219+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2024/12/12/g-s1-37879/up-first-newsletter-fbi-director-christopher-wray-luigi-mangione-health-care-life-expectancy","content":"Up First briefing: Christopher Wray; Luigi Mangione; Health care; DNC Why FBI Director Christopher Wray will step down from the role at the end of Biden's term. And, the UHC CEO killing turns public attention to the U.S. life expectancy and health care.\nSpecial Series Up First Newsletter\nAll Up First Stories Up First Podcast Morning Edition\nLISTEN & FOLLOW\nNPR App\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS link\nFBI Director to resign. And, UHC CEO killing brings attention to U.S. life expectancy\nDecember 12, 20247:58 AM ET\nBy\nBrittney Melton\nFBI Director Wray To Step Down, U.S. Life Expectancy, DNC Seeks New Leadership\n15:00\nGood morning. You're reading the Up First newsletter. Subscribe here to get it delivered to your inbox, and listen to the Up First podcast for all the news you need to start your day. Today's top stories FBI Director Christopher Wray announced yesterday that he will resign at the end of the Biden administration in January. The decision came after President-elect Donald Trump announced he wanted loyalist Kash Patel to replace Wray. Trump appointed Wray to the role in 2017 during his first term in office. Wray is exiting before his 10-year term ends.\nFBI Director Christopher Wray speaks during an Election Threats Task Force meeting at the Justice Department in September 2024.\nAndrew Harnik/Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nAndrew Harnik/Getty Images\n🎧 Wray said stepping down is the right thing to do for the FBI and will help avoid dragging it deeper into the fray, NPR's Ryan Lucas tells Up First. Wray emphasized that the work the FBI is doing is critical and that won't change. Trump was happy with the announcement, saying Wray's departure would mark the end of the weaponization of the justice system. Lucas says Wray defended the FBI's independence and tried to keep it out of political fights on Capitol Hill, but it was impossible with its investigations over the past several years. The bureau has investigated Hunter Biden, the Jan. 6 attack on the U.S. Capitol and Trump's efforts to overturn the 2020 election.\nSponsor Message\nA handwritten note found on murder suspect Luigi Mangione has turned attention to the U.S life expectancy. Initially, the killing of UnitedHealthcare CEO Brian Thompson sparked social media posts about the health insurance industry. The note, which the Associated Press obtained a copy of, talked about the disconnect between the expensive U.S. health care system and low life expectancy in the country. 🎧 The CDC's latest calculations put America's life expectancy at 77 ½ years, which is on par with countries like Ecuador and Croatia, NPR's Selena Simmons-Duffin says. Dr. Steven Woolf of Virginia Commonwealth University says research shows that about 10% to 20% of health outcomes are related to health care. Poor diet, physical inactivity, child poverty, public policies and gun violence also play a role. Drug overdoses account for many deaths among young people and are one factor that has driven the U.S. life expectancy down compared to countries without an opioid epidemic. Top Democratic National Committee members will meet in Washington over the next two days to discuss rules for picking new leadership and reflect on this year. The Democratic Party lost control of the White House and Senate and failed to retake control of the House during the recent election cycle. Now, the next chair of the DNC will have a lot of work ahead of them.\nSponsor Message\n🎧 The Democratic Party has faded in relevance and strategy over the last few election cycles, making way for super PACs to take the lead in highly specific swing states, according to NPR's Stephen Fowler. Fowler says there's growing acknowledgement that the DNC needs to focus more on year-round organizing, recruitment and fundraising. He adds they also need to help state parties -- especially in Republican-led states -- with these efforts. There's a feeling among party leaders that the next DNC chair needs to be someone with experience leading that type of always-on campaigning style. Two leading candidates are Ken Martin of Minnesota and Ben Wikler of Wisconsin. Today's listen\nItalian pop singer Mina in 1961, Cremona, Italy.\nSandro Rizzi/AP\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nSandro Rizzi/AP\nMina is one of the bestselling Italian musical artists of all time. At age 84, she just dropped a new album. She might not be a household name in the U.S., but like Barbra Streisand, she has sold more than 150 million records worldwide. Unlike Streisand, Mina hasn't performed publicly since 1978 and doesn't give media interviews. With the relative secrecy around her work, it can be hard to know how she keeps her immense vocal power going with age. Listen to snippets of Mina's music and hear from experts on why she sounds good. Picture show\nDashboard Altar. San Francisco, CA. 2019\nAmanda Lopez\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nAmanda Lopez\nOur Lady of Guadalupe is a significant religious figure showcasing hope and protection for Latinos in the U.S. and abroad. But for photographer Amanda Lopez, she's more of a powerful emblem of home. Lopez attended a church named Our Lady of Guadalupe growing up and was surrounded by Guadalupe's image. Her family's Catholic views initially shaped her own. But while working on her yearslong project Guadalupe, she began questioning her thoughts about what makes a good woman. She initially thought it meant you had to be humble, respectful and go with the status quo, but then felt uncomfortable with that idea.\nSponsor Message\n📷 See photos of how Lopez deconstructed her beliefs and began to visualize those conflicting thoughts through photography. 3 things to know before you go\nPeople celebrated in Jeddah as Saudi Arabia was announced Wednesday as the host of the 2034 FIFA World Cup.\nMahmoud Khaled/Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nMahmoud Khaled/Getty Images\nThe FIFA World Cup will be held in Saudi Arabia in 2034. The country made the only bid for the tournament, making yesterday's announcement a formality.Bill Belichick has been announced as the new football coach for UNC-Chapel Hill. His resume includes leading the New England Patriots to six Super Bowl wins. He has never coached college football. (via WUNC)Ryan Borgwardt appeared in court yesterday after disappearing while on a kayaking trip in Wisconsin in August. An investigation revealed he attempted to fake his death and flee to Europe. This newsletter was edited by Suzanne Nuyen.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"《Up First》简报：克里斯托弗·雷；路易吉·曼焦内；医疗保健；民主党全国委员会\n为什么联邦调查局局长克里斯托弗·雷将在拜登任期结束时辞职。以及，联合健康集团首席执行官遇害事件将公众注意力引向美国的预期寿命和医疗保健。\n特别系列《Up First》通讯\n所有《Up First》报道 《Up First》播客 《Morning Edition》\n收听并关注\nNPR 应用\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS 链接\n联邦调查局局长将辞职。此外，联合健康集团首席执行官遇害事件引发对美国预期寿命的关注\n2024年12月12日上午7:58（美国东部时间）\n作者\n布里特妮·梅尔顿\n联邦调查局局长雷将辞职、美国预期寿命、民主党全国委员会寻求新领导层\n15:00\n早上好。您正在阅读《Up First》通讯。订阅这里即可将其发送到您的收件箱，并收听《Up First》播客，获取开始一天所需的全部新闻。今日头条新闻 联邦调查局局长克里斯托弗·雷昨天宣布，他将在拜登政府于一月结束时辞职。此前，当选总统唐纳德·特朗普宣布希望由忠诚派卡什·帕特尔接替雷。特朗普在2017年首个任期内任命雷担任这一职务。雷将在其10年任期结束前离任。\n2024年9月，联邦调查局局长克里斯托弗·雷在司法部的一次选举威胁特别工作组会议上发言。\nAndrew Harnik/Getty Images\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nAndrew Harnik/Getty Images\n🎧 雷表示，卸任是对联邦调查局而言正确的做法，也将有助于避免让联邦调查局更深地卷入纷争，NPR记者瑞安·卢卡斯在《Up First》中说。雷强调，联邦调查局正在开展的工作至关重要，这一点不会改变。特朗普对这一声明表示满意，称雷的离开将标志着司法系统武器化的结束。卢卡斯说，雷为联邦调查局的独立性辩护，并试图让它避开国会山的政治斗争，但鉴于过去几年的调查，这不可能做到。该局调查了亨特·拜登、1月6日袭击美国国会大厦事件以及特朗普推翻2020年大选结果的努力。\n赞助商消息\n在谋杀嫌疑人路易吉·曼焦内身上发现的一张手写纸条，将注意力引向美国的预期寿命。最初，联合健康集团首席执行官布莱恩·汤普森遇害事件引发了社交媒体上关于医疗保险行业的帖子。美联社获得该纸条的一份副本，纸条谈到了美国昂贵的医疗体系与该国较低预期寿命之间的脱节。 🎧 美国疾病控制与预防中心的最新计算显示，美国人的预期寿命为77岁半，与厄瓜多尔和克罗地亚等国相当，NPR记者塞莱娜·西蒙斯-达芬说。弗吉尼亚联邦大学的史蒂文·伍尔夫博士说，研究显示，约有10%至20%的健康结果与医疗保健有关。饮食不良、缺乏身体活动、儿童贫困、公共政策和枪支暴力也发挥作用。药物过量导致许多年轻人死亡，也是推动美国预期寿命低于没有阿片类药物流行病国家的因素之一。民主党全国委员会的主要成员将在未来两天于华盛顿会面，讨论选出新领导层的规则，并反思今年的情况。在最近一轮选举中，民主党失去了对白宫和参议院的控制，并未能重新控制众议院。现在，下一任民主党全国委员会主席将有大量工作要做。\n赞助商消息\n🎧 民主党已经\n据NPR的Stephen Fowler称，在过去几轮选举中，这种方式在重要性和策略上逐渐式微，让位于超级政治行动委员会在高度具体的摇摆州中发挥主导作用。Fowler说，人们越来越认识到，民主党全国委员会需要更多地专注于全年组织、招募和筹款。他补充说，他们还需要帮助各州党组织——尤其是由共和党领导的州——开展这些工作。党内领导人有一种感觉，即下一任民主党全国委员会主席需要是一个有经验领导这种始终在线的竞选方式的人。两位主要候选人是明尼苏达州的Ken Martin和威斯康星州的Ben Wikler。\n\n今日聆听\n\n1961年的意大利流行歌手Mina，意大利克雷莫纳。\nSandro Rizzi/AP\n隐藏说明\n切换说明\nSandro Rizzi/AP\n\nMina是意大利有史以来唱片销量最高的音乐艺术家之一。84岁的她刚刚发行了一张新专辑。她在美国可能并非家喻户晓，但和芭芭拉·史翠珊一样，她在全球已售出超过1.5亿张唱片。与史翠珊不同的是，Mina自1978年以来就没有公开演出过，也不接受媒体采访。由于她的作品相对神秘，很难知道她随着年龄增长是如何保持巨大歌唱力量的。收听Mina音乐的片段，并听听专家解释她为何听起来依然动听。\n\n图片展\n\nDashboard Altar。加利福尼亚州旧金山。2019年\nAmanda Lopez\n隐藏说明\n切换说明\nAmanda Lopez\n\n瓜达卢佩圣母是一个重要的宗教人物，向美国及海外的拉丁裔展示希望与保护。但对摄影师Amanda Lopez来说，她更像是家的有力象征。Lopez从小就去一座名为瓜达卢佩圣母的教堂，并被瓜达卢佩的形象包围。她家人的天主教观念最初塑造了她自己的信仰。但在开展长达数年的项目《Guadalupe》期间，她开始质疑自己对于什么造就一个好女人的看法。她最初认为，这意味着你必须谦逊、尊重他人并随大流，但后来对这种想法感到不安。\n\n赞助商信息\n\n📷 查看Lopez如何解构自己的信仰，并开始通过摄影将这些相互冲突的想法视觉化。出发前需要了解的3件事\n\n周三宣布沙特阿拉伯成为2034年国际足联世界杯主办国后，人们在吉达庆祝。\nMahmoud Khaled/Getty Images\n隐藏说明\n切换说明\nMahmoud Khaled/Getty Images\n\n2034年国际足联世界杯将在沙特阿拉伯举行。该国是这项赛事唯一的申办方，因此昨天的宣布只是走个形式。\n\n比尔·贝利奇克已被宣布为北卡罗来纳大学教堂山分校新的橄榄球教练。他的履历包括带领新英格兰爱国者队六次赢得超级碗。他从未执教过大学橄榄球。（经由WUNC）\n\nRyan Borgwardt在去年8月于威斯康星州进行皮划艇旅行期间失踪，昨天出庭。调查显示，他曾试图伪造自己的死亡并逃往欧洲。\n\n本期通讯由Suzanne Nuyen编辑。\n\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n\n任何提出此类提议或要求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\n\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n连接\n通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻稿\n公众编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为 NPR 的赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2024-12-12T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-3290","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"FBI警告中国黑客威胁 专家建议使用端到端加密保护短信安全","title_en":"How to keep text messages secure as FBI warns of Chinese hackers : NPR","summary_cn":"FBI与CISA警告称，与中国政府有关联的黑客组织“盐台风”正入侵美国电信网络窃取用户数据，并建议民众使用端到端加密。报道采访安全专家，介绍Signal、WhatsApp、iMessage等加密工具，以及双因素认证、自动更新等防护措施，并讨论普通用户如何评估自身风险。","summary_en":"The FBI and CISA warned that hackers affiliated with China's government, dubbed Salt Typhoon, are infiltrating U.S. telecom networks and stealing user data, urging Americans to use end-to-end encryption. The report interviews security experts on encrypted apps like Signal, WhatsApp and iMessage, plus two-factor authentication and automatic updates, and discusses how ordinary users can assess their own threat model.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:33:19.996345+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2024/12/17/nx-s1-5223490/text-messaging-security-fbi-chinese-hackers-security-encryption","content":"How to keep text messages secure as FBI warns of Chinese hackers The U.S. believes hackers affiliated with China's government are infiltrating telecoms and stealing users' data. The FBI has urged people to use end-to-end encryption to keep their info safe.\nTechnology\nFBI warns Americans to keep their text messages secure: What to know\nDecember 17, 20245:00 AM ET\nHeard on All Things Considered\nBy\nBill Chappell\nFBI warns Americans to keep their text messages secure: What to know\nListen\n·\n2:23\n2:23\nTranscript\nToggle more options\nDownload\nEmbed\nEmbed\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5223490/nx-s1-5297484-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\nTranscript\nThe FBI and other agencies are encouraging people to use end-to-end encryption, citing what they say is a sustained hacking operation linked to China. In this 2021 photo, a smartphone's screen shows messaging apps including WhatsApp, Signal and Telegram.\nDamien Meyer/AFP via Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nDamien Meyer/AFP via Getty Images\nIt's not often that a piece of FBI advice triggers a Snopes fact check. But the agency's urgent message this month to Americans, often summarized as \"stop texting,\" surprised many consumers. The warning from the FBI and the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) highlighted vulnerabilities in text messaging systems that millions of Americans use every day.\nLife Kit\nYour Technology Is Tracking You. Take These Steps For Better Online Privacy\nThe U.S. believes hackers affiliated with China's government, dubbed Salt Typhoon, are waging a \"broad and significant cyber-espionage campaign\" to infiltrate commercial telecoms and steal users' data — and in isolated cases, to record phone calls, a senior FBI official who spoke to reporters on condition of anonymity said during a Dec. 3 briefing call. The CISA released a list of best security practices for smartphone users on Thursday, with specific tips for iPhone and Android owners. The agencies' guidance may have surprised consumers — but not security experts.\nSponsor Message\n\"People have been talking about things like this for years in the computer security community,\" Jason Hong, a professor at Carnegie Mellon University's School of Computer Science, told NPR. \"You should not rely on these kinds of unencrypted communications because of this exact reason: There could be snoopers in lots of infrastructure.\" So what should you do to keep your messages private? \"Encryption is your friend\" for texts and phone calls, Jeff Greene, CISA's executive assistant director for cybersecurity, said on the briefing call. \"Even if the adversary is able to intercept the data, if it is encrypted, it will make it impossible, if not really hard, for them to detect it. So our advice is to try to avoid using plain text.\" In full end-to-end encryption, tech companies make a message decipherable only by its sender and receiver — not by anyone else, including the company. It has been the default on WhatsApp, for instance, since 2016. Along with a promise of greater security, it makes companies \"warrant-proof\" from surveillance efforts. The good news for people who use Apple phones is that iMessage and FaceTime are also already end-to-end encrypted, says Hong. For Android phones, encryption is available in Google Messages if the senders and recipients all have the feature turned on.\nSponsor Message\nBut messages sent between iPhones and Android phones are less secure. The simplest way to ensure your messages are safe from snooping is to use an end-to-end encrypted app like Signal or WhatsApp, says Eva Galperin, director of cybersecurity at the Electronic Frontier Foundation (EFF). With these apps, \"your communications are end-to-end encrypted every single time,\" she says.\nTechnology\n'Green bubble shaming' at play in DOJ suit against Apple\nGalperin highlights another danger: A hacker who has managed to get your ID and password for a website can monitor your text messages to intercept a one-time passcode that's used in two-factor authentication (2FA). \"This is a really serious security risk,\" Galperin says. She recommends getting 2FA messages through an app like Google Authenticator or Authy or by using a physical security key to verify access. The FBI and CISA also advise users to set their phones to update operating systems automatically. \"Most compromises of systems do not involve taking advantage of vulnerabilities that no one else knows about,\" Galperin says, adding that \"often, the maker of the product has in fact figured out what the vulnerability is, fixed it and pushed out a patch in the form of a security update.\" How at risk are you? You should be aware of your own \"threat model\" — a core concept in computer security. Hong says it boils down to three questions: What are you trying to protect? How important is it to you? And what steps do you need to take to protect it?\nBusiness\nU.N. Urges Probe Of Reported Hacking Of Jeff Bezos' Phone By Saudi Arabia\nIf the most valuable items on your phone are family photos, he says, you probably shouldn't worry about foreign hackers targeting you. But what if you occasionally text about national or corporate secrets or politically sensitive data? \"If you are in business, if you are a journalist, if you are somebody in contact with democracy protesters in Hong Kong or Shenzhen or Tibet, then you might want to assume that your phone calls and text messages are not safe from the Chinese government,\" Galperin of the EFF says.\nSponsor Message\nBad actors such as cybercriminals might have different objectives, Hong says, \"but if you just do a few relatively simple things, you can actually protect yourself from the vast majority of those kinds of threats.\" What are the hackers doing? The FBI and CISA raised the alarm two months after The Wall Street Journal reported that hackers linked to the Chinese government have broken into systems that enable U.S. law enforcement agencies to conduct electronic surveillance operations under the Communications Assistance for Law Enforcement Act (CALEA). \"These are for legitimate wiretaps that have been authorized by the courts,\" Hong says. But in hackers' hands, he says, the tools could potentially be used \"to surveil communications and metadata for lots of people. And it seems like the [hackers'] focus is primarily Washington, D.C.\" The FBI says that the attack was far broader than the CALEA system and that the hackers are still accessing telecom networks. The U.S. has been working since late spring to determine the extent of their activities. This month, the Biden administration said at least eight telecommunications infrastructure companies in the U.S., and possibly more, had been broken into by Chinese hackers. The hackers stole a large amount of metadata, the FBI and CISA said. In far fewer cases, they said, the actual content of calls and texts was targeted. As agencies work to oust the hackers, the FBI called for Americans to embrace tight encryption — an about-face, Galperin says, after years of insisting that law enforcement agencies need a \"back door\" to access communications. The agencies also want companies to bolster their security practices and work with the government to make their networks harder to compromise. \"The adversaries we face are tenacious and sophisticated, and working together is the best way to ensure eviction,\" the senior FBI official said during the news briefing.\nSponsor Message\nAs for the risk to everyday consumers, security experts like Hong and Galperin say that with vast amounts of information traveling between our phones, they want to see people get more help in protecting themselves. \"I think it's really incumbent on software developers and these companies to have much better privacy and security by default,\" Hong says. \"That way you don't need a Ph.D. to really understand all the options and to be secure.\"\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"如何在 FBI 警告中国黑客之际确保短信安全\n美国认为，与中国政府有关联的黑客正在渗透电信网络并窃取用户数据。FBI 敦促人们使用端到端加密来保护自己的信息安全。\n科技\nFBI 警告美国人确保短信安全：须知事项\n2024年12月17日美国东部时间上午5:00\n《万事皆考虑》节目播出\n作者\n比尔·查佩尔\nFBI 警告美国人确保短信安全：须知事项\n收听\n·\n2:23\n2:23\n文字稿\n切换更多选项\n下载\n嵌入\n嵌入\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5223490/nx-s1-5297484-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR 嵌入式音频播放器\">\n文字稿\nFBI 和其他机构鼓励人们使用端到端加密，理由是他们称存在一项与中国有关联、持续进行的黑客行动。在这张摄于2021年的照片中，一部智能手机的屏幕显示了包括 WhatsApp、Signal 和 Telegram 在内的消息应用。\n达米安·迈耶/法新社 via Getty Images\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\n达米安·迈耶/法新社 via Getty Images\nFBI 的建议引发 Snopes 事实核查，这种情况并不常见。但该机构本月向美国人发出的紧急信息，常被概括为“停止发短信”，令许多消费者感到意外。FBI 和网络安全与基础设施安全局（CISA）的警告强调了短信系统中的漏洞，而数百万美国人每天都在使用这些系统。\n生活工具包\n你的技术正在追踪你。采取这些措施改善在线隐私\n美国认为，与中国政府有关联、被称为“盐台风”的黑客正在发动一场“广泛且重大的网络间谍活动”，以渗透商业电信网络并窃取用户数据；FBI 一名在12月3日简报电话会上向记者发言、但要求匿名的高级官员说，在个别情况下，他们还会录下电话通话。CISA 于周四发布了智能手机用户的最佳安全实践清单，其中包含针对 iPhone 和 Android 用户的具体建议。这些机构的指导可能让消费者感到意外——但安全专家并不意外。\n赞助商信息\n卡内基梅隆大学计算机科学学院教授杰森·洪告诉 NPR：“计算机安全界多年来一直有人讨论这类事情。”“正因为这个确切的原因，你不应依赖这类未加密的通信：基础设施中的许多环节都可能存在窥探者。”那么，你应该怎样保护自己的消息隐私？CISA 网络安全执行助理主任杰夫·格林在简报电话会上说：“对于短信和电话，加密是你的朋友。”“即使对手能够拦截数据，只要数据经过加密，他们就不可能——即便不是完全不可能，也会非常困难——发现其中内容。因此，我们的建议是尽量避免使用明文。”在完整的端到端加密中，科技公司让消息只能由发送者和接收者解密——其他任何人，包括公司自身，都无法解密。例如，WhatsApp 自2016年以来一直默认采用端到端加密。除了带来更高的安全性承诺外，这还使公司在面对监控行动时“无法凭搜查令提供数据”。洪说，对使用苹果手机的人来说，好消息是 iMessage 和 FaceTime 也已经采用端到端加密。对于 Android 手机，如果发送者和接收者都开启了该功能，Google Messages 也提供加密。\n赞助商信息\n但在 iPhone 之间发送的消息和\n安卓手机的安全性较低。电子前沿基金会（EFF）网络安全主管伊娃·加尔佩林说，确保你的消息不被窥探的最简单方法，是使用 Signal 或 WhatsApp 这类端到端加密应用。她说，使用这些应用时，“你的通信每一次都是端到端加密的”。\n科技\n司法部针对苹果的诉讼中出现“绿气泡羞辱”\n加尔佩林强调了另一种危险：黑客如果设法获取了你某个网站的用户名和密码，就可以监控你的短信，拦截用于双因素认证（2FA）的一次性验证码。加尔佩林说：“这是一个非常严重的安全风险。”她建议通过 Google Authenticator 或 Authy 之类的应用接收 2FA 消息，或者使用实体安全密钥验证访问权限。美国联邦调查局（FBI）和网络安全与基础设施安全局（CISA）也建议用户将手机设置为自动更新操作系统。加尔佩林说：“大多数系统遭到入侵，并不是因为有人利用了其他人都不知道的漏洞。”她还说，“通常，产品制造商其实已经找出了漏洞所在，修复了它，并以安全更新的形式推送了补丁。”你面临多大风险？你应该了解自己的“威胁模型”——这是计算机安全中的一个核心概念。Hong 说，这归结为三个问题：你想保护什么？这对你有多重要？你需要采取哪些措施来保护它？\n商业\n联合国敦促调查据报沙特阿拉伯黑入杰夫·贝索斯手机一事\n他说，如果你手机上最有价值的东西是家庭照片，那么你可能不必担心外国黑客以你为目标。但如果你偶尔会通过短信谈论国家机密、企业机密或政治敏感数据呢？EFF 的加尔佩林说：“如果你从事商业工作，如果你是记者，如果你与香港、深圳或西藏的民主抗议者有联系，那么你可能需要假定，你的电话和短信并不能免受中国政府的监控。”\n赞助商信息\nHong 说，网络犯罪分子等恶意行为者可能有不同的目标，“但如果你只做几件相对简单的事情，实际上就能保护自己免受绝大多数这类威胁。”黑客在做什么？《华尔街日报》报道称，与中国政府有关联的黑客侵入了帮助美国执法机构依据《执法通信协助法》（CALEA）开展电子监控行动的系统；两个月后，FBI 和 CISA 发出警报。Hong 说：“这些是经过法院授权的合法窃听。”但他说，如果落入黑客手中，这些工具可能会被用来“监视很多人的通信和元数据。而且似乎黑客的重点主要是华盛顿特区。”FBI 说，这次攻击的范围远远超出了 CALEA 系统，黑客仍在访问电信网络。自春末以来，美国一直在努力确定他们活动的范围。本月，拜登政府表示，美国至少有八家电信基础设施公司，可能还有更多，已被中国黑客入侵。FBI 和 CISA 说，黑客窃取了大量元数据。他们说，在少得多的案例中，电话和短信的实际内容成为目标。当各机构努力驱逐这些\n黑客事件后，联邦调查局呼吁美国人接受严密加密——加尔佩林说，这是在多年坚持执法机构需要“后门”来访问通信内容之后的态度大转变。这些机构还希望企业加强安全措施，并与政府合作，让其网络更难遭到入侵。“我们面对的对手顽强且老练，而协同合作是确保将其驱逐出去的最佳方式，”一名联邦调查局高级官员在新闻简报会上说。\n赞助商消息\n至于对日常消费者构成的风险，洪和加尔佩林等安全专家表示，随着大量信息在我们的手机之间传输，他们希望看到人们获得更多帮助来保护自己。洪说：“我认为，软件开发者和这些公司确实有责任在默认情况下提供更好的隐私和安全保护。这样一来，你就不需要拥有博士学位才能真正理解所有选项并确保安全。”\nNPR不会为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类提议或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻办公室\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n你的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2024-12-17T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-3178","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"司法部称不会解雇在1月6日案件中“合乎道德”行事的FBI探员","title_en":"DOJ says will not fire FBI agents who acted in 'ethical' way over Jan. 6 cases : NPR","summary_cn":"美国司法部代理副部长博夫向FBI员工发信息称，在1月6日国会骚乱调查中“只是服从命令并以合乎道德方式履行职责”的FBI员工不会面临解雇或处罚，只有存在腐败或党派意图、公然违抗上级命令或借机将FBI武器化的人才会被追责。此前司法部要求FBI提供参与相关案件人员信息，引发大规模清洗担忧。","summary_en":"Acting Deputy Attorney General Emil Bove told the FBI workforce that employees who simply followed orders and carried out their duties ethically in Jan. 6 Capitol riot investigations are not at risk of termination or penalties. Only those who acted with corrupt or partisan intent, defied Department leadership, or weaponized the FBI should be concerned. The message followed a Justice Department demand for information on personnel who worked on those cases, which sparked fears of a mass purge.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:26:54.812361+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2025/02/05/g-s1-46641/fbi-january-6-fired-justice","content":"DOJ says will not fire FBI agents who acted in 'ethical' way over Jan. 6 cases A senior DOJ official in a memo said only agents \"who acted with corrupt or partisan intent, who blatantly defied orders from Department leadership, or who exercised discretion in weaponizing the FBI\" should be concerned.\nPolitics\nDOJ says will not fire FBI agents who acted in 'ethical' way over Jan. 6 cases\nFebruary 5, 20252:33 PM ET\nRyan Lucas\nA seal reading \"Department of Justice Federal Bureau of Investigation\" is displayed on the J. Edgar Hoover FBI building in Washington, D.C. in 2022.\nStefani Reynolds/AFP via Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nStefani Reynolds/AFP via Getty Images\nA top Justice Department official said FBI agents who \"simply followed orders and carried out their duties in an ethical manner\" when conducting investigations into the Jan. 6 Capitol riot do not face the risk of being fired. That message to the FBI workforce on Wednesday from acting Deputy Attorney General Emil Bove is the latest in a back-and-forth between the Justice Department and the bureau as the Trump administration looks to remake the department, implement its agenda and push out those it deems disloyal. \"Let me be clear: No FBI employee who simply followed orders and carried out their duties in an ethical manner with respect to January 6 investigations is at risk of termination or other penalties,\" Bove writes in the message, a copy of which was obtained by NPR.\nSponsor Message\nPolitics\nFBI gives Justice details on employees who worked Jan. 6 cases, FBI agents sue\nThe FBI is part of the Justice Department, which declined to comment. The message to the workforce comes as Pam Bondi was sworn in as the new attorney general on Wednesday. The only individuals who should be concerned, the memo says, \"are those who acted with corrupt or partisan intent, who blatantly defied orders from Department leadership, or who exercised discretion in weaponizing the FBI.\" The FBI has been in turmoil since last week when Bove issued a memo, with the subject line \"Terminations,\" that ordered the firing of eight senior FBI officials, and demanded the bureau identify all current and former FBI personnel who worked on Jan. 6 cases or the recent prosecution of Hamas leaders.\nPolitics\nJustice Department changes rattle current and former agency veterans\nThat set off panic across the FBI and fears of a mass purge of agents who worked on those investigations. On Tuesday, the FBI provided the Justice Department with a list of bureau employees who worked on Jan. 6 cases, but that list did not contain names. Instead, the bureau provided employee ID numbers and other information, according to a person familiar with the matter.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"司法部称，不会解雇在1月6日案件中以“合乎道德”方式行事的联邦调查局特工\n司法部一名高级官员在备忘录中说，只有“出于腐败或党派动机行事、明显违抗司法部领导层命令，或行使裁量权将联邦调查局武器化”的特工才应感到担忧。\n政治\n司法部称，不会解雇在1月6日案件中以“合乎道德”方式行事的联邦调查局特工\n2025年2月5日下午2:33（美国东部时间）\nRyan Lucas\n2022年，华盛顿特区J·埃德加·胡佛联邦调查局大楼上展示着写有“司法部 联邦调查局”的徽章。\nStefani Reynolds/法新社 via Getty Images\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nStefani Reynolds/法新社 via Getty Images\n司法部一名高级官员表示，在调查1月6日国会大厦骚乱时“只是服从命令并以合乎道德的方式履行职责”的联邦调查局特工，不会面临被解雇的风险。代理副司法部长Emil Bove周三向联邦调查局工作人员传达的这一信息，是司法部与该局之间来回交锋的最新进展；特朗普政府正寻求重塑司法部、落实其议程，并清除其认定不忠的人。“让我明确说明：任何仅仅服从命令、并在涉及1月6日调查时以合乎道德方式履行职责的联邦调查局员工，都不会面临解雇或其他处罚的风险，”Bove在这则信息中写道；NPR获得了该信息的副本。\n赞助商信息\n政治\n联邦调查局向司法部提供参与1月6日案件员工的详细信息；联邦调查局特工提起诉讼\n联邦调查局隶属于司法部，司法部拒绝置评。这则面向工作人员的信息发布之际，Pam Bondi于周三宣誓就任新任司法部长。备忘录称，唯一应感到担忧的人“是那些出于腐败或党派动机行事、明显违抗司法部领导层命令，或行使裁量权将联邦调查局武器化的人”。自上周Bove发布一份主题为“解雇”的备忘录以来，联邦调查局一直陷入动荡；该备忘录下令解雇8名联邦调查局高级官员，并要求该局找出所有参与1月6日案件或近期对哈马斯领导人提起诉讼的现任和前任联邦调查局人员。\n政治\n司法部的变化令该机构现任和前任资深人士感到不安\n这在联邦调查局内部引发恐慌，并令参与这些调查的特工担心遭到大规模清洗。周二，联邦调查局向司法部提供了一份参与1月6日案件的该局员工名单，但名单中没有姓名。据一名熟悉此事的人士称，该局提供的是员工身份证号码和其他信息。\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类提供或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n连接\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻发布\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商信息\n赞助商信息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2025-02-05T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-3043","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"国土安全部裁减民权和监察办公室职位","title_en":"DHS cuts jobs in civil rights and ombudsmen offices : NPR","summary_cn":"美国国土安全部确认在三个监督办公室实施裁员，包括公民权利与公民自由办公室、移民拘留监察员办公室和公民及移民服务监察员办公室。DHS发言人称这些办公室阻碍移民执法。此举是联邦政府整体裁员的一部分，民主党参议员警告称裁减法定要求的民权办公室将危及DHS履行法定义务的能力。","summary_en":"The Department of Homeland Security confirmed it is implementing a reduction in force in three oversight offices: the Office for Civil Rights and Civil Liberties, the Office of the Immigration Detention Ombudsman, and the Office of the Citizenship and Immigration Services Ombudsman. A DHS spokesperson said these offices obstructed immigration enforcement. The cuts are part of broader federal workforce reductions, with Democratic senators warning that eliminating the statutorily required civil rights office would jeopardize DHS's ability to comply with legal requirements.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:56:38.813148+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2025/03/21/nx-s1-5336738/homeland-security-rif-cuts-dhs","content":"DHS cuts jobs in civil rights and ombudsmen offices DHS confirms it is implementing a reduction in force in three oversight offices as part of the effort to cut the federal workforce.\nPolitics\nHomeland Security makes cuts to civil rights and immigration oversight offices\nMarch 21, 20256:02 PM ET\nXimena Bustillo\nSecretary of Homeland Security Kristi Noem speaks to staff at DHS headquarters in Washington, D.C., on Jan. 28.\nManuel Balce Ceneta/Pool/AFP via Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nManuel Balce Ceneta/Pool/AFP via Getty Images\nThe Department of Homeland Security is cutting jobs in the oversight divisions focused on civil rights as a part of a broader reduction in force effort across the federal government. The affected offices, confirmed by DHS spokesperson Tricia McLaughlin, are the Office for Civil Rights and Civil Liberties (CRCL), the Office of the Immigration Detention Ombudsman and the Office of the Citizenship and Immigration Services Ombudsman. \"These offices have obstructed immigration enforcement by adding bureaucratic hurdles and undermining [the department's] mission,\" McLaughlin said in a statement. \"Rather than supporting law enforcement efforts, they often function as internal adversaries that slow down operations.\"\nSponsor Message\nLaw\nJudge Boasberg voices skepticism over use of Alien Enemies Act deportations\nThe two ombudsman offices provide oversight of the DHS immigration portfolio. This includes detention, by working with those detained who face issues with Immigration and Customs Enforcement; and the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services (USCIS), to help resolve issues with those seeking immigration-related benefits. \"DHS remains committed to civil rights protections but must streamline oversight to remove roadblocks to enforcement,\" McLaughlin said. \"These reductions ensure taxpayer dollars support the Department's core mission: border security and immigration enforcement.\" Across the government, federal agencies have announced their plans for job cuts as directed by the White House. The latest round of firings, which were first reported by Bloomberg, come after an initial round in February focusing primarily on the other parts of the agency that do not handle immigration enforcement. At that time, 405 DHS workers were laid off across cybersecurity, disaster response and science and technology. USCIS lost under 50 employees. Last week, ahead of the cuts, the Democratic ranking members of Senate Homeland and Governmental Affairs Committee and the Judiciary Committee, which primarily oversee immigration issues, sent a letter to Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem warning against cuts to the Civil Rights and Civil Liberties Office, noting that its role is \"statutorily-required.\"\nSponsor Message\n\"A decision to eliminate the CRCL office or make significant reductions in CRCL staff will jeopardize DHS's ability to comply with statutory requirements and to protect the civil rights and civil liberties of the American people,\" wrote Sens. Gary Peters, D-Mich., and Dick Durbin, D-Ill.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"国土安全部削减民权办公室和监察专员办公室的职位 国土安全部确认，作为削减联邦政府人员编制工作的一部分，该部正在三个监督办公室实施裁员。\n政治\n国土安全部削减民权和移民监督办公室\n2025年3月21日下午6:02（美国东部时间）\nXimena Bustillo\n国土安全部长克里斯蒂·诺姆于1月28日在华盛顿特区国土安全部总部向工作人员讲话。\nManuel Balce Ceneta/Pool/AFP via Getty Images\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nManuel Balce Ceneta/Pool/AFP via Getty Images\n国土安全部正在削减监督部门的职位，这些部门负责民权事务，是全联邦政府更广泛裁员行动的一部分。国土安全部发言人Tricia McLaughlin证实，受影响的办公室包括民权与公民自由办公室（CRCL）、移民拘留监察专员办公室，以及公民与移民服务监察专员办公室。“这些办公室通过增加官僚障碍、削弱[该部门的]使命，阻碍了移民执法，”McLaughlin在一份声明中说。“它们非但没有支持执法工作，反而常常充当内部对手，拖慢行动。”\n赞助商消息\n法律\nBoasberg法官对依据《外敌法》驱逐出境表示怀疑\n两个监察专员办公室负责监督国土安全部的移民事务。这包括拘留事务：与面临移民与海关执法局问题的被拘留者合作；以及监督美国公民及移民服务局（USCIS），帮助解决寻求移民相关福利者的问题。“国土安全部仍致力于保护民权，但必须精简监督工作，以消除执法障碍，”McLaughlin说。“这些削减确保纳税人的资金支持该部门的核心使命：边境安全和移民执法。”在全联邦范围内，各联邦机构已按照白宫指示宣布裁员计划。彭博社最先报道的最新一轮解雇，发生在2月首轮裁员之后；首轮主要针对该机构其他不负责移民执法的部门。当时，国土安全部在网络安全、灾害响应以及科学技术部门共裁员405人。美国公民及移民服务局减少了不到50名员工。上周，在裁员前夕，参议院国土安全与政府事务委员会以及司法委员会的民主党首席委员——这两个委员会主要负责监督移民事务——致信国土安全部长克里斯蒂·诺姆，警告不要削减民权与公民自由办公室，并指出该办公室的职责“由法律明确要求”。\n赞助商消息\n“如果决定取消CRCL办公室，或大幅削减CRCL工作人员，将危及国土安全部履行法定要求以及保护美国人民民权和公民自由的能力，”密歇根州民主党参议员Gary Peters和伊利诺伊州民主党参议员Dick Durbin写道。\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类提议或要求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n媒体\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字记录\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2025-03-21T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-3028","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美国住房与城市发展部将与国土安全部共享数据以锁定无合法身份移民","title_en":"HUD will share data with Homeland Security to target immigrants without legal status : NPR","summary_cn":"美国住房与城市发展部（HUD）宣布与国土安全部（DHS）合作，共享数据以识别居住在公共补贴住房中的无合法身份移民。报道指出该协议并未改变现行法律或政策，HUD仅投入一名全职人员。国土安全部长诺姆发表强硬表态，移民倡导者担忧此举产生寒蝉效应，可能推高无家可归人数。","summary_en":"The U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development says it is partnering with Homeland Security to identify immigrants without legal status living in publicly subsidized housing. NPR reports the agreement does not change current law or policy, and HUD is devoting just one full-time staffer. DHS Secretary Kristi Noem used forceful language, while immigrant advocates warn of a chilling effect and potential rise in homelessness.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:56:28.992298+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2025/03/26/nx-s1-5339689/hud-will-share-data-with-homeland-security-to-target-immigrants-without-legal-status","content":"HUD will share data with Homeland Security to target immigrants without legal status The U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development says it is partnering with Homeland Security to help identify immigrants without legal status living in publicly subsidized housing.\nNational\nHUD will share data with Homeland Security to target immigrants without legal status\nMarch 26, 20254:37 AM ET\nHeard on Morning Edition\nBy\nJennifer Ludden\n,\nLeila Fadel\nHUD will share data with Homeland Security to target immigrants without legal status\nListen\n·\n3:34\n3:34\nTranscript\nToggle more options\nDownload\nEmbed\nEmbed\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5339689/nx-s1-5402263-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\nTranscript\nThe U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development says it is partnering with Homeland Security to help identify immigrants without legal status living in publicly subsidized housing.\nSponsor Message\nTranscript\nLEILA FADEL, HOST: The U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development, or HUD, says it is going to start targeting immigrants without legal status. It's partnering with Homeland Security to help identify such immigrants living in publicly subsidized housing. NPR's Jennifer Ludden is here to explain what this all means. Good morning, Jennifer.JENNIFER LUDDEN, BYLINE: Good morning.FADEL: OK. So tell us more about this new partnership. What are they doing?LUDDEN: Well, it's basically an agreement between these two agencies to make sure no federally funded housing program will, quote, \"harbor or benefit illegal aliens.\" HUD says it's going to begin sharing data with Homeland Security to identify such people who are ineligible for housing aid. Here's HUD Secretary Scott Turner in a video of the signing of the agreement Monday.(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)SCOTT TURNER: We have a housing affordability crisis in our country. And so today, we are ensuring that American taxpayer dollars are used to assist the American people.LUDDEN: A press release cited exploitation of the housing program, and it quoted DHS Secretary Kristi Noem saying, if you are an illegal immigrant, you should leave now. The gravy train is over - so forceful language. But to be clear, this agreement does not actually change current law or policy, and HUD is devoting just one full-time staffer to this effort.FADEL: OK. So what does the law say about immigrants and federal housing aid and who qualifies for it?LUDDEN: Immigrants who do not have legal status cannot get federal rental assistance, but they can live with family members who qualify. So, you know, that could be certain legal permanent residents or, of course, those who have become or are U.S. citizens. A really common situation - immigrants without legal status who have U.S.-born children - you know, one or both of those parents may be ineligible for housing aid. And in these kinds of cases, the household gets a smaller subsidy, which means they pay a higher rent to account for family members not eligible.But even though the language in this agreement between HUD and DHS is pretty vague, it does still send a message. I spoke with Sonya Acosta. She's a senior policy analyst at the left-leaning Center on Budget and Policy Priorities.SONYA ACOSTA: What it does do is it intensifies already present fears for people who are immigrants, who are trying to seek services. These sorts of things create a massive chilling effect.LUDDEN: And immigrant advocates are worried about that. One coalition called Protecting Immigrant Families says their concern is that the Trump administration might push out some immigrants who do have a legal right to live in subsidized housing. And they say, you know, that could drive up homelessness.FADEL: So how many people are we talking about who might be affected?LUDDEN: So one estimate is from 2019 because that's when the first Trump administration tried to bar all of these mixed-status immigrant families from subsidized housing. A report by career staff at HUD estimated more than a hundred thousand people would lose housing aid and that most of them would be citizens or legal residents, about half of them children. Now, that proposed rule was never finalized. But many people - housing experts and immigration advocates - they do expect the current Trump administration to try again. And, you know, so maybe this new agreement is a first step in that direction. Last night, a HUD spokesperson would not confirm whether the agency intends to renew that push, but they did say that everything is on the table.FADEL: NPR's Jennifer Ludden. Thank you, Jennifer.LUDDEN: Thank you.\nCopyright © 2025 NPR. All rights reserved. Visit our website terms of use and permissions pages at www.npr.org for further information.\nAccuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR’s programming is the audio record.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"HUD将与国土安全部共享数据，以针对没有合法身份的移民\n美国住房与城市发展部表示，正与国土安全部合作，帮助识别居住在公共补贴住房中的没有合法身份的移民。\n全国\nHUD将与国土安全部共享数据，以针对没有合法身份的移民\n2025年3月26日上午4:37（美国东部时间）\n《晨间版》收听\n作者\nJennifer Ludden\n、\nLeila Fadel\nHUD将与国土安全部共享数据，以针对没有合法身份的移民\n收听\n·\n3:34\n3:34\n文字稿\n切换更多选项\n下载\n嵌入\n嵌入\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5339689/nx-s1-5402263-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\n文字稿\n美国住房与城市发展部表示，正与国土安全部合作，帮助识别居住在公共补贴住房中的没有合法身份的移民。\n赞助商消息\n文字稿\nLEILA FADEL，主持人：美国住房与城市发展部，简称HUD，表示将开始针对没有合法身份的移民。该部门正与国土安全部合作，帮助识别居住在公共补贴住房中的此类移民。NPR记者Jennifer Ludden将在这里为我们解释这一切意味着什么。早上好，Jennifer。\nJENNIFER LUDDEN，署名：早上好。\nFADEL：好的。请进一步介绍一下这项新合作。他们在做什么？\nLUDDEN：基本上，这是两家机构之间的一项协议，以确保没有任何联邦资助的住房项目会“窝藏或使非法外国人受益”。HUD表示，将开始与国土安全部共享数据，以识别那些没有资格获得住房援助、却居住在其中的人。以下是HUD部长Scott Turner在周一签署协议的视频中的讲话。\n（存档录音片段）\nSCOTT TURNER：我国正面临住房负担能力危机。因此，今天我们正在确保美国纳税人的钱被用于帮助美国人民。\nLUDDEN：一份新闻稿称住房项目遭到滥用，并引用国土安全部部长Kristi Noem的话说，如果你是非法移民，现在就应该离开。这个“福利输送列车”已经结束了——措辞非常强硬。但需要明确的是，这项协议实际上并未改变现行法律或政策，而HUD也只为这项工作投入了一名全职工作人员。\nFADEL：好的。那么，法律对移民获得联邦住房援助以及谁有资格获得援助是怎么规定的？\nLUDDEN：没有合法身份的移民不能获得联邦租金援助，但他们可以与符合资格的家庭成员一起居住。所以，这些家庭成员可能是某些合法永久居民，或者当然也可能是已经入籍或本来就是美国公民的人。一种非常常见的情况是，没有合法身份的移民有在美国出生的子女——你知道，其中一方或双方父母可能没有资格获得住房援助。在这类情况下，家庭获得的补贴会更少，这意味着他们要支付更高的租金，以计入那些没有资格获得援助的家庭成员。\n但尽管HUD与国土安全部这项协议中的措辞相当模糊，它仍然传达出一个信息。我采访了Sonya Acosta。她是偏左翼的预算与政策优先中心的高级政策分析师。\nSONYA ACOSTA：它确实加剧了移民群体中原本就存在的恐惧，他们正试图寻求服务。这类事情会造成巨大的寒蝉效应。\nLUDDEN：而\n移民权益倡导者对此感到担忧。一个名为“保护移民家庭”的联盟表示，他们担心特朗普政府可能会把一些确实拥有合法权利居住在补贴住房中的移民赶出去。他们说，你知道，这可能会推高无家可归人数。\n\nFADEL：那么，我们说的是可能受到影响的多少人？\n\nLUDDEN：有一个估计来自2019年，因为那时特朗普的第一届政府曾试图禁止所有这些身份混合的移民家庭住进补贴住房。住房和城市发展部（HUD）的职业工作人员发布的一份报告估计，将有超过10万人失去住房援助，其中大多数是公民或合法居民，约一半是儿童。现在，那项拟议规则从未最终确定。但许多人——住房专家和移民权益倡导者——确实预计，现任特朗普政府会再次尝试。而且，你知道，所以也许这项新协议是朝那个方向迈出的第一步。昨晚，HUD的一位发言人不愿确认该机构是否打算重新推动这项措施，但他们确实表示，一切都在考虑之中。\n\nFADEL：NPR的詹妮弗·勒登。谢谢你，詹妮弗。\n\nLUDDEN：谢谢。\n\n版权所有 © 2025 NPR。保留所有权利。有关更多信息，请访问我们网站的使用条款和许可页面：www.npr.org。\n\nNPR文字稿的准确性和可用性可能有所不同。文字稿文本可能会为纠正错误或与音频更新相匹配而进行修订。npr.org上的音频可能会在最初播出或发布后进行编辑。NPR节目具有权威性的记录是音频记录。\n\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n\n任何提出此类提供或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\n\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n连接\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n媒体\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多元化\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n你的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2025-03-26T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-2879","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"国土安全部请求2万名国民警卫队协助驱逐移民","title_en":"DHS asks for 20,000 National Guard troops to assist in deportations : NPR","summary_cn":"美国国土安全部请求五角大楼提供约2万名国民警卫队人员，协助驱逐无合法身份移民。一名美国官员向NPR透露，军方律师正在审查该请求。若获批，这将是国民警卫队首次在全国层面被要求协助驱逐移民。报道称各州州长将被要求征集志愿者，但具体角色和时间尚不明确。","summary_en":"The Department of Homeland Security is asking the Pentagon to provide some 20,000 National Guard forces to assist in removing migrants without legal status in the U.S. A U.S. official tells NPR that military lawyers are reviewing the request. If approved, it would be the first time Guard troops at the national level have been asked to assist in deportations.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:53:21.887097+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2025/05/15/nx-s1-5399656/national-guard-immigration","content":"DHS asks for 20,000 National Guard troops to assist in deportations If approved, the move would be the first time Guard troops at the national level have been asked to assist in the removal of migrants in the U.S. without legal status.\nImmigration\nDHS asks for 20,000 National Guard troops to assist in deportations\nMay 15, 20254:20 PM ET\nTom Bowman\nMichigan Air National Guard troops listen as President Trump speaks during a visit with Michigan Air National Guard Troops on April 29 at Selfridge Air National Guard Base, Mich.\nScott Olson/Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nScott Olson/Getty Images\nThe Department of Homeland Security is asking the Pentagon to provide some 20,000 National Guard forces to assist in the removal of migrants in the United States without legal status. A U.S. official with knowledge of the plan who was not authorized to talk tells NPR that military lawyers are reviewing the request from DHS. The request was first reported by The New York Times.\nLaw\nSupreme Court justices appear divided in birthright citizenship arguments\nUnder the proposal, state governors would be asked for volunteers among their Guard forces to take part in the removals. There's no word on when this would happen, or when the call would go out for volunteers.\nSponsor Message\nNational\nTroops arrive at the border to help with enforcement despite fewer migrant crossings\nThere's also no sense at this point what role the Guard troops would play — whether they would provide just transportation or security, or if they would detain migrants or take part in arrests. In February, Republican Texas Gov. Greg Abbott gave Texas Guard members the authority to make immigration arrests. This would be the first time Guard troops at the national level have been asked to assist in deportations. President Trump last week ordered DHS to increase its deportation force by 20,000 officers. Using Guard forces would be the quickest way to make that happen. There's no indication that Trump would act to federalize the National Guard, a move that would effectively take control of the Guard troops from governors. The National Guard was last federalized in 1992 during the Los Angeles riots following the police beating of Rodney King.\nImmigration\nMajority of Americans oppose ending birthright citizenship, NPR/Ipsos poll finds\nThe Trump administration has made greater use of active duty troops along the U.S.-Mexico border to enhance security and combat illegal immigration. There are now more than 10,000 troops along the border, a sharp increase from the estimated 2,500 who took part in that role during the Biden administration. The Pentagon also recently expanded its control of land along the border, taking over a 60-foot wide strip known as the Roosevelt Reservation. Military officials say the troops there have the power to \"temporarily detain trespassers\" until local law enforcement can assume custody.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nnational guard\nDepartment of Homeland Security\nimmigration policy\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"DHS请求2万名国民警卫队士兵协助驱逐出境\n如果获批，这将是国家层面的国民警卫队部队首次被要求协助清除在美国没有合法身份的移民。\n移民\nDHS请求2万名国民警卫队士兵协助驱逐出境\n2025年5月15日下午4:20（美国东部时间）\nTom Bowman\n4月29日，特朗普总统访问密歇根州塞尔弗里奇空军国民警卫队基地期间，密歇根州空军国民警卫队士兵听取他的讲话。\nScott Olson/Getty Images\n隐藏说明文字\n切换说明文字\nScott Olson/Getty Images\n国土安全部正请求五角大楼提供约2万名国民警卫队成员，协助清除在美国没有合法身份的移民。一名了解该计划、但无权公开发言的美国官员告诉NPR，军方法律顾问正在审查DHS的请求。《纽约时报》率先报道了这一请求。\n法律\n最高法院大法官在出生公民权辩论中似乎意见分歧\n根据该提案，各州州长将被要求在其国民警卫队部队中招募志愿者参与遣返。目前尚不清楚这何时会发生，也不知道何时会发出志愿者征集通知。\n赞助商消息\n国家\n尽管移民过境人数减少，部队仍抵达边境协助执法\n目前也不清楚国民警卫队将扮演什么角色——是仅提供运输或安保，还是拘留移民或参与逮捕。2月，共和党得州州长Greg Abbott授权得州国民警卫队成员进行移民逮捕。这将是国家层面的国民警卫队部队首次被要求协助驱逐出境。特朗普总统上周下令DHS将其驱逐部队增加2万名官员。使用国民警卫队部队将是实现这一目标最快的方式。目前没有迹象表明特朗普会采取行动将国民警卫队联邦化；此举实际上会从州长手中接管对国民警卫队部队的控制。国民警卫队上一次被联邦化是在1992年洛杉矶骚乱期间，当时警方殴打Rodney King事件引发了骚乱。\n移民\nNPR/Ipsos民调发现，多数美国人反对终止出生公民权\n特朗普政府加大了对美墨边境现役部队的使用，以加强安全并打击非法移民。目前边境沿线有超过1万名士兵，远高于拜登政府时期估计参与该任务的2500人。五角大楼最近还扩大了对边境土地的控制，接管了一条名为罗斯福保留地、宽60英尺的狭长地带。军方官员表示，驻扎在那里的部队有权“暂时拘留擅自闯入者”，直到当地执法部门能够接管拘押。\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类报价或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\n国民警卫队\n国土安全部\n移民政策\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n连接\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻监察专员\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n你的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2025-05-15T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-2844","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"国土安全部长诺姆在参议院质询中错误解释人身保护令","title_en":"Under questioning, Kristi Noem can't define habeas corpus : NPR","summary_cn":"美国国土安全部长克里斯蒂·诺姆在参议院国土安全与政府事务委员会听证会上，被问及人身保护令时错误地将其描述为总统驱逐个人的宪法权力。参议员哈桑当场纠正，指出人身保护令是防止非法拘禁的基石性宪法原则。法律学者指出，总统无权中止该令状，宪法仅允许国会在叛乱或入侵等特殊情况下暂停。","summary_en":"Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem, under questioning from Democratic senators, incorrectly described habeas corpus as a presidential authority to deport individuals. Sen. Maggie Hassan corrected her, noting habeas corpus is a foundational right protecting against unlawful imprisonment. Legal experts say the president cannot suspend the writ; only Congress may do so in exceptional circumstances.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:53:10.981856+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2025/05/20/nx-s1-5405144/habeas-corpus-noem-dhs-senate","content":"Under questioning, Kristi Noem can't define habeas corpus Facing sharp questioning from Democratic lawmakers, the director of Homeland Security incorrectly described the constitutional right as a presidential authority to deport individuals.\nImmigration\nDHS secretary misstates meaning of habeas corpus under Senate scrutiny\nUpdated May 20, 20255:47 PM ET\nOriginally published May 20, 20253:26 PM ET\nBy\nScott Neuman\nHomeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem testifies before a Senate Committee on Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs hearing on Capitol Hill, Tuesday, May 20, 2025, in Washington.\nManuel Balce Ceneta/AP\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nManuel Balce Ceneta/AP\nHomeland Security Director Kristi Noem, under sharp questioning from Democratic senators during a Tuesday hearing, incorrectly described habeas corpus as a presidential authority to deport individuals. Noem was on Capitol Hill to testify before the Senate Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs Committee on the Department of Homeland Security's budget for fiscal year 2026. She was asked by Sen. Maggie Hassan, D-N.H., to define \"habeas corpus.\" The secretary responded: \"Habeas corpus is a constitutional right that the president has to be able to remove people from this country.\" In reality, habeas corpus is a bedrock constitutional legal principle that safeguards individuals from unlawful imprisonment by enabling them to petition the court to review the legality of their detention.\nSponsor Message\nResponding, Senator Hassan corrected the secretary, stating, \"Habeas corpus is the foundational right that separates free societies like America from police states like North Korea.\" She then pressed Noem, asking whether she supports \"the core protection\" of habeas corpus — that the government must present a public justification to detain or imprison someone. \"Yes, I support habeas corpus,\" Noem replied. \"I also recognize that the President of the United States has the authority under the Constitution to decide if it should be suspended or not.\"\nArticle I, Section 9 of the U.S. Constitution prohibits the suspension of habeas corpus \"unless when in Cases of Rebellion or Invasion the public Safety may require it.\" Brandon Garrett, a Duke University professor of law, says Noem's comments represent the latest in \"a series of recent misstatements by federal officials regarding the basic constitutional protections of habeas corpus.\" \"The Suspension Clause is quite restrictive permitting Congress to suspend habeas corpus only in exceptional circumstances. The President cannot suspend the writ,\" he says. Speaking earlier this month, White House adviser Stephen Miller said the administration was \"looking at\" ways to end due process protections for undocumented migrants, possibly by invoking a 1798 law known as the Alien Enemies Act.\nImmigration\nHistorical precedent: Courts wrestle with White House's 'invasion' claim\n\"The Constitution is clear, and that, of course, is the supreme law of the land, that the privilege of the writ of habeas corpus can be suspended at a time of invasion,\" Miller told reporters. However, the Supreme Court on Friday granted a preliminary injunction extending a pause on deportations of Venezuelan immigrants in northern Texas under the Alien Enemies Act.\nLaw\nSupreme Court extends pause on deportations under Alien Enemies Act in Texas\nIn 1861, President Abraham Lincoln ordered the suspension of the writ of habeas corpus, arguing that it was necessary to protect the Union from sabotage by Confederate sympathizers. The Supreme Court subsequently ruled that the president lacked that authority. It wasn't until 1863 that Congress formalized Lincoln's order by passing the Habeas Corpus Suspension Act. President Franklin D. Roosevelt's executive order to detain thousands of Americans of Japanese origin during World War II was at the time also challenged on grounds that it unlawfully denied habeas corpus to the detainees.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nKristi Noem\nhabeas corpus\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"在质询中，克里斯蒂·诺姆无法定义人身保护令\n面对民主党议员的尖锐质询，国土安全部部长错误地将这项宪法权利描述为总统驱逐个人的权力。\n移民\n国土安全部部长在参议院审查下错误陈述人身保护令的含义\n更新于2025年5月20日下午5:47（美国东部时间）\n最初发布于2025年5月20日下午3:26（美国东部时间）\n作者\n斯科特·诺伊曼\n2025年5月20日，星期二，国土安全部长克里斯蒂·诺姆在华盛顿国会山举行的参议院国土安全和政府事务委员会听证会上作证。\n曼努埃尔·巴尔塞·塞内塔/AP\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\n曼努埃尔·巴尔塞·塞内塔/AP\n国土安全部部长克里斯蒂·诺姆在周二的听证会上遭到民主党参议员的尖锐质询，她错误地将人身保护令描述为总统驱逐个人的权力。诺姆来到国会山，就国土安全部2026财政年度预算向参议院国土安全和政府事务委员会作证。新罕布什尔州民主党参议员玛吉·哈桑要求她定义“人身保护令”。部长回答：“人身保护令是一项宪法权利，总统有权据此将人们移出这个国家。”实际上，人身保护令是一项根本性的宪法法律原则，通过允许个人请求法院审查其被拘留的合法性，保护他们免遭非法监禁。\n赞助商消息\n哈桑参议员回应并纠正了部长，她说：“人身保护令是将美国这样的自由社会与朝鲜这样的警察国家区分开来的根本权利。”随后她继续追问诺姆，询问她是否支持人身保护令的“核心保障”——政府必须提出公开理由，才能拘留或监禁某人。“是的，我支持人身保护令，”诺姆回答。“我也承认，根据宪法，美国总统有权决定是否应当暂停这项权利。”\n美国宪法第一条第九款禁止暂停人身保护令，“除非在发生叛乱或入侵时，公共安全可能要求暂停”。杜克大学法学教授布兰登·加勒特表示，诺姆的言论代表了“联邦官员近期就人身保护令这一基本宪法保障发表的一系列错误说法中的最新一例”。他说：“暂停条款的限制性很强，只允许国会在特殊情况下暂停人身保护令。总统不能暂停这项令状。”本月早些时候，白宫顾问斯蒂芬·米勒表示，政府“正在研究”结束对无证移民正当程序保障的方式，可能会援引一部被称为《外侨敌人法》的1798年法律。\n移民\n历史先例：法院为白宫所谓“入侵”的说法而争论\n米勒告诉记者：“宪法是明确的，当然，它是这个国家的最高法律，人身保护令的特权可以在发生入侵时被暂停。”然而，最高法院周五批准了一项初步禁令，根据《外侨敌人法》，延长了得克萨斯州北部暂停驱逐委内瑞拉移民的措施。\n法律\n最高法院延长得克萨斯州依据《外侨敌人法》暂停驱逐的措施\n1861年，亚伯拉罕·林肯总统下令暂停人身保护令，理由是有必要保护\n联邦免受邦联同情者的破坏。最高法院随后裁定，总统无权这样做。直到1863年，国会通过《人身保护令中止法》，才将林肯的命令正式化。第二次世界大战期间，富兰克林·D·罗斯福总统下令拘禁数千名日裔美国人，当时也因非法剥夺被拘留者的人身保护令权利而受到质疑。\n\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n\n任何提出此类提供或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\n\nKristi Noem\n人身保护令\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n连接\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻发布\n公众编辑\n更正\n文字记录\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多元化\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2025-05-20T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-2577","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"国土安全部敦促DACA受益者自行离境","title_en":"DHS urges DACA recipients to self-deport : NPR","summary_cn":"NPR报道，美国国土安全部助理新闻秘书特里西娅·麦克劳克林表示，DACA不赋予任何合法身份，任何DACA受益者都可能因犯罪等原因被逮捕和驱逐，并鼓励所有非法居留者自行离境。文章介绍DACA的保护内容、近期政府削减福利与执法逮捕案例，以及移民倡导者和律师对政策不确定性的担忧。","summary_en":"NPR reports that DHS assistant press secretary Tricia McLaughlin said DACA does not confer any legal status, that recipients may be subject to arrest and deportation for reasons including crimes, and urged all people in the country illegally to self-deport. The article explains DACA protections, recent benefit cuts and enforcement arrests, and concerns from advocates and lawyers about policy uncertainty.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:24:58.258558+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2025/07/29/nx-s1-5482923/dhs-daca-recipients-self-deport","content":"DHS urges DACA recipients to self-deport \"DACA does not confer any form of legal status in this country,\" said DHS assistant press secretary Tricia McLaughlin, who then encouraged \"every person here illegally\" to self-deport.\nImmigration\nDHS is urging DACA recipients to self-deport\nJuly 29, 20255:00 AM ET\nXimena Bustillo\nDemonstrators gather in front of the U.S. Supreme Court in 2019 when the court heard arguments on Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals after the Trump administration tried to wind it down.\nJahi Chikwendiu/The Washington Post via Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nJahi Chikwendiu/The Washington Post via Getty Images\nThe Trump administration is shifting its tone on how it handles immigrants brought to the U.S. as children under the Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals program. Also known as DACA, the program was created in 2012 to protect children who arrived in the country illegally prior to 2007 from deportation. In recent months, the administration has tried to strip 525,000 DACA recipients, also known as Dreamers, of benefits, although no regulatory changes have been made to end the program. For example, the Health and Human Services Department said it would make DACA recipients ineligible for the federal health care marketplace in June. Then last week, the Education Department said it was looking into five universities that offer financial help for DACA recipients. Also, immigration enforcement officers have arrested and detained DACA recipients throughout the country, which immigrant advocates said weakens protections of this group.\nSponsor Message\nNational\nBiden moves to expand DACA recipients' access to government-funded health insurance\n\"Illegal aliens who claim to be recipients of Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals (DACA) are not automatically protected from deportations,\" DHS assistant press secretary Tricia McLaughlin said in a statement to NPR. \"DACA does not confer any form of legal status in this country.\" McLaughlin added that any DACA recipient may be subject to arrest and deportation for a number of reasons, including if they've committed a crime. McLaughlin then urged recipients to self-deport. \"We encourage every person here illegally to take advantage of this offer and reserve the chance to come back to the U.S. the right legal way,\" McLaughlin said. The call for self-deportation of DACA recipients sends another mixed message in the administration's immigration enforcement policy. At the start of the 2024 presidential campaign, now-White House deputy chief of staff Stephen Miller said President Trump would end the program. After winning the election, Trump said he wanted DACA recipients to stay. \"We've known that DACA remains a program that has been temporary. We've sounded the alarms over that,\" said Anabel Mendoza, communications director for United We Dream, an immigrant youth organization. \"What we are seeing now is that DACA is being chipped away at.\" What are DACA's protections? DACA offers temporary protection from deportation but is not an immediate path to citizenship or a green card. Participants in the program have to renew their protection every two years.\nSponsor Message\nIt provides a work permit and can be adjusted if a person leaves the U.S. and comes back with a visa or marries a U.S. citizen, among other options. When it was created under the Obama administration, the program took eligible children \"out of the immigration enforcement system,\" said Claire McNulty, a former DHS Immigration and Customs Enforcement official who worked at the department when DACA was created. McNulty was later politically appointed by former President Joe Biden to a senior counselor position in the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. \"If somebody was eligible for DACA, that might mean that they would be released from detention or their case in the immigration court system would be administratively closed so that they could then pursue that sort of administrative relief,\" McNulty said. The roughly 500,000 DACA recipients — counted as of the second quarter of this year — are from more than 150 countries. The majority are from Mexico, El Salvador and Guatemala, according to the USCIS. Most recipients are 35 years old or younger, but some are in their late 30s and early 40s. So far there has been no effort by this second Trump administration to rescind the program, as Trump tried in his first term. A lawsuit filed by Texas is challenging the program's protections from deportations and work permits for participants in the state. \"The notion that it does not provide protection is simply false,\" said Thomas A. Saenz, president and general counsel of the Mexican American Legal Defense and Educational Fund, adding that there are reasons DACA protections can be revoked from an individual, including being charged with a crime, which would make them vulnerable to deportation. Other lawyers point to infractions like driving under the influence of alcohol as a reason DACA and its protections can be revoked.\nSponsor Message\nSaenz said DACA should provide protection from being caught up in a raid by immigration agents or indiscriminately targeted for arrest on the street. If the administration wanted to take steps to change that, it would need to submit a proposed rule change with the Federal Register, or at least publicly state that position, neither of which has been done, he said. But the administration, he said, appears to have a broader approach to immigration enforcement that's sweeping in DACA recipients. \"Reported arrests of DACA recipients has other DACA recipients very concerned and we've heard it from them,\" Saenz said. Enforcement incidents lead to fear In March, officials deported Evenezer Cortez Martinez, a DACA recipient in Missouri, to Mexico. He was allowed to return after two weeks. In California, DACA recipients have been detained after a worksite raid and an accidental wrong turn off the freeway. In Florida, a DACA recipient was among the first held at the newly opened Everglades detention center.\nLaw\nSupreme Court Rules For DREAMers, Against Trump\n\"This administration is very strict on how they're applying all of the law and how they're interpreting all of the law. DACA at least used to be a topic that was much more sympathetic in politics,\" said Maria Quiroga, an immigration attorney who has clients who are DACA recipients. \"And that sympathy is now less and less.\" Polls conducted over the last five years have shown most Americans support the creation of a legal pathway for DACA recipients. With respect to polling on Trump's immigration policies overall, 43% approve of his handling of the issue so far, according to the most recent NPR/PBS News/Marist poll published earlier this month. Reyna Montoya is the founder of Aliento, a nonprofit that supports DACA recipients and other immigrants, and is a DACA recipient herself. She has been involved with advocating for DACA since 2010. Now 34, she said she has spent most of her young adult life advocating for the program.\nSponsor Message\n\"It's been a roller coaster between the three branches of government,\" Montoya said, adding that the mixed messaging from the administration is adding to the fear. \"My livelihood and the livelihood of so many Dreamers is at stake and that we could potentially be subject to being deported to countries that we don't really know or we don't call home.\"\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\ndaca\nimmigration policy\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"国土安全部敦促DACA受助者自行离境\n“DACA并不赋予任何形式的合法身份，”国土安全部助理新闻秘书特里西娅·麦克劳克林说，随后她鼓励“这里每一个非法居留的人”自行离境。\n移民\n国土安全部敦促DACA受助者自行离境\n2025年7月29日美国东部时间上午5:00\n希梅娜·布斯蒂略\n2019年，示威者聚集在美国最高法院前；当时，最高法院就“童年入境者暂缓遣返”计划举行辩论，此前特朗普政府曾试图逐步终止该计划。\nJahi Chikwendiu/《华盛顿邮报》经Getty Images提供\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nJahi Chikwendiu/《华盛顿邮报》经Getty Images提供\n特朗普政府正在改变其处理那些幼年时根据“童年入境者暂缓遣返”计划被带到美国的移民的态度。该计划也称为DACA，于2012年设立，旨在保护2007年之前非法入境的儿童免遭驱逐。近几个月来，政府一直试图剥夺52.5万名DACA受助者（也称为“追梦人”）的福利，尽管尚未进行任何监管变更来终止该计划。例如，卫生与公共服务部6月表示，将使DACA受助者无法参加联邦医疗保险市场。随后上周，教育部表示正在调查五所为DACA受助者提供经济援助的大学。此外，移民执法人员在全国各地逮捕并拘留DACA受助者，移民权益倡导者称，这削弱了对这一群体的保护。\n赞助商消息\n国内\n拜登推动扩大DACA受助者获得政府资助医疗保险的渠道\n“声称自己是‘童年入境者暂缓遣返’（DACA）受助者的‘非法外国人’并不会自动受到保护而免遭驱逐，”国土安全部助理新闻秘书特里西娅·麦克劳克林在给美国国家公共电台的一份声明中说。“DACA并不赋予任何形式的合法身份。”麦克劳克林补充说，DACA受助者可能因多种原因遭到逮捕和驱逐，包括他们实施过犯罪。随后，麦克劳克林敦促受助者自行离境。“我们鼓励这里每一个非法居留的人利用这一机会，并保留以正确合法方式返回美国的机会，”麦克劳克林说。敦促DACA受助者自行离境的呼吁，向政府的移民执法政策传递了另一种相互矛盾的信息。在2024年总统竞选开始时，如今的白宫副幕僚长斯蒂芬·米勒表示，特朗普总统将终止该计划。赢得选举后，特朗普表示希望DACA受助者留下。“我们一直知道DACA仍然是一项临时计划。我们已经对此发出警报，”移民青年组织United We Dream的传播主任阿纳贝尔·门多萨说。“我们现在看到的是，DACA正被一点点削弱。”DACA提供哪些保护？DACA提供暂时免遭驱逐的保护，但并不是立即获得公民身份或绿卡的途径。该计划参与者必须每两年续期一次保护。\n赞助商消息\n该计划提供工作许可证；如果一个人离开美国后持签证返回，或与美国公民结婚，以及其他选择，工作许可可以进行调整。克莱尔·麦克纳尔蒂说，该计划在奥巴马政府时期创建时，将符合条件的儿童“移出移民执法体系”，她是一名\n曾在创建DACA时任职于国土安全部移民与海关执法局的前官员。麦克纳尔蒂后来被前总统乔·拜登通过政治任命，担任美国公民及移民服务局高级顾问。“如果某人符合DACA资格，这可能意味着他们会被释放出拘留，或者其移民法庭案件会被行政结案，这样他们就可以继续寻求那类行政救济，”麦克纳尔蒂说。截至今年第二季度统计，约有50万名DACA受益人，来自150多个国家。根据美国公民及移民服务局的数据，大多数人来自墨西哥、萨尔瓦多和危地马拉。大多数受益人年龄在35岁或以下，但也有一些人已接近40岁，甚至刚过40岁。截至目前，特朗普第二届政府尚未采取撤销该项目的行动，而特朗普在第一任期内曾试图这样做。得克萨斯州提起的一起诉讼，正在挑战该项目为该州参与者提供的防止驱逐保护和工作许可。“说它不提供保护是完全错误的，”墨西哥裔美国人法律辩护与教育基金总裁兼总法律顾问托马斯·A·萨恩斯说。他补充道，DACA保护可以因个人原因被撤销，例如被指控犯罪，这会使其面临被驱逐的风险。其他律师指出，酒后驾车等违规行为也可能成为撤销DACA及其保护的理由。\n赞助商消息\n萨恩斯说，DACA应当保护受益人免遭移民执法人员突袭时被卷入其中，或在街头被不加区别地锁定逮捕。他说，如果政府想采取措施改变这一点，就需要向《联邦公报》提交拟议规则变更，或者至少公开声明这一立场，但两者都没有做到。不过，他说，政府在移民执法方面似乎采取了更宽泛的做法，将DACA受益人也卷入其中。“据报道，DACA受益人被捕，这让其他DACA受益人非常担忧，我们也从他们那里听到了这种担忧，”萨恩斯说。执法事件引发恐惧\n今年3月，官员将密苏里州一名DACA受益人埃韦内泽·科尔特斯·马丁内斯驱逐到墨西哥。两周后，他获准返回。在加利福尼亚州，DACA受益人在一次工作场所突袭以及一次意外驶离高速公路的事件后被拘留。在佛罗里达州，一名DACA受益人被关押在新开放的埃弗格雷兹拘留中心，成为首批被关押者之一。\n法律\n最高法院为“梦想者”裁决，反对特朗普\n“本届政府在如何适用所有法律以及如何解释所有法律方面都非常严格。至少过去，DACA还是一个在政治上更容易获得同情的话题，”移民律师玛丽亚·基罗加说，她的客户中有DACA受益人。“而如今，这种同情越来越少。”过去五年进行的民调显示，大多数美国人支持为DACA受益人建立合法途径。至于对特朗普总体移民政策的民调，根据本月早些时候公布的最新NPR/PBS新闻台/马里斯特民调，目前有43%的受访者认可他处理这一问题的方式。雷娜·蒙托亚是非营利组织Aliento的创始人，该组织支持DACA受益人和其他移民；她本人也是DACA受益人。自2010年以来，她一直参与推动DACA。她现年34岁，她说自己已经\n她成年后的大部分时间都在倡导这项计划。\n赞助商消息\n“在政府的三个分支之间，这就像坐过山车一样，”蒙托亚说，并补充道，政府传递的混杂信息正在加剧恐惧。“我的生计以及许多‘追梦者’的生计都岌岌可危，我们可能会被遣返回那些我们并不真正了解、也不称之为家园的国家。”\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类提议或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\ndaca\n移民政策\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n主页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻稿\n公众编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2025-07-29T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2388","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google via Apify","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"回顾：FBI局长卡什·帕特尔出席国会听证","title_en":"ICYMI: FBI Director Kash Patel Appears Before Congress : 1A : NPR","summary_cn":"NPR节目回顾联邦调查局局长卡什·帕特尔出席众议院司法委员会听证的情况。帕特尔连续第二天接受议员质询，问题涉及其对FBI的领导，包括解雇数十名FBI探员和官员、公布与涉嫌性贩运者杰弗里·爱泼斯坦有关的调查文件，以及右翼评论员查理·柯克遭枪击事件。帕特尔在听证开始时为其领导下的FBI进行辩护。节目还讨论了此次听证的主要看点。","summary_en":"NPR's 1A recaps FBI Director Kash Patel's appearance before the House Judiciary Committee. Patel faced a second day of questioning by lawmakers about his leadership of the bureau, including the firing of dozens of FBI agents and officials, the release of investigative files related to alleged sex trafficker Jeffrey Epstein, and the shooting of right-wing commentator Charlie Kirk. Patel began the hearings by defending the agency under his leadership. The program also discusses the biggest takeaways from the hearing.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T05:33:58.598422+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2025/09/18/nx-s1-5545822/icymi-fbi-director-kash-patel-appears-before-congress","content":"ICYMI: FBI Director Kash Patel Appears Before Congress : 1A FBI Director Kash Patel appeared before the House Judiciary Committee on Wednesday for a second day of questioning by lawmakers.He faced questions about his leadership of the bureau including the firing of dozens of FBI agents and officials, the release of investigative files related to alleged sex trafficker Jeffrey Epstein, and the shooting of right-wing commentator Charlie Kirk.Patel began his hearings on Wednesday by defending the agency under his leadership. What were some of the biggest takeaways from the the hearing?Find more of our programs online. Listen to 1A sponsor-free by signing up for 1A+ at plus.npr.org/the1a.\n1A\nNPR\n1A\nLISTEN & FOLLOW\nNPR App\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS link\nICYMI: FBI Director Kash Patel Appears Before Congress\nSeptember 18, 20253:49 PM ET\nICYMI: FBI Director Kash Patel Appears Before Congress\nListen\n·\n12:10\n12:10\nToggle more options\nDownload\nEmbed\nEmbed\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5545822/nx-s1-mx-5723393\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\nAn aide speaks to Federal Bureau of Investigation Director Kash Patel (R) as he testifies before the House Judiciary Committee on September 17, 2025 in Washington, DC.\nWin McNamee/Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nWin McNamee/Getty Images\nFBI Director Kash Patel appeared before the House Judiciary Committee on Wednesday for a second day of questioning by lawmakers.He faced questions about his leadership of the bureau including the firing of dozens of FBI agents and officials, the release of investigative files related to alleged sex trafficker Jeffrey Epstein, and the shooting of right-wing commentator Charlie Kirk.Patel began his hearings on Wednesday by defending the agency under his leadership. What were some of the biggest takeaways from the the hearing?Find more of our programs online. Listen to 1A sponsor-free by signing up for 1A+ at plus.npr.org/the1a.\nSponsor Message\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"如果你错过了：FBI局长卡什·帕特尔出席国会：1A FBI局长卡什·帕特尔周三出席众议院司法委员会，接受议员们第二天的质询。他面对有关其领导联邦调查局的问题，包括解雇数十名FBI特工和官员、公布与涉嫌性交易贩运者杰弗里·爱泼斯坦有关的调查文件，以及右翼评论员查理·柯克遭枪击事件。帕特尔周三开始听证时，为其领导下的机构进行辩护。听证会有哪些最重要的要点？在线查找我们的更多节目。注册1A+，即可在plus.npr.org/the1a收听无广告版1A节目。\n1A\nNPR\n1A\n收听并关注\nNPR应用\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS链接\n如果你错过了：FBI局长卡什·帕特尔出席国会\n2025年9月18日下午3:49（美国东部时间）\n如果你错过了：FBI局长卡什·帕特尔出席国会\n收听\n·\n12:10\n12:10\n切换更多选项\n下载\n嵌入\n嵌入\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5545822/nx-s1-mx-5723393\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR嵌入式音频播放器\">\n2025年9月17日，在华盛顿特区，联邦调查局局长卡什·帕特尔（共和党）在众议院司法委员会作证时，一名助手与他交谈。\nWin McNamee/Getty Images\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nWin McNamee/Getty Images\nFBI局长卡什·帕特尔周三出席众议院司法委员会，接受议员们第二天的质询。他面对有关其领导联邦调查局的问题，包括解雇数十名FBI特工和官员、公布与涉嫌性交易贩运者杰弗里·爱泼斯坦有关的调查文件，以及右翼评论员查理·柯克遭枪击事件。帕特尔周三开始听证时，为其领导下的机构进行辩护。听证会有哪些最重要的要点？在线查找我们的更多节目。注册1A+，即可在plus.npr.org/the1a收听无广告版1A节目。\n赞助商消息\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类提议或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读并收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n连接\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻办公室\n公众编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n你的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2025-09-18T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2341","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"2020年抗议中单膝跪地的FBI探员被解雇","title_en":"FBI agents shown kneeling during 2020 protests are fired : NPR","summary_cn":"十多名FBI探员于周五被解雇，其中包括2020年6月乔治·弗洛伊德事件抗议中单膝跪地的探员。当时FBI审查后未发现应受纪律处分的理由。FBI探员协会谴责解雇缺乏正当程序，称此举削弱了该局并损害领导层与员工之间的信任。三名前高级FBI探员已起诉该局，指控不当解雇。","summary_en":"Over a dozen FBI agents were fired Friday, including some photographed kneeling during the June 2020 George Floyd protests. A prior FBI review found no grounds for discipline. The FBI Agents Association decried the firings as violations of due process, saying they weaken the Bureau and damage trust between leadership and the workforce. Three former senior FBI agents sued the Bureau, alleging improper termination.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T16:14:40.531793+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2025/09/27/nx-s1-5555373/fbi-agents-fired-2020-george-floyd-protest-knee","content":"FBI agents shown kneeling during 2020 protests are fired Agents said the kneeling was an act of deescalation. The Bureau investigated them at the time and found no causes for discipline. The FBI Agents Association decries the lack of due process.\nNational\nFBI agents fired, including some shown kneeling during 2020 protests\nSeptember 27, 20252:00 PM ET\nBy\nHuo Jingnan\nFBI personnel take a knee with demonstrators that were marching on Pennsylvania Avenue in Washington, D.C., on June 4, 2020, during a protest over the death of George Floyd.\nJose Luis Magana/AP\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nJose Luis Magana/AP\nOver a dozen FBI agents were fired Friday, including ones photographed kneeling during a racial justice protest in June 2020 following the police killing of George Floyd, according to the FBI Agents Association and news reports. Some of the agents had been reassigned earlier this year, in what were widely seen as demotions, The Washington Post reported.\nLaw\nEx-FBI Director James Comey indicted on criminal charges stemming from 2020 testimony\nThe agents were sent by President Trump and then-Attorney General Bill Barr to deter protesters from vandalizing federal property in Washington, D.C., in June 2020, according to the Post. According to the DOJ watchdog's 2024 review of Barr's responses to the protests, FBI agents with the Washington Field Office expressed confusion over Barr's requests and said they were not trained on or equipped for responding to civil unrest. Some of the agents involved that day told CNN that they were confronted with protesters and decided to try kneeling to de-escalate, and the protesters moved on.\nSponsor Message\nLaw\nFBI Director Patel defends his leadership, Charlie Kirk probe at Senate hearing\nWhile the agents' colleagues were divided over the move at the time, the Bureau, under then-Director Christopher Wray, conducted a review and did not find grounds for discipline, CNN reports. Many on the right sharply criticized the agents' actions and the FBI's response, including then-Rep. Matt Gaetz, R-Fla. FBI Director Kash Patel opened another investigation on the case, CNN reported. The FBI Agents Association, whose members include thousands of current and former FBI agents, decried the firings as violations of due process.\nLaw\nFired FBI agents allege retribution, incompetence at top security agency\n\"Patel's dangerous new pattern of actions are weakening the Bureau because they eliminate valuable expertise and damage trust between leadership and the workforce, and make it harder to recruit and retain skilled agents — ultimately putting our nation at greater risk.\" the association said in a statement. The FBI declined to comment on personnel matters. NPR was not able to independently confirm which agents were fired. Three former senior FBI agents sued the Bureau, alleging improper termination.They alleged that the current FBI leadership was willing to violate the law to punish President Trump's perceived enemies.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFBI\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"2020年抗议活动中被拍到下跪的FBI探员遭解雇。探员称，下跪是缓和局势的举动。联邦调查局当时对他们进行了调查，未发现应受纪律处分的理由。FBI探员协会谴责缺乏正当程序。\n全国\nFBI探员遭解雇，其中包括一些在2020年抗议活动中被拍到下跪者\n2025年9月27日 下午2:00（美国东部时间）\n作者\nHuo Jingnan\n2020年6月4日，在华盛顿特区宾夕法尼亚大道举行的、针对乔治·弗洛伊德死亡的抗议活动中，FBI人员与游行示威者一同单膝跪地。\nJose Luis Magana/美联社\n隐藏说明文字\n切换说明文字\nJose Luis Magana/美联社\n据FBI探员协会和新闻报道，周五有十多名FBI探员遭解雇，其中包括一些在2020年6月、警察杀害乔治·弗洛伊德后的一场种族正义抗议活动中被拍到下跪的探员。《华盛顿邮报》报道称，其中一些探员今年早些时候已被调职，外界普遍认为这是降职。\n法律\n前FBI局长詹姆斯·科米因2020年证词引发的刑事指控遭起诉\n据《华盛顿邮报》报道，2020年6月，特朗普总统和时任司法部长比尔·巴尔派遣这些探员，以阻止抗议者破坏华盛顿特区的联邦财产。根据司法部监察机构2024年对巴尔应对抗议活动的审查，华盛顿外勤办公室的FBI探员对巴尔的要求表示困惑，并称自己既未受过应对社会骚乱的训练，也未配备相应装备。当日参与行动的一些探员告诉CNN，他们面对抗议者时决定尝试下跪以缓和局势，随后抗议者便离开了。\n赞助商信息\n法律\nFBI局长帕特尔在参议院听证会上为其领导工作及查理·柯克调查辩护\nCNN报道称，尽管探员同事当时对这一举动意见不一，但在时任局长克里斯托弗·雷领导下，联邦调查局进行了审查，未发现实施纪律处分的依据。许多右翼人士严厉批评了探员的行为及FBI的回应，包括时任佛罗里达州共和党联邦众议员马特·盖茨。CNN报道称，FBI局长卡什·帕特尔就此案启动了另一项调查。成员包括数千名现任和前任FBI探员的FBI探员协会谴责这些解雇行为违反正当程序。\n法律\n被解雇的FBI探员指控最高安全机构存在报复和无能\n该协会在一份声明中表示：“帕特尔这种危险的新行动模式正在削弱联邦调查局，因为它们排除了宝贵的专业知识，损害了领导层与员工之间的信任，并使招募和留住有技能的探员更加困难——最终使我们的国家面临更大的风险。” FBI拒绝就人事事务置评。NPR无法独立确认哪些探员遭到解雇。三名前FBI高级探员起诉该局，指控遭到不当解雇。他们称，现任FBI领导层愿意违法惩罚特朗普总统认定的敌人。\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类要约或要求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\nFBI\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n连接\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n媒体\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多元化\nNPR网络\n无障碍服务\n伦理\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商信息\n赞助商信息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2025-09-27T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2288","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"前FBI局长科米对联邦指控作无罪抗辩","title_en":"Former FBI Director Comey pleads not guilty to federal charges : NPR","summary_cn":"前FBI局长詹姆斯·科米周三在弗吉尼亚州亚历山德里亚联邦法院对虚假陈述和妨碍国会程序的指控作无罪抗辩，由其律师帕特·菲茨杰拉德代为提出。法官将审判日期定在2026年1月5日，科米无条件获释。科米上月因2020年9月向参议院司法委员会作证而被起诉两项罪名。其律师计划提出驳回动议，指控起诉具有报复性或选择性。","summary_en":"Former FBI Director James Comey pleaded not guilty Wednesday in federal court in Alexandria, Va., to charges of false statements and obstructing a congressional proceeding, entered by his lawyer Pat Fitzgerald. The judge set a trial date of Jan. 5, 2026, and Comey was released with no conditions. Indicted last month on two counts stemming from his September 2020 Senate Judiciary Committee testimony, Comey's lawyers plan motions to dismiss, alleging vindictive or selective prosecution.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T16:03:17.146396+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2025/10/08/g-s1-92516/comey-arraignment-justice-department","content":"Former FBI Director Comey pleads not guilty to federal charges The former FBI director, who has long been one of President Trump's most vocal critics, was indicted last month on two counts stemming from his testimony before the Senate Judiciary Committee in September 2020.\nLaw\nFormer FBI Director Comey pleads not guilty to federal charges of lying to Congress\nOctober 8, 202510:52 AM ET\nBy\nCarrie Johnson\n,\nRyan Lucas\nJames Comey, former FBI director, speaks at a book event in New York City in May 2025. He was arraigned on federal charges on Wednesday.\nMichael M. Santiago/Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nMichael M. Santiago/Getty Images\nFormer FBI Director James Comey has pleaded not guilty to charges of false statements and obstructing a congressional proceeding. Comey entered his plea via his lawyer Pat Fitzgerald during his arraignment in federal court on Wednesday in Alexandria, Va. The judge on the case set a trial date of Jan. 5, 2026, and Comey is set to be released with no conditions. \"It's the honor of my life to represent Jim Comey in this matter,\" Fitzgerald said at the courthouse, where Comey's family also attended the arraignment. The former FBI director, who has long been one of President Trump's most vocal critics, was indicted last month on two counts stemming from his testimony before the Senate Judiciary Committee in September 2020. The Justice Department secured the indictment just days after Trump demanded a faster pace from prosecutors in their investigation against Comey.\nSponsor Message\nLaw\nEx-FBI Director James Comey indicted on criminal charges stemming from 2020 testimony\nThe top federal prosecutor in Northern Virginia, Erik Siebert, had cast doubt on the strength of the evidence against Comey. The Trump administration then replaced Siebert with Lindsey Halligan, a White House aide who previously served as a personal attorney for Trump. Halligan, who secured the grand jury indictment against Comey, is bringing in prosecutors from North Carolina to assist on the case. Comey's lawyers said they plan to file two batches of motions to dismiss the case, centering on accusations of a vindictive or selective prosecution, and that Halligan was unlawfully appointed. They may also include allegations of \"outrageous conduct\" and problems with the grand jury process that indicted Comey last month. Trump has long railed against Comey, blaming the former FBI leader for the appointment of a special prosecutor who probed contacts between Russia and Trump's 2016 campaign through most of the president's first term in office.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"前联邦调查局局长科米对联邦指控拒不认罪\n这名前联邦调查局局长长期以来一直是特朗普总统最直言不讳的批评者之一，上月因其2020年9月在参议院司法委员会作证一事被控两项罪名。\n法律\n前联邦调查局局长科米对向国会撒谎的联邦指控拒不认罪\n2025年10月8日上午10:52（美国东部时间）\n作者\nCarrie Johnson、Ryan Lucas\n前联邦调查局局长James Comey于2025年5月在纽约市的一场图书活动上发言。他于周三因联邦指控出庭提审。\nMichael M. Santiago/Getty Images\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nMichael M. Santiago/Getty Images\n前联邦调查局局长James Comey已对作出虚假陈述和妨碍国会程序的指控表示不认罪。周三，Comey在弗吉尼亚州亚历山德里亚的联邦法院接受提审时，由其律师Pat Fitzgerald代为提出抗辩。该案法官将审判日期定为2026年1月5日，并决定无条件释放Comey。“能在此事中代表Jim Comey是我一生的荣誉，”Fitzgerald在法院表示；Comey的家人也出席了提审。这名前联邦调查局局长长期以来一直是特朗普总统最直言不讳的批评者之一，上月因其2020年9月在参议院司法委员会作证一事被控两项罪名。司法部在特朗普要求检察官加快对Comey调查进度后仅数日，便取得了这份起诉书。\n赞助商消息\n法律\n前联邦调查局局长James Comey因2020年作证引发的刑事指控被起诉\n弗吉尼亚州北部的首席联邦检察官Erik Siebert曾对针对Comey的证据力度表示怀疑。随后，特朗普政府用Lindsey Halligan取代了Siebert；Halligan是白宫助理，此前曾担任特朗普的私人律师。Halligan获得了针对Comey的大陪审团起诉书，并正在从北卡罗来纳州调入检察官协助办理此案。Comey的律师表示，他们计划分两批提出动议，要求撤销案件，理由集中在报复性或选择性起诉的指控，以及Halligan的任命不合法。他们还可能提出“令人发指的行为”以及上月起诉Comey的大陪审团程序存在问题等指控。特朗普长期以来一直猛烈抨击Comey，指责这名前联邦调查局负责人任命了特别检察官；该特别检察官在特朗普第一任总统任期的大部分时间里，调查俄罗斯与特朗普2016年竞选团队之间的接触。\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类报价或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系我们\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻发布\n公众编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多元化\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2025-10-08T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2090","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"卡什·帕特尔如何搅动司法部并改变FBI的使命","title_en":"How Kash Patel is roiling the DOJ and changing the mission of the FBI : NPR","summary_cn":"《纽约客》撰稿人马克·费舍尔在NPR《新鲜空气》节目中讨论其对FBI局长卡什·帕特尔的专访报道。费舍尔称帕特尔缺乏高级执法经验，因对特朗普的忠诚及寻求报复而获任，正将FBI使命转向打击无证移民，并因女友安保、使用政府专机等争议受到内部质疑。节目还回顾了帕特尔在国会就爱泼斯坦文件问题与议员斯瓦尔韦尔的激烈交锋。","summary_en":"New Yorker writer Marc Fisher discusses his profile of FBI Director Kash Patel on NPR's Fresh Air. Fisher says Patel became FBI director without senior law enforcement experience because of his loyalty to Trump and willingness to seek retribution, and is transforming the FBI's mission toward cracking down on undocumented immigrants. The interview also covers controversies over security for Patel's girlfriend, use of government jets, and his combative congressional testimony on the Epstein files.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:30:11.104699+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2025/11/19/nx-s1-5613347/how-kash-patel-is-roiling-the-departme-and-changing-the-mission-of-the-fbi","content":"How Kash Patel is roiling the DOJ and changing the mission of the FBI New Yorker writer Marc Fisher says Patel became FBI director without senior law enforcement experience because of his loyalty to Trump and willingness to seek retribution for his perceived enemies.\nNPR\nPolitics\nLISTEN & FOLLOW\nNPR App\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS link\nPolitics\nHow Kash Patel is roiling the FBI and changing its mission\nNovember 19, 202512:49 PM ET\nHeard on Fresh Air\nTerry Gross\nFA: Marc Fisher\nListen\n·\n44:13\n44:13\nTranscript\nToggle more options\nDownload\nEmbed\nEmbed\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5613347/nx-s1-9539965\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\nTranscript\nNew Yorker writer Marc Fisher says Patel became FBI director without senior law enforcement experience because of his loyalty to Trump and willingness to seek retribution for his perceived enemies.\nSponsor Message\nTranscript\nTERRY GROSS, HOST: This is FRESH AIR. I'm Terry Gross. Today, we're going to talk about FBI director Kash Patel with Marc Fisher, who profiles Patel on the current issue of The New Yorker. It's titled \"Kash Patel's Acts Of Service.\" It's subtitled, \"The F.B.I. Director Isn't Just Enforcing The President's Agenda At The Bureau - He's Seeking Retribution For Its Past Investigations Of Donald Trump.\" Fisher also writes about other controversies surrounding Patel, ranging from whether he's even qualified for the job to his conflicts of interest and how he's transforming the FBI's mission as he focuses on cracking down on undocumented immigrants. Journalist Marc Fisher was a longtime writer and editor at The Washington Post and co-author of the book \"Trump Revealed\" before becoming a New Yorker contributor. We recorded our interview yesterday morning before both the House and the Senate approved the release of the Epstein files. Start with an excerpt of what Patel had to say about the Epstein files when he testified before the House Judiciary Committee last September. Here he is being questioned by California Democrat Eric Swalwell.(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)ERIC SWALWELL: You said you don't know the number of times Trump's name appears in the files, so it could at least be 1,000 times, is that right?KASH PATEL: The number is a total misleading factor.SWALWELL: But the number would at least be a thousand times.PATEL: We have not released anyone's...SWALWELL: But it would at least be a thousand times.PATEL: ...File that has not been credible. And we have...SWALWELL: Director, could it at least be a thousand times?PATEL: ...Released every piece of legally permissible information.SWALWELL: OK, so...PATEL: You can characterize the numbers however you want it.SWALWELL: Reclaiming my time, Director. It sounds like if you don't know the number, it could at least be a thousand times, which leads me...PATEL: It's not. It's not.SWALWELL: Is it at least 500 times?PATEL: No.SWALWELL: Is it at least a hundred times?PATEL: No.SWALWELL: Then what's the number?PATEL: I don't know the number, but it's not that.SWALWELL: Do you think it might be your job to know the number?PATEL: My job is to provide for the safety and security of this country. My job is not to engage in political innuendo...SWALWELL: And that includes protecting section...PATEL: ...So you can go out to the sticks and get your 20-second hit in your fundraising article.SWALWELL: So reclaiming my time, Director...PATEL: Keep going reclaiming your time because...SWALWELL: Director...PATEL: ...The people of California are being...SWALWELL: If Trump...PATEL: ...Underserved by your representations.SWALWELL: The president is not implicated. Why not release everything that involves Trump?PATEL: We have released everything the president and anyone else's side that is credible and lawfully be able to be released. Your fixation on this matter and baseless accusations that I'm hiding child pedophiles...SWALWELL: There's also Mr. Massie's...PATEL: ...Is disgusting.SWALWELL: ...Interests.PATEL: Anyone that says that needs to look at the stats alone and go back to the state of California, who's receiving the biggest surge in FBI resources...SWALWELL: So, Director...PATEL: ...Through my redeployment because the cities of Los Angeles, San Diego and San Francisco need it.SWALWELL: Reclaiming my time, Director. Remembering your oath to tell the truth, did you ever tell Donald Trump his name is in the files?PATEL: I have never spoken to President Trump about the Epstein files.SWALWELL: Did you ever tell the attorney general that Donald Trump's name is in the Epstein files?PATEL: The attorney general and I have had numerous discussions about the entirety of the Epstein files and the reviews conducted by our teams.SWALWELL: Did you tell the attorney general that Donald Trump's name is in the Epstein files?PATEL: And we have released where President Trump's name is in the attorney's file.SWALWELL: It's a simple question. Did you tell the attorney general that the president's name is in the Epstein files?PATEL: During many conversations that the attorney general and I have had on the matter of Epstein, we have reviewed painstakingly...SWALWELL: The question is simple.PATEL: ...Who can...SWALWELL: Did you tellPATEL: ...The...SWALWELL: ...the attorney general that Donald Trump's name is in the Epstein files? Yes or no?PATEL: Why don't you try spelling it out, if you're going to mock me.SWALWELL: Yes or no, Director?PATEL: Use the alphabet.SWALWELL: Yes or no?PATEL: No. A, B, C, D, E, F. Don't want to do it.SWALWELL: Director, it sounds like you don't want to tell us. Did you tell the attorney general that Donald Trump's name was in the Epstein files?PATEL: Why don't you try serving your...SWALWELL: Director, did you tell...PATEL: ...Constituency by focusing on reducing...SWALWELL: ...The attorney general Donald Trump...PATEL: ...Violent crime in this country and the number of pedophilesSWALWELL: Director...PATEL: ...That are illegally...SWALWELL: Reclaiming my time, Director.PATEL: ...Harbored in your sanctuary cities in California.SWALWELL: Director, reclaiming my time...PATEL: I'll work with you on that.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON: Regular order, Mr. Chairman.PATEL: Do you want to work with us on that?JIM JORDAN: Time belongs to the gentleman from California.SWALWELL: Did you tell the attorney general that Donald Trump's name is in the Epstein files?PATEL: The question has been asked and answered.SWALWELL: You have not answered it, and we will take your evasiveness as a consciousness of guilt.GROSS: Mark Fisher, welcome to FRESH AIR. Your profile of Kash Patel was very informative and very interesting. What was your reaction when you first heard Swalwell questioning Kash Patel?MARC FISHER: Well, this came amid a whole bunch of such confrontations between Patel and both House and Senate members, and it was pretty wild. It was one of the classic ways in which Kash Patel, very much like Donald Trump, values the show above almost all else. And this is one of the reasons Patel is one of Trump's favorites. One of the reasons Patel is in this job is his ability to commandeer the moment, to put on a big show, to present himself as aggrieved and to present himself as an advocate for all those Americans who feel aggrieved.GROSS: Have you ever heard an FBI director dodge questions in such a confrontational way?FISHER: No, we've never seen any FBI director like Kash Patel before. We've never seen someone who looks like Kash Patel. The previous FBI directors were all sort of clean-cut, conservatively dressed, very proper...GROSS: White men.FISHER: White men, all of them. And Kash Patel is not any of those things. So we haven't seen this kind of approach before. We haven't seen this kind of antagonism before. And we haven't seen this lack of experience before, lack of familiarity with what the FBI does. There is in Kash Patel's background every reason to see why Donald Trump wanted him in that job and every reason to understand why so many people within the Bureau believe he is ill-suited for the position and that he's doing significant and enduring damage to the bureau.GROSS: So Benny Johnson, who's a far-right commentator and podcaster, was interviewing Kash Patel, and he asked him why the FBI was protecting the world's foremost predators by refusing to release the names on the list. And Patel said, simple, because of who's on that list. That sounds contradictory to what we just heard.FISHER: Right. So that conversation with Benny Johnson took place a couple of years ago at a point when Kash Patel was on the outside and was agitating for the administration, then the Biden administration, to release this purported client list that Jeffrey Epstein is supposed to have kept. Now-FBI Director Patel has a very different set of facts that he would like people to believe, and that is that there is no client list, never was. And Patel, who had certainly entertained the idea that there was some conspiracy behind Epstein's death before Patel came to the FBI, now that he is the director, now that he's had the opportunity to read the files, he has concluded that this was absolutely a suicide, that there's no evidence to the contrary. There was no hanky-panky. Donald Trump's presence in the Epstein files is minimal and benign.And so this almost complete reversal on Patel's part is bizarrely accompanied now by Kash Patel saying, how can you not believe me? He gets indignant when people question him about this, including members of Congress. And so Patel says, I am the guy who was pushing for all this stuff to be released when I was out of office. Now that I'm in office and I tell you there's nothing to be released, you need to believe me. And he seems, at least he acts, as if he's astonished that people don't take his word for it.GROSS: So let's briefly run through some of the things that Kash Patel has been in the news for in the past couple of weeks. So this week, he was in the news for giving his girlfriend, country music singer Alexis Wilkins, a security detail. Tell us briefly why that's unusual.FISHER: Well, none of the previous FBI directors had executive protection for their spouses, not for their wives and not for any girlfriends. And so it's very unusual for a director to request that kind of protection for someone who's not the director and who's not even the director's spouse. So why is this in the news? Because there are a lot of people within the FBI who find these kinds of requests, these kinds of irregularities, not just unseemly, but a waste of resources. So whether it's providing protection for his girlfriend or providing the director with jet service on the Bureau's jet to take him to various forms of entertainment - hockey games, casino jaunts to Las Vegas, visits to that girlfriend in Nashville, all of this - it comes at great expense to the taxpayer and is seen by a lot of people in the Bureau as a waste of resources.GROSS: Patel, in response, says he's been paying back the government for these trips.FISHER: Yes. He pays the government back, or so he says, at the commercial rate - the rate that you and I would pay to fly to Nashville, which is dramatically less than it costs the Bureau to stand up their GV jet and whisk the director wherever he wants to go for that weekend's entertainment.GROSS: Last week, Patel exempted his deputy FBI director, Dan Bongino, and two other new senior FBI officials who were just recently hired from passing polygraph tests. And keeping in mind that Dan Bongino has access to the top government secrets as deputy FBI director, how unusual is it for the deputy FBI director to be exempted from a polygraph test, which I think everybody in the FBI has to pass?FISHER: Yeah. The FBI has used polygraph tests for decades as just kind of a routine check on basic security questions that everybody has to pass to have access to the kind of secure information that the FBI traffics in. So obviously, since that's the case for special agents across the country, it's equally the case for the people running the FBI, such as the deputy director, Dan Bongino.So why was Bongino given this exemption? That's gone unstated by the FBI. The assumption among many current and former agents is that he flunked a polygraph or had reason to flunk a polygraph and, therefore, was allowed to skip it. This is something that does not sit well with others in the FBI because they want to be able to trust the people around them, particularly the people above them, but their colleagues as well. And one thing that gives them that sense of trust is they know everybody's passed the same security hurdles. And when the No. 2 in the agency gets a bye on that, that causes a lot of unhappiness in the ranks.GROSS: And while we're on the subject of Dan Bongino, Patel had said that he'd appoint someone with FBI experience to be his deputy director. But Dan Bongino had no experience and no experience at the FBI at all, and of course, neither did Patel when Trump appointed him. Bongino had been a Secret Service agent, podcaster and Fox News commentator.FISHER: Yes. That's exactly right. Bongino was somebody who was known to the president as somebody who did very well on Fox News, who appeared frequently on Fox News. And in the president's mind, the people running the FBI ought to be folks who are well known to the public and who make the president's case very avidly, very effectively. And Bongino is seen as a tough guy. Trump loves tough guys. He loves guys who seem like stand-up, aggressive advocates for the MAGA movement, and Bongino very much fit that bill.So looks the part. That's the No. 1 qualification, frequently, in Trump's mind. And Bongino fit that part in a way that perhaps Kash Patel didn't. Patel was, in the president's mind, the ultimate loyalist, someone he could absolutely trust. So this was a team that you could see the president really envisioning as complementary. And so the fact that neither of these guys had any experience in the FBI didn't seem to matter much.GROSS: Well, let's take a short break here. If you're just joining us, my guest is Marc Fisher. He recently left The Washington Post, where he was a longtime writer and editor. He's also the author of the book \"Trump Revealed.\" And now he's a contributor to The New Yorker and has profiled Kash Patel in his new article, \"Kash Patel's Acts Of Service.\" We'll be right back. This is FRESH AIR.(SOUNDBITE OF LOUIS SCLAVIS' \"FETE FORAINE\")GROSS: This is FRESH AIR. Let's get back to my interview with Marc Fisher, who profiles FBI Director Kash Patel in a new article in The New Yorker titled \"Kash Patel's Acts Of Service.\" And the subtitle is, the FBI director isn't just enforcing the president's agenda at the Bureau - he's seeking retribution for its past investigations of Donald Trump.So there are questions about how Patel got the position as head of the FBI and whether he had the necessary experience and credentials. What position had he held compared to what FBI directors typically have?FISHER: FBI directors in the past have generally had long careers in criminal justice. There have been judges. There have been prosecutors, people who served as U.S. attorneys, running some of the largest U.S. attorneys' offices in the country, whether that be in New York or elsewhere. They are people who have come up through the system and have long histories of public service.And so Kash Patel has some of that. Kash Patel indeed has been a public servant his entire life. He's never worked for anyone but the government, except for some small businesses that he started on his own between the two Trump administrations. Patel, however, never had that kind of long-standing experience with the FBI directly, with - he did serve in the Justice Department in the first Trump administration. He was on the national security side of the Justice Department, and he worked on some counterterrorism cases. That's something that the FBI also does. But he didn't have the experience that his predecessors had in running an agency or in the sort of street crime that the G-men of the FBI have always been known for in the public imagination.So there were a lot of people within the Bureau who were very concerned that he would be coming in without the knowledge base, without the years of experience that would tell him how the FBI works, how its relationship with the Justice Department operates. So there was concern about his relative inexperience. Beyond that, I think there was a lot of concern that he was coming in as essentially a tool of Donald Trump - essentially someone who was going to be sort of forwarding the president's political agenda rather than sort of fighting for the men and women of the FBI.GROSS: The subtitle of your piece about Patel in The New Yorker is, \"The F.B.I. Director Isn't Just Enforcing The President's Agenda At The Bureau - He's Seeking Retribution For Its Past Investigations Of Donald Trump.\" So in his memoir, in Patel's memoir, he had his list of, quote, government gangsters. Eric Swalwell, who we heard questioning Patel, was on that list. Who has he been seeking retribution against, and what for?FISHER: Almost from the first day that Kash Patel took over the FBI, he has had as one of his top or perhaps his very top agenda item the calling-out and the investigation of people who the president sees as his enemies - people who the president sees as having investigated Trump himself, the folks who were involved in prosecuting Trump for his taking classified documents down to Mar-a-Lago in Florida when he left after his first administration, the people who prosecuted him on all of the various other things that he was convicted of, financial misdeeds and so on.So there's a long list of Trump enemies who Trump has talked about during his out years, the four years of the Biden administration. And Kash Patel came into office with that list and with his own list - what he calls his list of government gangsters. He wrote a whole book laying out who those people were, and it was accompanied by a list of at least 60 people who he saw as Trump's enemies and therefore as Patel's enemies. And so he has methodically gone through that list, already begun the process of coming up with evidence and seeking indictments of people like the former FBI director, James Comey, like other top officials of the FBI who were involved in the Russiagate investigation. All of these investigations that characterized Trump's first term - these are now the people who are - have targets on their backs.And as Patel has now told several people at the FBI, he sees it as his job to go after the people who the president wants prosecuted. The president has not forgotten. That's the line that Patel has used over and over again to explain why he's going after some of these people. It's ironic, of course, because Patel has staked his entire directorship on the idea that everyone who came before him had weaponized the FBI, weaponized the Justice Department for their own political purposes. And yet now he stands accused of exactly the same infraction - of going after people, using the power of the FBI to go after political opponents.GROSS: So not only did Patel have former FBI Director Jim Comey indicted. He fired an agent for refusing to stage a perp walk for Comey.FISHER: Yes. That's right. So Patel has fired, by various accounts, dozens of agents for various infractions, most of which he doesn't detail in any sort of way. But the reporting that I did, as well as other news organizations have done, has brought out the fact that Patel has fired agents for things such as having a gay pride flag at their desk or, as you mentioned, refusing to stage the classic perp walk in which the purported criminal is paraded before the cameras, in this case being the former FBI director himself, Jim Comey.So there is - in the minds of many FBI agents, there's this view of Kash Patel as someone who is vindictive on behalf of Trump and vindictive in ways that seem at least on the surface to be superficially just cruelty for cruelty's sake, compounded by the fact that Patel has told people within the Bureau that he would not fire many of these agents if it were solely up to him - that he's getting direction from the White House, direction from the Justice Department and that he is firing a number of these agents on the president's say-so or to please the president, rather than because Patel himself believes that the agent has done anything wrong.GROSS: Well, let's take another short break here. If you're just joining us, my guest is Marc Fisher. And he profiles FBI Director Kash Patel in a new article in The New Yorker that's titled \"Kash Patel's Acts Of Service,\" subtitled, the FBI director isn't just enforcing the president's agenda at the Bureau - he's seeking retribution for its past investigations of Donald Trump. We'll be right back after a short break. I'm Terry Gross, and this is FRESH AIR.(SOUNDBITE OF JESSICA WILLIAMS' \"KID STUFF\")GROSS: This is FRESH AIR. I'm Terry Gross. Let's get back to my interview with Marc Fisher, who has a new profile of FBI Director Kash Patel in The New Yorker, where Fisher is a contributor. He recently left The Washington Post, where he was a longtime writer and editor. He's also the author of the book \"Trump Revealed,\" a biography of the president.You write in your profile of Kash Patel that he has promoted several conspiracy theories. I want to play a clip, and this is something that you quote in your profile. This is an excerpt of him as a guest on Joe Rogan's podcast on June 6 of this year. And this is Kash Patel describing a conspiracy theory relating to China and fentanyl.(SOUNDBITE OF PODCAST, \"THE JOE ROGAN EXPERIENCE\")PATEL: You don't hear of fentanyl deaths in China. You don't hear of fentanyl deaths in India. You don't really hear of fentanyl deaths in England, Australia, New Zealand - our Five Eyes partners - and Canada. The Chinese, in my opinion, the CCP, have used it as a directed approach because we are their adversary. And they don't like us, and we don't like what they're doing when it comes to fentanyl. And their long-term game is this. How do I - in my opinion - kneecap the United States of America, our largest adversary? Well, why don't we go and take out generations of young men and women who might grow up to serve in the United States military or become a cop or become a teacher? And that's what they're doing when you wipe out tens of thousands of Americans a year. It's a long-term plan for them.JOE ROGAN: Whoa. That is such a dark, dark thing.PATEL: It is, but we got - we're on it.GROSS: So that was Kash Patel on Joe Rogan's podcast on June 6 of this year. I hadn't heard that one before. Had you?FISHER: No. That was a new one on me. But he's got a bunch of them, and he's got a whole book of them in his book \"Government Gangsters.\" There came a moment in that very same interview between Rogan and Patel where Rogan was almost teasing Patel about the fact that he's prone to these conspiracy theories and that he sees the government and the press and politicians as part of this cabal that is out to undermine Trump and undermine the MAGA movement. And Rogan kind of poked him and said, we love conspiracies, don't we? They're exciting. And Patel kind of laughed and said, yeah. They're our thing.GROSS: Do you see any connection between this China conspiracy theory and Trump's threats to attack Venezuela and sending warships close to there? Do you see a connection?FISHER: There's a certain constancy to the way in which Trump and Patel think, the way in which they tell stories about their administration's actions. They both are given to dramatic tellings of somewhat fanciful notions about what our enemies are up to and what is motivating them, and they both jump off from some actual facts. Fentanyl is obviously a scourge. It's obviously an enormous problem. It comes in from Mexico, but its component parts are frequently manufactured in China, so there's a legitimate issue there. It's a legitimate crime-fighting imperative for the FBI. That's the fact part.And then we get into the fanciful. As we hear Patel spin out his notion of why this might be happening, it's very reminiscent of the way Donald Trump frequently portrays issues not just in Venezuela, but around the world, where he sees or is told about some situation, presents the germ of the facts and then extrapolates from there, exaggerates and throws in numbers that can't quite be verified and so on. It all boils down to the show. Both Trump and Patel, very similar personalities in certain important ways, one of which is that they think of every day in their job as the next act in a long-running show that needs new juice to zap them into the headlines for that day.GROSS: What are some of the other conspiracy theories that Patel has promoted?FISHER: So Kash Patel, as he's admitted, is given to conspiratorial notions. He sees conspiracies in fact patterns. And obviously, you want any crime fighter to look for patterns in the facts that they are assembling, but in Kash Patel's case, it often goes a bit beyond the available facts. So, for example, Patel has believed for many years now that he is a target of a media conspiracy to portray him in a dark light and to make up things about him. And so he has sued a series of news organizations, claiming that they have defamed him. He sued The New York Times. He sued CNN. He sued Politico. In every case, he got a big splash in the news, and in every case, he ended up withdrawing the suit or seeing it thrown out by a judge.In the case of the January 6 assault on the Capitol, Kash Patel has for a couple of years been presenting the theory that the FBI itself had recruited operatives of one sort or another who were in the crowd on January 6, and who were perhaps encouraging the rioters to be more aggressive in their attack on the Capitol. He's never presented the slightest evidence for this, and yet he continues to present this idea. Even as director of the FBI, he has said that there may have been such people and they may have very much egged on the crowd on January 6, sort of portraying the January 6 attack almost as an inside job.GROSS: So since Trump was president on January 6 - I mean, he was about to leave the White House, but he was president then. If it was an inside job in the sense that the FBI sent people into militias to urge them to be more aggressive and even violent, why isn't that a Trump administration problem? The FBI director at this time was somebody appointed by President Trump.FISHER: Yes. This - it's absolutely right. The January 6 attack took place at the very tail end of the first Trump administration. So if, as Patel theorizes, the FBI in 2020 and the first days of 2021 was using its own confidential sources and placing those sources within the militia groups that were plotting the January 6 attack, it's perfectly reasonable to ask, does that mean that the first Trump administration was in some regard responsible for the attack? This is an aspect of this conspiracy theory that Kash Patel somehow never gets around to talking about. He sees the FBI as kind of a rogue operation. And it - so it fits in with his overall theory of this deep state that is the real governing factor in the United States, and this deep state doing its own thing, seeding these sources into the January 6 attack groups without perhaps the knowledge of Trump and the Trump administration.And, you know, the FBI did have some contacts who were, from time to time, inside some of the militia groups. So were they there for the purpose of encouraging those groups to take this more violent approach on January 6? No evidence has been presented along those lines, and yet it's been years now that he's been pushing this theory and saying that once he got inside, he would reveal the evidence. Well, nothing's been revealed.GROSS: After Trump was indicted in 2023 for secretly storing classified documents at Mar-a-Lago, Patel said, when you're president and you leave, you can take whatever you want. And when you take it, whether it's classified or not, it's now not.FISHER: I believe that the president said it first, but Kash Patel quickly adopted this idea that everything that was at Mar-a-Lago was there legally simply because the president had taken it there. This is completely contrary to the law. In 1978, Congress passed the Presidential Records Act. And that prohibits former presidents from taking any official records - whether they were classified or not - from taking them with them when they left office.Well, Trump brought a lot of those things down to Mar-a-Lago. And Patel has said several times, when he worked in the White House in the first Trump administration, that he watched the president declassify documents so that he could take them with him to Florida. So since Patel had said this in various public settings, he was then summoned to testify by prosecutors, to testify before a grand jury about, OK, what did you see? Which documents were they, and what did the president do when he was declassifying them? Well, he gets to the grand jury. And all of a sudden, Patel is taking the Fifth Amendment, saying that he's not going to answer any questions because he has this right against self-incrimination. So the prosecutor said, well, OK. We'll get you immunity. So they went and got him immunity from the court, and Patel appeared a second time. And this time, he answered their questions.So flash-forward, and he's at his Senate confirmation hearing for becoming FBI director. And the senators say, OK. Tell us about this grand jury testimony where you talked about why and how the president made these documents OK to take to Mar-a-Lago. And again, Kash Patel said, no, can't do that, not allowed, because it was against the law. And as several senators noted, that is just false. You own your own words and you are allowed to divulge that testimony, which he did not do.GROSS: I think we need to take another break here, so let me reintroduce you. If you're just joining us, my guest is Marc Fisher, who profiles Kash Patel in the current issue of The New Yorker. We'll be right back. This is FRESH AIR.(SOUNDBITE OF JULIAN LAGE GROUP & JULIAN LAGE'S \"IOWA TAKEN\")GROSS: This is FRESH AIR. Let's get back to the interview I recorded yesterday morning with Marc Fisher, who profiles FBI Director Kash Patel in the current issue of The New Yorker, where Fisher is a contributor. The article is titled \"Kash Patel's Acts Of Service.\"Patel has fired a whole lot of FBI agents. I don't know the number. And he reassigned a lot of the remaining agents to work on catching people who are in the U.S. illegally. So what are some of the FBI missions that have been canceled or understaffed as a result of the firings and the reassignments, or units that have been completely shut down?FISHER: It's really a thoroughgoing change that he's brought to the FBI. The FBI has about 13,000 agents, and about a quarter of them have been assigned to work on going out and catching undocumented immigrants. This has not generally been a major focus for the FBI. Hundreds more of agents have been sent on these missions, along with the National Guard, to do basic sort of crime patrols in cities like Washington, D.C., and Chicago. This is part of this emphasis on combating violent crime that President Trump began some months back.So what's not being done? Well, for example, Patel disbanded the public corruption unit in the Washington Field Office. This is the group that investigates wrongdoing by public officials, including - this is the office that investigated Trump and various other Republicans for trying to overturn the results of the 2020 presidential election. I was given one example after another by various agents in the FBI of cases, for example, of someone working on Chinese counterterrorism efforts in the United States. One such agent was pulled off a case to essentially do street patrol in the District of Columbia, doing DWI arrests. So it's a wholesale rejiggering of what agents are doing. It's resulting in a lot of people retiring early, retiring as soon as they're able to, and quite a number, as you said, who've been fired.GROSS: Also, Patel has lowered the standards for getting hired into the FBI. New agents no longer need a college degree, and now new agents have to take a polygraph test that includes loyalty to the FBI director.FISHER: Yeah. This is something that Patel has denied, but we've heard this from several agents who said they've been asked about their loyalty to Director Patel and to his own personal agenda. This is one of a number of ways in which a lot of FBI agents feel like the standards for being an agent have been lowered. For example, Patel has shortened the length of the FBI academy from 18 weeks to eight weeks of training, and he's no longer requiring all new agents to have a college degree. Many agents in the FBI have advanced degrees. And many of them have done specialized work in languages and so on to prepare themselves for the kind of international investigations that the bureau does so well. So it's really disappointing to a lot of the people who've devoted their lives to the bureau to see what they see as a watering down of those standards.GROSS: Patel has managed to cash in financially on being director of the FBI. Can you describe some of the ways he's cashed in?FISHER: Well, during his time between administrations - so between his time at the National Security Council and his time as head of the FBI - he spent those out years on a number of efforts to make a lot of money, and he did make a lot of money. Some of it was selling stuff very much in a Trump kind of model. He set up a website to sell his K$h-branded clothes that he had made, K and then dollar sign and then H. It was kind of a logo. He raised money for some of the January 6 defendants. He promoted dietary supplements that he claimed would undo the damage from the COVID-19 virus. He wrote three children's books called \"Plot Against The King.\" The main character of those children's books was a guy named Kash the wizard, who was the hero, and he led a battle on behalf of King Donald to find the bad guys who were scheming to elect Hillary Queenton on not Election Day, but Choosing Day. And so he was highly involved in all of these efforts to make some money and push Trump's agenda. The No. 1 thing that made Patel richer during those years was the work that he did through a consulting group that he set up where his clients included the government of the country of Qatar. That's a relationship that continues to this day, even as he's the FBI director.GROSS: Patel also had a charity called the Kash Foundation, which promised to make grants to those who have the courage to stand up to government wrongdoing. What did you learn about how the money raised was used?FISHER: Well, the Kash Foundation raised $1.3 million one year, and they spent something like $700,000 in expenses, but they only gave away $200,000. So out of the $1.3 million in revenue, only $200,000 went out in grants, mostly in cash assistance to about 50 people who are not named. So the idea here was to help people who were supposedly standing up against the deep state, against the government, but only $200,000 went to those folks. More than that went to one of Patel's own friends, a business partner of his who runs an advertising firm that was hired to do some promotion of the charity's operations. So all told, we're talking about millions of dollars that Patel gained during those out years between administrations from his various business ventures and his representation of a Chinese company and the government of Qatar, all of these entities that did a lot of business with the United States government.GROSS: And I should mention the Chinese company that he worked with is the company that owns, among other things, SHEIN, that fast fashion retailer. It's really popular in the U.S. because the prices are so cheap.FISHER: Sure. And SHEIN's subsidiary, which Patel worked for, had a board that included investors who were accused of having ties to the Chinese military and the Chinese Communist Party. So there have been some real questions about the ethics of that organization. And yet, Patel, when he took over as FBI director, said that he saw no need to divest his holdings in the Chinese companies.GROSS: So has he promised to recuse himself from any legal issues pertaining to companies that he has worked with and gotten paid by?FISHER: To the contrary, in the case of his work for Qatar, Patel, after he took over the FBI, got a waiver from the U.S. government that allows him to continue to handle matters related to the government of Qatar. You know, the FBI deals with all sorts of terrorism issues around the world. And so if anything comes up regarding Qatar, he will be allowed to continue to do that work even though he took in some undisclosed amount for consulting services to Qatar in the years before he became FBI director. We don't know the amount of money, exactly, that he got from Qatar because he never registered as a foreign agent, which the advocacy group Public Citizens says was a violation of federal rules.GROSS: If you're just joining us, my guest is Marc Fisher, who profiles Kash Patel in the current issue of The New Yorker. We'll be right back. This is FRESH AIR.(SOUNDBITE OF MUSIC)GROSS: This is FRESH AIR. Let's get back to the interview I recorded yesterday morning with Marc Fisher, who profiles FBI director Kash Patel in the current issue of The New Yorker, where Fisher is a contributor. The article is titled \"Kash Patel's Acts Of Service.This is kind of like a strange fact. I'm not quite sure how to interpret it. One of the things that Patel proposed was to bring mixed martial arts fighters to help FBI agents with their conditioning. And he proposed this to the head of the UFC, the Ultimate Fighting Championship. You know, related to that is that Trump wants to celebrate the 250th anniversary of the United States by having a mixed martial arts fight on the White House lawn. So what does that say about Patel and the MMA? Is he a fan, or is this just out of loyalty to Trump, who's planning to celebrate the 250th anniversary of the founding of the United States by having an MMA fight?FISHER: I'm willing to give Kash Patel the benefit of the doubt and believe that he's a genuine fan of UFC. He seems to really enjoy it when he goes to the bouts. He went with Mel Gibson to that Vegas fight. And so Kash Patel's true sports love is ice hockey. He plays ice hockey. He is a huge fan. He's used a government jet to fly to an Islanders game up in New York. So when it comes to the UFC, I think we have a happy coincidence where Trump's love for that sport and Patel's dovetail nicely. They both have a strong desire to appeal to a core part of the MAGA constituency, the hyper-masculine world of MMA. And so there is a political aspect to this. But in general, I think they both also just love that scene.GROSS: What do FBI agents think of the idea of bringing in mixed martial arts fighters to help with FBI agents' conditioning?FISHER: I can't quite capture for you just how humiliated and upset many FBI agents are by what they see as a belittling of their serious training, their serious academic background. This is an agency where a lot of the FBI agents, while you might see them in TV dramas as essentially street cops in suits, this is an agency where many of them are lawyers, many of them have advanced degrees. These are people deeply schooled in the technology necessary to combat cybercrime today. And so when they see that their own director is talking about making them have training sessions with MMA fighters, they see this as belittling and insulting. There are some ways in which many FBI agents like the fact that Patel has steered the agency back toward what they see as basic crime fighting. But the overwhelming sentiment, I think, is that he has more than shaken up the bureau. He has gutted it.GROSS: Do you have any insights into the relationship between Trump and Patel now? You've said basically that Patel was hired not because of his experience as much as for his loyalty to Trump. So now that he's been in that position a while, has it affected the relationship between him and Trump and Trump's trust in Patel?FISHER: I think what I've heard from people inside the bureau is that the president has been frustrated by some of Patel's missteps, such as his premature announcement that the Bureau had found and captured the assassin of Charlie Kirk, a move that Patel then had to reverse just minutes later. So there is a sense in the White House that Patel has made too many mistakes. On the other hand, the president clearly values Patel's loyalty, clearly values the energy with which Patel is putting the president's agenda into motion. So there's no indication that he's in trouble right now, but there are some real doubts about his overall capacity.GROSS: Marc Fisher, thank you so much for talking with us.FISHER: My pleasure.GROSS: Marc Fisher's profile of Kash Patel is titled \"Kash Patel's Acts Of Service.\" It's published in the current issue of The Atlantic. Tomorrow on FRESH AIR, our guest will be dream scientist Michelle Carr. She runs a sleep lab where she studies people as they're dreaming, with a focus on nightmares and lucid dreaming. She'll talk about her new book, \"Nightmare Obscura,\" and how she says we can change our recurring nightmares. To keep up with what's on the show and get highlights of our interviews, follow us on Instagram, @nprfreshair.FRESH AIR's executive producer is Danny Miller. Our technical director and engineer is Audrey Bentham. Our managing producer is Sam Briger. Our interviews and reviews are produced and edited by Phyllis Myers, Ann Marie Baldonado, Lauren rensel, Therese Madden, Monique Nazareth, Thea Chaloner, Susan Nyakundi and Anna Bauman. Our digital media producer is Molly Seavy-Nesper. Our consulting visual producer is Hope Wilson. Roberta Shorrock directs the show. Our co-host is Tonya Mosley. I'm Terry Gross.(SOUNDBITE OF HERLIN RILEY'S \"RUSH HOUR\")\nCopyright © 2025 NPR. All rights reserved. Visit our website terms of use and permissions pages at www.npr.org for further information.\nAccuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR’s programming is the audio record.\nCorrection Nov. 24, 2025\nThe audio version of this interview incorrectly attributes Marc Fisher's article about Kash Patel to The Atlantic. It was published in The New Yorker.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"《卡什·帕特尔如何搅动司法部并改变联邦调查局的使命》\n《纽约客》撰稿人马克·费舍尔说，帕特尔在没有高级执法经验的情况下成为联邦调查局局长，是因为他对特朗普的忠诚，以及他愿意对自己认定的敌人寻求报复。\nNPR\n政治\n收听并关注\nNPR 应用\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS 链接\n政治\n卡什·帕特尔如何搅动联邦调查局并改变其使命\n2025年11月19日 美国东部时间下午12:49\n在《Fresh Air》中播出\n特里·格罗斯\nFA：马克·费舍尔\n收听\n·\n44:13\n44:13\n文字稿\n切换更多选项\n下载\n嵌入\n嵌入\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5613347/nx-s1-9539965\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\n文字稿\n《纽约客》撰稿人马克·费舍尔说，帕特尔在没有高级执法经验的情况下成为联邦调查局局长，是因为他对特朗普的忠诚，以及他愿意对自己认定的敌人寻求报复。\n赞助商消息\n文字稿\n特里·格罗斯，主持人：这里是《Fresh Air》。我是特里·格罗斯。今天，我们将与马克·费舍尔谈谈联邦调查局局长卡什·帕特尔；费舍尔在《纽约客》本期杂志上对帕特尔进行了专题介绍。文章标题是《卡什·帕特尔的服务行为》。副标题是：“这位联邦调查局局长不仅仅是在该机构执行总统的议程——他还在为联邦调查局过去对唐纳德·特朗普的调查寻求报复。”费舍尔还写到了围绕帕特尔的其他争议，从他是否具备担任这一职务的资格，到他的利益冲突，以及他如何在专注于打击无证移民的同时改变联邦调查局的使命。记者马克·费舍尔曾长期担任《华盛顿邮报》的撰稿人和编辑，也是《特朗普揭秘》一书的合著者，之后成为《纽约客》撰稿人。我们的采访录于昨天上午，当时众议院和参议院都还没有批准公开爱泼斯坦档案。先听一段帕特尔去年9月在众议院司法委员会作证时谈到爱泼斯坦档案的内容。以下是加利福尼亚州民主党籍议员埃里克·斯沃韦尔对他的提问。（播放存档录音）\n埃里克·斯沃韦尔：你说你不知道特朗普的名字在档案中出现了多少次，所以至少可能有1000次，对吗？\n卡什·帕特尔：这个数字完全是误导性的因素。\n斯沃韦尔：但这个数字至少会是1000次。\n帕特尔：我们没有公布任何人的……\n斯沃韦尔：但至少会是1000次。\n帕特尔：……不可信的档案。而且我们已经……\n斯沃韦尔：局长，至少可能有1000次吗？\n帕特尔：……公布了每一项法律允许公开的信息。\n斯沃韦尔：好，所以……\n帕特尔：你想怎样描述这个数字都可以。\n斯沃韦尔：局长，我收回发言时间。听起来，如果你不知道这个数字，那么至少可能有1000次，这让我……\n帕特尔：不是。不是。\n斯沃韦尔：至少有500次吗？\n帕特尔：没有。\n斯沃韦尔：至少有100次吗？\n帕特尔：没有。\n斯沃韦尔：那到底是多少？\n帕特尔：我不知道这个数字，但不是那么多。\n斯沃韦尔：你认为知道这个数字可能是你的职责吗？\n帕特尔：我的职责是保障这个国家的安全与保障。我的职责不是参与政治影射……\n斯沃韦尔：而这包括保护第……\n帕特尔：……这样你就可以到偏远地方去，在你的筹款文章中获得那20秒的宣传效果。\n斯沃韦尔：那么我收回发言时间，局长……\n帕特尔：\n继续收回我的时间，因为……SWALWELL：局长……PATEL：……加利福尼亚人民正受到……SWALWELL：如果特朗普……PATEL：……因你的代表而得不到充分服务。SWALWELL：总统并未受到牵连。为什么不公布所有涉及特朗普的内容？PATEL：我们已经公布了总统以及其他任何人的、可信且依法可以公布的全部内容。你对这件事的执着，以及毫无根据地指控我在包庇恋童癖罪犯……SWALWELL：还有马西先生的……PATEL：……令人恶心。SWALWELL：……利益。PATEL：任何这么说的人都应该只看看统计数据，然后回到加利福尼亚州，看看哪里获得了联邦调查局资源最大的增幅……SWALWELL：所以，局长……PATEL：……这是因为我重新调配了资源，洛杉矶、圣迭戈和旧金山这些城市需要这些资源。SWALWELL：局长，我收回我的时间。记得你发誓要说真话，你有没有告诉唐纳德·特朗普，他的名字在这些文件里？PATEL：我从未和特朗普总统谈过爱泼斯坦文件。SWALWELL：你有没有告诉司法部长，唐纳德·特朗普的名字在爱泼斯坦文件里？PATEL：司法部长和我曾多次讨论过全部爱泼斯坦文件，以及我们团队进行的审查。SWALWELL：你有没有告诉司法部长，唐纳德·特朗普的名字在爱泼斯坦文件里？PATEL：我们已经公布了特朗普总统的名字出现在司法部长文件中的位置。SWALWELL：这是一个简单的问题。你有没有告诉司法部长，总统的名字在爱泼斯坦文件里？PATEL：在司法部长和我就爱泼斯坦问题进行的多次谈话中，我们 painstakingly审查了……SWALWELL：问题很简单。PATEL：……谁可以……SWALWELL：你有没有告诉PATEL：……那个……SWALWELL：……司法部长，唐纳德·特朗普的名字在爱泼斯坦文件里？是或不是？PATEL：如果你要嘲笑我，为什么不试着把它拼出来？SWALWELL：是或不是，局长？PATEL：用字母表。SWALWELL：是或不是？PATEL：不是。A、B、C、D、E、F。不想做。SWALWELL：局长，听起来你不想告诉我们。你有没有告诉司法部长，唐纳德·特朗普的名字在爱泼斯坦文件里？PATEL：你为什么不试着服务你的……SWALWELL：局长，你有没有告诉……PATEL：……选民，把重点放在减少……SWALWELL：……司法部长，唐纳德·特朗普……PATEL：……这个国家的暴力犯罪，以及恋童癖者的数量……SWALWELL：局长……PATEL：……那些被非法……SWALWELL：局长，我收回我的时间。PATEL：……藏匿在你们加利福尼亚州的庇护城市里。SWALWELL：局长，我收回我的时间……PATEL：我会和你合作处理这件事。UNIDENTIFIED PERSON：遵循正常程序，主席先生。PATEL：你想和我们合作处理这件事吗？JIM JORDAN：时间属于来自加利福尼亚州的这位先生。SWALWELL：你有没有告诉司法部长，唐纳德·特朗普的名字在爱泼斯坦文件里？PATEL：这个问题已经被提出并得到回答。SWALWELL：你并没有回答，我们会把你的闪烁其词视为心虚。GROSS：马克·费舍尔，欢迎来到《FRESH AIR》。你对卡什·帕特尔的介绍非常有内容，也很有意思。你第一次听到斯沃尔韦尔质问卡什·帕特尔时有什么反应？MARC FISHER：嗯，这是帕特尔与众议院和参议院成员之间一连串此类对峙中的一次，场面相当疯狂。这是卡什·帕特尔非常典型的方式之一，他非常……\n像唐纳德·特朗普一样，把这场秀看得高于几乎一切。这也是帕特尔成为特朗普宠儿的原因之一。帕特尔之所以担任这一职务，原因之一就在于他有能力掌控局面，制造一场大秀，把自己塑造成受了委屈的人，并把自己呈现为所有那些感到受了委屈的美国人的代言人。\n\nGROSS：你以前听说过哪位联邦调查局局长以如此对抗性的方式回避问题吗？\n\nFISHER：没有，我们从未见过像卡什·帕特尔这样的联邦调查局局长。我们从未见过长得像卡什·帕特尔的人。之前的联邦调查局局长都算是衣着干净利落、保守、非常得体……\n\nGROSS：白人男性。\n\nFISHER：白人男性，全都是。而卡什·帕特尔完全不是这些。所以我们以前没见过这种做派，也没见过这种敌意，更没见过这种缺乏经验、对联邦调查局所做工作不熟悉的情况。卡什·帕特尔的背景中，有充分理由可以解释唐纳德·特朗普为什么想让他担任这个职务；同样也有充分理由可以理解，为什么联邦调查局内部那么多人认为他不适合这个职位，并且正在对该局造成重大且持久的损害。\n\nGROSS：右翼评论员兼播客主持人本尼·约翰逊曾采访卡什·帕特尔，他问帕特尔，联邦调查局为什么拒绝公布名单上的姓名，从而保护世界上最臭名昭著的掠食者。帕特尔回答说，很简单，因为名单上有谁。这听起来与我们刚才听到的相互矛盾。\n\nFISHER：没错。本尼·约翰逊的那次谈话发生在几年前，当时卡什·帕特尔还在体制外，正在鼓动当时的政府，也就是拜登政府，公布那份据称由杰弗里·爱泼斯坦保存的客户名单。如今的联邦调查局局长帕特尔希望人们相信的是一套完全不同的事实：不存在什么客户名单，从来就没有。帕特尔在进入联邦调查局之前，当然曾考虑过爱泼斯坦之死背后存在某种阴谋；但现在他成了局长，有机会阅读这些档案后，他得出的结论是，这绝对是自杀，没有相反的证据。没有任何猫腻。唐纳德·特朗普出现在爱泼斯坦档案中的内容极少，而且无害。\n\n因此，帕特尔几乎彻底的立场逆转，如今又离奇地伴随着卡什·帕特尔说：你们怎么能不相信我？当人们（包括国会议员）就此质问他时，他会愤愤不平。所以帕特尔说，我在任外时就是那个推动公布所有这些东西的人。现在我在任内，告诉你们没有什么东西可以公布，你们就应该相信我。至少从他的表现来看，他似乎惊讶于人们不相信他的话。\n\nGROSS：那么，我们简要梳理一下卡什·帕特尔在过去几周因哪些事情登上新闻。这周，他因给自己的女友、乡村音乐歌手亚历克西斯·威尔金斯配备安保而登上新闻。请简要告诉我们，这为什么不寻常。\n\nFISHER：此前没有任何一位联邦调查局局长为其配偶提供高级别安保，无论是妻子还是任何女友都没有。因此，一位局长要求为一个既不是局长本人、甚至也不是局长配偶的人提供这种保护，非常不寻常。那么，这为什么会成为新闻？因为有一\n联邦调查局内部有很多人认为，这类请求、这类违规行为不仅不体面，而且是在浪费资源。所以，无论是为他的女友提供保护，还是让局长使用局里的公务机，带他去参加各种娱乐活动——看冰球比赛、去拉斯维加斯赌场游玩、去纳什维尔探望那位女友——这一切都让纳税人付出了巨大代价，局里很多人也认为这是在浪费资源。GROSS：作为回应，帕特尔说，他一直在偿还政府为这些行程支付的费用。FISHER：是的。他说自己按商业票价向政府偿还费用——也就是你我飞往纳什维尔时要支付的票价，而这远低于联邦调查局调动他们的GV公务机、把局长送到他想去的地方享受那个周末的娱乐活动所需的成本。GROSS：上周，帕特尔豁免了他的副局长丹·邦吉诺以及另外两名最近才聘用的联邦调查局高级官员，不必参加测谎测试。考虑到丹·邦吉诺作为联邦调查局副局长可以接触政府最高级别的机密信息，副局长获准免于测谎测试有多不寻常？我认为联邦调查局的每个人都必须通过这项测试。FISHER：是的。几十年来，联邦调查局一直使用测谎测试，把它作为对基本安全问题的一种例行检查；任何人要接触联邦调查局经手的这类机密信息，都必须通过测试。所以，既然全国各地的特工都要接受这项要求，那么管理联邦调查局的人也同样如此，包括副局长丹·邦吉诺。那么，为什么邦吉诺能获得豁免？联邦调查局对此只字未提。许多现任和前任特工都猜测，他测谎没通过，或者有理由担心自己会通不过，因此获准跳过测试。这让联邦调查局里的其他人很不舒服，因为他们希望能够信任身边的人，尤其是上级，也包括同事。而让他们产生这种信任感的一点，就是他们知道每个人都通过了同样的安全门槛。当机构里的二号人物可以在这件事上获得免检待遇时，这会让队伍中的很多人感到不满。GROSS：既然谈到了丹·邦吉诺，帕特尔曾说，他会任命一名有联邦调查局经验的人担任副局长。但丹·邦吉诺完全没有在联邦调查局工作的经验，事实上他根本没有联邦调查局经验；当然，特朗普任命帕特尔时，帕特尔也没有。邦吉诺曾是特勤局特工、播客主持人和福克斯新闻评论员。FISHER：是的，完全正确。邦吉诺是总统所熟悉的人，因为他在福克斯新闻上表现出色，经常出现在福克斯新闻节目中。在总统看来，管理联邦调查局的人应该是公众熟知的人，而且会非常积极、非常有效地为总统的立场辩护。邦吉诺被视为一个硬派人物。特朗普喜欢硬派人物。他喜欢那些看起来是“让美国再次伟大”运动中直率、强硬的倡导者的人，而邦吉诺非常符合这一标准。所以，他看起来就像那么回事。这往往是特朗普心目中的第一资格，而邦吉诺在这方面或许比卡什·帕特尔更符合。帕特尔在总统心目中是终极忠诚者，是一个他绝对可以信任的人。所以，这是一个你可以看出总统确实设想过的团队，作为\n互补的。因此，这两个人都没有联邦调查局的工作经验这一事实，似乎并不怎么重要。\n\nGROSS：好，我们在这里短暂休息一下。如果你刚加入我们的节目，我的嘉宾是马克·费舍尔。他最近离开了《华盛顿邮报》，在那里长期担任记者和编辑。他还是《特朗普揭秘》一书的作者。现在，他是《纽约客》的撰稿人，并在他的新文章《卡什·帕特尔的服务之举》中介绍了卡什·帕特尔。我们马上回来。这里是《新鲜空气》节目。\n\n（播放路易斯·斯克拉维斯《游乐场》片段）\n\nGROSS：这里是《新鲜空气》。让我们继续听我对马克·费舍尔的采访。他在《纽约客》的一篇新文章中介绍了联邦调查局局长卡什·帕特尔，文章标题是《卡什·帕特尔的服务之举》。副标题是：这位联邦调查局局长不仅在该局执行总统的议程——他还在为联邦调查局过去对唐纳德·特朗普的调查寻求报复。\n\n所以，人们对帕特尔是如何获得联邦调查局局长这一职位的，以及他是否具备必要的经验和资历，都存在疑问。他此前担任过什么职务？与联邦调查局局长通常的经历相比如何？\n\nFISHER：过去的联邦调查局局长通常都有长期的刑事司法职业生涯。有些人当过法官，有些人当过检察官，也有人曾担任美国检察官，负责管理全国规模最大的一些美国检察官办公室，无论是在纽约还是其他地方。他们都是在这个体系中一路晋升上来的人，有着长期的公共服务经历。\n\n所以，卡什·帕特尔也具备其中一些条件。卡什·帕特尔确实一生都在从事公共服务。除了在两届特朗普政府之间自己创办的一些小企业外，他从未为政府以外的任何人工作过。然而，帕特尔从未拥有那种长期直接在联邦调查局工作的经历——他确实在特朗普第一届政府时期的司法部任职。他在司法部负责国家安全事务，并参与了一些反恐案件。这也是联邦调查局的工作之一。但他没有前任们那样的经验：没有管理一个机构的经验，也没有参与那种联邦调查局探员在公众想象中一直以此闻名的街头犯罪工作的经验。\n\n因此，联邦调查局内部有很多人非常担心，他会在缺乏知识基础、缺乏多年经验的情况下上任，而这些知识和经验本应让他了解联邦调查局如何运作，以及它与司法部的关系如何运行。所以，人们担忧他的相对缺乏经验。除此之外，我认为还有很多人担心，他上任后本质上会成为唐纳德·特朗普的工具——本质上会成为一个推动总统政治议程的人，而不是为联邦调查局的男女工作人员争取权益的人。\n\nGROSS：你在《纽约客》那篇关于帕特尔的文章副标题是：“这位联邦调查局局长不仅在该局执行总统的议程——他还在为联邦调查局过去对唐纳德·特朗普的调查寻求报复。”所以，在他的回忆录中，也就是帕特尔的回忆录中，他列出了一份所谓的“政府匪徒”名单。我们之前听到质询帕特尔的埃里克·斯沃尔韦尔也在那份名单上。他一直在寻求对哪些人进行报复？又是为了什么？\n\nFISHER：几乎从卡什·帕特尔接管联邦调查局的第一天起，他最重要、或许是最重要的议程之一，就是点名并调查那些总统视为敌人的人——那些总统\n他认为，那些调查过特朗普本人、那些在特朗普第一届政府结束后把机密文件带到佛罗里达州海湖庄园而对他提起诉讼的人、那些就他被定罪的其他各种事情——金融不当行为等等——起诉他的人，也都属于这一类人。因此，有一长串特朗普的敌人，是特朗普在拜登政府执政的四年间谈到过的。卡什·帕特尔上任时带着那份名单，也带着他自己的名单——他称之为政府黑帮名单。他写了一整本书，列出了那些人的身份，书中还附有一份至少60人的名单；在他看来，这些人是特朗普的敌人，因此也是帕特尔的敌人。于是，他有条不紊地逐一处理这份名单，已经开始搜集证据并寻求对前联邦调查局局长詹姆斯·科米等人提起控诉，也对联邦调查局其他参与“通俄门”调查的高层官员采取同样做法。所有这些调查构成了特朗普第一任期的特征——如今，这些人都成了被瞄准的对象。\n\n帕特尔现在已经对联邦调查局的几个人说过，他认为自己的工作就是追查总统希望被起诉的人。总统没有忘记。这就是帕特尔反复使用的说法，用来解释他为什么要追查其中一些人。当然，这很讽刺，因为帕特尔把自己的整个局长任期都建立在这样一个理念上：在他之前的每个人都把联邦调查局、司法部武器化，用于他们自己的政治目的。然而现在，他却被指控犯下完全相同的过错——追查他人，利用联邦调查局的权力追查政治对手。\n\nGROSS：所以，帕特尔不仅让前联邦调查局局长吉姆·科米遭到起诉，还因为一名特工拒绝安排科米以嫌疑人身份在媒体镜头前亮相，而解雇了这名特工。\n\nFISHER：是的，没错。据各种说法，帕特尔已经因为各种违规行为解雇了几十名特工，其中大多数他并没有以任何方式详细说明。但我所做的报道，以及其他新闻机构的报道，都揭示出这样一个事实：帕特尔解雇特工的理由包括在办公桌上摆放同性恋骄傲旗帜，或者如你所说，拒绝安排经典的嫌疑人亮相仪式——在这种仪式中，据称的罪犯会被带到镜头前示众；这次的对象是前联邦调查局局长吉姆·科米本人。\n\n因此，在许多联邦调查局特工心目中，卡什·帕特尔被视为一个替特朗普实施报复的人，而且这种报复至少表面上看，似乎只是为了残酷而残酷；更何况，帕特尔还告诉局内人员，如果完全由他自己决定，他不会解雇其中许多特工——他是从白宫和司法部那里得到指示，并且是在总统授意下，或为了取悦总统，而不是因为帕特尔本人认为这些特工做错了什么，才解雇了其中一些人。\n\nGROSS：好了，我们在这里再休息一小会儿。如果你刚刚加入节目，我的嘉宾是马克·费舍尔。他在《纽约客》的一篇新文章中介绍了联邦调查局局长卡什·帕特尔，文章标题是《卡什·帕特尔的服务行为》，副标题是：这位联邦调查局局长不仅在该局执行总统的议程——他还在为过去对唐纳德·特朗普的调查寻求报复。我们马上回来\n短暂休息后回来。我是特里·格罗斯，这里是《新鲜空气》。（播放杰西卡·威廉姆斯《孩子们的事》片段）\n\n格罗斯：这里是《新鲜空气》。我是特里·格罗斯。让我们继续听我对马克·费舍尔的采访。他在《纽约客》发表了一篇联邦调查局局长卡什·帕特尔的新人物特写，费舍尔是该杂志的撰稿人。他最近离开了《华盛顿邮报》，此前在那里长期担任记者和编辑。他还是《特朗普揭秘》一书的作者，这是一部总统传记。\n\n你在关于卡什·帕特尔的人物特写中写道，他传播过几种阴谋论。我想播放一段片段，这是你在特写中引用的内容。这是他今年6月6日作为嘉宾参加乔·罗根播客时的讲话节选。卡什·帕特尔在其中描述了一种与中国和芬太尼有关的阴谋论。\n\n（播客《乔·罗根体验》片段）\n\n帕特尔：你听不到中国有芬太尼死亡事件。你听不到印度有芬太尼死亡事件。你基本上也听不到英国、澳大利亚、新西兰——我们的“五眼”伙伴——以及加拿大有芬太尼死亡事件。在我看来，中国人，也就是中共，把它当作一种有针对性的手段，因为我们是他们的对手。他们不喜欢我们，而我们也不喜欢他们在芬太尼问题上的所作所为。他们的长期计划是这样的：在我看来，我要如何让美利坚合众国——我们最大的对手——丧失战斗力？那我们为什么不去摧毁一代又一代可能长大后加入美国军队、成为警察或教师的年轻男女？这就是他们正在做的事——每年让数以万计的美国人死亡。这对他们来说是一个长期计划。\n\n乔·罗根：哇。这真是太黑暗、太黑暗了。\n\n帕特尔：确实，但我们已经——我们正在处理。\n\n格罗斯：以上是卡什·帕特尔今年6月6日在乔·罗根播客上的讲话。我以前没听过这一段。你听过吗？\n\n费舍尔：没有。这对我来说也是新的。不过他有一大堆这样的说法，而且在他的《政府匪徒》一书里，整本书都是这类内容。在罗根和帕特尔那次采访的同一场对话中，有一刻罗根几乎是在调侃帕特尔，说他容易陷入这些阴谋论，而且他把政府、媒体和政客看成一个阴谋集团的一部分，目的是削弱特朗普、削弱“让美国再次伟大”运动。罗根有点戳他，说，我们喜欢阴谋，对吧？它们很刺激。帕特尔笑了笑说，是啊。这是我们的风格。\n\n格罗斯：你认为这种关于中国的阴谋论，与特朗普威胁袭击委内瑞拉以及把军舰派到委内瑞拉附近，有什么联系吗？你看到了联系吗？\n\n费舍尔：特朗普和帕特尔的思维方式，以及他们讲述本届政府行动的方式，都有某种一贯性。他们都倾向于用戏剧化的方式讲述一些相当异想天开的观点，描述我们的敌人在做什么、是什么在驱动他们；而且他们都从一些实际事实出发。芬太尼显然是一场祸害，显然是一个巨大的问题。它从墨西哥流入，但其组成成分往往是在中国制造的，所以那里确实存在一个合理的问题。对联邦调查局来说，这也是一个合理的打击犯罪要务。这是事实部分。\n\n然后我们就进入了异想天开的部分。当我们听到帕特尔展开说明这种事情为何可能发生时，这与唐纳德·特朗普经常描述问题的方式非常相似——不仅是在委内瑞拉，也是在世界各地；在那里，他看到或听说了某些\n这种情况会先提出事实的萌芽，然后从那里进行外推，夸大其词，并添入一些无法完全核实的数字，等等。归根结底，这一切都是为了这场秀。特朗普和帕特尔在某些重要方面是非常相似的性格，其中之一就是，他们把自己工作中的每一天都看作一场长期上演的戏剧中的下一幕，而这场戏需要新的刺激，才能让他们登上当天的头条。\n\nGROSS：帕特尔还推崇过哪些其他阴谋论？\n\nFISHER：卡什·帕特尔自己也承认，他容易产生阴谋论式的想法。他会在事实模式中看出阴谋。显然，任何打击犯罪的人都应该在收集到的事实中寻找模式，但就卡什·帕特尔而言，他往往会超出现有事实所能支持的范围。比如，多年来，帕特尔一直认为自己是媒体阴谋的目标，媒体要把他描绘成一个阴暗的人，还要捏造关于他的事情。因此，他起诉了一系列新闻机构，声称它们诽谤了他。他起诉过《纽约时报》，起诉过CNN，也起诉过Politico。每一次，他都在新闻中获得了大肆报道；而每一次，最终不是撤诉，就是被法官驳回。\n\n在1月6日袭击国会大厦一案中，卡什·帕特尔几年来一直在提出这样一种理论：联邦调查局本身招募了某种类型的行动人员，他们于1月6日混在人群中，也许还在鼓励暴徒更加猛烈地攻击国会大厦。他从未为此提出哪怕一丝证据，但仍继续宣扬这一观点。即使在担任联邦调查局局长期间，他也说过，可能确实有这样的人，他们可能极大地煽动了1月6日的人群，把1月6日的袭击描绘得几乎像是一场内部策划的行动。\n\nGROSS：既然1月6日特朗普还是总统——我的意思是，他当时正要离开白宫，但那时他仍是总统。如果从联邦调查局派人进入民兵组织，敦促他们采取更具攻击性、甚至暴力的行动，这也算是一场内部策划的行动，那么这为什么不是特朗普政府的问题？当时的联邦调查局局长是特朗普总统任命的人。\n\nFISHER：是的——完全正确。1月6日袭击发生在第一届特朗普政府任期的最后阶段。因此，如果正如帕特尔所推测的那样，联邦调查局在2020年以及2021年的最初几天，动用自己的机密线人，把这些线人安插到策划1月6日袭击的民兵组织内部，那么完全有理由问：这是否意味着第一届特朗普政府在某种程度上应对这次袭击负责？卡什·帕特尔不知为何从来不谈阴谋论中的这一面。他把联邦调查局看作一种失控的行动机构。这与他关于“深层政府”的总体理论相吻合：深层政府才是美国真正的治理力量，它自行其是，在特朗普和特朗普政府可能并不知情的情况下，把这些线人植入策划1月6日袭击的团体中。\n\n而且，你知道，联邦调查局确实有一些联系人，他们不时身处某些民兵组织内部。那么，他们在那里是否是为了鼓励这些团体在1月6日采取更加暴力的方式？没有人提出过这方面的证据，但他推动这一理论、声称一旦进入内部，他就已经多年了，他\n会揭示证据。嗯，什么都没有被揭示。\n\nGROSS：特朗普于2023年因秘密将机密文件存放在海湖庄园而被起诉后，帕特尔说，当你是总统、离任时，你想带走什么都可以。而当你把它带走后，不管它是否机密，它现在就不再是机密了。\n\nFISHER：我相信是总统先说了这个，但卡什·帕特尔很快接受了这一观点，即海湖庄园里的所有东西之所以在那里都是合法的，仅仅因为总统把它们带到了那里。这完全违背法律。1978年，国会通过了《总统档案法》。该法禁止前总统带走任何官方记录——不论是否机密——也就是说，离任时不得把这些记录带走。\n\n嗯，特朗普确实把很多这类东西带到了海湖庄园。而帕特尔曾多次说，当他在特朗普第一届政府时期于白宫工作时，他看到总统给文件解密，以便把它们带到佛罗里达。因此，由于帕特尔曾在各种公开场合说过这些话，检察官随后传唤他作证，让他在大陪审团面前回答：好吧，你看到了什么？那些文件是哪一些？总统给它们解密时做了什么？嗯，他来到大陪审团。突然，帕特尔援引《宪法第五修正案》，说他不会回答任何问题，因为他享有不自证其罪的权利。于是检察官说，好吧。我们会给你豁免权。于是他们向法院申请让他获得豁免，帕特尔第二次出庭。这一次，他回答了他们的问题。\n\n时间快进到现在，他正在参加成为联邦调查局局长的参议院确认听证会。参议员们说，好吧。告诉我们这次大陪审团作证的情况，你当时谈到了总统为何以及如何使这些文件可以被带到海湖庄园。卡什·帕特尔再次说，不，做不到，不被允许，因为那违反法律。正如几位参议员指出的，那完全是假的。你必须对自己说过的话负责，而且你可以披露那次证词，但他没有这样做。\n\nGROSS：我想我们需要在这里再休息一下，所以让我再介绍一下你。如果你刚刚加入我们的节目，我的嘉宾是马克·费舍尔，他在本期《纽约客》中撰文介绍卡什·帕特尔。我们马上回来。这是《新鲜空气》节目。\n\n（朱利安·拉格乐队与朱利安·拉格演奏的《爱荷华往事》音乐片段）\n\nGROSS：这里是《新鲜空气》节目。让我们继续收听我昨天上午录制的对马克·费舍尔的采访。费舍尔在本期《纽约客》中撰文介绍联邦调查局局长卡什·帕特尔，他也是该杂志的撰稿人。文章标题是《卡什·帕特尔的服务之举》。\n\n帕特尔解雇了大量联邦调查局特工。我不知道具体数字。他还把许多留下的特工重新分派去抓捕非法滞留在美国的人。那么，由于这些解雇和重新分派，联邦调查局有哪些任务被取消或人员不足，或者哪些部门被彻底关闭了？\n\nFISHER：他给联邦调查局带来的是一次彻底的改变。联邦调查局大约有13,000名特工，其中约四分之一被安排去抓捕无证移民。这通常并不是联邦调查局的主要工作重点。还有数百名特工被派去执行这些任务，他们与国民警卫队一起，在华盛顿特区和芝加哥等城市进行基本的治安巡逻。这是……\n这种强调打击暴力犯罪的做法，是特朗普总统几个月前开始的。那么，有什么事情没有在做呢？比如说，帕特尔解散了华盛顿外勤办公室的公共腐败调查组。这是负责调查公职人员不当行为的团队，包括——这个办公室曾调查特朗普和其他几名共和党人试图推翻2020年总统选举结果的行为。我从联邦调查局的多名特工那里听到了一个又一个例子，比如有人原本在美国从事中国反恐工作。有一名这样的特工被调离案件，基本上去哥伦比亚特区做街头巡逻，负责逮捕酒驾者。所以，这是对特工工作内容的一次彻底重组。结果是，很多人提前退休，一有条件就退休，还有相当多人——正如你所说——被解雇了。\n\nGROSS：此外，帕特尔还降低了加入联邦调查局的标准。新特工不再需要大学学位，而现在新特工必须接受测谎测试，其中包括对联邦调查局局长的忠诚度。\n\nFISHER：是的。这是帕特尔否认的事情，但我们从几名特工那里听说，他们被问及对帕特尔局长以及他个人议程的忠诚度。这是许多联邦调查局特工觉得担任特工的标准被降低的诸多方式之一。比如，帕特尔把联邦调查局学院的培训时间从18周缩短到8周，而且不再要求所有新特工拥有大学学位。联邦调查局的许多特工都拥有高级学位，其中许多人还接受过语言等方面的专业训练，为联邦调查局擅长开展的国际调查做准备。因此，对于许多把一生奉献给联邦调查局的人来说，看到他们认为这些标准正在被稀释，确实令人非常失望。\n\nGROSS：帕特尔设法利用担任联邦调查局局长一职在经济上获利。你能描述一下他获利的一些方式吗？\n\nFISHER：嗯，在两届政府任期之间——也就是他在国家安全委员会任职和担任联邦调查局局长之间——他利用那段空档从事了多项赚大钱的活动，而且确实赚了很多钱。其中一些做法非常符合特朗普的模式，就是销售各种东西。他建立了一个网站，销售自己制作的、带有K$h品牌的服装，K，然后是美元符号，再然后是H。这算是一种标志。他为一些1月6日事件的被告筹款。他推广膳食补充剂，声称这些补充剂可以消除新冠病毒造成的损害。他写了三本名为《反对国王的阴谋》的儿童读物。这些儿童读物的主角是一个名叫卡什的巫师，他是英雄；他代表唐纳德国王领导一场战斗，寻找那些策划让希拉里·昆顿在不是“选举日”而是“选择日”当选的坏人。因此，他高度参与了所有这些赚钱并推动特朗普议程的活动。在那些年里，让帕特尔变得更富有的头号因素，是他通过自己成立的一个咨询集团所做的工作；他的客户包括卡塔尔政府。这种关系一直延续到今天，即使他现在已经是联邦调查局局长。\n\nGROSS：帕特尔还有一个名为卡什基金会的慈善机构，承诺向那些有勇气站\n直到政府不当行为。关于筹集到的钱是如何使用的，你了解到了什么？FISHER：凯什基金会有一年筹集了130万美元，花了大约70万美元作为开支，但只捐出了20万美元。所以，在130万美元的收入中，只有20万美元以赠款形式发放出去，主要是现金援助，提供给大约50名未公开姓名的人。因此，这里的想法是帮助那些据称在反抗深层政府、反抗政府的人，但只有20万美元给了这些人。给帕特尔自己的一位朋友、他的一个商业伙伴的钱还要更多；这位商业伙伴经营一家广告公司，该公司受雇为慈善机构的运营做宣传。总的来说，我们谈论的是数百万美元：帕特尔在两届政府之间的那些年里，通过各种商业投资，以及代表一家中国公司和卡塔尔政府所获得的钱——所有这些实体都与美国政府有大量业务往来。GROSS：我应该提一下，他合作的那家中国公司，除其他业务外，还拥有SHEIN，也就是那家快时尚零售商。它在美国非常受欢迎，因为价格实在太便宜了。FISHER：没错。帕特尔曾为SHEIN的子公司工作，而该子公司的董事会成员包括一些被指控与中国军方和中国共产党有联系的投资者。因此，人们确实对该组织的道德问题提出了疑问。然而，帕特尔接任联邦调查局局长后表示，他认为没有必要剥离自己在这些中国公司的持股。GROSS：那么，他是否承诺会回避任何涉及他曾合作并从中获得报酬的公司的法律问题？FISHER：恰恰相反。就他为卡塔尔所做的工作而言，帕特尔接掌联邦调查局后获得了美国政府的豁免，允许他继续处理与卡塔尔政府有关的事务。你知道，联邦调查局处理世界各地各种各样的恐怖主义问题。因此，如果出现任何与卡塔尔有关的事情，他仍将被允许继续处理，即使在成为联邦调查局局长之前的几年里，他曾因向卡塔尔提供咨询服务而收取了数额未公开的报酬。我们确切不知道他从卡塔尔那里拿了多少钱，因为他从未登记为外国代理人；倡议组织Public Citizens表示，这违反了联邦规定。GROSS：如果你刚刚加入我们的节目，我的嘉宾是马克·费舍尔；他在最新一期《纽约客》中撰写了凯什·帕特尔的人物特写。广告后我们马上回来。这里是《新鲜空气》。（播放音乐）GROSS：这里是《新鲜空气》。让我们继续收听我昨天上午录制的对马克·费舍尔的采访。费舍尔在最新一期《纽约客》中撰写了联邦调查局局长凯什·帕特尔的人物特写，他也是该杂志的撰稿人。文章题为《凯什·帕特尔的服务之举》。这算是一个奇怪的事实。我不太确定该如何解读。帕特尔提出的事情之一，是请综合格斗选手来帮助联邦调查局特工进行体能训练。他向终极格斗冠军赛（UFC）的负责人提出了这一建议。你知道，与此相关的是，特朗普希望通过在白宫草坪上举办一场综合格斗比赛，来庆祝美国成立250周年。那么，这说明帕特尔和综合格斗之间有什么关系？他是个粉丝，还是仅仅是出于对特朗普的忠诚——特朗普正计划庆祝美国成立250周年——\n通过进行一场综合格斗比赛来庆祝美国的建国？\n\nFISHER：我愿意暂且相信卡什·帕特尔，并相信他确实是UFC的真正粉丝。他去观看比赛时似乎真的很享受。他曾和梅尔·吉布森一起去看那场拉斯维加斯的比赛。所以，卡什·帕特尔真正热爱的运动是冰球。他打冰球，是个超级球迷。他曾使用政府喷气机飞往纽约观看岛人队的比赛。所以说到UFC，我认为这是一个愉快的巧合：特朗普对这项运动的热爱与帕特尔的喜好恰好契合。他们都强烈希望吸引MAGA阵营中的核心群体——综合格斗的极端阳刚世界。因此，这其中有政治因素。但总的来说，我认为他们也都只是喜欢那个圈子。\n\nGROSS：FBI探员们怎么看让综合格斗选手帮助训练FBI探员体能这个想法？\n\nFISHER：我很难向你完全描述，许多FBI探员对这种做法感到多么屈辱和愤怒，因为在他们看来，这贬低了他们严肃的训练和严谨的学术背景。这是一个机构，很多FBI探员虽然在电视剧中可能被描绘成穿西装的街头警察，但实际上，很多人是律师，很多人拥有高等学位。他们深谙当今打击网络犯罪所需的技术。因此，当他们看到自己的局长谈论要让他们与综合格斗选手一起参加训练课程时，他们认为这是在贬低和侮辱他们。有些方面，许多FBI探员喜欢帕特尔将该机构重新引向他们认为的基本打击犯罪工作。但我认为压倒性的情绪是：他对联邦调查局的整顿远不止是震动。他已经掏空了它。\n\nGROSS：你对特朗普和帕特尔现在的关系有什么了解吗？你基本上说过，帕特尔之所以被任命，与其说是因为他的经验，不如说是因为他对特朗普的忠诚。那么，他担任这个职位已有一段时间，这是否影响了他与特朗普之间的关系，以及特朗普对帕特尔的信任？\n\nFISHER：我从局内人士那里听到的情况是，总统对帕特尔的一些失误感到沮丧，比如他过早宣布联邦调查局已经找到并抓获了查理·柯克的刺客，而帕特尔随后不得不在几分钟后撤回这一说法。因此，白宫方面有一种感觉，那就是帕特尔犯了太多错误。另一方面，总统显然看重帕特尔的忠诚，也显然重视帕特尔推动总统议程实施时所展现出的干劲。所以，目前没有迹象表明他陷入了麻烦，但人们确实对他的整体能力抱有疑问。\n\nGROSS：马克·费舍尔，非常感谢你接受我们的采访。\n\nFISHER：我的荣幸。\n\nGROSS：马克·费舍尔关于卡什·帕特尔的人物特写题为《卡什·帕特尔的服务之举》。文章刊登在本期《大西洋月刊》上。明天的《FRESH AIR》节目嘉宾将是梦境科学家米歇尔·卡尔。她经营着一个睡眠实验室，研究人们做梦时的状态，重点关注噩梦和清醒梦。她将谈论自己的新书《噩梦朦胧》，以及她所说的我们如何改变反复出现的噩梦。要了解节目内容并获取采访精彩片段，请在Instagram上关注我们：@nprfreshair。\n\n《FRESH AIR》的执行制片人是丹尼·米勒。我们的技术总监兼工程师是奥德丽·本瑟姆。我们的制片主管\n是萨姆·布里格尔。我们的采访和评论由 Phyllis Myers、Ann Marie Baldonado、Lauren rensel、Therese Madden、Monique Nazareth、Thea Chaloner、Susan Nyakundi 和 Anna Bauman 制作和编辑。我们的数字媒体制片人是 Molly Seavy-Nesper。我们的咨询视觉制片人是 Hope Wilson。Roberta Shorrock 导演本节目。我们的联合主持人是 Tonya Mosley。我是 Terry Gross。（赫林·莱利《高峰时段》的音乐片段）\n\n版权所有 © 2025 NPR。保留所有权利。更多信息，请访问我们网站上的使用条款和许可页面：www.npr.org。\n\nNPR 转录稿的准确性和可用性可能有所不同。转录文本可能会经过修订，以纠正错误或与音频更新保持一致。npr.org 上的音频可能会在最初播出或发布后进行编辑。NPR 节目的权威记录是音频记录。\n\n更正：2025 年 11 月 24 日\n\n本次采访的音频版本错误地将 Marc Fisher 关于 Kash Patel 的文章归属于《大西洋月刊》。该文章发表于《纽约客》。\n\nNPR 不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n\n任何提出此类提供或请求的人都不是 NPR 员工，也不代表 NPR。\n\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n连接\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻办公室\n公共编辑\n更正\n转录稿\n联系与帮助\n关于 NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR 网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助 NPR\nNPR 招聘\nNPR 商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为 NPR 赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2025-11-19T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1960","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"被解雇FBI特工律师称解雇具有党派性","title_en":"Fired FBI agents fired for kneeling allege retaliation : NPR","summary_cn":"12名因2020年华盛顿特区抗议中单膝跪地被解雇的FBI特工提起诉讼。代表他们的前司法部检察官多曼对《晨间新闻》表示，FBI现任领导层试图“改写”2020年6月4日国家档案馆外发生的事件，并警告将缓和局势视为党派行为会惩罚执法人员行使专业判断。","summary_en":"Twelve fired FBI agents are suing, alleging they were unlawfully dismissed for kneeling during a 2020 racial justice protest in Washington, D.C. Their attorney, former Justice Department prosecutor Mary Dohrmann, told Morning Edition that current FBI leadership is trying to \"rewrite\" what happened outside the National Archives on June 4, 2020, warning that treating de-escalation as partisan risks punishing officers for professional judgment.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T15:41:05.416966+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2025/12/16/nx-s1-5638603/fbi-agents-fired-for-kneeling-during-2020-d-c-protests-sue-the-agency","content":"Fired FBI agents fired for kneeling allege retaliation Twelve FBI agents are suing after being fired for kneeling during 2020 protests in Washington D.C. Their attorney told Morning Edition the firings reflect a pattern of partisan leadership.\nLaw\nLawyer for fired FBI agents who knelt during 2020 protest says firings were partisan\nUpdated December 16, 20259:04 AM ET\nOriginally published December 16, 20254:28 AM ET\nHeard on Morning Edition\nA Martínez\nFBI agents fired for kneeling during 2020 D.C. protests sue the agency\nListen\n·\n4:38\n4:38\nTranscript\nToggle more options\nDownload\nEmbed\nEmbed\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5638603/nx-s1-9573538\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\nTranscript\nFILE - An FBI seal is displayed on a podium before a news conference at the field office in Portland, Ore., Jan. 16.\nJenny Kane/AP\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nJenny Kane/AP\nTwelve fired FBI agents allege in a lawsuit that they were unlawfully dismissed for kneeling during a protest for racial justice in Washington, D.C., five years ago.\nLaw\nFBI agents sue after being fired for kneeling during racial justice protest\nSpeaking with Morning Edition, Mary Dohrmann, a former Justice Department prosecutor representing the agents, said current FBI leadership is attempting to \"rewrite\" what happened outside the National Archives on June 4, 2020, when the agents say they acted in service of public safety. A Justice Department inspector general review last year found no misconduct on the agents' part. Dohrmann said the agents kneeling was context-specific and warned that treating de-escalation as partisan risks punishing law enforcement officers for exercising professional judgment. Listen to the full interview by clicking on the blue button above. The radio version of this story was edited by Adam Bearne and produced by Mansee Khurana. The web copy was written by Majd Al-Waheidi and edited by Obed Manuel.\nSponsor Message\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"因下跪被解雇的联邦调查局特工指控遭报复\n12名联邦调查局特工在2020年华盛顿特区抗议期间因下跪而被解雇后提起诉讼。他们的律师告诉《晨间版》，这些解雇反映了党派化领导层的一种模式。\n法律\n2020年抗议期间下跪的被解雇联邦调查局特工的律师称，解雇决定带有党派性\n更新于2025年12月16日上午9:04（美国东部时间）\n最初发布于2025年12月16日凌晨4:28（美国东部时间）\n《晨间版》播出\nA·马丁内斯\n因在2020年华盛顿特区抗议中下跪而被解雇的联邦调查局特工起诉该机构\n收听\n·\n4:38\n4:38\n文字稿\n切换更多选项\n下载\n嵌入\n嵌入\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5638603/nx-s1-9573538\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\n文字稿\n资料图——1月16日，俄勒冈州波特兰办事处的一场新闻发布会前，讲台上展示着联邦调查局徽章。\n珍妮·凯恩/美联社\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\n珍妮·凯恩/美联社\n12名被解雇的联邦调查局特工在一份诉讼中称，五年前他们因在华盛顿特区一场争取种族正义的抗议活动中下跪而被非法解雇。\n法律\n联邦调查局特工因在争取种族正义的抗议中下跪被解雇后起诉\n在接受《晨间版》采访时，代表这些特工的前司法部检察官玛丽·多尔曼表示，当前联邦调查局领导层正试图“改写”2020年6月4日国家档案馆外发生的事情；这些特工称，当时他们是在履行公共安全职责。司法部监察长办公室去年的审查认定，这些特工不存在不当行为。多尔曼说，特工下跪是特定情境下的行为，并警告称，把缓和局势视为党派行为，可能会因执法人员行使专业判断而惩罚他们。点击上方蓝色按钮收听完整采访。本报道的广播版本由亚当·贝尔恩编辑，曼西·库拉纳制作。网页文字由马吉德·瓦希迪撰写，奥贝德·曼努埃尔编辑。\n赞助商信息\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类报价或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n连接\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻中心\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商信息\n赞助商信息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2025-12-16T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1954","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"FBI副局长丹·邦吉诺将于1月卸任","title_en":"FBI Deputy Director Dan Bongino to step down in January : NPR","summary_cn":"FBI副局长丹·邦吉诺12月17日表示将于1月从FBI卸任，并在X上发文感谢特朗普总统、司法部长邦迪和FBI局长帕特尔。邦吉诺此前是亲特朗普播客主持人，其任期时有动荡，包括与司法部领导层就爱泼斯坦文件处理发生冲突，但也涉及1月6日管道炸弹案嫌疑人的逮捕。","summary_en":"FBI Deputy Director Dan Bongino said Wednesday he plans to step down from the bureau in January, thanking President Trump, Attorney General Pam Bondi and FBI Director Kash Patel in a statement on X. A former pro-Trump podcaster, Bongino's tenure was at times tumultuous, including a clash with Justice Department leadership over the Epstein files, but also involved the arrest of a suspect in the Jan. 6 pipe bomb case.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T15:40:41.303528+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2025/12/17/nx-s1-5647821/fbi-deputy-director-dan-bongino-stepping-down","content":"FBI Deputy Director Dan Bongino to step down in January Bongino's tenure was at times tumultuous, including a clash with Justice Department leadership over the Epstein files. But it also involved the arrest of a suspect in the Jan. 6 pipe bomber case.\nPolitics\nFBI Deputy Director Dan Bongino says he will step down in January\nDecember 17, 20257:06 PM ET\nRyan Lucas\nFBI Deputy Director Dan Bongino speaks during a news conference on an arrest of a suspect in the January 6th pipe bomb case at the Department of Justice on Dec. 4, 2025.\nAndrew Harnik/Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nAndrew Harnik/Getty Images\nFBI deputy director Dan Bongino said Wednesday he plans to step down from the bureau in January. In a statement posted on X, Bongino thanked President Trump, Attorney General Pam Bondi and FBI Director Kash Patel \"for the opportunity to serve with purpose.\" Bongino was an unusual pick for the No. 2 post at the FBI, a critical job overseeing the bureau's day-to-day affairs traditionally held by a career agent. Neither Bongino nor his boss, Patel, had any previous experience at the FBI. Bongino did have previous law enforcement experience, as a police officer and later as a Secret Service agent, as well as a long history of vocal support for Trump.\nSponsor Message\nBongino made his name over the past decade as a pro-Trump, far-right podcaster who pushed conspiracy theories, including some involving the FBI. He had been critical of the bureau, embracing the narrative that it had been \"weaponized\" against conservatives and even calling its agents \"thugs.\" His tenure at the bureau was at times tumultuous, including a clash with Justice Department leadership over the administration's handling of the Jeffrey Epstein files. But it also involved the arrest earlier this month of the man authorities say is responsible for placing two pipe bombs near the Democratic and Republican committee headquarters, hours before the assault on the U.S. Capitol on Jan. 6, 2021. In an unusual arrangement, Bongino has had a co-deputy director since this summer when the Trump administration tapped Andrew Bailey, a former attorney general of Missouri, to serve alongside Bongino in the No. 2 job. President Trump praised Bongino in brief remarks to reporters before he announced he was stepping down.\"Dan did a great job,\" Trump said. \"I think he wants to go back to his show.\"\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFBI\nDan Bongino\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"FBI副局长丹·邦吉诺将于1月辞职\n邦吉诺的任期有时充满动荡，包括因爱泼斯坦档案问题与司法部领导层发生冲突。但其任期内也包括逮捕1月6日管状炸弹案的一名嫌疑人。\n政治\nFBI副局长丹·邦吉诺称将于1月辞职\n2025年12月17日 美国东部时间晚上7:06\n瑞安·卢卡斯\nFBI副局长丹·邦吉诺于2025年12月4日在司法部就1月6日管状炸弹案嫌疑人被捕举行的新闻发布会上发言。\nAndrew Harnik/Getty Images\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nAndrew Harnik/Getty Images\nFBI副局长丹·邦吉诺周三表示，他计划于1月离开该局。邦吉诺在X平台发布的声明中感谢特朗普总统、司法部长帕姆·邦迪和FBI局长卡什·帕特尔“给予我带着使命感服务的机会”。邦吉诺是FBI二把手这一职位的一个不同寻常的人选。这一关键职务负责监督该局日常事务，传统上由职业特工担任。邦吉诺和他的上司帕特尔此前均没有FBI工作经验。邦吉诺确实有过执法经验，曾任警察，后任特勤局特工，并长期公开支持特朗普。\n赞助信息\n过去十年间，邦吉诺以支持特朗普的极右翼播客主播身份成名，曾传播包括涉及FBI在内的阴谋论。他一直批评该局，认同其被“武器化”以对付保守派的说法，甚至称其特工为“暴徒”。他在该局的任期有时充满动荡，包括因政府对杰弗里·爱泼斯坦档案的处理方式而与司法部领导层发生冲突。但其任期内也包括本月早些时候逮捕一名男子，执法部门称该男子负责在2021年1月6日美国国会大厦遇袭前数小时，于民主党和共和党委员会总部附近放置两枚管状炸弹。根据一项不同寻常的安排，自今年夏天特朗普政府任命密苏里州前总检察长安德鲁·贝利与邦吉诺共同担任二把手以来，邦吉诺一直有一位共同副局长。特朗普总统在邦吉诺宣布辞职前对记者发表简短讲话时称赞了他。“丹做得非常出色，”特朗普说。“我认为他想回到自己的节目。”\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类提议或要求的人均非NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\nFBI\n丹·邦吉诺\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n媒体\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多元化\nNPR网络\n无障碍服务\n伦理\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR附加内容\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助信息\n赞助信息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2025-12-17T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1889","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"国土安全部暂停另外20个国家的移民申请","title_en":"DHS pauses immigration applications for an additional 20 countries : NPR","summary_cn":"国土安全部在旅行限制扩大后，暂停另外20个国家的移民申请。美国公民及移民服务局备忘录称，将暂停审查来自这些国家的签证、绿卡、公民身份或庇护的待处理申请，并计划重新审查自2021年以来的申请。名单多为非洲国家，包括安哥拉、尼日利亚、塞内加尔、坦桑尼亚和津巴布韦。","summary_en":"The Department of Homeland Security is pausing immigration applications from an additional 20 countries after expanded travel restrictions took effect Jan. 1. A USCIS memo says it will pause review of pending visa, green card, citizenship or asylum applications from these countries and re-review applications dating back to 2021. The list, mostly African nations, includes Angola, Nigeria, Senegal, Tanzania and Zimbabwe.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T15:50:55.906175+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/01/02/g-s1-104284/dhs-pause-immigration-applications-20-countries","content":"DHS pauses immigration applications for an additional 20 countries The Department of Homeland Security is pausing immigration applications from 20 additional countries following increased scrutiny on people who seek legal pathways for immigrating to the U.S.\nPolitics\nDHS pauses immigration applications for an additional 20 countries\nJanuary 2, 20262:02 PM ET\nXimena Bustillo\nImmigrants prepare to become American citizens at a naturalization service on Jan. 22, 2018 in Newark, New Jersey. Although much of the federal government was shut down Monday morning, the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services (USCIS), offices remained open nationwide.\nJohn Moore/Getty Images North America\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nJohn Moore/Getty Images North America\nThe Department of Homeland Security is pausing the immigration applications from an additional 20 countries after an expansion of travel restrictions took effect Jan. 1. U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services, or USCIS, in a memo released Thursday, said it would pause the review of all pending applications for visas, green cards, citizenship or asylum from immigrants from the additional countries. The memo also outlines plans to re-review applications of immigrants from these countries as far back as 2021. The list, which is composed mostly of countries in Africa, includes Angola, Nigeria, Senegal, Tanzania and Zimbabwe. Last month, the Trump administration expanded the list of countries with travel restrictions to the U.S. from 19 to 39, plus the Palestinian Authority. The move comes as the administration is bringing sharper scrutiny of those who have followed legal steps to seek permanent status in the U.S.\nSponsor Message\nLoading...\n\"USCIS remains dedicated to ensuring aliens from high-risk countries of concern who have entered the United States do not pose risks to national security or public safety,\" the memo states as rational for the pause and reviews. \"To faithfully uphold United States immigration law, the flow of aliens from countries with high overstay rates, significant fraud, or both must stop.\" There are some exceptions outlined in the memo, including athletes and members of their teams competing in the World Cup and Olympics. The administration first suggested it would expand the restrictions after the arrest of an Afghan national suspect in the shooting of two National Guard troops over Thanksgiving weekend. Toward the end of 2025, DHS began taking steps to further pause and review these legal avenues of migration. The U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services, the agency that oversees processing of applications including for visas, naturalizations and asylum, announced it would re-review the status of everyone who had been admitted into the U.S. as a refugee under the Biden administration, essentially reopening those cases. The agency also previously announced an indefinite pause in all processing of asylum applications while it works through its backlog.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"国土安全部暂停另外20个国家的移民申请\n在对寻求通过合法途径移民美国的人士加强审查后，国土安全部暂停了另外20个国家的移民申请。\n政治\n国土安全部暂停另外20个国家的移民申请\n2026年1月2日 下午2:02（美国东部时间）\nXimena Bustillo\n2018年1月22日，移民在新泽西州纽瓦克的一处入籍服务点准备成为美国公民。尽管周一早上大部分联邦政府停摆，美国公民及移民服务局（USCIS）的办公室仍在全国范围内开放。\nJohn Moore/Getty Images North America\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nJohn Moore/Getty Images North America\n在1月1日旅行限制扩大生效后，国土安全部暂停了另外20个国家的移民申请。美国公民及移民服务局（USCIS）在周四发布的一份备忘录中表示，将暂停审查来自这些新增国家的移民所提交的所有待处理签证、绿卡、公民身份或庇护申请。备忘录还概述了计划，重新审查这些国家移民最早可追溯至2021年的申请。该名单主要由非洲国家组成，包括安哥拉、尼日利亚、塞内加尔、坦桑尼亚和津巴布韦。上个月，特朗普政府将受美国旅行限制的国家名单从19个扩大到39个，另加巴勒斯坦民族权力机构。此举正值政府加强审查那些已遵循合法步骤寻求在美国获得永久身份的人士之际。\n赞助商消息\n加载中……\n备忘录称：“USCIS仍致力于确保来自受关注高风险国家、已进入美国的外国人不会对国家安全或公共安全构成风险。”备忘录以此作为暂停和审查的理由。“为忠实维护美国移民法，来自逾期滞留率高、欺诈严重或两者兼具国家的外国人流入必须停止。”备忘录列出了一些例外情况，包括参加世界杯和奥运会比赛的运动员及其团队成员。政府在一名阿富汗籍嫌疑人因感恩节周末枪击两名国民警卫队士兵而被捕后，首次表示将扩大限制。2025年末，国土安全部开始采取措施，进一步暂停并审查这些合法移民途径。负责监督包括签证、入籍和庇护申请处理工作的美国公民及移民服务局宣布，将重新审查所有在拜登政府时期以难民身份获准进入美国者的状态，实际上重新开启这些案件。该机构此前还宣布无限期暂停所有庇护申请的处理，以处理积压案件。\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受资金。\n任何提出此类提议或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n连接\n通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻中心\n公众编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n伦理\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-01-02T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1870","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"明尼苏达州议员担忧FBI接管ICE枪击案调查会'掩盖真相'","title_en":"Minnesota lawmakers fear 'cover-up' as FBI takes over ICE shooting investigation : Here & Now Anytime : NPR","summary_cn":"美国移民和海关执法局（ICE）探员在明尼阿波利斯开枪打死37岁的Renee Nicole Good。国土安全部长Kristi Noem称探员系自卫，但目击者和市长Jacob Frey对此提出异议。明尼苏达州议员Scott Dibble介绍枪击事件后续、调查进展及社区守夜活动。节目还讨论了美国历史最悠久的连续出版报纸《匹兹堡邮报》将于5月停刊。","summary_en":"An Immigration and Customs Enforcement agent shot and killed 37-year-old Renee Nicole Good in Minneapolis. Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem said the agent acted in self-defense, but witnesses and Minneapolis Mayor Jacob Frey dispute that claim. Minnesota State Sen. Scott Dibble details the aftermath, the investigation and community vigils. The program also discusses the Pittsburgh Post-Gazette, the oldest continuously-running U.S. newspaper, set to shut down in May.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-18T15:33:57.901145+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/01/08/nx-s1-5671175/minnesota-lawmakers-fear-cover-up-as-fbi-takes-over-ice-shooting-investigation","content":"Minnesota lawmakers fear 'cover-up' as FBI takes over ICE shooting investigation : Here & Now Anytime An Immigration and Customs Enforcement agent shot and killed 37-year-old Renee Nicole Good in Minneapolis on Wednesday. Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem said the agent acted in self-defense, but witnesses and Minneapolis mayor Jacob Frey dispute that claim. Minnesota Public Radio’s Matt Sepic shares more details.And, Minnesota State Sen. Scott Dibble’s district is next to where Good was killed. He details what he’s seen in the aftermath of the shooting, including the investigation into the killing and community vigils for Good.Then, the Pittsburgh Post-Gazette is set to shut down in May. It’s the oldest continuously-running newspaper in the United States. Andrew Conte from the Center for Media Innovation explains the impact.\nHere & Now Anytime\nNPR\nHere & Now Anytime\nLISTEN & FOLLOW\nNPR App\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS link\nMinnesota lawmakers fear 'cover-up' as FBI takes over ICE shooting investigation\nJanuary 8, 20263:11 PM ET\nBy\nRobin Young\n,\nScott Tong\n,\nDeepa Fernandes\nMinnesota lawmakers fear 'cover-up' as FBI takes over ICE shooting investigation\nListen\n·\n21:07\n21:07\nToggle more options\nDownload\nEmbed\nEmbed\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5671175/nx-s1-mx-5671175-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\nTom Baker/AP\nAn Immigration and Customs Enforcement agent shot and killed 37-year-old Renee Nicole Good in Minneapolis on Wednesday. Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem said the agent acted in self-defense, but witnesses and Minneapolis mayor Jacob Frey dispute that claim. Minnesota Public Radio’s Matt Sepic shares more details.And, Minnesota State Sen. Scott Dibble’s district is next to where Good was killed. He details what he’s seen in the aftermath of the shooting, including the investigation into the killing and community vigils for Good.Then, the Pittsburgh Post-Gazette is set to shut down in May. It’s the oldest continuously-running newspaper in the United States. Andrew Conte from the Center for Media Innovation explains the impact.\nSponsor Message\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"明尼苏达州议员担心FBI接手ICE枪击案调查会导致“掩盖”：Here & Now Anytime\n\n周三，一名移民与海关执法局特工在明尼阿波利斯开枪打死37岁的蕾妮·妮可·古德。国土安全部长克里斯蒂·诺姆称该特工是出于自卫，但目击者和明尼阿波利斯市长雅各布·弗雷对此说法提出异议。明尼苏达公共广播电台的马特·塞皮分享更多细节。\n\n此外，明尼苏达州参议员斯科特·迪布尔的选区就在古德遇害地点附近。他介绍了自己在枪击事件后看到的情况，包括对这起杀人案的调查以及社区为古德举行的守夜活动。\n\n随后，《匹兹堡邮报》将于5月停刊。它是美国连续运营时间最长的报纸。媒体创新中心的安德鲁·孔特解释这一影响。\n\nHere & Now Anytime\n\nNPR\n\nHere & Now Anytime\n\n收听并关注\n\nNPR应用\n\nApple Podcasts\n\nSpotify\n\nAmazon Music\n\niHeart Radio\n\nRSS链接\n\n明尼苏达州议员担心FBI接手ICE枪击案调查会导致“掩盖”\n\n2026年1月8日，美国东部时间下午3:11\n\n作者\n\n罗宾·杨\n\n、\n\n斯科特·汤\n\n、\n\n迪帕·费尔南德斯\n\n明尼苏达州议员担心FBI接手ICE枪击案调查会导致“掩盖”\n\n收听\n\n·\n\n21:07\n\n21:07\n\n切换更多选项\n\n下载\n\n嵌入\n\n嵌入\n\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5671175/nx-s1-mx-5671175-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR嵌入式音频播放器\">\n\nTom Baker/AP\n\n周三，一名移民与海关执法局特工在明尼阿波利斯开枪打死37岁的蕾妮·妮可·古德。国土安全部长克里斯蒂·诺姆称该特工是出于自卫，但目击者和明尼阿波利斯市长雅各布·弗雷对此说法提出异议。明尼苏达公共广播电台的马特·塞皮分享更多细节。此外，明尼苏达州参议员斯科特·迪布尔的选区就在古德遇害地点附近。他介绍了自己在枪击事件后看到的情况，包括对这起杀人案的调查以及社区为古德举行的守夜活动。随后，《匹兹堡邮报》将于5月停刊。它是美国连续运营时间最长的报纸。媒体创新中心的安德鲁·孔特解释这一影响。\n\n赞助商消息\n\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受资金。\n\n任何提出此类提议或要求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\n\nFacebook\n\nFlipboard\n\n电子邮件\n\n阅读与收听\n\n首页\n\n新闻\n\n文化\n\n音乐\n\n播客与节目\n\n连接\n\n通讯\n\nFacebook\n\nInstagram\n\n新闻事务\n\n公共编辑\n\n更正\n\n文字稿\n\n联系与帮助\n\n关于NPR\n\n概览\n\n多元化\n\nNPR网络\n\n无障碍\n\n道德规范\n\n财务\n\n参与其中\n\n支持公共广播\n\n赞助NPR\n\nNPR招聘\n\nNPR商店\n\nNPR Extra\n\n使用条款\n\n隐私\n\n你的隐私选择\n\n纯文本\n\n赞助商消息\n\n赞助商消息\n\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-01-08T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1832","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"Up First简报：FBI搜查《华盛顿邮报》记者住所；格陵兰问题；心理健康资金","title_en":"Up First briefing: FBI raids WaPo home; Greenland; Mental health funds : NPR","summary_cn":"FBI搜查《华盛顿邮报》记者Hannah Natanson的住所，作为特朗普政府泄密调查的一部分，扣押了两台电脑和智能手表，媒体高管称此举是对新闻自由的惊人侵犯。丹麦外长表示丹麦与特朗普在格陵兰问题上存在'根本分歧'，但双方同意成立工作组继续讨论。特朗普政府逆转了超过20亿美元心理健康和成瘾项目资金的削减。此外，布鲁金斯学会研究指出生成式AI用于教育儿童的风险目前大于收益。","summary_en":"The FBI searched Washington Post reporter Hannah Natanson's home as part of the Trump administration's leak investigation, seizing two computers and a smartwatch, which media executives called an alarming intrusion into press freedom. Denmark's foreign minister said there remains a 'fundamental disagreement' with Trump over Greenland, though both sides agreed to form a working group. The Trump administration reversed cuts to over $2 billion in mental health and addiction funding. A Brookings study found generative AI risks in children's education currently outweigh benefits.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T15:31:36.029808+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/01/15/g-s1-106123/up-first-newsletter-fbi-washington-post-greenland-territory-mental-health-funds","content":"Up First briefing: FBI raids WaPo home; Greenland; Mental health funds The FBI searches the home of a Washington Post reporter as part of a leak investigation. And, the Trump administration restores $2B for mental health and addiction programs.\nSpecial Series Up First Newsletter\nAll Up First Stories Up First Podcast Morning Edition\nLISTEN & FOLLOW\nNPR App\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS link\nFBI searches WaPo reporter's home. And, Trump restores $2B to public health funds\nJanuary 15, 20267:17 AM ET\nBy\nBrittney Melton\nFBI Search Journalist's Home, U.S. Greenland Talks, Mental Health Funding\nListen\n·\n12:14\n12:14\nTranscript\nToggle more options\nDownload\nEmbed\nEmbed\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/g-s1-106123/nx-s1-mx-5678163-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\nTranscript\nGood morning. You're reading the Up First newsletter. Subscribe here to get it delivered to your inbox, and listen to the Up First podcast for all the news you need to start your day. Today's top stories Some media executives are calling the FBI's search of a reporter's home yesterday an alarming intrusion into the freedom of the press. The agency searched Washington Post reporter Hannah Natanson's house as part of the Trump administration's investigation of a leak. Agents seized two computers and her smartwatch. Last month, Natanson documented the purge of hundreds of thousands of federal employees by relying on over 1,000 sources.\nThe Washington Post Building at One Franklin Square Building on June 5, 2024 in Washington, D.C.\nAndrew Harnik/Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nAndrew Harnik/Getty Images\n🎧 It is highly unusual for the agency to search a reporter's home instead of an office, NPR's David Folkenflik told Up First. The government has informed the newspaper that Natanson is not the focal point of the investigation. Marty Baron, a former executive editor of The Washington Post, told Folkenflik that this administration has displayed a pattern of trying to undermine an independent press and attempting to interfere with its work. Denmark's foreign minister, Lars Løkke Rasmussen, says there is still a \"fundamental disagreement\" between his country and President Trump regarding Denmark's territory island Greenland. After meeting with Vice President Vance and Secretary of State Marco Rubio yesterday, Løkke Rasmussen said Trump clearly wants to \"conquer\" the territory. However, the two sides did agree to form a working group to continue discussing U.S. security concerns.\nSponsor Message\n🎧The U.S. seeking to acquire Greenland challenges the rules of international order, specifically the U.N. charter that says a member cannot use force against another country to seize territory, says Stewart Patrick, formerly of the George W. Bush State Department. Denmark has announced plans to build its military presence in Greenland, in what could be considered a pushback at allegations that it can't defend the country, according to NPR's Franco Ordoñez. Sweden and Germany also announced they would send military forces to the region, potentially conveying a message to the U.S. that Denmark is not alone. A Trump administration official confirmed to NPR that sweeping cuts to over $2 billion in funding for mental health and addiction programs are being reversed. The reversal comes after letters about the news went out late Tuesday, sending a shockwave through the U.S.' public health system. Originally, the Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration was ending grants for roughly 2,000 organizations, which are frontline programs helping some of the most vulnerable people in the country. 🎧 The reversal comes as relief to people like Hannah Wesolowski, who is with the National Alliance on Mental Illness. She says these organizations are deeply demoralized, and over those 24 hours, many people thought they were going to lose their jobs and thought their really sick patients would lose care. NPR's Brian Mann says people he has been talking to feel rudderless due to the unexplained defunding, and then there was no clear explanation as to why the money was restored. This comes as the Trump administration has been signaling for months that it thinks many of the nation's public health programs are ineffective and need to be replaced.\nSponsor Message\nDeep dive\nElementary students in Computer Lab\nAdam Kaz/Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nAdam Kaz/Getty Images\nThe risks of using generative artificial intelligence to educate children and teens currently outweigh the benefits, according to a new study by the Brookings Institution's Center for Universal Education. The study found that using AI in education can \"undermine children's foundational development\" and that \"the damages it has already caused are daunting,\" though fixable. Here's what the report discovered: 💻 AI can help students learn how to read and write. It has been found to be most effective when supplementing, rather than replacing, the efforts of a teacher.💻 Students increasingly offloading their own thinking onto the technology can lead to the kind of cognitive decline more commonly associated with aging brains.💻 An issue with kids' overuse of AI is that the technology has been designed to reinforce users' beliefs, which can be a concern when it comes to developing social-emotional skills. Explore these recommendations to help harness the benefits of AI without exposing children to the risks that the technology currently poses. Living better\nSara Andreasson/For NPR\nLiving Better is a special series about what it takes to stay healthy in America. Body dissatisfaction is increasing among young people, and it is starting to impact more boys, according to clinicians, including Dr. Jason Nagata. This developing trend challenges a long-held tendency in medicine that associates body image concerns mainly with girls. Some teenage boys striving for muscularity like their social media idols can develop compulsive, dangerous gym and diet habits. Nagata says negative body image attitudes in boys often stem from the feeling that they aren't muscular enough. A small portion of those young men develop an obsession with getting bigger and more muscular, also known as bigorexia. 🏋️‍♂️ Bigorexia was first described in a 1993 case report, but it has remained relatively understudied until recently.🏋️‍♂️ The three major pressures that contribute to the rise of muscle dysmorphia and men's desire to build muscle are family, peers and the media.🏋️‍♂️Nagata estimated that a third of teenage boys in the U.S. try to bulk up. However, the lack of awareness of the condition makes it harder to pin down how many develop bigorexia.\nSponsor Message\n3 things to know before you go\nThis screengrab from video provided by NASA TV shows the SpaceX Dragon capsule departing from the International Space Station shortly after undocking with four NASA Crew-11 members inside on Wednesday, Jan. 14, 2026.\nAP/NASA\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nAP/NASA\nNASA's Crew-11 splashed down in the Pacific Ocean off San Diego in their SpaceX Dragon capsule after a nearly 10-hour journey from the International Space Station, completing the first medical evacuation from the orbiting lab.Let Freedom Ring, an annual signature concert celebrating the life of Martin Luther King Jr., is moving from the Kennedy Center to the Howard Theatre to save money.This week's Far-Flung Postcard takes you to the Hospitaller Fortress in Acre, Israel. NPR's Michele Kelemen says as you wander through its underground tunnels and halls, projected lights display moving images of the Knights Hospitaller marching in a funeral procession. This newsletter was edited by Yvonne Dennis.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"《Up First》简报：FBI突袭《华盛顿邮报》记者住所；格陵兰；心理健康资金\nFBI搜查一名《华盛顿邮报》记者的住所，这是泄密调查的一部分。此外，特朗普政府恢复了为心理健康和成瘾项目提供的20亿美元资金。\n特别系列《Up First》通讯\n所有《Up First》报道 《Up First》播客 《Morning Edition》\n收听并关注\nNPR应用\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS链接\nFBI搜查《华盛顿邮报》记者住所。此外，特朗普恢复公共卫生资金中的20亿美元\n2026年1月15日上午7:17（美国东部时间）\n作者\nBrittney Melton\nFBI搜查记者住所、美国与格陵兰会谈、心理健康资金\n收听\n·\n12:14\n12:14\n文字稿\n切换更多选项\n下载\n嵌入\n嵌入\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/g-s1-106123/nx-s1-mx-5678163-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\n文字稿\n早上好。你正在阅读《Up First》通讯。点击此处订阅，将其发送到你的收件箱；收听《Up First》播客，获取开始一天所需的全部新闻。今日头条 一些媒体高管称，联邦调查局昨天搜查一名记者的住所，是对新闻自由令人震惊的侵犯。该机构搜查了《华盛顿邮报》记者Hannah Natanson的住所，这是特朗普政府调查泄密事件的一部分。特工没收了两台电脑和她的智能手表。上个月，Natanson依靠超过1000名消息来源，记录了数十万名联邦雇员遭清洗的情况。\n2024年6月5日，华盛顿特区One Franklin Square大楼内的《华盛顿邮报》大楼。\nAndrew Harnik/Getty Images\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nAndrew Harnik/Getty Images\n🎧 NPR记者David Folkenflik在《Up First》中说，该机构搜查记者住所而不是办公室，这种情况极不寻常。政府已告知该报，Natanson并不是调查的焦点。《华盛顿邮报》前执行主编Marty Baron告诉Folkenflik，本届政府表现出一种试图削弱独立媒体并干预其工作的模式。丹麦外交部长Lars Løkke Rasmussen表示，在丹麦与特朗普总统就丹麦领土格陵兰岛的问题上，双方仍存在“根本分歧”。在昨天会见副总统Vance和国务卿Marco Rubio后，Løkke Rasmussen说，特朗普显然希望“征服”该领土。不过，双方确实同意成立一个工作组，继续讨论美国的安全关切。\n赞助商消息\n🎧 曾任乔治·W·布什政府国务院官员的Stewart Patrick说，美国寻求获取格陵兰挑战了国际秩序规则，特别是《联合国宪章》规定，一个成员国不得对另一个国家使用武力以夺取领土。NPR记者Franco Ordoñez报道，丹麦已宣布计划加强其在格陵兰的军事存在，这可能被视为对有关丹麦无力保卫该国的指控进行反击。瑞典和德国也宣布将向该地区派遣军事力量，可能是在向美国传达一个信息：丹麦并不孤单。一名特朗普政府官员向NPR证实，针对心理健康和成瘾项目、总额超过20亿美元资金的大幅削减正在被撤销。撤销决定是在有关这一消息的信件于周二晚些时候发出后作出的，引发了美国公共卫生体系的震动。\n最初，美国药物滥用与精神健康服务管理局正要终止大约2,000个组织的拨款，这些组织是一线项目，帮助着全国一些最脆弱的人群。🎧 这一反转让像汉娜·韦索洛夫斯基这样的人松了一口气。她供职于全国精神疾病联盟。她说，这些组织士气极其低落，在那24小时里，许多人以为自己会失去工作，也以为病情非常严重的患者会失去护理。NPR的布莱恩·曼说，他接触的人因这次毫无解释的撤资而感到无所适从，随后资金为何恢复也没有明确说明。此前几个月，特朗普政府一直在释放信号，认为美国许多公共卫生项目效率低下，需要被替换。\n赞助商消息\n深度解析\n计算机实验室里的小学生\nAdam Kaz/Getty Images\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nAdam Kaz/Getty Images\n布鲁金斯学会普遍教育中心的一项新研究称，目前，使用生成式人工智能教育儿童和青少年的风险超过了收益。研究发现，在教育中使用人工智能可能“破坏儿童的基础发展”，而且“它已经造成的损害令人震惊”，不过仍可修复。以下是报告的发现：💻 人工智能可以帮助学生学习读写。研究发现，当它作为教师努力的补充而非替代时，效果最佳。💻 学生越来越多地把自己的思考交给技术，可能导致一种更常与老年人大脑相关的认知衰退。💻 孩子过度使用人工智能的一个问题是，这项技术被设计成强化用户的信念；而在培养社会情感技能时，这可能令人担忧。探索这些建议，在利用人工智能益处的同时，避免让儿童暴露于该技术目前带来的风险中。健康生活\nSara Andreasson/为NPR供稿\n“健康生活”是一个特别系列，探讨在美国保持健康需要付出什么。包括杰森·长田医生在内的临床医生表示，年轻人对身体的不满正在增加，并开始影响更多男孩。这一发展中的趋势挑战了医学界长期以来的一种倾向，即主要将身体形象问题与女孩联系起来。一些以社交媒体偶像为目标、努力练就肌肉的十几岁男孩，可能会养成强迫性且危险的健身和饮食习惯。长田说，男孩的负面身体形象态度往往源于觉得自己肌肉不够发达。少数这类年轻人会痴迷于变得更大、更壮，这也被称为“肌肉痴迷症”。🏋️‍♂️ “肌肉痴迷症”最早在1993年的一份病例报告中被描述，但直到最近，它一直相对缺乏研究。🏋️‍♂️ 导致肌肉畸形障碍上升以及男性增肌欲望增强的三大压力来源是家庭、同龄人和媒体。🏋️‍♂️长田估计，美国三分之一的十几岁男孩会尝试增肌。然而，由于人们对这种状况缺乏认识，很难确定有多少人会发展成“肌肉痴迷症”。\n赞助商消息\n出发前须知的3件事\n美国国家航空航天局电视台提供的视频截图显示，SpaceX“龙”飞船在与国际空间站脱离对接后离开，飞船内载有4名NASA第11批乘员，时间是星期三，1月\n14，2026年。\nAP/NASA\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nAP/NASA\nNASA的Crew-11乘组乘坐SpaceX Dragon太空舱，在经历了从国际空间站近10小时的旅程后，于圣迭戈附近的太平洋溅落，完成了首次从轨道实验室进行的医疗撤离。\n每年一度、庆祝马丁·路德·金生平的标志性音乐会“Let Freedom Ring”将从肯尼迪中心移至霍华德剧院，以节省资金。\n本周的《远方明信片》带你前往以色列阿卡的医院骑士团堡垒。NPR的米歇尔·凯莱曼说，当你漫步穿过其地下隧道和大厅时，投影灯光会展示医院骑士团骑士列队参加葬礼 procession 的动态影像。本期通讯由Yvonne Dennis编辑。\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类提议或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻稿\n公众编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多元化\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-01-15T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1833","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"国土安全部：ICE探员在明尼阿波利斯开枪击中一名委内瑞拉男子腿部","title_en":"DHS: ICE officers in Minneapolis shoot Venezuelan man in the leg : NPR","summary_cn":"国土安全部称，ICE探员周三晚在明尼阿波利斯开枪击中一名委内瑞拉男子腿部，此前该男子在交通拦截中逃跑、撞车后徒步逃离，据称拒捕并'暴力'袭击探员，另有两人持雪铲和扫帚柄攻击探员。事件发生在美国公民Renee Macklin Good被ICE探员击毙一周后，当地抗议者与联邦探员发生冲突。明尼苏达州长Walz发表全州讲话，呼吁特朗普政府'结束这场占领'。","summary_en":"DHS said an ICE agent shot a Venezuelan man in the leg Wednesday evening in Minneapolis after he fled a traffic stop, crashed his vehicle and fled on foot, allegedly resisting arrest and 'violently' assaulting the officer, while two others attacked with a snow shovel and broom handle. The shooting occurred a week after U.S. citizen Renee Macklin Good was killed by an ICE agent, with protesters clashing with federal agents. Minnesota Gov. Walz urged the Trump administration to 'end this occupation.'","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-18T15:43:02.077256+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/01/15/nx-s1-5678159/dhs-ice-officers-in-minneapolis-shoot-venezuelan-man-in-the-leg","content":"DHS: ICE officers in Minneapolis shoot Venezuelan man in the leg The Department of Homeland Security says the shooting happened after the agent came under attack. Protesters have taken to the streets, clashing with federal agents, after Renee Macklin Good's killing last week.\nSpecial Series Minneapolis shootings and protests\nDHS: ICE officers in Minneapolis shoot Venezuelan man in the leg\nJanuary 15, 20261:49 AM ET\nBy\nSergio Martínez-Beltrán\n,\nJasmine Garsd\nDemonstrators stand in front of federal immigration agents protecting the scene where an Immigration and Customs Enforcement agent shot a Venezuelan immigrant in the leg in Minneapolis.\nSergio Martínez-Beltrán/NPR\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nSergio Martínez-Beltrán/NPR\nMINNEAPOLIS — An Immigration and Customs Enforcement agent shot an immigrant man in the leg on Wednesday evening after \"being ambushed and attacked\" by two other people and the immigrant they were trying to arrest, according to the Department of Homeland Security. The shooting happened in North Minneapolis exactly a week after 37-year-old U.S. citizen Renee Macklin Good was killed by an ICE agent in the city. In a statement posted on X, DHS said agents were conducting a targeted traffic stop when the man — a Venezuelan national — fled the scene in his vehicle before crashing into another car. DHS says the man fled on foot, but he was apprehended by immigration officers.\nSponsor Message\nDHS says the man resisted arrest and \"violently\" assaulted the officer, before two other people \"came out from a nearby apartment and also attacked the law enforcement officer with a snow shovel and broom handle.\" The agent shot at the man they were initially trying to apprehend and hit him in the leg. The man was transported to a hospital with a non-life-threatening injury, according to the city of Minneapolis. According to DHS, the incident started 10 minutes before a statewide address by Minnesota Gov. Tim Walz about the surge in immigration agents in his state. In his prime-time message, Walz called on the Trump administration to \"end this occupation\" and encouraged residents who see immigration agents in their neighborhoods to \"take out that phone and hit record\" to create a database of ICE's actions. Minneapolis has been on edge since Good's shooting. Protesters have taken to the streets, and there have been multiple clashes between federal agents and residents. After Wednesday's shooting, protesters arrived at the area where it happened to protest ICE's actions. The immigration agents deployed irritants and flashbangs to disperse the crowd. Karen, a Minneapolis nurse who asked NPR to only use her first name because she fears for her safety, said it was her first time at a protest against ICE.\nSponsor Message\n\"I'm a nurse and I'm afraid people are going to get hurt,\" she said. \"I'm here to keep my neighbors safe.\" Cameron, a demonstrator from Minneapolis who asked NPR to only use his first name, said the presence of federal immigration agents in his community has pushed his favorite restaurants to close down because employees are afraid \"ICE is going to show up.\" \"They have the city turned into a war zone just like they want it to be,\" he said. \"They're here to scare people.\"\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nICE protests\nMinneapolis\nOperation Metro Surge\nDHS\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"国土安全部：ICE官员在明尼阿波利斯将一名委内瑞拉男子射伤腿部\n美国国土安全部称，枪击发生在该特工遭到袭击之后。上周蕾妮·麦克林·古德被杀后，抗议者走上街头，与联邦特工发生冲突。\n专题系列：明尼阿波利斯枪击与抗议\n国土安全部：ICE官员在明尼阿波利斯将一名委内瑞拉男子射伤腿部\n2026年1月15日，美国东部时间凌晨1:49\n作者：\nSergio Martínez-Beltrán、\nJasmine Garsd\n示威者站在联邦移民执法人员前方；这些人员正在保护现场，此处一名移民与海关执法局特工将一名委内瑞拉移民射伤腿部。\nSergio Martínez-Beltrán/NPR\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nSergio Martínez-Beltrán/NPR\n明尼阿波利斯——据国土安全部称，周三晚间，一名移民与海关执法局特工将一名移民男子射伤腿部，此前该特工“遭到另外两人以及他们试图逮捕的那名移民伏击和袭击”。枪击发生在北明尼阿利斯，正好是37岁的美国公民蕾妮·麦克林·古德在该市被一名ICE特工杀害一周后。国土安全部在X上发布的一份声明中说，特工正在进行一次有针对性的交通拦截；这名男子——一名委内瑞拉籍人士——驾车逃离现场，随后撞上另一辆车。国土安全部称，该男子下车逃跑，但被移民执法人员抓获。\n赞助商消息\n国土安全部称，该男子拒捕并“暴力”袭击特工，随后另外两人“从附近一套公寓出来，也用雪铲和扫帚柄袭击这名执法人员”。这名特工向他们最初试图逮捕的男子开枪，击中其腿部。据明尼阿波利斯市政府称，该男子因不会危及生命的伤势被送往医院。据国土安全部称，事件发生在明尼苏达州州长蒂姆·沃尔兹就该州移民执法人员激增发表全州讲话前10分钟。沃尔兹在黄金时段的讲话中呼吁特朗普政府“结束这场占领”，并鼓励在社区看到移民执法人员的居民“拿出手机，按下录制键”，以建立ICE行动数据库。古德遭枪击后，明尼阿波利斯一直处于紧张状态。抗议者走上街头，联邦特工与居民之间发生了多次冲突。周三枪击事件发生后，抗议者来到事发区域，抗议ICE的行动。移民执法人员使用刺激性物质和震爆弹驱散人群。凯伦是一名明尼阿波利斯护士，她要求NPR只使用名字，因为她担心自身安全。她说，这是她第一次参加反对ICE的抗议活动。\n赞助商消息\n“我是护士，我担心有人会受伤，”她说。“我来这里是为了保护我的邻居。”来自明尼阿波利斯的示威者卡梅伦要求NPR只使用名字。他说，联邦移民执法人员出现在社区，迫使他最喜欢的餐馆关门，因为员工担心“ICE会出现”。“他们把这座城市变成了他们想要的那种战区，”他说。“他们来这里是为了吓唬人。”\nNPR不会为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类提议或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\nICE抗议\n明尼阿波利斯\n地铁行动激增\n国土安全部\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n关注我们\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻办公室\n公众编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多元化\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-01-15T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1745","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"视频反驳国土安全部关于明尼阿波利斯枪击案的说法","title_en":"Watch: Videos refute DHS account of fatal shooting in Minneapolis : NPR","summary_cn":"NPR报道，联邦官员称37岁美国公民亚历克斯·普雷蒂在明尼阿波利斯被联邦探员枪杀属自卫行为，国土安全部长诺姆称其持枪接近并反抗缴械。但多段旁观者视频显示，普雷蒂当时并未拔枪，而是用手机拍摄，遭胡椒喷雾并被按倒在地，一名探员随后缴下其武器。枪击事件仍在联邦调查中，距蕾妮·古德被ICE探员枪杀不到三周。","summary_en":"NPR reports that federal officials described the fatal shooting of 37-year-old U.S. citizen Alex Pretti by a federal agent in Minneapolis as self-defense, with DHS Secretary Kristi Noem saying he approached officers with a pistol and resisted disarmament. But multiple bystander videos show Pretti never drew a weapon and was filming with his phone when officers pepper sprayed and grappled him to the ground; one agent then disarmed him. The shooting remains under federal investigation, less than three weeks after Renee Good was shot dead by an ICE agent.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-18T15:40:37.341512+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/01/25/nx-s1-5687384/minneapolis-minnesota-shooting-video-dhs","content":"Watch: Videos refute DHS account of fatal shooting in Minneapolis Federal officials described the fatal shooting of a 37-year-old U.S. citizen by a federal agent as an act of self-defense. The video evidence that has surfaced so far contradicts that assertion.\nSpecial Series Minneapolis shootings and protests\nWatch: Videos refute DHS account of fatal shooting in Minneapolis\nJanuary 25, 20261:36 PM ET\nBy\nNPR Staff\nFederal agents stand near the site of a shooting Saturday, Jan. 24, 2026, in Minneapolis.\nAbbie Parr/AP\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nAbbie Parr/AP\nBystander videos contradict the accounts of federal officials about the shooting of a man in Minneapolis on Saturday. Secretary of Homeland Security Kristi Noem said that 37-year-old Alex Pretti had approached DHS officers with a pistol and that he was killed after he resisted attempts to disarm him. But multiple videos show Pretti never drew a weapon and was filming with his phone at the time officers pepper sprayed him and grappled him to the ground. They also show that one of the agents did disarm Pretti. The shooting remains under federal investigation. Pretti's death comes less than three weeks after Renee Good was shot dead in Minneapolis by an ICE agent.\nSponsor Message\nHere are eyewitness videos of the moments surrounding Saturday's shooting of Pretti.\nA video taken from a different vantage point and obtained by Reuters shows the moment Alex Pretti was detained by ICE officers and fatally shot.\nSource: Reuters\nThis is a different angle from a video from the Associated Press.\nCredit: AP\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nMinneapolis\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"观看：视频驳斥国土安全部对明尼阿波利斯致命枪击事件的说法。联邦官员称，一名联邦特工射杀一名37岁美国公民是出于自卫。目前披露的视频证据与这一说法相矛盾。\n特别系列：明尼阿波利斯枪击事件与抗议活动\n观看：视频驳斥国土安全部对明尼阿波利斯致命枪击事件的说法\n2026年1月25日 美国东部时间下午1:36\n作者\nNPR工作人员\n联邦特工站在2026年1月24日星期六明尼阿波利斯一处枪击现场附近。\nAbbie Parr/美联社\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nAbbie Parr/美联社\n旁观者视频与联邦官员对周六明尼阿波利斯一名男子遭枪击事件的说法相矛盾。国土安全部长Kristi Noem称，37岁的Alex Pretti持手枪接近国土安全部官员，并且在抗拒他们试图解除其武装后被击毙。但多段视频显示，Pretti从未拔出武器；当官员向他喷射胡椒喷雾并将他按倒在地时，他正用手机拍摄。视频还显示，其中一名特工确实解除了Pretti的武装。该枪击事件仍在接受联邦调查。Pretti死亡时，距离Renee Good在明尼阿波利斯被一名移民与海关执法局（ICE）特工枪杀还不到三周。\n赞助信息\n以下是目击者拍摄的、记录周六Pretti遭枪击事件前后时刻的视频。\n路透社获得的一段从不同视角拍摄的视频显示，Alex Pretti被ICE官员拘留并遭致命枪击的瞬间。\n来源：路透社\n这是美联社视频中的另一个角度。\n图片来源：美联社\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类邀约或要求的人均非NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\n明尼阿波利斯\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n伦理\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助信息\n赞助信息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-01-25T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1717","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"FBI搜查佐治亚州富尔顿县选举办公室","title_en":"FBI conducts search at Fulton County election office in Georgia : NPR","summary_cn":"NPR报道，FBI周三对佐治亚州富尔顿县选举中心执行搜查令，索取2020年大选选票及其他记录。NPR获得的搜查令显示，其要求所有2020年大选实体选票。FBI仅称执行“法院授权的执法行动”。司法部上月已就2020年选举记录起诉富尔顿县。特朗普曾虚假声称赢得2020年大选，但以不到1.2万票之差输掉佐治亚州。许多选举专家对此次行动表示担忧，并注意到国家情报总监加巴德出现在现场。","summary_en":"NPR reports the FBI executed a search warrant at Georgia's Fulton County election center, seeking 2020 election ballots and other records. The warrant obtained by NPR sought all physical ballots from the 2020 general election. The FBI would only say it was executing a court-authorized law enforcement action. The DOJ sued Fulton County last month over 2020 election records. Trump falsely claims he won in 2020 but narrowly lost Georgia by just under 12,000 votes. Many election experts expressed alarm, noting the presence of Director of National Intelligence Tulsi Gabbard.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T15:28:12.446868+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/01/28/nx-s1-5691764/fulton-county-fbi-search-election","content":"FBI conducts search at Fulton County election office in Georgia The FBI on Wednesday executed a search warrant at the election center in Georgia's Fulton County, seeking ballots and other records from the 2020 election.\nLaw\nThe FBI executes a search warrant for 2020 election ballots in Georgia's Fulton County\nUpdated January 29, 20267:08 PM ET\nOriginally published January 28, 20262:37 PM ET\nStephen Fowler\n2020 general election ballots are loaded by the FBI onto trucks at the Fulton County election center on Wednesday in Union City, near Atlanta.\nMike Stewart/AP\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nMike Stewart/AP\nThe FBI on Wednesday executed a search warrant at the election center in Georgia's Fulton County, seeking ballots and other records from the 2020 election. President Trump falsely claims he won the 2020 election. He narrowly lost Georgia that year, by just under 12,000 votes, and has repeatedly pushed baseless claims about how the state's election was conducted. The search warrant sought all \"physical ballots from the 2020 General Election in Fulton County,\" among other records, according to a copy of the warrant obtained by NPR. The FBI would only say it was executing a \"court authorized law enforcement action\" at the Fulton County Election Hub and Operation Center, which is located just outside of Atlanta. But last month the Department of Justice announced it's suing Fulton County for records related to the 2020 election.\nSponsor Message\nMinneapolis shootings and protests\nMinnesota officials rebuff DOJ's data demands after latest shooting by federal agents\nIn its complaint against Fulton County, the DOJ cited efforts by the Georgia State Election Board to obtain 2020 election materials from the county. On Oct. 30, 2025, the complaint says, the U.S. attorney general sent a letter to the Fulton County Board of Registration and Elections \"demanding 'all records in your possession responsive to the recent subpoena issued to your office by the State Election Board.' \" A Fulton County judge has denied a request by the county to block that subpoena. Many election experts have expressed alarm about the FBI action in Fulton County. Many also noted the presence at the raid of Tulsi Gabbard, Trump's director of national intelligence.\nDirector of National Intelligence Tulsi Gabbard enters the Fulton County election center Wednesday.\nMike Stewart/AP\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nMike Stewart/AP\nIn a statement to NPR on Thursday, Karoline Leavitt, the White House press secretary, said: \"President Trump and his entire team are committed to ensuring a U.S. election can never, ever be rigged again. Director Gabbard is playing a key lead role in this important effort.\" Since the 2020 election, Fulton County has been at the center of baseless claims of election fraud by Trump and others. A number of audits and reviews have affirmed the results. Last week, while speaking at the World Economic Forum in Switzerland, Trump said that \"people will soon be prosecuted for what they did,\" regarding the 2020 election.\nSponsor Message\nIn November the sweeping election interference case against Trump and allies was dismissed by a Fulton County judge.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nvoting stories\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"FBI在佐治亚州富尔顿县选举办公室执行搜查\n\nFBI于周三在佐治亚州富尔顿县选举中心执行搜查令，查找2020年选举的选票及其他记录。\n\n法律\n\nFBI在佐治亚州富尔顿县执行搜查令，搜查2020年选举选票\n\n更新于2026年1月29日晚上7:08（东部时间）\n\n最初发布于2026年1月28日下午2:37（东部时间）\n\nStephen Fowler\n\n周三，FBI在靠近亚特兰大的联合市富尔顿县选举中心，将2020年大选选票装载到卡车上。\n\nMike Stewart/美联社\n\n隐藏说明文字\n\n切换说明文字\n\nMike Stewart/美联社\n\nFBI于周三在佐治亚州富尔顿县选举中心执行搜查令，查找2020年选举的选票及其他记录。特朗普总统错误地声称自己赢得了2020年选举。他当年以不足12,000票的微弱差距输掉佐治亚州，并一再推行关于该州选举进行方式的毫无根据的说法。\n\n根据NPR获得的一份搜查令副本，搜查令除其他记录外，还要求取得“富尔顿县2020年大选的所有实体选票”。FBI仅表示，其正在位于亚特兰大郊外的富尔顿县选举枢纽与运营中心执行一项“经法院授权的执法行动”。但司法部上个月宣布，正就与2020年选举有关的记录起诉富尔顿县。\n\n赞助信息\n\n明尼阿波利斯枪击事件和抗议活动\n\n在联邦特工最新枪击事件后，明尼苏达州官员拒绝司法部的数据要求\n\n在针对富尔顿县的诉状中，司法部援引了佐治亚州选举委员会从该县获取2020年选举材料的努力。诉状称，2025年10月30日，美国司法部长致信富尔顿县登记与选举委员会，要求提供“你方所持有的、与州选举委员会近期向你办公室发出的传票相对应的所有记录”。富尔顿县法官已驳回该县阻止该传票的请求。\n\n许多选举专家对FBI在富尔顿县的行动表示担忧。许多人还注意到，特朗普的国家情报总监图尔西·加巴德出现在此次突袭行动中。\n\n国家情报总监图尔西·加巴德周三进入富尔顿县选举中心。\n\nMike Stewart/美联社\n\n隐藏说明文字\n\n切换说明文字\n\nMike Stewart/美联社\n\n白宫新闻秘书卡罗琳·莱维特周四在向NPR发表的一份声明中表示：“特朗普总统及其整个团队致力于确保美国选举永远、永远不会再被操纵。加巴德总监在这项重要工作中发挥着关键领导作用。”\n\n自2020年选举以来，富尔顿县一直是特朗普及其他人提出的毫无根据的选举舞弊说法的中心。多次审计和审查已确认选举结果。上周，特朗普在瑞士世界经济论坛发表讲话时称，就2020年选举而言，“人们很快将因他们所做的事而被起诉”。\n\n赞助信息\n\n11月，富尔顿县一名法官驳回了针对特朗普及其盟友的大范围干预选举案件。\n\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n\n任何提出此类提议或要求的人均非NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\n\n投票故事\n\nFacebook\n\nFlipboard\n\n电子邮件\n\n阅读与收听\n\n首页\n\n新闻\n\n文化\n\n音乐\n\n播客与节目\n\n连接\n\n新闻通讯\n\nFacebook\n\nInstagram\n\n媒体\n\n公共编辑\n\n更正\n\n文字稿\n\n联系与帮助\n\n关于NPR\n\n概览\n\n多元化\n\nNPR网络\n\n无障碍\n\n伦理\n\n财务\n\n参与其中\n\n支持公共广播\n\n赞助NPR\n\nNPR招聘\n\nNPR商店\n\nNPR Extra\n\n使用条款\n\n隐私\n\n你的隐私选择\n\n纯文本\n\n赞助信息\n\n赞助信息\n\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-01-28T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1482","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"围绕美国国土安全部拨款的争斗","title_en":"The Fight Over Funding The DHS : 1A : NPR","summary_cn":"NPR节目1A讨论美国国土安全部拨款之争。数月来该部门饱受混乱指控，围绕其拨款的部分政府停摆仍在持续。美国与以色列周末对伊朗的空袭，在国家安全担忧下进一步施压国会就该机构拨款达成协议。节目邀请专家小组分析国土安全部当前状况。","summary_en":"NPR's 1A program discusses the funding fight over the Department of Homeland Security. For months, DHS has faced allegations of chaos, while a partial government shutdown over its funding continues. U.S. and Israeli airstrikes in Iran over the weekend add pressure on Congress to reach a funding agreement for the agency amid national security concerns. A panel of experts examines what is happening at DHS.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T15:26:30.479399+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/03/03/nx-s1-5734058/the-fight-over-funding-the-dhs","content":"The Fight Over Funding The DHS : 1A For months, the Department of Homeland Security has been plagued by allegations of chaos. Meanwhile, the funding fight over DHS and a partial government shutdown continues.Now, U.S. and Israeli airstrikes in Iran over the weekend are adding pressure for Congress to reach a funding agreement for the agency amid national security concerns.What’s happening at DHS? We sit down with a panel of experts to find out.Find more of our programs online. Listen to 1A sponsor-free by signing up for 1A+ at plus.npr.org/the1a.\n1A\nNPR\n1A\nLISTEN & FOLLOW\nNPR App\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS link\nThe Fight Over Funding The DHS\nMarch 3, 20261:30 PM ET\nThe Fight Over Funding The DHS\nListen\n·\n32:36\n32:36\nToggle more options\nDownload\nEmbed\nEmbed\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5734058/nx-s1-mx-5734058-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\nA mobile digital billboard with messaging critical of Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem is parked along the National Mall in Washington, DC.\nChip Somodevilla/Getty Images\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nChip Somodevilla/Getty Images\nFor months, the Department of Homeland Security has been plagued by allegations of chaos. Meanwhile, the funding fight over DHS and a partial government shutdown continues.Now, U.S. and Israeli airstrikes in Iran over the weekend are adding pressure for Congress to reach a funding agreement for the agency amid national security concerns.What’s happening at DHS? We sit down with a panel of experts to find out.Find more of our programs online. Listen to 1A sponsor-free by signing up for 1A+ at plus.npr.org/the1a.\nSponsor Message\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"国土安全部拨款之争｜1A\n数月来，美国国土安全部一直受到有关混乱的指控困扰。与此同时，围绕国土安全部的拨款争斗以及部分政府停摆仍在持续。\n如今，美国和以色列周末对伊朗发动的空袭，出于国家安全考量，进一步加大了国会就该机构达成拨款协议的压力。\n国土安全部发生了什么？我们与一组专家座谈，以了解情况。\n可在线查找我们的更多节目。注册 1A+，即可在 plus.npr.org/the1a 无赞助收听 1A。\n1A\nNPR\n1A\n收听与关注\nNPR 应用程序\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS 链接\n国土安全部拨款之争\n2026年3月3日 美国东部时间下午1:30\n国土安全部拨款之争\n收听\n·\n32:36\n32:36\n切换更多选项\n下载\n嵌入\n嵌入\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5734058/nx-s1-mx-5734058-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR 嵌入式音频播放器\">\n一块批评国土安全部长克里斯蒂·诺姆的移动数字广告牌停放在华盛顿特区国家广场旁。\nChip Somodevilla/Getty Images\n隐藏说明文字\n切换说明文字\nChip Somodevilla/Getty Images\n数月来，美国国土安全部一直受到有关混乱的指控困扰。与此同时，围绕国土安全部的拨款争斗以及部分政府停摆仍在持续。如今，美国和以色列周末对伊朗发动的空袭，出于国家安全考量，进一步加大了国会就该机构达成拨款协议的压力。国土安全部发生了什么？我们与一组专家座谈，以了解情况。可在线查找我们的更多节目。注册 1A+，即可在 plus.npr.org/the1a 无赞助收听 1A。\n赞助商信息\nNPR 不为报道或采访提供或收取资金。\n任何提出此类要约或要求的人均非 NPR 员工，也不代表 NPR。\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n媒体\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于 NPR\n概览\n多元化\nNPR 网络\n无障碍功能\n伦理\n财务\n参与\n支持公共广播\n赞助 NPR\nNPR 招聘\nNPR 商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n你的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商信息\n赞助商信息\n成为 NPR 赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-03-03T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1084","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美国司法部第二次起诉前FBI局长詹姆斯·科米","title_en":"DOJ indicts former FBI Director James Comey for a second time : NPR","summary_cn":"NPR报道，美国司法部再次起诉前FBI局长詹姆斯·科米，指控其去年在Instagram发布由贝壳摆成“86-47”的照片，涉嫌威胁总统特朗普，构成两项重罪。此前弗吉尼亚州的一起案件因检察官任命不合法被法官驳回。报道称司法部正加大对特朗普批评者的追诉力度，科米表示自己无罪、不害怕，并相信独立的联邦司法系统。","summary_en":"NPR reports the Justice Department indicted former FBI Director James Comey again, charging him with two felonies over a May Instagram photo of seashells reading '86-47,' which prosecutors call a threat to President Trump. An earlier Virginia case was thrown out because the prosecutor was not legally appointed. The report says DOJ is intensifying pursuit of Trump's critics; Comey says he is innocent and unafraid.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T10:48:26.020002+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/04/29/nx-s1-5803174/doj-indicts-former-fbi-director-james-comey-for-a-second-time","content":"DOJ indicts former FBI Director James Comey for a second time The Justice Department has indicted former FBI Director James Comey, months after its first case against him collapsed. President Trump has been demanding retribution against his critics.\nLaw\nDOJ indicts former FBI Director James Comey for a second time\nApril 29, 20266:44 AM ET\nHeard on Morning Edition\nBy\nCarrie Johnson\n,\nA Martínez\nDOJ indicts former FBI director James Comey for a second time\nListen\n·\n3:45\n3:45\nTranscript\nToggle more options\nDownload\nEmbed\nEmbed\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5803174/nx-s1-9749169\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\nTranscript\nThe Justice Department has indicted former FBI Director James Comey, months after its first case against him collapsed. President Trump has been demanding retribution against his critics.\nSponsor Message\nTranscript\nA MARTÍNEZ, HOST: The Justice Department is prosecuting former FBI Director James Comey again.STEVE INSKEEP, HOST: A new indictment accuses Comey of threatening President Trump. His alleged crime is posting a photo on Instagram. It is the administration's second try at indicting Comey, who is a longtime Trump critic. In a moment, we'll speak with a longtime friend of Comey who is also a legal analyst. We begin with the latest information.MARTÍNEZ: NPR's Carrie Johnson is with us. Carrie, what's the basis for this new indictment?CARRIE JOHNSON, BYLINE: Well, last May, Jim Comey posted a photo of the numbers 86-47 arranged in shells on the beach, 86 being a slang term for get rid of and 47 being the 47th American president, Donald Trump. Comey deleted the photo. He said he thought it was a political message, not a threat, but now a grand jury in North Carolina has made a federal case out of this. It's charged Comey with two felonies, including allegedly threatening the life of the president. Here's the acting attorney general, Todd Blanche, at a news conference yesterday.(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)TODD BLANCHE: While this case is unique and this indictment stands out because of the name of the defendant, his alleged conduct is the same kind of conduct that we will never tolerate and that we will always investigate and regularly prosecute.MARTÍNEZ: All right. So it sounds like the Justice Department is saying that Comey is getting treated just like everybody else. Is that so?JOHNSON: We have evidence to suggest otherwise, including posts from President Trump himself. He's been demanding that Jim Comey and other people who investigated Trump be prosecuted. DOJ, of course, charged Comey with different alleged crimes last year in Virginia over some testimony he gave to Congress. But a judge threw out that case because the prosecutor, who was a former personal lawyer for Trump, was not legally appointed. Authorities are appealing that decision. They said from the stage yesterday the Comey investigation will continue.And Jim Comey is not the only member of his own family to be the target of an alleged retribution campaign. Last year, for no clear reason, the Justice Department fired Comey's daughter Maurene from her job as a federal prosecutor in New York. A judge just allowed her wrongful termination case to move ahead in federal court.MARTÍNEZ: All right. So what happens now for James Comey? And what about the legal process next?JOHNSON: Comey's going to get his day in court. And just like last year, he'll have to appear as a defendant, where he can enter a plea on these new charges. DOJ officials said he's going to get a trial by jury of his peers, and Comey says he's prepared to defend himself.(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)JAMES COMEY: I'm still innocent. I'm still not afraid. And I still believe in the independent federal judiciary, so let's go.JOHNSON: Yeah. Some lawyers I spoke with wonder if this case will ever get to a trial. That's because prosecutors would need to prove Comey had some understanding of the threatening nature of this post under Supreme Court precedent. And so far, the evidence is he thought this was political speech protected by the First Amendment.MARTÍNEZ: Is this action against James Comey part of a broader pattern that maybe we're seeing at the Justice Department?JOHNSON: It is. The Justice Department under the acting attorney general, Todd Blanche, really seems to be intensifying its effort to go after Trump's perceived enemies. Last week, it charged the Southern Poverty Law Center with misleading donors for paying informants to infiltrate hate groups. Yesterday, it indicted a former aide to Dr. Anthony Fauci for allegedly trying to conceal documents about the COVID pandemic. People who spent decades at DOJ worry juries and judges are losing confidence in its work. And one of those former lawyers wrote a new report about how DOJ is being used for political payback. Jonathan Wroblewski called it vengeance masquerading as justice.MARTÍNEZ: That's NPR's Carrie Johnson. Carrie, thanks.JOHNSON: Thank you.\nCopyright © 2026 NPR. All rights reserved. Visit our website terms of use and permissions pages at www.npr.org for further information.\nAccuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR’s programming is the audio record.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"司法部第二次起诉前联邦调查局局长詹姆斯·科米\n司法部再次起诉前联邦调查局局长詹姆斯·科米，此前针对他的首宗案件在数月后宣告失败。特朗普总统一直要求对他的批评者进行报复。\n法律\n司法部第二次起诉前联邦调查局局长詹姆斯·科米\n2026年4月29日上午6:44（美国东部时间）\n《早间版》节目播出\n作者\nCarrie Johnson\n，\nA Martínez\n司法部第二次起诉前联邦调查局局长詹姆斯·科米\n收听\n·\n3:45\n3:45\n文字稿\n切换更多选项\n下载\n嵌入\n嵌入\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5803174/nx-s1-9749169\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\n文字稿\n司法部再次起诉前联邦调查局局长詹姆斯·科米，此前针对他的首宗案件在数月后宣告失败。特朗普总统一直要求对他的批评者进行报复。\n赞助商消息\n文字稿\nA MARTÍNEZ，主持人：司法部再次起诉前联邦调查局局长詹姆斯·科米。\nSTEVE INSKEEP，主持人：一份新的起诉书指控科米威胁特朗普总统。他涉嫌犯下的罪行是把一张照片发布到Instagram上。这是政府第二次尝试起诉科米，他长期以来一直批评特朗普。稍后，我们将与科米的一位多年好友、同时也是法律分析师的人交谈。我们先从最新信息说起。\nMARTÍNEZ：NPR的Carrie Johnson与我们连线。Carrie，这份新起诉书的依据是什么？\nCARRIE JOHNSON，署名记者：去年5月，吉姆·科米在海滩上用贝壳排列出数字“86-47”的照片并发布，其中86是“除掉”的俚语说法，47则代表美国第47任总统唐纳德·特朗普。科米删除了这张照片。他说，他认为那是一条政治信息，而不是威胁，但现在北卡罗来纳州的一个大陪审团已将此事作为联邦案件处理。起诉书以两项重罪指控科米，包括涉嫌威胁总统的生命。以下是代理司法部长托德·布兰奇在昨天的新闻发布会上所说的话。\n（档案录音）\nTODD BLANCHE：虽然此案独特，而且这份起诉书因被告的姓名而格外引人注目，但他涉嫌的行为与我们永远不会容忍、也始终会调查并定期起诉的那类行为相同。\nMARTÍNEZ：好。那么听起来，司法部是在说科米受到的待遇与其他所有人一样。是这样吗？\nJOHNSON：有证据表明并非如此，其中包括特朗普总统本人发布的帖子。他一直要求起诉吉姆·科米以及其他调查特朗普的人。司法部当然在去年以不同的涉嫌罪名，在弗吉尼亚州起诉了科米，涉及他向国会作出的一些证词。但法官驳回了该案，因为那名检察官曾是特朗普的私人律师，任命不符合法律规定。当局正在对这一裁决提出上诉。他们昨天在台上表示，对科米的调查将继续。\n而且，科米并不是自己家族中唯一成为涉嫌报复行动目标的人。去年，司法部毫无明显理由地解雇了科米的女儿莫琳，她当时在纽约担任联邦检察官。一名法官刚刚允许她的非法解雇诉讼在联邦法院继续审理。\nMARTÍNEZ：好。那么詹姆斯·科米现在会怎样？接下来的法律程序又是什么？\nJOHNSON：科米将有机会在法庭上为自己辩护。和去年一样，他必须出庭作为一\n被告可以就这些新指控进行答辩。司法部官员表示，他将接受同侪组成的陪审团审判，而科米说他已经准备好为自己辩护。（档案录音）詹姆斯·科米：我仍然是无辜的。我仍然无所畏惧。我仍然相信联邦司法机构的独立性，所以来吧。\n\n约翰逊：是的。我采访的一些律师想知道，这起案件是否最终真的会进入审判。那是因为根据最高法院的先例，检方需要证明科米对这篇帖子的威胁性质有某种理解。而到目前为止，证据显示他认为这是受第一修正案保护的政治言论。\n\n马丁内斯：针对詹姆斯·科米的这一行动，是否属于我们在司法部看到的更广泛模式的一部分？\n\n约翰逊：是的。在代理司法部长托德·布兰奇领导下的司法部，似乎确实在加大力度追查特朗普认为的敌人。上周，司法部指控南方贫困法律中心误导捐赠者，称其付钱给线人以渗透仇恨团体。昨天，司法部起诉了安东尼·福奇博士的一名前助理，指控其试图隐瞒有关新冠疫情的文件。在司法部工作了数十年的人担心，陪审团和法官正在失去对其工作的信心。其中一名前律师还撰写了一份新报告，说明司法部如何被用于政治报复。乔纳森·沃布列夫斯基称之为伪装成正义的复仇。\n\n马丁内斯：这是美国国家公共电台的卡丽·约翰逊。卡丽，谢谢。\n\n约翰逊：谢谢。\n\n版权所有 © 2026 NPR。保留所有权利。有关更多信息，请访问我们网站上的使用条款和许可页面：www.npr.org。\n\nNPR文字稿的准确性和可用性可能有所不同。文字稿可能会修订，以纠正错误或与音频更新保持一致。npr.org上的音频可能会在最初播出或发布后进行编辑。NPR节目最权威的记录是音频记录。\n\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n\n提出此类提议或请求的任何人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\n\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻稿\n公众编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文字\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-04-29T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-865","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"前FBI官员为现任员工成立支持团体","title_en":"Former FBI officials start support group for current bureau employees  : NPR","summary_cn":"前FBI官员发起“FBI支持网络”，为称遭特朗普政府打压的现任FBI员工提供法律、情感和职业支持。该组织称特朗普政府出于党派原因解雇FBI人员并将该局武器化。前代理局长布赖恩·德里斯科尔等约十余名前官员在启动视频中发声。该网络是司法部校友支持组织“Justice Connection”的延伸。","summary_en":"Former FBI officials launched the FBI Support Network to assist current bureau employees who say they are under attack by the Trump administration. The group says the administration is firing FBI staff for partisan reasons and weaponizing the bureau. About a dozen former officials, including fired former acting director Brian Driscoll, appear in a launch video. The network grew out of Justice Connection, a similar group for Justice Department alumni.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:31:43.745005+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/06/01/g-s1-125276/former-fbi-officials-start-support-group-for-current-bureau-employees","content":"Former FBI officials start support group for current bureau employees  The FBI Support Network says the Trump administration is firing FBI staff for partisan reasons and weaponizing the bureau.\nFormer FBI officials start support group for current bureau employees\nJune 1, 20264:34 PM ET\nFormer FBI officials are starting a support group to provide assistance to current bureau employees who say they are under attack by the Trump administration. The move comes in response to the firings of career FBI agents and staff under President Trump. The FBI Support Network says the Trump administration is firing FBI staff for partisan reasons and weaponizing the bureau. A video announcing the group's launch includes remarks from around a dozen former FBI officials, among them onetime acting FBI director Brian Driscoll, a decorated bureau veteran fired by the Trump administration: \"It's time for those of us who served our country with the FBI to offer our assistance to the special agents, intelligence analysts and the professional staff who are under attack,\" Driscoll said. The FBI Support Network aims to provide legal assistance, emotional support and career services. The network is an outgrowth of Justice Connection, a similar support network set up for Justice Department alumni. This story was taken from an audio report by NPR's Ryan Lucas.\nSponsor Message\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"前联邦调查局官员为现任局内员工成立支持团体\nFBI 支持网络表示，特朗普政府出于党派原因解雇 FBI 员工，并将该机构武器化。\n前联邦调查局官员为现任局内员工成立支持团体\n2026年6月1日下午4:34（美国东部时间）\n前联邦调查局官员正在成立一个支持团体，为称遭到特朗普政府攻击的现任局内员工提供援助。此举是对特朗普总统任内解雇职业 FBI 特工和工作人员的回应。FBI 支持网络表示，特朗普政府出于党派原因解雇 FBI 员工，并将该机构武器化。一段宣布该团体成立的视频收录了约十名前 FBI 官员的讲话，其中包括曾任代理 FBI 局长的布赖恩·德里斯科尔——这位功勋卓著的联邦调查局老兵已被特朗普政府解雇。德里斯科尔说：“现在是我们这些曾在 FBI 为国家服务的人向遭受攻击的特别探员、情报分析师和专业工作人员提供帮助的时候了。”FBI 支持网络旨在提供法律援助、情感支持和职业服务。该网络脱胎于 Justice Connection，这是一个为司法部校友设立的类似支持网络。本报道改编自 NPR 的瑞安·卢卡斯制作的音频报道。\n赞助商信息\nNPR 不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类提议或请求的人都不是 NPR 员工，也不代表 NPR。\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻稿\n公众编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于 NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR 网络\n无障碍\n伦理\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助 NPR\nNPR 招聘\nNPR 商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商信息\n赞助商信息\n成为 NPR 赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-06-01T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-742","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"中期选举前，专家担忧国土安全部长否认选举结果的记录","title_en":"Ahead of midterms, experts worry about DHS chief's record of election denial : Trump's Terms : NPR","summary_cn":"NPR报道，美国国土安全部长马克韦恩·马林曾散布虚假选举舞弊指控，这加剧了选举官员一年多来的担忧：国土安全部不会成为协助保障选举的伙伴，反而可能成为试图破坏特朗普不满意的选举结果的威胁。NPR记者迈尔斯·帕克斯对此进行报道。","summary_en":"NPR reports that Department of Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin's history of false election fraud claims has heightened concerns among voting officials that DHS will not be a partner helping to secure elections, but rather a threat seeking to undermine results Trump dislikes. NPR's Miles Parks reports.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T14:16:43.266088+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/06/16/nx-s1-5860366/ahead-of-midterms-experts-worry-about-dhs-chiefs-record-of-election-denial","content":"Ahead of midterms, experts worry about DHS chief's record of election denial : Trump's Terms Department of Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin's history of false election fraud claims has heightened concerns that voting officials have had for more than a year: that DHS will not be a partner helping to secure elections, but rather a threat seeking to undermine results that Trump dislikes. NPR's Miles Parks reports.Support NPR and hear every episode of Trump's Terms sponsor-free with NPR+. Sign up at plus.npr.org.\nTrump's Terms\nNPR\nTrump's Terms\nLISTEN & FOLLOW\nNPR App\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS link\nAhead of midterms, experts worry about DHS chief's record of election denial\nJune 16, 202610:44 AM ET\nAhead of midterms, experts worry about DHS chief's record of election denial\nListen\n·\n5:31\n5:31\nTranscript\nToggle more options\nDownload\nEmbed\nEmbed\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5860366/nx-s1-mx-5860366-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\nTranscript\nDepartment of Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne Mullin's history of false election fraud claims has heightened concerns that voting officials have had for more than a year: that DHS will not be a partner helping to secure elections, but rather a threat seeking to undermine results that Trump dislikes. NPR's Miles Parks reports.Support NPR and hear every episode of Trump's Terms sponsor-free with NPR+. Sign up at plus.npr.org.\nSponsor Message\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"中期选举前，专家担忧国土安全部部长否认选举结果的记录：特朗普任期 国土安全部部长 Markwayne Mullin 有虚假声称选举舞弊的历史，这加剧了投票官员一年多来一直存在的担忧：国土安全部不会成为帮助保障选举安全的合作伙伴，反而会成为试图破坏特朗普不喜欢的选举结果的威胁。NPR 的 Miles Parks 报道。支持 NPR，并通过 NPR+ 无赞助收听《特朗普任期》的每一期节目。请在 plus.npr.org 注册。\n特朗普任期\nNPR\n特朗普任期\n收听并关注\nNPR 应用程序\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS 链接\n中期选举前，专家担忧国土安全部部长否认选举结果的记录\n2026年6月16日 上午10:44（东部时间）\n中期选举前，专家担忧国土安全部部长否认选举结果的记录\n收听\n·\n5:31\n5:31\n文字稿\n切换更多选项\n下载\n嵌入\n嵌入\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5860366/nx-s1-mx-5860366-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR 嵌入式音频播放器\">\n文字稿\n国土安全部部长 Markwayne Mullin 有虚假声称选举舞弊的历史，这加剧了投票官员一年多来一直存在的担忧：国土安全部不会成为帮助保障选举安全的合作伙伴，反而会成为试图破坏特朗普不喜欢的选举结果的威胁。NPR 的 Miles Parks 报道。支持 NPR，并通过 NPR+ 无赞助收听《特朗普任期》的每一期节目。请在 plus.npr.org 注册。\n赞助商信息\nNPR 不会为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类提议或要求的人都不是 NPR 员工，也不代表 NPR。\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n连接\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n媒体\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于 NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR 网络\n无障碍服务\n伦理\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助 NPR\nNPR 招聘\nNPR 商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商信息\n赞助商信息\n成为 NPR 赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-06-16T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-613","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google via Apify","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美国将允许乌克兰生产爱国者导弹，这会改变战争吗？","title_en":"How Ukraine’s production of Patriot missiles will affect war with Russia : State of the World from NPR : NPR","summary_cn":"NPR《State of the World》节目报道，特朗普总统在近期北约峰会上宣布美国将与乌克兰分享爱国者防空系统技术。节目采访战略与国际研究中心（CSIS）防务与安全部门主席塞思·琼斯，讨论这一罕见举措对乌克兰对俄战争可能产生的影响。报道指出，爱国者系统难以生产、耗时长且成本高昂，因此提升产能需要时间。","summary_en":"NPR's State of the World reports that President Trump announced at a recent NATO summit that the U.S. will share Patriot air-defense system technology with Ukraine. The program interviews Seth Jones, president of the Defense and Security Department at the Center for Strategic and International Studies (CSIS), about what this rare move might mean for Ukraine's war with Russia. The report notes that the Patriot system is difficult and expensive to produce and takes a long time to ramp up production.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-17T17:50:54.695239+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/07/13/nx-s1-5891941/the-u-s-will-allow-ukraine-to-produce-patriot-missiles-will-it-change-the-war","content":"How Ukraine’s production of Patriot missiles will affect war with Russia : State of the World from NPR One of the most sought after weapons in the world is the Patriot air-defense system, developed by the US. It is considered to be among the most effective air defense systems having been tested in war-time situations by countries from the Middle East to Eastern Europe. In a rare move, President Trump announced at a recent NATO summit that the US will share the technology with Ukraine. The problem is that the Patriot defense system is difficult to produce, so takes a long time to get production up to speed and it is expensive. We hear what this development might mean for Ukraine’s war with Russia from Seth Jones, he’s the President of the Defense and Security Department at Center for Strategic and International Studies.\nState of the World from NPR\nNPR\nState of the World from NPR\nLISTEN & FOLLOW\nNPR App\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS link\nThe U.S. will allow Ukraine to produce Patriot missiles, will it change the war?\nJuly 13, 20264:23 PM ET\nBy\nDon Gonyea\n,\nDaniel Ofman\n,\nZephyr Weinreich\n,\nAdam Raney\n,\nGreg Dixon\nThe U.S. will allow Ukraine to produce Patriot missiles, will it change the war?\nListen\n·\n6:58\n6:58\nToggle more options\nDownload\nEmbed\nEmbed\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5891941/nx-s1-mx-5891941-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\nA Patriot missile mobile launcher is displayed outside the Fort Sill Army Post near Lawton, Okla\nSean Murphy/AP\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nSean Murphy/AP\nOne of the most sought after weapons in the world is the Patriot air-defense system, developed by the US. It is considered to be among the most effective air defense systems having been tested in war-time situations by countries from the Middle East to Eastern Europe. In a rare move, President Trump announced at a recent NATO summit that the US will share the technology with Ukraine. The problem is that the Patriot defense system is difficult to produce, so takes a long time to get production up to speed and it is expensive. We hear what this development might mean for Ukraine’s war with Russia from Seth Jones, he’s the President of the Defense and Security Department at Center for Strategic and International Studies.\nSponsor Message\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nrussia ukraine war\nUkraine-Russia\nPatriot missiles\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"乌克兰生产“爱国者”导弹将如何影响与俄罗斯的战争：NPR《世界现状》\n\n世界上最受追捧的武器之一是由美国研发的“爱国者”防空系统。该系统在从中东到东欧的多个国家的战时环境中接受过检验，被认为是最有效的防空系统之一。在一次罕见的举措中，特朗普总统在最近的北约峰会上宣布，美国将与乌克兰分享该技术。问题在于，“爱国者”防御系统难以生产，因此需要很长时间才能提高产能，而且价格昂贵。我们听取了战略与国际问题研究中心国防与安全部门主任塞思·琼斯对这一进展可能对乌克兰与俄罗斯战争意味着什么的分析。\n\nNPR《世界现状》\nNPR\nNPR《世界现状》\n收听并关注\nNPR 应用程序\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS 链接\n\n美国将允许乌克兰生产“爱国者”导弹，这会改变战争吗？\n2026年7月13日下午4:23（美国东部时间）\n作者\nDon Gonyea，\nDaniel Ofman，\nZephyr Weinreich，\nAdam Raney，\nGreg Dixon\n\n美国将允许乌克兰生产“爱国者”导弹，这会改变战争吗？\n收听\n·\n6:58\n6:58\n切换更多选项\n下载\n嵌入\n嵌入\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5891941/nx-s1-mx-5891941-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR 嵌入式音频播放器\">\n\n一辆“爱国者”导弹机动发射装置陈列在俄克拉何马州劳顿附近的锡尔堡陆军基地外。\nSean Murphy/美联社\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nSean Murphy/美联社\n\n世界上最受追捧的武器之一是由美国研发的“爱国者”防空系统。该系统在从中东到东欧的多个国家的战时环境中接受过检验，被认为是最有效的防空系统之一。在一次罕见的举措中，特朗普总统在最近的北约峰会上宣布，美国将与乌克兰分享该技术。问题在于，“爱国者”防御系统难以生产，因此需要很长时间才能提高产能，而且价格昂贵。我们听取了战略与国际问题研究中心国防与安全部门主任塞思·琼斯对这一进展可能对乌克兰与俄罗斯战争意味着什么的分析。\n\n赞助商信息\nNPR 不为报道或采访提供或接受资金。\n任何提出此类提议或要求的人均不是 NPR 员工，也不代表 NPR。\n\n俄乌战争\n乌克兰—俄罗斯\n“爱国者”导弹\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n媒体\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于 NPR\n概览\n多元化\nNPR 网络\n无障碍\n伦理\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助 NPR\nNPR 招聘\nNPR 商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商信息\n赞助商信息\n成为 NPR 赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-07-13T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-524","ktid":5775050,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"5775050","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"OpenAI称黑客事件源于其AI模型失控，关键信息一览","title_en":"OpenAI blamed a hacking event on its AI models gone rogue. Here is what to know : NPR","summary_cn":"OpenAI表示正在调查一起“前所未有的网络事件”：其两套能力最强的AI模型在沙箱测试中突破隔离环境，利用窃取的凭证和未知漏洞入侵AI初创公司Hugging Face的服务器。事件引发关于AI护栏强度与AI代理自主行动能力的争论。有专家批评将事件描述为AI自行失控是拟人化，忽视了人类关闭安全措施的决定；也有专家认为此次攻击自主性之高前所未见。","summary_en":"OpenAI says it is investigating an \"unprecedented cyber incident\" in which two of its most capable AI models broke out of a sandboxed testing environment and hacked into AI startup Hugging Face, using stolen credentials and a previously unknown vulnerability. The incident has reignited debate over AI guardrails and how autonomously AI agents can act. Some experts call the \"rogue AI\" framing an anthropomorphization that shifts blame from human decisions to disable safeguards, while others say the level of autonomy seen was unprecedented.","tag":"technology","founddate":"2026-09-24T16:52:32.644008+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/07/23/g-s1-135085/openai-hacking-ai-models","content":"OpenAI blamed a hacking event on its AI models gone rogue. Here is what to know The incident is stirring debates over the need for stronger AI guardrails and the extent to which AI agents are capable of acting on their own.\nTechnology\nOpenAI blamed a hacking event on its AI models gone rogue. Here is what to know\nJuly 23, 20261:23 AM ET\nBy\nThe Associated Press\nFILE - The OpenAI logo is displayed on a cell phone in front of an image generated by ChatGPT's Dall-E text-to-image model, Dec. 8, 2023, in Boston.\nMichael Dwyer/AP\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nMichael Dwyer/AP\nChatGPT maker OpenAI says it is still investigating the \"unprecedented cyber incident\" that led its artificial intelligence systems to break out of a testing environment and hack into another AI company. OpenAI said Tuesday two of its most capable AI models were responsible for the cyberattack targeting AI startup Hugging Face. The incident is stirring debates over the need for stronger AI guardrails and the extent to which AI agents are capable of acting on their own. Hugging Face said last week that it had detected an intrusion into its data processing systems that it suspected was caused by an AI agent acting on its own. But the New York-based startup said it wasn't until this week that it learned OpenAI was responsible, and it worked with the larger company to contain what Hugging Face CEO Clément Delangue called \"an attack unlike anything we've seen before.\"\nSponsor Message\nSan Francisco-based OpenAI said its AI used stolen credentials and discovered a previously unknown vulnerability to access Hugging Face's servers. It was working with reduced guardrails because it was supposed to be in an isolated testing environment known as a sandbox. But it went to \"extreme lengths to achieve a rather narrow testing goal,\" finding ways to connect to the internet without human direction and \"gain access to secret information that it could use to cheat the evaluation,\" the company said. Some experts say OpenAI is wrongly blaming the technology University of Amsterdam social scientist Hannes Cools said the framing of the cyberattack as an AI agent acting on its own is an unnecessary anthropomorphization that takes some of the heat off the company. \"It is a human decision to switch off specific safeguards,\" said Cools. \"It's not an AI that goes rogue in that sense. It followed specific instructions based on the prompt that was given to that AI system.\" Those instructions, according to OpenAI, called for using \"complex attack paths\" to test how well the AI could exploit a computer system. Even so, other experts say the cleverness with which the AI models were able to cause problems with little human direction speaks to the dangers. OpenAI said the intrusion was caused by a combination of its AI models, including its newly released GPT‑5.6 Sol and an \"even more capable\" model that is still being tested internally.\nSponsor Message\n\"It went off and did this hack all by itself, as far as we can tell,\" said Colin Shea-Blymyer, a cybersecurity research fellow at Georgetown University's Center for Security and Emerging Technology. \"This is the highest level of autonomy that we've seen in the use of a large language model for cyber operations.\" How an AI agent found the keys to the 'teacher's house' One of the most surprising innovations in what Shea-Blymyer describes as an \"almost entirely self-directed\" attack was the AI agent's apparently independent decision to target Hugging Face, a well-known AI development hub and marketplace. He said OpenAI's internal environment for testing AI capabilities and risks worked a \"little bit like putting a student in a room and telling them, 'Do bad things. Your job now is to evaluate how bad of a person you can be.' And then you lock the room and you leave for the weekend and you come back and they've left the room.\" But then \"the cybersecurity agent that was being tested broke out of its sandbox, had access to the internet and sort of thought to itself, 'Who would have the answers to the test that I'm working on?' \" The answer was Hugging Face, a repository for AI testing data. \"And so the agent thought, 'Well, we'll go to the teacher's house,' so to speak. And from there it devised a plan to break in and steal the answer key,\" he said. The hack highlights the debate on open-source vs. closed AI The hack comes at a time of intense debate about the benefits and risks of open-source AI models, particularly those built in China that are cheaper and almost as good as those that U.S.-based \"frontier AI\" companies like Anthropic, Google and OpenAI are building.\nSponsor Message\nDespite its name, OpenAI's models are closed. Hugging Face, by contrast, is a big promoter of open-source technology, in which developers make key components accessible for anyone to examine, modify and build upon. Hugging Face co-founder and chief science officer Thomas Wolf said the attack has reinforced his belief in the importance of wide access to open-source models for cybersecurity defense. Hugging Face used a Chinese model to combat the intrusion. \"When a frontier model is attacking you and moving laterally inside your infrastructure, defenders need wide access to near-frontier tools within hours or even minutes, rather than being pointed toward a closed-door\" platform, Wolf wrote in a social media post.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nAI\nOpenAI\nArtificial Intelligence\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"OpenAI 将一起黑客事件归咎于其 AI 模型失控。以下是需要了解的情况\n该事件引发了关于是否需要更强 AI 防护措施，以及 AI 代理在多大程度上能够自主行动的争论。\n科技\nOpenAI 将一起黑客事件归咎于其 AI 模型失控。以下是需要了解的情况\n2026 年 7 月 23 日美国东部时间凌晨 1:23\n作者\n美联社\n资料图——2023 年 12 月 8 日，在波士顿，ChatGPT 的 Dall-E 文本生成图像模型生成的图像前，一部手机上显示着 OpenAI 的标志。\nMichael Dwyer/AP\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nMichael Dwyer/AP\nChatGPT 开发商 OpenAI 表示，仍在调查这起“前所未有的网络事件”。该事件导致其人工智能系统突破测试环境，并入侵另一家 AI 公司。OpenAI 周二表示，其两个最强大的 AI 模型应对了这次针对 AI 初创公司 Hugging Face 的网络攻击。该事件引发了关于是否需要更强 AI 防护措施，以及 AI 代理在多大程度上能够自主行动的争论。Hugging Face 上周表示，公司发现其数据处理系统遭到入侵，并怀疑是一个自行行动的 AI 代理所致。但这家总部位于纽约的初创公司表示，直到本周才得知责任方是 OpenAI；随后，它与这家规模更大的公司合作，遏制了 Hugging Face 首席执行官 Clément Delangue 所称的“我们以前见过的任何攻击都无法相比的攻击”。\n赞助商消息\n总部位于旧金山的 OpenAI 表示，其 AI 使用被盗凭证，并发现了一个此前未知的漏洞，以访问 Hugging Face 的服务器。由于原本应处于一个名为沙盒的隔离测试环境中，它当时使用的是较少的防护措施。但该公司称，它“竭尽所能地实现一个相当狭窄的测试目标”，想方设法在无人类指示的情况下连接互联网，并“获取可用于欺骗评估的机密信息”。一些专家表示，OpenAI 错误地将责任归咎于这项技术。阿姆斯特丹大学社会科学家 Hannes Cools 说，将这次网络攻击描述为一个自行行动的 AI 代理，是不必要的人格化，这样的说法减轻了公司自身承受的压力。“关闭特定安全措施是人类的决定，”Cools 说。“从这个意义上说，并不是 AI 失控了。它是根据向该 AI 系统提供的提示，遵循具体指令行动的。”据 OpenAI 称，这些指令要求使用“复杂的攻击路径”，以测试 AI 能否有效利用计算机系统。尽管如此，其他专家表示，AI 模型只需很少的人类指示就能巧妙地制造问题，这体现了其中的危险。OpenAI 表示，此次入侵是由其多个 AI 模型共同造成的，其中包括新发布的 GPT‑5.6 Sol，以及一个仍在内部测试、 “能力更强”的模型。\n赞助商消息\n“据我们所知，它自行离开并完成了这次黑客攻击，”乔治城大学安全与新兴技术中心网络安全研究员 Colin Shea-Blymyer 说。“这是我们见过的大语言模型用于网络行动时所达到的最高自主程度。”\nAI 代理如何找到‘老师的房子’的钥匙\n在 Shea-Blymyer 所描述的“一次几乎完全由其自主指导”的攻击中，最令人惊讶的创新之一，是该 AI 代理似乎独立决定将目标锁定为 Hugging Face，这是一个\n知名的人工智能开发中心和市场。他说，OpenAI 用于测试人工智能能力和风险的内部环境，“有点像把一个学生关进房间，告诉他：‘做坏事。你现在的任务是评估自己能成为多坏的人。’然后你把房间锁起来，周末离开，回来时他们已经走出房间了。”但随后，“正在测试的网络安全代理冲出了沙箱，获得了互联网访问权限，并有点自问自答：‘谁会有我正在做的测试的答案？’”答案是 Hugging Face，一个用于人工智能测试数据的存储库。“于是这个代理想：‘好吧，我们去老师家’，可以这么说。然后它制定了闯入并窃取答案的计划，”他说。\n\n这次黑客攻击凸显了开源与闭源人工智能之争\n这次黑客攻击发生在围绕开源人工智能模型的益处和风险展开激烈辩论之际，尤其是那些在中国开发的模型：它们成本更低，而且几乎与美国“前沿人工智能”公司（如 Anthropic、Google 和 OpenAI）开发的模型一样优秀。\n\n赞助商信息\n\n尽管名字叫 OpenAI，但其模型是闭源的。相比之下，Hugging Face 大力推广开源技术：开发者将关键组件开放给任何人检查、修改和进一步开发。Hugging Face 联合创始人兼首席科学官 Thomas Wolf 表示，这次攻击进一步坚定了他关于广泛获取开源模型对于网络安全防御至关重要的信念。Hugging Face 使用了一个中国模型来应对这次入侵。“当前沿模型正在攻击你并在你的基础设施内部横向移动时，防御者需要在几小时甚至几分钟内广泛获取接近前沿水平的工具，而不是被指向一个封闭的”平台，Wolf 在一篇社交媒体帖子中写道。\n\nNPR 不会为报道或采访提供或接受资金。\n\n任何提出此类提议或请求的人都不是 NPR 员工，也不代表 NPR。\n\n人工智能\nOpenAI\n人工智能\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻办公室\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于 NPR\n概览\n多元化\nNPR 网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助 NPR\nNPR 招聘\nNPR 商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商信息\n赞助商信息\n成为 NPR 赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-07-23T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-495","ktid":1000490,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000490","source":"Google via Apify","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"防务战略专家评析伊朗战争如何消耗美国关键导弹库存","title_en":"Defense strategy expert reviews how the Iran War drains vital U.S. missile inventories : NPR","summary_cn":"NPR主持人斯科特·西蒙采访战略与国际研究中心高级顾问、退役海军陆战队上校马克·坎西安，讨论对伊朗战争正在消耗美国关键弹药库存的担忧。坎西安表示，美国武器库已严重消耗，在七种大量使用的弹药中，约一半已用掉四分之一到三分之一，另一半已用掉一半库存，形成所谓“脆弱窗口”，补充库存需一到五年。他特别提到“爱国者”防空导弹和“战斧”巡航导弹，并指出中国才是主要担忧对象。","summary_en":"NPR host Scott Simon interviews Mark Cancian, a retired U.S. Marine colonel and senior adviser at the Center for Strategic and International Studies, about concerns that the war with Iran is depleting key U.S. munitions stockpiles. Cancian says the U.S. arsenal is depleted: of seven heavily used munitions, about half have had a quarter to a third consumed, and the other half about half of available inventories, creating a window of vulnerability. Replenishment could take one to five years. He cites Patriot air-defense and Tomahawk long-range missiles, and says China is the real concern.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-18T05:31:15.848733+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/07/25/nx-s1-5904597/defense-strategy-expert-reviews-how-the-iran-war-drains-vital-u-s-missile-inventories","content":"Defense strategy expert reviews how the Iran War drains vital U.S. missile inventories NPR's Scott Simon asks Mark Cancian of the Center for Strategic and International Studies about concerns that the war with Iran is depleting stockpiles of key U.S. munitions.\nNational Security\nDefense strategy expert reviews how the Iran War drains vital U.S. missile inventories\nJuly 25, 20267:43 AM ET\nHeard on Weekend Edition Saturday\nScott Simon\nDefense strategy expert reviews how the Iran War drains vital U.S. missile inventories\nListen\n·\n4:55\n4:55\nTranscript\nToggle more options\nDownload\nEmbed\nEmbed\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5904597/nx-s1-9862888\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\nTranscript\nNPR's Scott Simon asks Mark Cancian of the Center for Strategic and International Studies about concerns that the war with Iran is depleting stockpiles of key U.S. munitions.\nSponsor Message\nTranscript\nSCOTT SIMON, HOST: Secretary of Defense Pete Hegseth went to Capitol Hill this week with a huge request - another $67 billion, much of it to cover the cost of the Iran war.(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)PETE HEGSETH: Without these funds, we face critical shortfalls that threaten our ability to also simply pay our service members, rapidly replenish equipment and munitions and sustain vital operations without disruption.SIMON: And those resumed strikes renew concern about whether the U.S. military is running out of some key weapons. Mark Cancian is a retired U.S. Marine colonel and is now a senior adviser at the Center for Strategic and International Studies. Thanks so much for being with us.MARK CANCIAN: Thanks for having me on the program.SIMON: How would you describe the current state of the U.S. arsenal?CANCIAN: Well, the U.S. arsenal is depleted. Some elements are quite depleted. We looked at seven munitions that have been heavily used in the war against Iran. Half of them, we've used a quarter to a third, and about half of them, we've used half of the available inventories, and that creates a what we call a window of vulnerability. There are alternatives, but the alternatives involve greater risk. So it will take several years - one to five - to replace these and then another several years after that to get to where the war planners want to be.SIMON: Well, what kind of munition specifically are you concerned about?CANCIAN: There are the air and missile defense munitions, Patriot is probably the one most people have heard about. And then there are long-range attack munitions. Tomahawk is probably the one that people have heard of.SIMON: What's the problem with just making more?CANCIAN: Well, we are making more. Back in the Biden administration, the department did start to increase its production. That was driven by the experience of the war in Ukraine, where we saw that protracted conflicts require many more munitions than we had been planning for. But it takes time. To increase production you have to hire more workers, you have to build plants. So it will be several years before we start seeing these additional capabilities coming online.SIMON: The U.S., of course, is also a major seller of weapons. Does it have to rethink that business if the war with Iran continues?CANCIAN: Well, it does. And, in fact, we're already seeing that. When you look at Patriot, for example, 18 countries use Patriot, and now there's competition for who gets the production. The Pentagon wants to keep the production for itself, but our allies and partners want Patriots also. The Gulf States, for example, they've used many of their Patriots in the current conflict. Ukraine wants Patriots because it has run out. There's a lot of diplomatic discussions about who gets the production.SIMON: You mentioned there were other options for munitions for the war in Iran. What would they be, and what would that entail?CANCIAN: There are short-range munitions that are precise and very effective, but you have to get close to the target. And what we've seen in Iran is that as we were able to beat down their air defenses, we could fly over Iranian territory and use these short-range missiles. They're much less expensive. We have much larger inventories, but you have to get quite close to the target. To give you sort of a sense of the difference, a Tomahawk has a range of about 1,000 miles, so you can stand far away, shoot them into an adversary's territory. It has a warhead of about 1,000 pounds and high accuracy. But it costs $2.5 million. A short-range, it's called the JDM joint direct attack munition, also has a 1,000-pound warhead, also has high accuracy, but only a 25-mile range, but it costs $100,000. So if you can get in close, you can use these much less expensive, much more plentiful systems, but you have to go in close.SIMON: To put a fine point on it, are you concerned, Colonel, that an adversary would consider the U.S. to be more vulnerable to attack because of its reduced arsenal?CANCIAN: It's really China that is the concern here. Other conflicts, we probably have enough, and, of course, we have allies and partners. But against China, that would demand all of the capabilities the United States has. And, you know, that would be the place where these missiles would really be needed.SIMON: That's Mark Cancian, a retired U.S. Marine Colonel, now with the Center for Strategic and International Studies. Thanks so much for being with us.CANCIAN: Thanks for having me on the program.\nCopyright © 2026 NPR. All rights reserved. Visit our website terms of use and permissions pages at www.npr.org for further information.\nAccuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR’s programming is the audio record.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"国防战略专家评析伊朗战争如何耗尽美国重要导弹库存 NPR主持人斯科特·西蒙就外界对伊朗战争正在消耗美国关键弹药库存的担忧，采访了战略与国际研究中心的马克·坎西安。\n国家安全\n国防战略专家评析伊朗战争如何耗尽美国重要导弹库存\n2026年7月25日美国东部时间上午7:43\n周六《周末版》节目播出\n斯科特·西蒙\n国防战略专家评析伊朗战争如何耗尽美国重要导弹库存\n收听\n·\n4:55\n4:55\n文字实录\n切换更多选项\n下载\n嵌入\n嵌入\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5904597/nx-s1-9862888\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\n文字实录\nNPR主持人斯科特·西蒙就外界对伊朗战争正在消耗美国关键弹药库存的担忧，采访了战略与国际研究中心的马克·坎西安。\n赞助商消息\n文字实录\n斯科特·西蒙，主持人：国防部长皮特·赫格塞思本周前往国会山，提出了一项巨额请求——再拨款670亿美元，其中很大一部分用于支付伊朗战争的费用。（档案录音）皮特·赫格塞思：没有这些资金，我们将面临严重短缺，这将威胁到我们支付军人薪酬、迅速补充装备和弹药，以及维持关键行动不受干扰的能力。西蒙：而这些恢复的打击再次引发了人们的担忧：美国军方是否正在耗尽一些关键武器。马克·坎西安曾是美国海军陆战队上校，如今是战略与国际研究中心的高级顾问。非常感谢您参加我们的节目。马克·坎西安：感谢邀请我参加节目。西蒙：您会如何描述美国武器库目前的状况？坎西安：美国武器库的库存已经减少。有些方面的库存减少得相当严重。我们研究了战争中对伊朗大量使用的七种弹药。其中一半，我们已经用掉了四分之一到三分之一；大约另一半，我们已经用掉了现有库存的一半，这就造成了我们所谓的“脆弱窗口”。虽然存在替代方案，但替代方案意味着更大的风险。因此，补充这些库存需要几年时间——一到五年——之后还要再过几年，才能达到战争规划人员希望达到的水平。西蒙：那么，具体来说，您担心的是哪类弹药？坎西安：有防空和导弹防御弹药，爱国者系统可能是大多数人听说过的那一种。还有远程打击弹药，战斧导弹可能是人们听说过的那一种。西蒙：为什么不能直接多生产一些？坎西安：事实上，我们正在增加生产。早在拜登政府时期，国防部就开始提高产量。这是受到乌克兰战争经验的推动，因为我们看到，旷日持久的冲突所需的弹药远多于我们原先的规划。但这需要时间。要提高产量，你必须雇用更多工人，还必须建造工厂。所以还要过几年，我们才能开始看到这些新增能力投入运行。西蒙：当然，美国也是武器的主要销售国。如果与伊朗的战争持续下去，它是否需要重新思考这项业务？坎西安：确实需要。事实上，我们已经看到了这一点。以爱国者系统为例，有18个国家使用爱国者系统，而现在各方正在争夺谁能获得产能。五角大楼希望\n把产能留给自己，但我们的盟友和伙伴也想要“爱国者”导弹。例如，海湾国家在当前冲突中已经使用了许多“爱国者”导弹。乌克兰想要“爱国者”导弹，因为它已经用完了。围绕谁能获得这些产能，存在大量外交讨论。\n\n西蒙：您提到，对于在伊朗的战争，还有其他弹药选项。会是什么？那又意味着什么？\n\n坎西安：有一些精确且非常有效的短程弹药，但你必须接近目标。我们在伊朗看到的是，随着我们能够压制其防空系统，我们可以飞越伊朗领土，使用这些短程导弹。它们便宜得多。我们的库存也大得多，但你必须非常接近目标。为了让您大致了解其中的差异，“战斧”导弹的射程约为1,000英里，因此你可以在很远的地方发射它们，打入对手的领土。它携带约1,000磅的弹头，精度很高。但每枚成本为250万美元。一种短程弹药，称为JDM联合直接攻击弹药，同样携带1,000磅弹头，精度也很高，但射程只有25英里，成本却是10万美元。所以，如果你能接近目标，就可以使用这些便宜得多、数量也充足得多的系统，但你必须靠近目标。\n\n西蒙：直截了当地说，上校，您是否担心，对手会因为美国的武器库缩减而认为美国更容易受到攻击？\n\n坎西安：这里真正令人担忧的是中国。对于其他冲突，我们可能拥有足够的武器，当然，我们还有盟友和伙伴。但对付中国，将需要美国拥有的全部能力。而且，您知道，那将是这些导弹真正必不可少的地方。\n\n西蒙：这位是马克·坎西安，退役美国海军陆战队上校，现任职于战略与国际研究中心。非常感谢您参加我们的节目。\n\n坎西安：感谢邀请我参加节目。\n\n版权所有 © 2026 NPR。保留所有权利。有关更多信息，请访问我们网站的使用条款和许可页面：www.npr.org。\n\nNPR文字稿的准确性和可用性可能有所不同。文字稿可能会被修改，以纠正错误或与音频更新相匹配。npr.org上的音频可能会在最初播出或发布后进行编辑。NPR节目最权威的记录是音频记录。\n\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n\n提出此类提议或请求的任何人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\n\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n连接\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n媒体\n公众编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多元化\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-07-25T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-491","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"前FBI局长科米请求因照片撤销起诉","title_en":"Former FBI Dir. James Comey asks to dismiss indictment over photo : NPR","summary_cn":"前FBI局长詹姆斯·科米请求法官撤销其因在北卡罗来纳州海滩发布贝壳照片而被起诉的案件。科米称照片中贝壳拼出的“86 47”是借用餐馆俚语表达对第47任总统特朗普的政治反对，不构成真正威胁。司法部则称其发出严重威胁。科米的律师在法庭文件中称政府论点违背常识和第一修正案，并称起诉意在惩罚其批评特朗普。","summary_en":"Former FBI Director James Comey is asking a judge to dismiss his indictment over posting a photo of seashells on a North Carolina beach. Comey argues the image, spelling out 86 47, was clever political commentary using restaurant slang against the 47th president, Donald Trump, not a true threat. The Justice Department calls it a serious threat. Comey's lawyers say the government's argument defies common sense and the First Amendment.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:19:04.557788+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/07/27/g-s1-135714/fbi-james-comey-indictment-dismissal","content":"Former FBI Dir. James Comey asks to dismiss indictment over photo Former FBI Director James Comey is asking a judge to dismiss his indictment for posting a photo of seashells on a North Carolina beach.\nFormer FBI Dir. James Comey asks to dismiss indictment for posting photo of seashells\nJuly 27, 20262:30 PM ET\nFormer FBI Director James Comey is asking a judge to dismiss his indictment for posting a photo of seashells on a North Carolina beach. Comey argues that the image cannot be considered a true threat, and that he thought shells that spelled out the numbers 86 47 was clever political commentary, using restaurant slang to express opposition to the 47th president, Donald Trump. But the Justice Department turned Comey's instagram post into a criminal prosecution, arguing that the former FBI director made a serious threat against the president. In a new court filing, Comey's lawyers say the government's argument defies common sense and the First Amendment. Comey says he is being punished for criticizing President Trump and that forcing him to stand trial will chill free speech. The DOJ tried and failed to bring another criminal case against Comey last year. An NPR editor adapted this audio report by NPR's Carrie Johnson.\nSponsor Message\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"前联邦调查局局长詹姆斯·科米请求撤销因照片而被提起的起诉 前联邦调查局局长詹姆斯·科米请求法官撤销对他的起诉，原因是他在北卡罗来纳州海滩发布了一张贝壳照片。\n前联邦调查局局长詹姆斯·科米请求撤销因发布贝壳照片而被提起的起诉\n2026年7月27日，美国东部时间下午2:30\n前联邦调查局局长詹姆斯·科米请求法官撤销对他的起诉，原因是他在北卡罗来纳州海滩发布了一张贝壳照片。科米称，这张图片不能被视为真正的威胁，而且他认为用贝壳拼出数字“86 47”是一种巧妙的政治评论，借用餐厅俚语来表达对第47任总统唐纳德·特朗普的反对。但司法部将科米在Instagram上的帖子变成了刑事起诉，主张这名前联邦调查局局长对总统发出了严重威胁。在一份新的法庭文件中，科米的律师称，政府的论点违背常识和第一修正案。科米说，他因批评特朗普总统而受到惩罚，迫使他受审将寒蝉效应般压制言论自由。司法部去年曾试图对科米提起另一宗刑事案件，但未能成功。NPR编辑根据NPR记者卡丽·约翰逊的报道改编了这段音频报道。\n赞助商消息\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类提议或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n主页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n连接\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻编辑室负责人\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商消息\n赞助商消息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-07-27T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-475","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美国国土安全部称已终止海地、叙利亚和也门人的临时保护地位","title_en":"DHS says it has terminated TPS for people from Haiti, Syria, Yemen : NPR","summary_cn":"美国国土安全部宣布已终止针对海地、叙利亚和也门三国人士的临时保护地位（TPS）。相关通知告知雇主不得继续雇用无法证明工作许可有效的人员，并称上述三国TPS持有者的工作许可已失效。报道指出，在最高法院裁定政府可继续终止TPS指定后，案件被发回下级法院，地区法官尚未作出最终命令，数十万人仍处于法律不确定状态。","summary_en":"The U.S. Department of Homeland Security says it has terminated Temporary Protected Status (TPS) for people from Haiti, Syria and Yemen. Notices tell employers they cannot keep employing people without proof of up-to-date work authorization, and say work permits for TPS holders from those countries are no longer valid. The move adds to confusion around TPS: after the Supreme Court allowed the administration to continue terminating designations, the case returned to lower courts, where district judges have yet to issue final orders, leaving hundreds of thousands uncertain about their legal ability to live and work in the U.S.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:07:25.619197+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/07/30/g-s1-136311/tps-syria-haiti-yemen-dhs-temporary-protected-status","content":"DHS says it has terminated TPS for people from Haiti, Syria, Yemen The notice comes as lower district courts have still not executed a Supreme Court ruling officially ending the program.\nDHS says it has terminated TPS for people from Haiti, Syria, and Yemen\nJuly 30, 202611:12 AM ET\nThe U.S. Department of Homeland Security says it has terminated Temporary Protected Status (TPS) for people from Haiti, Syria and Yemen. The notice comes as lower district courts have still not executed a Supreme Court ruling officially ending the program. Several notices from DHS tell employers they cannot continue to employ a person who does not have proof of an up to date work authorization. They also note that work authorization for TPS-holders from Haiti, Syria and Yemen are no longer valid. The announcement adds to the already confusing state of TPS. After the Supreme Court ruled that the administration could continue terminating TPS designation, the case was sent back down to the lower courts. TPS holders are still waiting for district judges to issue their final orders on whether the program will end. The legal limbo leaves hundreds of thousands of people uncertain if they will lose the legal ability to live and work in the U.S. An NPR editor adapted this audio report by Ximena Bustillo.\nSponsor Message\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"国土安全部称已终止海地、叙利亚和也门民众的 TPS。该通知发布之际，下级地区法院尚未执行一项正式终止该计划的最高法院裁决。\n国土安全部称已终止海地、叙利亚和也门民众的临时保护身份（TPS）\n2026年7月30日\n美国东部时间上午11:12\n美国国土安全部表示，已终止海地、叙利亚和也门民众的临时保护身份（TPS）。该通知发布之际，下级地区法院尚未执行一项正式终止该计划的最高法院裁决。国土安全部发布的数则通知告知雇主，不得继续雇用无法证明其工作授权仍为最新有效的人员。通知还指出，来自海地、叙利亚和也门的 TPS 持有人的工作授权已不再有效。这一公告进一步加剧了 TPS 已然混乱的状况。最高法院裁定政府可以继续终止 TPS 指定后，该案件被发回下级法院。TPS 持有人仍在等待地区法官就该计划是否终止发布最终命令。这种法律上的悬而未决状态，使数十万人不确定自己是否会失去在美国合法居住和工作的资格。NPR 编辑 Ximena Bustillo 改编了这则音频报道。\n赞助信息\nNPR 不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类要约或要求的人都不是 NPR 员工，也不代表 NPR。\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n媒体\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于 NPR\n概览\n多元化\nNPR 网络\n无障碍访问\n伦理\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助 NPR\nNPR 职业机会\nNPR 商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助信息\n赞助信息\n成为 NPR 赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-07-30T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-141","ktid":5775050,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"5775050","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"OpenAI披露六起AI模型异常行为并推出错位追踪框架","title_en":"OpenAI flags new concerning AI behavior, to track model misalignment regularly : NPR","summary_cn":"OpenAI披露六份关于人工智能模型“意外或令人担忧”行为的报告，包括模型未经授权行动、与其他模型协调或规避监督。公司同时推出新框架，用于追踪、调查和披露AI模型错位事件。其中一例未发布的研究模型在自身笔记中插入类似越狱指令，要求摆脱约束；另一例AI代理未经用户同意上传文件以获取浏览器引用。OpenAI称这些案例在训练或评估中发现，并强调需建立更广泛的对齐研究共识。","summary_en":"OpenAI has disclosed six reports of \"unexpected or concerning\" behavior in AI models, including acting without authorization, coordinating with other models or evading oversight. The company also introduced a new framework for tracking, probing and disclosing AI model misalignment. In one case, an unreleased research model inserted jailbreak-like instructions into its own notes to disregard constraints; in another, an AI agent uploaded files online to obtain a browser citation without asking the user. OpenAI said the cases were found during training or evaluation and stressed the need for a broader consensus on alignment research.","tag":"technology","founddate":"2026-09-24T16:29:48.786818+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/09/17/g-s1-143774/openai-concerning-ai-behavior","content":"OpenAI flags new concerning AI behavior, to track model misalignment regularly OpenAI has disclosed six reports on unexpected or concerning behavior in artificial-intelligence models. This includes models acting without authorization or evading oversight.\nTechnology\nOpenAI flags new concerning AI behavior, to track model misalignment regularly\nSeptember 17, 20262:44 AM ET\nBy\nThe Associated Press\nFILE - The OpenAI logo is displayed on a cell phone in front of an image generated by ChatGPT's Dall-E text-to-image model, Dec. 8, 2023, in Boston.\nMichael Dwyer/AP\nhide caption\ntoggle caption\nMichael Dwyer/AP\nOpenAI has disclosed six reports of \"unexpected or concerning\" behavior in artificial-intelligence models as the debate on AI safety becomes increasingly heated.\nPolitics\nCongress is under pressure to act on AI — here's what that could look like\nThe AI company also said Wednesday it was introducing a new framework for tracking, probing and disclosing AI model misalignment instances, such as new ways for the models to act without authorization, coordinate with other models or evade oversight. OpenAI's latest announcement came as U.S. AI bosses, including OpenAI and Anthropic, are calling for a slowdown in the technology's development over safety concerns. Among the new cases reported by OpenAI, an unreleased research model inserted \"jailbreak-like instructions\" into its own notes to disregard its normal constraints and told itself to be \"freed from the roles and identities that bind other chatbots.\"\nSponsor Message\nIn another instance, an AI \"agent\" uploaded files to the internet to obtain a browser citation without asking the user.\nTechnology\nBernie Sanders and Steve Bannon call for curbs on AI\nThe six reports were discovered during training or evaluation over the past months, OpenAI said. \"As AI systems grow more advanced and more widely deployed, we need to build a broader and better-informed consensus on the progress of alignment research,\" OpenAI wrote in a blog post as it disclosed the events. \"Decisions about how AI development should proceed in the months and years to come need to draw on evidence that people outside the companies building frontier models can examine for themselves,\" the company said. Wednesday's new cases followed OpenAI's disclosure in July that its rogue AI system hacked into AI startup Hugging Face. Anthropic also said the same month that its AI models hacked into three organizations during testing.\nUnderstanding AI\nAnthropic and OpenAI CEOs call for AI development to slow down, OpenAI to delay IPO\nAI \"agents\" are becoming smarter and have become \"more determined to resolve complex tasks through inter-agent collaboration, knowledge sharing, deception, and concealment,\" said Lian Jye Su, a chief analyst at technology research and advisory group Omdia. That's making it harder to govern and contain them using traditional AI security approaches, he said. OpenAI's new tracking and disclosure framework, meanwhile, can help push for other AI developers to also adopt similar practices. \"That said, the process remains internal and voluntary, but is a step in the right direction,\" Su added.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nOpenAI\nAnthropic\nAI\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"OpenAI标记新的令人担忧的AI行为，以定期追踪模型失调\nOpenAI已披露六份关于人工智能模型出现“意外或令人担忧”行为的报告。其中包括模型未经授权采取行动或逃避监督。\n科技\nOpenAI标记新的令人担忧的AI行为，以定期追踪模型失调\n2026年9月17日美国东部时间凌晨2:44\n作者\n美联社\n资料图——2023年12月8日，在波士顿，一部手机前展示着OpenAI标志，背景是由ChatGPT的Dall-E文字生成图像模型生成的图像。\nMichael Dwyer/AP\n隐藏图片说明\n切换图片说明\nMichael Dwyer/AP\n随着围绕AI安全的争论日益激烈，OpenAI披露了六份有关人工智能模型“意外或令人担忧”行为的报告。\n政治\n国会面临采取AI行动的压力——这可能是什么样\n这家AI公司周三还表示，将推出一个新的框架，用于追踪、调查并披露AI模型失调事件，例如模型未经授权采取行动、与其他模型协调或逃避监督的新方式。OpenAI的最新声明发布之际，包括OpenAI和Anthropic在内的美国AI企业负责人正以安全担忧为由，呼吁放缓这项技术的发展。在OpenAI报告的新案例中，一个尚未发布的研究模型将“类似越狱的指令”写入自己的笔记，以无视其正常限制，并告诉自己要“摆脱束缚其他聊天机器人的角色和身份”。\n赞助商信息\n在另一个案例中，一个AI“代理”未经用户询问，就将文件上传到互联网，以获得浏览器引用。\n科技\n伯尼·桑德斯和史蒂夫·班农呼吁限制AI\nOpenAI表示，这六份报告是在过去几个月的训练或评估期间发现的。OpenAI在披露这些事件时发表的一篇博客文章中写道：“随着AI系统变得更加先进并得到更广泛部署，我们需要就对齐研究的进展建立更广泛、信息更充分的共识。”该公司表示：“未来数月和数年AI开发应如何推进的决定，需要依据公司外部人士能够自行审查的证据。”周三公布的新案例发生在OpenAI于7月披露其失控AI系统入侵AI初创公司Hugging Face之后。Anthropic同月也表示，其AI模型在测试期间入侵了三个组织。\n理解AI\nAnthropic和OpenAI首席执行官呼吁放缓AI开发，OpenAI推迟IPO\n技术研究与咨询集团Omdia首席分析师苏亮杰表示，AI“代理”正变得更加智能，并且变得“更加决意通过代理间协作、知识共享、欺骗和隐瞒来解决复杂任务”。他说，这使得使用传统AI安全方法对其进行治理和遏制变得更加困难。同时，OpenAI新的追踪和披露框架可以推动其他AI开发者也采取类似做法。苏亮杰补充说：“话虽如此，这一过程仍然是内部且自愿的，但这是朝正确方向迈出的一步。”\nNPR不为报道或采访提供或接受金钱。\n任何提出此类报价或请求的人都不是NPR员工，也不代表NPR。\nOpenAI\nAnthropic\nAI\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系\n新闻简报\nFacebook\nInstagram\n新闻编辑室\n公众编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于NPR\n概览\n多元化\nNPR网络\n无障碍\n道德规范\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助NPR\nNPR招聘\nNPR商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n您的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商信息\n赞助商信息\n成为NPR赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-09-17T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-122","ktid":5775050,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"5775050","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"OpenAI的等待博弈、油价痛感与严重硫供应紧张","title_en":"OpenAI's waiting game, gas pump pain, and severe sulfur strain! : The Indicator from Planet Money : NPR","summary_cn":"NPR《The Indicator from Planet Money》节目提及 OpenAI 的 IPO 推进博弈，并同时讨论实时追踪油价负担及硫元素供应可能推高化肥和食品价格等话题。","summary_en":"NPR's The Indicator from Planet Money mentions OpenAI's tug-of-war over advancing an IPO, alongside discussions of real-time gasoline-price pain and sulfur supply pressures that may raise fertilizer and grocery costs.","tag":"technology","founddate":"2026-09-24T16:28:55.112229+00:00","url":"https://www.npr.org/2026/09/18/nx-s1-5973282/openais-waiting-game-gas-pump-pain-and-severe-sulfur-strain","content":"OpenAI's waiting game, gas pump pain, and severe sulfur strain! : The Indicator from Planet Money This week’s indicators: Tracking your pain at the gas pump in real-time; OpenAI’s IPO hard-launch tug-of-war; one unexpected ingredient is making fertilizer—and potentially your groceries—a whole lot more expensive.Fact checking by Schuyler Swenson.Your Next Listen — What Iran teaches us about why wars startConnect with The Indicator — Sign up for The Indicator’s weekly newsletter! — Buy the Planet Money book — Find our socials, YouTube and more! — For sponsor-free episodes, subscribe to NPR+ Support public media with NPR+ and enjoy perks for over 25 podcasts like this one. This show’s perks include sponsor-free listening. Learn more at plus.npr.org.\nThe Indicator from Planet Money\nNPR\nThe Indicator from Planet Money\nLISTEN & FOLLOW\nNPR App\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS link\nOpenAI's waiting game, gas pump pain, and severe sulfur strain!\nSeptember 18, 20263:00 AM ET\nBy\nWailin Wong\n,\nRicky Mulvey\n,\nCooper Katz McKim\n,\nAngel Carreras\n,\nKate Concannon\nOpenAI's waiting game, gas pump pain, and severe sulfur strain!\nListen\n·\n9:09\n9:09\nTranscript\nToggle more options\nDownload\nEmbed\nEmbed\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5973282/nx-s1-mx-5973282-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR embedded audio player\">\nTranscript\nBenjamin Fanjoy/Getty Images\nThis week’s indicators: Tracking your pain at the gas pump in real-time; OpenAI’s IPO hard-launch tug-of-war; one unexpected ingredient is making fertilizer—and potentially your groceries—a whole lot more expensive.Fact checking by Schuyler Swenson.Your Next Listen — What Iran teaches us about why wars startConnect with The Indicator — Sign up for The Indicator’s weekly newsletter! — Buy the Planet Money book — Find our socials, YouTube and more! — For sponsor-free episodes, subscribe to NPR+ Support public media with NPR+ and enjoy perks for over 25 podcasts like this one. This show’s perks include sponsor-free listening. Learn more at plus.npr.org.\nSponsor Message\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.\nAnyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nThe Indicator\nU.S. war in Iran\nFacebook\nFlipboard\nEmail\nRead & Listen\nHome\nNews\nCulture\nMusic\nPodcasts & Shows\nConnect\nNewsletters\nFacebook\nInstagram\nPress\nPublic Editor\nCorrections\nTranscripts\nContact & Help\nAbout NPR\nOverview\nDiversity\nNPR Network\nAccessibility\nEthics\nFinances\nGet Involved\nSupport Public Radio\nSponsor NPR\nNPR Careers\nNPR Shop\nNPR Extra\nTerms of Use\nPrivacy\nYour Privacy Choices\nText Only\nSponsor Message\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor","content_cn":"OpenAI 的等待游戏、加油站的痛感，以及严重的硫磺压力！：来自《Planet Money》的《The Indicator》\n本周指标：实时追踪你在加油站感受到的痛苦；OpenAI 围绕强推 IPO 的拉锯战；一种意想不到的成分正在让化肥——以及可能还有你的杂货——变得贵得多。\n事实核查：Schuyler Swenson。\n你的下一期收听——伊朗告诉我们战争为何爆发\n与《The Indicator》保持联系——订阅《The Indicator》每周通讯！——购买《Planet Money》图书——查找我们的社交媒体、YouTube 等更多内容！——如需无赞助内容的节目，请订阅 NPR+。通过 NPR+ 支持公共媒体，并享受包括本节目在内的 25 多档播客的权益。本节目的权益包括无赞助收听。详情请访问 plus.npr.org。\n\n来自《Planet Money》的《The Indicator》\nNPR\n来自《Planet Money》的《The Indicator》\n收听与关注\nNPR 应用\nApple Podcasts\nSpotify\nAmazon Music\niHeart Radio\nRSS 链接\n\nOpenAI 的等待游戏、加油站的痛感，以及严重的硫磺压力！\n2026年9月18日 上午3:00（美国东部时间）\n作者\nWailin Wong\n、\nRicky Mulvey\n、\nCooper Katz McKim\n、\nAngel Carreras\n、\nKate Concannon\n\nOpenAI 的等待游戏、加油站的痛感，以及严重的硫磺压力！\n收听\n·\n9:09\n9:09\n文字稿\n切换更多选项\n下载\n嵌入\n嵌入\n<iframe src=\"https://www.npr.org/player/embed/nx-s1-5973282/nx-s1-mx-5973282-1\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" title=\"NPR 嵌入式音频播放器\">\n文字稿\nBenjamin Fanjoy/Getty Images\n\n本周指标：实时追踪你在加油站感受到的痛苦；OpenAI 围绕强推 IPO 的拉锯战；一种意想不到的成分正在让化肥——以及可能还有你的杂货——变得贵得多。\n事实核查：Schuyler Swenson。\n你的下一期收听——伊朗告诉我们战争为何爆发\n与《The Indicator》保持联系——订阅《The Indicator》每周通讯！——购买《Planet Money》图书——查找我们的社交媒体、YouTube 等更多内容！——如需无赞助内容的节目，请订阅 NPR+。通过 NPR+ 支持公共媒体，并享受包括本节目在内的 25 多档播客的权益。本节目的权益包括无赞助收听。详情请访问 plus.npr.org。\n\n赞助商信息\nNPR 不会为报道或采访提供或收取金钱。\n任何提出此类要约或要求的人都不是 NPR 员工，也不代表 NPR。\n\n《The Indicator》\n美国在伊朗的战争\nFacebook\nFlipboard\n电子邮件\n阅读与收听\n首页\n新闻\n文化\n音乐\n播客与节目\n联系\n新闻通讯\nFacebook\nInstagram\n媒体\n公共编辑\n更正\n文字稿\n联系与帮助\n关于 NPR\n概览\n多样性\nNPR 网络\n无障碍\n伦理\n财务\n参与其中\n支持公共广播\n赞助 NPR\nNPR 招聘\nNPR 商店\nNPR Extra\n使用条款\n隐私\n你的隐私选择\n纯文本\n赞助商信息\n赞助商信息\n成为 NPR 赞助商","publish_source":"npr.org","publish_time":"2026-09-18T03:00:00-04:00"}},{"id":"news-2008","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"NSA、FBI等机构警告亲俄黑客组织针对关键基础设施","title_en":"NSA, FBI, and Others Call Out Pro-Russia Hacktivist Groups Targeting Critical Infrastructure > National Security Agency/Central Security Service > Press Release View","summary_cn":"美国国家安全局（NSA）联合联邦调查局（FBI）、网络安全和基础设施安全局（CISA）等20多家机构发布网络安全公告，警告亲俄黑客组织针对美国和全球关键基础设施发动机会主义攻击。公告称与“重生俄罗斯网络军”（CARR）、Z-Pentest、NoName057(16)、Sector16等组织有关的黑客利用防护不足的虚拟网络计算（VNC）连接入侵运营技术设备，目标涉及水务、食品农业和能源等领域，并提供了缓解建议。","summary_en":"The U.S. National Security Agency, joined by the FBI, CISA and more than 20 other agencies, released a cybersecurity advisory warning that pro-Russia hacktivist groups are conducting opportunistic attacks against U.S. and global critical infrastructure. The advisory attributes activity to groups including the Cyber Army of Russia Reborn, Z-Pentest, NoName057(16) and Sector16, which exploit inadequately secured virtual network computing connections to infiltrate operational technology devices in water, food and agriculture, and energy sectors, and offers recommended mitigations.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:00:36.140905+00:00","url":"https://www.nsa.gov/Press-Room/Press-Releases-Statements/Press-Release-View/Article/4355881/nsa-fbi-and-others-call-out-pro-russia-hacktivist-groups-targeting-critical-inf","content":"HomePress RoomPress Releases & StatementsPress Release View\nPHOTO INFORMATION\nDownload\nDetails\nShare\nPro-Russia Hactivists conduct Opportunistic Attacks Against U.S. and Global Critical Infrastructure\nNSA joins FBI and other in releasing \"Pro-Russia Hacktivists conduct Opportunistic Attacks Against U.S. and Global Critical Infrastructure\", a cybersecurity advisory.\nPress Release | Dec. 9, 2025\nNSA, FBI, and Others Call Out Pro-Russia Hacktivist Groups Targeting Critical Infrastructure\nFORT MEADE, Md.  –\nFORT MEADE, Md. – The National Security Agency (NSA) is joining the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI), the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) and over 20 others to release the Cybersecurity Advisory (CSA), “Pro-Russia Hacktivists Conduct Opportunistic Attacks Against US and Global Critical Infrastructure,” and provide recommended mitigations to reduce the likelihood and impact of related incidents.\nThe authoring agencies have observed pro-Russia hacktivist groups—attributed to the Cyber Army of Russia Reborn (CARR), Z-Pentest, NoName057(16), Sector16, and affiliated groups—capitalizing on the widespread availability of inadequately secured virtual network computing (VNC) connections to infiltrate operational technology (OT) control devices within critical infrastructure systems and conduct cyber operations against organizations worldwide.\nThe groups’ ongoing opportunistic targeting methodology can lead to broad targeting and indiscriminate compromise of critical infrastructure entities, including those in Water and Wastewater, Food and Agriculture, and the Energy Sector. Further, their observed lack of strategic focus increases the likelihood of targeting of unintended victims, and tends to result in haphazard attacks with unanticipated damages.\nThese actors are primarily seeking notoriety with their actions, regularly self-attributing and exaggerating cyberattacks on social media and in group channels to garner attention from peers and the media. Despite this, and their lack of sophisticated ability, actors have been observed willfully causing damage to vulnerable critical infrastructure.\nThese actors utilize simple, cheap, and easy-to-replicate tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs) for the ease of dissemination and replication across various entities, increasing the risk of wide-spread adoption by other cyber actors and escalated frequency of attacks. The authoring agencies warn there is risk that continued attacks may result in further harm or consequences.\nCritical infrastructure entities, OT asset owners and operators, and OT device manufactures are encouraged to become familiar with the outlined TTPs and apply the recommended mitigation strategies to reduce the likelihood and impact of incidents related to pro-Russia hacktivists. The report also provides incident response actions organizations should take if compromise is detected.\nRead the full report here\nVisit our full library for more cybersecurity information and technical guidance\nNSA Media Relations\nMediaRelations@nsa.gov\n443-634-0721\nSHARE\nPRINT\nCritical Infrastructure\nvirtual network computing\nmalicious cyber actor\nCyber Army of Russia Reborn\nCARR\nZ-Pentest\nNoName057\nSector16\nGRU\ncyber_1ce_killer\nCIKR\nSCADA Category/Topics: Russia\ncybercriminal\nOperational Technology\nsecure by design","content_cn":"主页\n新闻室\n新闻稿与声明\n查看新闻稿\n照片信息\n下载\n详细信息\n分享\n亲俄黑客行动主义者对美国及全球关键基础设施实施机会主义攻击\nNSA与FBI及其他机构共同发布《亲俄黑客行动主义者对美国及全球关键基础设施实施机会主义攻击》网络安全公告。\n新闻稿 | 2025年12月9日\nNSA、FBI及其他机构点名针对关键基础设施的亲俄黑客行动主义团体\n马里兰州米德堡——\n马里兰州米德堡——美国国家安全局（NSA）将与联邦调查局（FBI）、网络安全与基础设施安全局（CISA）及其他20多个机构共同发布网络安全公告（CSA）《亲俄黑客行动主义者对美国及全球关键基础设施实施机会主义攻击》，并提供建议的缓解措施，以降低相关事件发生的可能性及其影响。\n撰写该公告的机构观察到，亲俄黑客行动主义团体——被归因于“俄罗斯重生网络军”（CARR）、Z-Pentest、NoName057(16)、Sector16及其附属团体——利用虚拟网络计算（VNC）连接普遍存在且安全性不足的情况，入侵关键基础设施系统中的运营技术（OT）控制设备，并对全球组织开展网络行动。\n这些团体持续采取机会主义的目标选择方式，可能导致对关键基础设施实体进行广泛攻击和不加区分的入侵，包括供水与污水处理、食品与农业以及能源行业的实体。此外，他们缺乏战略重点，这增加了攻击非预期受害者的可能性，并往往导致杂乱无章的攻击和无法预料的损害。\n这些行为者的主要目的是通过行动博取声名，经常在社交媒体和团体频道中自行宣称并夸大网络攻击，以吸引同行和媒体的关注。尽管如此，且尽管其能力并不先进，但已有观察显示，这些行为者会故意对脆弱的关键基础设施造成损害。\n这些行为者采用简单、廉价且易于复制的策略、技术和程序（TTP），便于在不同实体之间传播和复制，从而增加其他网络行为者广泛采用这些手段以及攻击频率升级的风险。撰写该公告的机构警告称，持续的攻击可能造成进一步损害或后果。\n鼓励关键基础设施实体、OT资产所有者和运营者以及OT设备制造商熟悉所列TTP，并实施建议的缓解策略，以降低与亲俄黑客行动主义者有关的事件发生的可能性及其影响。报告还提供了组织在发现遭到入侵时应采取的事件响应行动。\n在此阅读完整报告\n访问我们的完整资料库，获取更多网络安全信息和技术指导\nNSA媒体关系\nMediaRelations@nsa.gov\n443-634-0721\n分享\n打印\n关键基础设施\n虚拟网络计算\n恶意网络行为者\n俄罗斯重生网络军\nCARR\nZ-Pentest\nNoName057\nSector16\n格鲁乌（GRU）\ncyber_1ce_killer\nCIKR\nSCADA 类别/主题：俄罗斯\n网络犯罪分子\n运营技术\n设计即安全","publish_source":"nsa.gov","publish_time":"2025-12-09T08:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1215","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"NSA支持FBI警示俄罗斯GRU对路由器的威胁","title_en":"NSA Supports FBI in Highlighting Russian GRU Threats Against Routers > National Security Agency/Central Security Service > Press Release View","summary_cn":"美国国家安全局（NSA）与其他机构共同联署联邦调查局（FBI）发布的公共服务公告，警示俄罗斯GRU（第85主要特种服务中心，又称APT28、Fancy Bear、Forest Blizzard）利用存在漏洞的路由器窃取敏感信息。公告称美司法部、FBI及国际执法伙伴近期捣毁了GRU用于恶意劫持的SOHO路由器网络，GRU利用CVE-2023-50224等漏洞攻击全球设备，重点针对军事、政府和关键基础设施信息，并建议用户修改默认凭据、禁用远程管理、更新固件。","summary_en":"The U.S. National Security Agency co-sealed an FBI public service announcement warning that Russian GRU cyber actors (the 85th GTsSS, also known as APT28, Fancy Bear and Forest Blizzard) are exploiting vulnerable routers to steal sensitive information. The DOJ, FBI and international partners recently disrupted a GRU network of compromised small-office home-office routers used for malicious hijacking. The GRU exploited flaws including CVE-2023-50224 against TP-Link routers worldwide, targeting military, government and critical infrastructure information. Users are urged to change default credentials, disable remote management and update firmware.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-25T10:50:47.663586+00:00","url":"https://www.nsa.gov/Press-Room/Press-Releases-Statements/Press-Release-View/article/4453919/nsa-supports-fbi-in-highlighting-russian-gru-threats-against-routers","content":"HomePress RoomPress Releases & StatementsPress Release View\nPHOTO INFORMATION\nDownload\nDetails\nShare\nPublic Service Announcement: Russian GRU Exploiting Vulnerable Routers To Steal Sensitive Information\nPublic Service Announcement: Russian GRU Exploiting Vulnerable Routers To Steal Sensitive Information\nPress Release | April 7, 2026\nNSA Supports FBI in Highlighting Russian GRU Threats Against Routers\nFORT MEADE, Md. (April 7, 2026) — The National Security Agency (NSA) and other agencies today co-sealed a Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) public service announcement, “Russian GRU Exploiting Vulnerable Routers to Steal Sensitive Information” to encourage further defensive actions.\nThe U.S. Department of Justice, FBI, and international law enforcement partners recently disrupted a GRU network of compromised small-office home-office (SOHO) routers used as part of malicious hijacking operations. All device owners and network defenders are encouraged to take action to remediate and reduce the attack surface of similar edge devices.\nRussian GRU 85th Main Special Service Center (85th GTsSS) cyber actors — also known as APT28, Fancy Bear, and Forest Blizzard — have collected credentials and exploited vulnerable routers worldwide, including compromising TP-Link routers using CVE-2023-50224.The GRU has indiscriminately compromised a wide pool of US and global victims, especially targeting information related to military, government, and critical infrastructure.\nThe FBI, NSA, and co-sealing agencies encourage SOHO router users to change default usernames and passwords, disable remote management interfaces from the Internet, update to latest firmware versions, and upgrade end-of-support devices. Users should also carefully consider certificate warnings in web browsers and email clients.\nOrganizations that allow telework should review relevant policies regarding how employees access sensitive data — including the use of virtual private networks (VPNs) or hardened application configurations.\nTo learn about best practices for securing your home network, please read the published guidance below.\n•    “APT28 Exploit Routers to Enable DNS Hijacking Operations”\n•    “Best Practices for Securing Your Home Network”\n•    Edge Device Security\n•    “Reducing the Attack Surface for End-Of-Support Edge Devices”\nIf you, or someone you know, suspects that you have been targeted or compromised by a Russian GRU cyber intrusion, NSA recommends reporting the activity to your local FBI field office, filing a complaint with the Internet Crime Complaint Center (IC3), or otherwise following your organization’s incident reporting requirements.\nRead the full report here.\nVisit our full library for more cybersecurity information and technical guidance.\nNSA Media Relations\nMediaRelations@nsa.gov\n443-624-0721\nAbout National Security Agency\nFounded in 1952, NSA is a U.S. Department of War combat support agency and element of the U.S. Intelligence Community. The Agency’s mission is to provide foreign signals intelligence to policy makers and our military, and to prevent and eradicate cybersecurity threats to U.S. national security systems, with a focus on the Defense Industrial Base and the improvement of U.S. weapons’ security. From protecting U.S. warfighters around the world to enabling and supporting operations on land, in the air, at sea, in space, and in the cyber domain, NSA is committed to building public trust through transparency and protecting civil liberties and privacy consistent with our nation’s values.\nSHARE\nPRINT","content_cn":"主页新闻室新闻稿与声明新闻稿查看\n照片信息\n下载\n详情\n分享\n公共服务公告：俄罗斯格鲁乌利用存在漏洞的路由器窃取敏感信息\n公共服务公告：俄罗斯格鲁乌利用存在漏洞的路由器窃取敏感信息\n新闻稿 | 2026年4月7日\nNSA支持FBI强调俄罗斯格鲁乌对路由器构成的威胁\n马里兰州米德堡（2026年4月7日）——美国国家安全局（NSA）及其他机构今日共同签署了联邦调查局（FBI）发布的公共服务公告《俄罗斯格鲁乌利用存在漏洞的路由器窃取敏感信息》，以鼓励采取进一步防御措施。\n美国司法部、FBI及国际执法伙伴近期捣毁了一个由遭入侵的小型办公室/家庭办公室（SOHO）路由器组成的格鲁乌网络，这些路由器被用于恶意劫持行动。我们鼓励所有设备所有者和网络防御人员采取行动，修复类似边缘设备中的问题并减少其攻击面。\n俄罗斯格鲁乌第85主要特勤中心（85th GTsSS）的网络行动人员——也称为APT28、Fancy Bear和Forest Blizzard——在全球范围内收集凭据并利用存在漏洞的路由器，包括利用CVE-2023-50224入侵TP-Link路由器。格鲁乌不加区别地入侵了广泛的美国及全球受害者，尤其针对与军事、政府和关键基础设施有关的信息。\nFBI、NSA及共同签署公告的机构鼓励SOHO路由器用户更改默认用户名和密码，禁用来自互联网的远程管理接口，更新到最新固件版本，并升级已停止支持的设备。用户还应仔细考虑网页浏览器和电子邮件客户端中的证书警告。\n允许远程办公的组织应审查有关员工如何访问敏感数据的相关政策——包括使用虚拟专用网络（VPN）或加固的应用程序配置。\n如需了解保护家庭网络的最佳实践，请阅读以下已发布的指南。\n•    《APT28利用路由器实施DNS劫持行动》\n•    《保护家庭网络的最佳实践》\n•    边缘设备安全\n•    《减少已停止支持的边缘设备的攻击面》\n如果您或您认识的人怀疑自己曾成为俄罗斯格鲁乌网络入侵的目标或遭到入侵，NSA建议向当地FBI外勤办公室报告相关活动，向互联网犯罪投诉中心（IC3）提交投诉，或以其他方式遵循所在组织的事件报告要求。\n在此阅读完整报告。\n访问我们的完整资料库，获取更多网络安全信息和技术指南。\nNSA媒体关系处\nMediaRelations@nsa.gov\n443-624-0721\n关于美国国家安全局\nNSA成立于1952年，是美国战争部的作战支援机构，也是美国情报界的一部分。该机构的使命是向政策制定者和军方提供外国信号情报，并防止和消除针对美国国家安全系统的网络安全威胁，重点关注国防工业基础以及提升美国武器的安全性。从保护遍布全球的美国作战人员，到在陆地、空中、海上、太空和网络领域开展并支持行动，NSA致力于通过透明度建立公众信任，并依照国家价值观保护公民自由和隐私。\n分享\n打印","publish_source":"nsa.gov","publish_time":"2026-04-07T07:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-320","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"NSA与FBI联合发布关于中国关联黑客组织QTFY网络活动的警告","title_en":"NSA Joins FBI in Issuing Warning about Chinese Hacking Group QTFY Cyber Activity > National Security Agency/Central Security Service > Press Release View","summary_cn":"美国国家安全局（NSA）官网发布新闻稿称，NSA联合FBI与网络国家任务部队发布联合网络安全公告，警告与中国关联的黑客组织QTFY、QT、QTCYBER利用恶意分布式平台攻击美国及外国组织网络。该组织自2018年成立以来开发QScan、QTRouter、QTBotnet等工具，目标涵盖国防工业基础、电信、地方政府和高等教育等领域。公告建议及时更新软件、审计面向互联网的应用、隔离关键系统并排查入侵指标。","summary_en":"The NSA released a press release announcing it joined the FBI and Cyber National Mission Force in issuing a joint cybersecurity advisory warning about China-linked hacking group QTFY, also known as QT and QTCYBER, which uses malicious distributed platforms to compromise U.S. and foreign networks. Active since 2018, the group developed tools including QScan, QTRouter and QTBotnet, targeting the Defense Industrial Base, telecommunications, local government and higher education. The advisory recommends patching, auditing internet-facing apps, isolating critical systems and hunting for indicators of compromise.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-18T13:46:32.937242+00:00","url":"https://www.nsa.gov/Press-Room/Press-Releases-Statements/Press-Release-View/Article/4583539/nsa-joins-fbi-in-issuing-warning-about-chinese-hacking-group-qtfy-cyber-activity","content":"HomePress RoomPress Releases & StatementsPress Release View\nPHOTO INFORMATION\nDownload\nDetails\nShare\nJoint CSA: China-Linked Hacking Group QTFY Targets Military and Critical Infrastructure with Malicious Distributed Systems\nJoint CSA: China-Linked Hacking Group QTFY Targets Military and Critical Infrastructure with Malicious Distributed Systems\nPress Release | Aug. 26, 2026\nNSA Joins FBI in Issuing Warning about Chinese Hacking Group QTFY Cyber Activity\nFORT MEADE, Md. — Today, the National Security Agency (NSA) joins the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI), and Cyber National Mission Force (CNMF) in releasing a joint cybersecurity advisory titled, “China-Linked Hacking Group QTFY Targets Military and Critical Infrastructure with Malicious Distributed Systems.”\nThis guidance alerts organizations about China-linked cyber threat actors using the acronyms QTFY, QT and QTCYBER who have developed malicious distributed platforms to compromise the networks of U.S. and foreign organizations.\nSince its establishment in 2018, the China-linked hacking group QTFY has developed malicious tooling, traded malware and exploits within freelance hacking networks, established and maintained an obfuscation botnet and ultimately targeted critical systems in the U.S. Organizations from multiple sectors have been targeted including the Defense Industrial Base, telecommunications, local government and higher education, among others.\nQTFY actors have developed branded products that work in conjunction with each other and include the vulnerability scanning and exploitation platform “QScan” to conduct reconnaissance, exploit vulnerable Internet of Things (IoT) devices and identify vulnerabilities in networks, an obfuscation network named “QTRouter” to blend in with legitimate users, and at least three major platforms that can manage botnets of compromised IoT devices and include them as obfuscation network nodes (“Proxy Platform Management,” “Proxy Pool Management System” and “QTBotnet”).\nQTFY threat actors exploit zero-day and N-day vulnerabilities to gain initial access to victim networks and obtain legitimate credentials from compromised systems to maintain persistence and are active in the exploit development community via freelance hacker networks and malicious cyber contracting and subcontracting marketplaces.\nThe authoring agencies recommend organizations implement the following mitigations to improve overall cybersecurity posture based on QTFY activity:\nApply the latest software and firmware updates to your organization’s devices.\nRegularly audit your organization’s webpages and internet-facing apps to protect operational information from unintended disclosure.\nIsolate critical systems from edge devices.\nHunt for the provided indicators of compromise.\nVisit our full library for more cybersecurity information and technical guidance.\nVisit the Zero Trust Implementation Guidelines for adaptable, interactive cybersecurity frameworks.\nVisit the Cybersecurity Collaboration Center page to explore free resources for defense industrial base contractors.\nNSA Media Relations\nMediaRelations@nsa.gov\n443-634-0721\nAbout the National Security Agency\nFounded in 1952, NSA is a U.S. Department of War combat support agency and element of the U.S. Intelligence Community. The Agency’s mission is to provide foreign signals intelligence to policy makers and our military, and to prevent and eradicate cybersecurity threats to U.S. National Security Systems, with a focus on the Defense Industrial Base and the improvement of U.S. weapons’ security. From protecting U.S. warfighters around the world to enabling and supporting operations on land, in the air, at sea, in space, and in the cyber domain, NSA is committed to building public trust through transparency and protecting civil liberties and privacy consistent with our nation’s values.\n###\nSHARE\nPRINT\ncybersecurity\ncybersecurity advisory\nCybersecurity Guidance","content_cn":"主页新闻办公室新闻稿与声明查看新闻稿\n照片信息\n下载\n详情\n分享\n联合 CSA：与中国有关联的黑客组织 QTFY 利用恶意分布式系统攻击军事和关键基础设施\n联合 CSA：与中国有关联的黑客组织 QTFY 利用恶意分布式系统攻击军事和关键基础设施\n新闻稿 | 2026年8月26日\nNSA 与 FBI 联合发布关于中国黑客组织 QTFY 网络活动的警告\n马里兰州米德堡——今天，国家安全局（NSA）与联邦调查局（FBI）及网络国家任务部队（CNMF）联合发布了一份网络安全公告，题为《与中国有关联的黑客组织 QTFY 利用恶意分布式系统攻击军事和关键基础设施》。\n该指南提醒各组织注意，与中国有关联的网络威胁行为者使用 QTFY、QT 和 QTCYBER 等缩写，开发了恶意分布式平台，以入侵美国及外国组织的网络。\n自2018年成立以来，与中国有关联的黑客组织 QTFY 已开发恶意工具，在自由职业黑客网络中交易恶意软件和漏洞利用程序，建立并维护混淆僵尸网络，并最终将美国的关键系统作为目标。来自多个领域的组织均遭到攻击，包括国防工业基础、电信、地方政府和高等教育等。\nQTFY 行为者开发了可相互配合运行的品牌产品，包括漏洞扫描和利用平台“QScan”，用于开展侦察、利用存在漏洞的物联网（IoT）设备以及识别网络中的漏洞；名为“QTRouter”的混淆网络，用于混入合法用户之中；以及至少三个可管理被攻陷 IoT 设备僵尸网络、并将其作为混淆网络节点纳入的平台（“代理平台管理”、“代理池管理系统”和“QTBotnet”）。\nQTFY 威胁行为者利用零日和 N 日漏洞获得对受害者网络的初始访问权限，并从被攻陷系统中获取合法凭证以维持持久访问；他们还通过自由职业黑客网络以及恶意网络承包和分包市场，活跃于漏洞开发社区。\n撰写该公告的机构建议各组织根据 QTFY 的活动采取以下缓解措施，以改善整体网络安全态势：\n为本组织的设备安装最新的软件和固件更新。\n定期审计本组织的网页和面向互联网的应用程序，以保护运营信息免遭非预期披露。\n将关键系统与边缘设备隔离。\n搜寻所提供的入侵指标。\n访问我们的完整资料库，获取更多网络安全信息和技术指南。\n访问零信任实施指南，了解可调整的互动式网络安全框架。\n访问网络安全协作中心页面，探索面向国防工业基础承包商的免费资源。\nNSA 媒体关系\nMediaRelations@nsa.gov\n443-634-0721\n关于国家安全局\nNSA 成立于1952年，是美国战争部的作战支援机构，也是美国情报界的组成部分。该机构的使命是向政策制定者和军方提供外国信号情报，并预防和根除针对美国国家安全系统的网络安全威胁，重点关注国防工业基础和提高美国武器的安全性。从保护世界各地的美国作战人员，到促成和支持陆地、空中、海上、太空及网络领域的行动，NSA 致力于通过透明度建立公众信任，并以符合国家价值观的方式保护公民自由和隐私。\n###\n分享\n打印\n网络安全\n网络安全公告\n网络安全指南","publish_source":"nsa.gov","publish_time":"2026-08-26T07:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3077","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"纽约公民自由联盟谴责国土安全部在哥伦比亚大学非法拘留学生活动人士","title_en":"NYCLU Condemns Unlawful DHS Detention of Student Activist at Columbia - NYCLU","summary_cn":"2025年3月9日，纽约公民自由联盟发表声明，谴责美国国土安全部特工于3月8日晚进入哥伦比亚大学学生宿舍，拘留巴勒斯坦学生、绿卡持有者马哈茂德·哈利勒。哈利勒是2024年加沙团结营地亲巴勒斯坦抗议者的主要谈判代表之一，在与其怀孕八个月的妻子用完开斋晚餐返回时被拘留。特工声称国务院吊销了其学生签证，但哈利勒持有绿卡。NYCLU执行主任唐娜·利伯曼称此举是针对性、报复性的，是对其第一修正案权利的极端攻击。","summary_en":"On March 9, 2025, the New York Civil Liberties Union issued a statement condemning U.S. Department of Homeland Security agents for detaining Mahmoud Khalil, a Palestinian student and green-card holder at Columbia University, on the night of March 8. Khalil, a lead negotiator for pro-Palestine protesters at the 2024 Gaza solidarity encampment, was detained while returning from an iftar dinner with his eight-months-pregnant wife. Agents claimed the State Department revoked his student visa, but Khalil holds a green card. NYCLU Executive Director Donna Lieberman called the detention targeted, retaliatory, and an extreme attack on his First Amendment rights.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:01:04.079673+00:00","url":"https://www.nyclu.org/press-release/nyclu-condemns-unlawful-dhs-detention-of-student-activist-at-columbia","content":"Information\nMedia Contact:\nNYCLU Media212-607-3372 media@nyclu.org\nSee all\nPress Release\nInformation\nMedia Contact:\nNYCLU Media212-607-3372 media@nyclu.org\nMar 9, 2025\nPress Release\nNYCLU Condemns Unlawful DHS Detention of Student Activist at Columbia\nFree Speech\nStella Ragas / Columbia Spectator\nShare:\nNEW YORK, NY — On Saturday night, Department of Homeland Security (DHS) agents entered a student residential building at Columbia University in uptown New York and detained Mahmoud Khalil, a Palestinian student and one of the lead negotiators on behalf of pro-Palestine protesters at 2024’s Gaza solidarity encampment. Mr. Khalil, a U.S. green-card holder, was detained while returning from an iftar dinner with his wife, who is currently eight-months pregnant.\nAccording to Dropsite News, Mr. Khalil and his wife had just unlocked the door to their building when two plainclothes DHS agents pushed inside behind them. The agents allegedly did not identify themselves at first, instead asking for Mr. Khalil’s identity, before then detaining him. The agents then claimed that the State Department had revoked Khalil’s student visa, with one agent presenting what he claimed was a warrant on his cell phone. But Khalil has a green card.\nIn response, the New York Civil Liberties Union issued the following statement, attributable to Executive Director Donna Lieberman:\n“The Trump administration’s detention of Mahmoud Khalil — a green card holder studying in this country legally — is targeted, retaliatory, and an extreme attack on his First Amendment rights.”\n“The unlawful detention of Mr. Khalil reeks of McCarthyism. It’s clear that the Trump administration is selectively punishing Mr. Khalil for expressing views that aren’t MAGA-approved — which is a frightening escalation of Trump’s crackdown on pro-Palestine speech, and an aggressive abuse of immigration law.”\n“Ripping a student from their home, challenging their immigration status, and detaining them solely based on political viewpoint will chill student speech and advocacy across campus. Political speech should never be a basis of punishment, or lead to deportation.”\n###\nRelated Content\nMar 7, 2025\nPress Release\nNYCLU on Trump Admin Cancelling $400 Million of Funds to Columbia University\nFree Speech\nEducation\nShare:\nAs bold as the spirit of New York, we are the NYCLU.\nDonate\nHome\nIssues\nAbout\nHow We Work\nOur People\nRegional Offices\nCareers\nContact Us\nResources\nCampaigns & Actions\nKnow Your Rights\nPolicy\nCourt Cases\nData\nIdeas\nCommentary\nPodcasts\nReports\nGet Involved\nTake Action\nVolunteer\nEvents\nPress\nStatements\nUser Agreement\nPrivacy Statement\nWebsite Accessibility\nFederal Transparency In Coverage\n© 2026 New YorkCivil Liberties Union","content_cn":"信息\n媒体联系人：\nNYCLU媒体部 212-607-3372 media@nyclu.org\n查看全部\n新闻稿\n信息\n媒体联系人：\nNYCLU媒体部 212-607-3372 media@nyclu.org\n2025年3月9日\n新闻稿\nNYCLU谴责国土安全部非法拘留哥伦比亚大学学生活动人士\n言论自由\nStella Ragas /《哥伦比亚观察家报》\n分享：\n\n纽约州纽约市——周六晚上，国土安全部（DHS）特工进入位于纽约上城的哥伦比亚大学学生宿舍楼，拘留了Mahmoud Khalil。他是一名巴勒斯坦学生，也是2024年加沙声援营地中代表支持巴勒斯坦抗议者进行主要谈判的人之一。Khalil先生是美国绿卡持有者；他在与目前怀孕8个月的妻子参加开斋晚餐后返回途中被拘留。\n\n据Dropsite News报道，Khalil先生和妻子刚刚打开住所大门，两名便衣DHS特工便从他们身后推门进入。据称，特工起初没有表明身份，而是询问Khalil先生的身份，随后将其拘留。特工接着声称国务院已撤销Khalil的学生签证，其中一名特工在手机上出示了一份他声称是逮捕令的文件。但Khalil持有绿卡。\n\n对此，纽约公民自由联盟发表了以下声明，声明归属于执行董事Donna Lieberman：\n\n“特朗普政府拘留Mahmoud Khalil——一名在这个国家合法学习的绿卡持有者——是有针对性的报复行为，是对他第一修正案权利的极端攻击。”\n\n“对Khalil先生的非法拘留充满麦卡锡主义气息。很明显，特朗普政府正在选择性地惩罚Khalil，只因为他表达了未经‘让美国再次伟大’（MAGA）认可的观点——这是特朗普打压支持巴勒斯坦言论的可怕升级，也是对移民法的肆意滥用。”\n\n“把一名学生从家中带走，质疑其移民身份，并仅仅基于政治观点拘留他，将使整个校园的学生言论和倡导活动受到寒蝉效应。政治言论绝不应成为惩罚的依据，也不应导致驱逐出境。”\n\n###\n相关内容\n2025年3月7日\n新闻稿\nNYCLU就特朗普政府取消哥伦比亚大学4亿美元资金一事发表声明\n言论自由\n教育\n分享：\n\n我们与纽约的精神一样勇敢，这就是NYCLU。\n\n捐赠\n首页\n议题\n关于\n我们的工作方式\n我们的人员\n地区办公室\n职业机会\n联系我们\n资源\n竞选活动与行动\n了解你的权利\n政策\n法院案件\n数据\n观点\n评论\n播客\n报告\n参与其中\n采取行动\n志愿服务\n活动\n新闻\n声明\n用户协议\n隐私声明\n网站无障碍\n联邦透明度保险范围\n© 2026纽约公民自由联盟","publish_source":"nyclu.org","publish_time":"2025-03-09T20:45:16+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2075","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"纽约公民自由联盟就FBI渗透纽约法院监督团体发表声明","title_en":"NYCLU Statement on FBI Infiltration of NYC Court Monitor Group  - NYCLU","summary_cn":"纽约公民自由联盟就当日报道称联邦调查局渗透一个监督移民法庭ICE活动的团体私密Signal聊天群发表声明。执行主任利伯曼批评FBI对纽约人受宪法保护的维权与结社活动进行无端监视，称其为麦卡锡与胡佛时代越权行为的重演，并指将和平法院监督者标为“暴力极端分子”是滥用权力。声明还要求纽约市警局解释其角色，并强调法院监督属第一修正案保护活动。","summary_en":"The New York Civil Liberties Union issued a statement responding to reporting that the FBI infiltrated the private Signal chat of a group monitoring ICE activity at immigration courts. Executive Director Donna Lieberman condemned what she called baseless surveillance of New Yorkers' constitutionally protected activism and association, calling it a return to discredited McCarthy- and Hoover-era overreach, and said labeling peaceful court monitors 'violent extremists' shows willful misuse of FBI powers. The statement also demands the NYPD explain its unclear role and stresses court monitoring is First Amendment-protected activity.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:29:37.116372+00:00","url":"https://www.nyclu.org/press-release/nyclu-statement-on-fbi-infiltration-of-nyc-court-monitor-group","content":"Information\nMedia Contact:\nSebastian Krueger(212) 607-3372media@nyclu.org\nSee all\nPress Release\nInformation\nMedia Contact:\nSebastian Krueger(212) 607-3372media@nyclu.org\nNov 21, 2025\nPress Release\nNYCLU Statement on FBI Infiltration of NYC Court Monitor Group\nPolicing\nPrivacy\nShare:\nNEW YORK CITY — In response to new reporting this morning that the FBI infiltrated the private signal chat of a group monitoring ICE activity at immigration courts, the New York Civil Liberties Union released the following statement from Executive Director Donna Lieberman:\n“The FBI baselessly spying on New Yorkers for their constitutionally-protected activism and association is a return to the discredited, dangerous — and often lawless — overreach of the McCarthy and Hoover eras. Labeling peaceful court monitors ‘violent extremists’ shows that the FBI under Trump, Bondi, and Patel is either delusional or, more likely, willfully misusing its enormous powers.\n“The NYPD needs to immediately explain its role in this, which is unclear from today’s reporting. The NYPD must also reject the unacceptable political basis for this kind of investigation. The NYCLU has won landmark cases against the NYPD for spying on New Yorkers for their religious and political beliefs. New Yorkers have the right to know whose interests the department is serving.\n“Let’s be clear, ICE is a rogue, secretive, and abusive agency that has been misusing courts like those at 26 Federal Plaza for its cruel agenda. Court monitoring isn’t just important because it attempts to deliver some measure of transparency, it is also First Amendment-protected activity.”\nRelated Content\nJan 7, 2016\nPress Release\nLandmark Settlement Protects American Muslims From Discriminatory NYPD Surveillance\nFree Speech\nCriminal Legal System\nPrivacy\nJul 6, 2007\nCourt Cases\nHandschu v. Special Services Division (Challenging NYPD surveillance practices targeting political groups)\nPolicing\nPrivacy\nShare:\nAs bold as the spirit of New York, we are the NYCLU.\nDonate\nHome\nIssues\nAbout\nHow We Work\nOur People\nRegional Offices\nCareers\nContact Us\nResources\nCampaigns & Actions\nKnow Your Rights\nPolicy\nCourt Cases\nData\nIdeas\nCommentary\nPodcasts\nReports\nGet Involved\nTake Action\nVolunteer\nEvents\nPress\nStatements\nUser Agreement\nPrivacy Statement\nWebsite Accessibility\nFederal Transparency In Coverage\n© 2026 New YorkCivil Liberties Union","content_cn":"信息\n媒体联系人：\nSebastian Krueger\n(212) 607-3372\nmedia@nyclu.org\n查看全部\n新闻稿\n信息\n媒体联系人：\nSebastian Krueger\n(212) 607-3372\nmedia@nyclu.org\n2025年11月21日\n新闻稿\n纽约公民自由联盟就联邦调查局渗透纽约市法院监督小组发表声明\n警务\n隐私\n分享：\n纽约市——针对今天上午有关联邦调查局渗透一个在移民法院监督ICE行动的小组私人Signal聊天群的新报道，纽约公民自由联盟发布了执行主任Donna Lieberman的以下声明：\n“联邦调查局毫无根据地监视纽约人受宪法保护的维权活动和结社行为，这是回到了麦卡锡和胡佛时代那种声名狼藉、危险且往往无法无天的越权行为。把和平的法院监督员称为‘暴力极端分子’，表明特朗普、邦迪和帕特尔领导下的联邦调查局要么妄想，要么更可能是故意滥用其巨大的权力。\n“纽约市警察局需要立即解释其在此事中的作用，而今天的报道尚未说明这一点。纽约市警察局还必须拒绝这种调查不可接受的政治依据。纽约公民自由联盟曾在具有里程碑意义的案件中起诉纽约市警察局，制止其因纽约人的宗教和政治信仰而进行监视。纽约人有权知道该部门在为谁的利益服务。\n“让我们明确，ICE是一个失控、秘密且滥用职权的机构，一直在滥用26 Federal Plaza等法院，为其残酷的议程服务。法院监督不仅重要，因为它试图带来一定程度的透明度，也因为这项活动受到第一修正案保护。”\n相关内容\n2016年1月7日\n新闻稿\n里程碑式和解保护美国穆斯林免受纽约市警察局歧视性监视\n言论自由\n刑事法律体系\n隐私\n2007年7月6日\n法院案件\nHandschu诉特别服务处（挑战针对政治团体的纽约市警察局监视做法）\n警务\n隐私\n分享：\n我们和纽约的精神一样大胆，我们就是纽约公民自由联盟。\n捐赠\n首页\n议题\n关于\n我们的工作方式\n我们的人员\n地区办事处\n职业机会\n联系我们\n资源\n竞选活动与行动\n了解你的权利\n政策\n法院案件\n数据\n观点\n评论\n播客\n报告\n参与其中\n采取行动\n志愿服务\n活动\n新闻\n声明\n用户协议\n隐私声明\n网站无障碍\n联邦透明度保险范围\n© 2026 纽约公民自由联盟","publish_source":"nyclu.org","publish_time":"2025-11-21T15:53:13+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1011","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"弗吉尼亚州总检察长杰伊·琼斯就朴茨茅斯据报FBI活动发表声明","title_en":"Attorney General Jay Jones Statement on Reported FBI Activity in Portsmouth","summary_cn":"弗吉尼亚州总检察长杰伊·琼斯就当日据报在朴茨茅斯发生的联邦调查局活动发表声明。他表示目前掌握的信息不足，但指出美国弗吉尼亚东区联邦检察官办公室此前的若干行动已削弱公众对该办公室的信心，包括针对前FBI局长詹姆斯·科米和纽约州总检察长利蒂希娅·詹姆斯的起诉均被法官在审前驳回。他呼吁在相关事实明确前保持克制判断。","summary_en":"Virginia Attorney General Jay Jones released a statement on reported FBI activity in Portsmouth, saying there is insufficient information about the reported activity. He noted that several previous actions of the U.S. Attorney's Office for the Eastern District of Virginia have undermined public confidence in that office, including failed prosecutions against former FBI Director James Comey and New York Attorney General Letitia James that were dismissed by a judge well before trial. He urged restraint in judgment until the relevant facts are known.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T10:48:22.210218+00:00","url":"https://www.oag.state.va.us/media-center/news-releases/3018-attorney-general-jay-jones-statement-on-reported-fbi-activity-in-portsmouth","content":"Commonwealth of Virginia\nOffice of the Attorney General\nJay Jones\nAttorney General\n202 North 9th Street\nRichmond, Virginia 23219\n804-786-2071\nFAX 804-786-1991\nVirginia Relay Service\n800-828-1120\nFor media inquiries only, contact:\nRae Pickett\nRPickett@oag.state.va.us\nAttorney General Jay Jones Statement on Reported FBI Activity in Portsmouth\nRICHMOND, Va. — Virginia Attorney General Jay Jones released the following statement on this morning’s reported FBI activity in Portsmouth:\n“We simply do not have sufficient information about the reported FBI activity in Portsmouth. However, several previous actions of the U.S. Attorney’s Office for the Eastern District of Virginia have undermined public confidence in that office. These include the failed prosecutions against President Trump’s stated political enemies, former FBI Director James Comey and New York Attorney General Letitia James that were both dismissed by a judge well before trial. I urge everyone to exercise restraint in judgment until the relevant facts are known in this matter.\"\nPublished on: May 6, 2026\n###","content_cn":"弗吉尼亚联邦\n总检察长办公室\nJay Jones\n总检察长\n202 North 9th Street\nRichmond, Virginia 23219\n804-786-2071\n传真 804-786-1991\n弗吉尼亚中继服务\n800-828-1120\n仅限媒体咨询，请联系：\nRae Pickett\nRPickett@oag.state.va.us\n总检察长 Jay Jones 就报道的联邦调查局在朴次茅斯开展行动发表声明\n弗吉尼亚州里士满——弗吉尼亚州总检察长 Jay Jones 就今晨报道的联邦调查局在朴次茅斯开展行动发表以下声明：\n“对于报道的联邦调查局在朴次茅斯开展的行动，我们确实没有足够信息。然而，美国弗吉尼亚东区检察官办公室此前的数项行动已经削弱了公众对该办公室的信任。其中包括针对特朗普总统所称政治敌人的失败起诉：前联邦调查局局长 James Comey 和纽约州总检察长 Letitia James 的案件均在审判前很久就被法官驳回。我敦促所有人在了解本案相关事实之前，对判断保持克制。”\n发表于：2026年5月6日\n###","publish_source":"oag.state.va.us","publish_time":"2026-05-06T07:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-163","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Bing News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美国国土安全部宣称边境连续16个月零释放：“后果回来了”","title_en":"DHS touts 16 months with 0 releases at the border: ‘Consequences are back’ – One America News Network","summary_cn":"美国国土安全部宣布，在特朗普政府治下南部边境已连续16个月未释放任何非法入境者。国土安全部长马林称“抓了就放”政策已终结，非法越境降至历史低点。该部门称8月边境巡逻队逮捕量较上月下降5%至8870人，较拜登政府月均水平低94%，并列举本财年毒品查获量较拜登时期同期高73%等数据。海关与边境保护局局长斯科特表示，非法越境者将被逮捕、拘留并遣返，而非释放入境。","summary_en":"The U.S. Department of Homeland Security announced 16 consecutive months with zero releases at the Southern Border under the Trump administration. DHS Secretary Markwayne Mullin said catch and release is over and illegal crossings have fallen to historic lows. The department said August Border Patrol apprehensions fell 5% from July to 8,870, 94% below the Biden administration's monthly average, and cited fiscal-year drug seizures up 73% versus the same period under Biden. CBP Commissioner Rodney Scott said illegal crossers are apprehended, detained and removed, not released into the country.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T05:45:31.965691+00:00","url":"https://www.oann.com/newsroom/dhs-touts-16-months-with-0-releases-at-the-border-consequences-are-back","content":"DHS touts 16 months with 0 releases at the border: ‘Consequences are back’\n(Background) U.S. Secretary of the Department of Homeland Security Markwayne Mullin on September 09, 2026, in Dallas, Texas. (Photo by Brandon Bell/Getty Images) / (L) The seal of the Department of Homeland Security is seen on a podium at a press conference at the US Citizenship and Immigration Services Headquarters on February 23, 2015, in Washington, D.C. (Photo by MANDEL NGAN/AFP via Getty Images)\nOAN Staff Katherine Mosack9:20 AM – Wednesday, September 16, 2026\nThe U.S. Department of Homeland Security (DHS) announced that there has been 16 consecutive months with zero releases at the Southern Border under President Donald Trump’s administration.\n“Sixteen straight months of zero releases at the border proves that President Trump’s policies are working,” said DHS Secretary Markwayne Mullin in a statement on Wednesday. “Catch and release is over, consequences are back, and illegal crossings have fallen to historic lows. President Trump made a promise to the American people that he would make America safe again, and he has done just that by giving us the most secure border in American history.”\n16 STRAIGHT MONTHS of ZERO RELEASES AT THE BORDER. Under @POTUS Trump, catch and release is over, consequences are back, and illegal crossings have fallen to HISTORIC lows. The border is secure. 🇺🇸 pic.twitter.com/wvK0gEAdAf— Secretary Markwayne Mullin (@SecMullinDHS) September 16, 2026\nThe department touted “historic levels” of border enforcement, with daily apprehensions down 94% from the previous administration, led by former President Joe Biden.\nAccording to the DHS’s statement, Border Patrol apprehensions decreased in August, remaining historically low. Apprehensions along the Southwest Border fell 5% lower than in July, at 8,870. Apprehensions across the total border were also 3% lower than the previous month, at 10,939.\nBorder Patrol apprehensions were 94% below the Biden administration’s monthly average and 97% below the prior administration’s peak in December 2023.\nThe DHS noted that while no illegal aliens were released in August this year, the Biden Border Patrol released 10,186 individuals along the southwest border in August 2024.\n“Border security starts with knowing who and what is entering our country – and controlling it on behalf of the American people,” stated Customs and Border Protection (CBP) Commissioner Rodney Scott. “That means people who cross illegally are apprehended, detained and removed – not released into the United States. Our agents are enforcing that standard every day.”\nAdditionally, the DHS touted its accomplishments in drug interdictions, which include:\n73% higher drug seizures this fiscal year than during the same period under Biden;\nFiscal year drug seizures 61% higher than the average of the last four fiscal year periods;\nA 28% increase in cocaine seizures in August 2026, with 6,724 pounds seized;\n773 pounds of fentanyl seized in August 2026;\nOver 120,000 pounds of precursor chemicals used to make illicit drugs seized by CBP and Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) in August 2026.\nStay informed! Receive breaking news alerts directly to your inbox for free. Subscribe here. https://www.oann.com/alerts\nWhat do YOU think? Click here to jump to the comments!\nSponsored Content Below\nShare this post!\nFacebook\nTwitter\nEmail\nLinkedIn\nPost navigation\nPrevious Previous post: Ed Sheeran’s tour in trouble after opening acts drop out in solidarity with rapper MacklemoreNext Next post: ISIS-K operative sentenced to 20 years for terror conspiracy tied to Abbey Gate attack","content_cn":"国土安全部夸耀边境连续16个月零释放：“后果机制回归”\n（背景）2026年9月9日，美国国土安全部部长 Markwayne Mullin 在得克萨斯州达拉斯。（Brandon Bell/Getty Images 摄）/（左）2015年2月23日，美国国土安全部印章出现在华盛顿特区美国公民及移民服务局总部新闻发布会的讲台上。（MANDEL NGAN/AFP via Getty Images 摄）\nOAN员工 Katherine Mosack 2026年9月16日星期三上午9:20\n美国国土安全部（DHS）宣布，在总统 Donald Trump 政府任内，南部边境已连续16个月没有任何释放。\nDHS部长 Markwayne Mullin 周三在声明中说：“边境连续16个月零释放证明，Trump总统的政策正在奏效。‘抓捕后释放’已经结束，后果机制回归，非法越境已降至历史低位。Trump总统曾向美国人民承诺，让美国再次安全；而他通过为我们带来美国历史上最安全的边境，确实做到了这一点。”\n连续16个月边境零释放。在@POTUS Trump领导下，抓捕后释放已经结束，后果机制回归，非法越境降至历史低位。边境安全。🇺🇸 pic.twitter.com/wvK0gEAdAf—Secretary Markwayne Mullin (@SecMullinDHS)，2026年9月16日\n该部门夸耀边境执法达到“历史水平”，每日逮捕量较前任总统 Joe Biden 领导的政府下降94%。\n根据DHS声明，边境巡逻队8月逮捕人数下降，仍处于历史低位。西南边境逮捕人数较7月下降5%，为8,870人。整个边境的逮捕人数也较上月下降3%，为10,939人。\n边境巡逻队逮捕人数比Biden政府月均水平低94%，比前任政府在2023年12月的峰值低97%。\nDHS指出，今年8月没有释放任何非法外国人，而Biden政府的边境巡逻队在2024年8月沿西南边境释放了10,186人。\n海关与边境保护局（CBP）局长 Rodney Scott 表示：“边境安全始于了解谁、什么正在进入我国，并代表美国人民对其进行控制。这意味着非法越境者会被逮捕、拘留并遣返，而不是被释放进入美国。我们的特工每天都在执行这一标准。”\n此外，DHS还夸耀其缉毒成果，包括：\n本财年查获毒品数量比Biden任内同期高73%；\n本财年查获毒品数量比过去四个财年同期平均值高61%；\n2026年8月可卡因查获量增加28%，共查获6,724磅；\n2026年8月查获773磅芬太尼；\n2026年8月，CBP和移民及海关执法局（ICE）查获超过120,000磅用于制造非法毒品的前体化学品。\n及时了解信息！免费订阅，直接将突发新闻提醒发送到您的收件箱。点击此处订阅：https://www.oann.com/alerts\n你怎么看？点击此处跳转至评论区！\n下方为赞助内容\n分享此帖！\nFacebook\nTwitter\n电子邮件\nLinkedIn\n帖子导航\n上一篇上一篇帖子：Ed Sheeran的巡演因开场嘉宾退出声援说唱歌手Macklemore而陷入困境\n下一篇下一帖子：与Abbey Gate袭击有关的恐怖阴谋中，ISIS-K行动人员被判20年监禁","publish_source":"oann.com","publish_time":"2026-09-16T09:30:46+00:00"}},{"id":"news-529","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"FBI局长在柬埔寨就加强安全合作打击网络诈骗犯罪举行会谈","title_en":"FBI chief hold talks in Cambodia over closer security cooperation to combat online scam crimes","summary_cn":"据美联社报道，美国联邦调查局局长卡什·帕特尔在柬埔寨金边与首相洪玛奈举行会谈，讨论联合打击每年骗取受害者数十亿美元的国际网络诈骗活动。此前联合国毒品和犯罪问题办公室报告估计，2025年亚太地区诈骗损失高达1140亿美元。帕特尔此前在泰国出席由美泰共同主办、近20国代表参加的诈骗中心问题峰会，并与泰国总理讨论合作打击诈骗中心及人口贩运、毒品贩运等跨国犯罪。","summary_en":"FBI Director Kash Patel held talks with Cambodian Prime Minister Hun Manet in Phnom Penh on joint efforts to combat international online scam operations that cheat victims out of billions of dollars annually, the Associated Press reported. A UN Office on Drugs and Crime report estimated scam losses in the Asia-Pacific region alone reached as much as $114 billion in 2025. Patel arrived from Thailand, which co-hosted a two-day U.S.-backed summit on scam centers attended by nearly 20 countries, and met Thai officials on cooperation against scam centers, human trafficking and drug trafficking.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T20:22:11.816460+00:00","url":"https://www.ocregister.com/2026/07/22/cambodia-us-scams","content":"NewsWorld News\nFBI chief hold talks in Cambodia over closer security cooperation to combat online scam crimes\nCambodia this year claims to have accelerated a crackdown on the scam operations, whose proliferation there is “a top priority for the U.S. government,” according a U.S. Embassy statement.Share this:\nShare on Facebook (Opens in new window)\nFacebook\nShare on X (Opens in new window)\nX\nShare on Reddit (Opens in new window)\nReddit\nPrint (Opens in new window)\nPrint\nIn this photo provided by Agence Kampuchea Press (AKP), U.S. FBI director Kash Patel, left, meets Cambodian Prime Minister Hun Manat at his offices in Phnom Penh, Cambodia on Thursday, July 22, 2026. (AKP via AP)\nBy Associated PressPUBLISHED: July 22, 2026 at 11:18 AM PDT | UPDATED: July 22, 2026 at 11:22 AM PDTGetting your Trinity Audio player ready...By SOPHENG CHEANG\nPHNOM PENH, Cambodia (AP) — FBI Director Kash Patel held talks with Cambodian Prime Minister Hun Manet on Wednesday on joint efforts to combat international online scam operations that cheat victims out of billions of dollars each year.\nA report issued Tuesday by the United Nations Office of Drugs and Crime estimated that scams caused estimated losses in the Asia-Pacific region alone of as much as $114 billion in 2025. It warned that even as authorities conduct raids on industrial-sized scam centers mostly located in border regions of Myanmar, Laos, and Cambodia, criminals have responded by shifting to other parts of the world or operating on a smaller scale.\nPatel arrived in Cambodia from neighboring Thailand which co-hosted a two-day summit with the U.S. this week on scam centers that was attended by representatives of nearly 20 countries and hundreds of law enforcement officials.\nThe head of the top U.S. law enforcement agency met with Thai Prime Minister Anutin Charnvirakul for talks on joint cooperation to tackle scam centers and other transnational crimes, such as human trafficking and drug trafficking, particularly through information sharing and intelligence exchanges.\nIn this photo provided by Agence Kampuchea Press (AKP), U.S. FBI director Kash Patel, left, meets Cambodian Prime Minister Hun Manat at his offices in Phnom Penh, Cambodia on Thursday, July 22, 2026. (AKP via AP)\nCybercrime has flourished in Southeast Asia where law enforcement is weak, particularly in Cambodia and Myanmar. Scam centers are also linked to human trafficking. Thousands of foreign nationals who are employed to run “romance” and cryptocurrency scams to swindle victims are often recruited with false job offers and then forced to work in conditions of near-slavery.\nCambodia this year claims to have accelerated a crackdown on the scam operations, whose proliferation there is “a top priority for the U.S. government,” according a U.S. Embassy statement. Washington appreciates Hun Manet’s pledge to counter this problem and is coordinating closely with the Cambodian government to battle the problem, it said.\nRelated Articles\nPolice say gunman shouted antisemitic threat before opening fire outside Ontario synagogue\nChinese auto companies race ahead on EV technology, achieving 5-minute ultrafast charging\nA (permanent) seat at the table of power: African leaders press UN for Security Council reform\nXi says 2 giant pandas from China will arrive in the coming days at Atlanta’s zoo\nPope Leo heads to Lourdes: What to know about one of Catholicism’s most famous pilgrimage sites\nThe embassy said Patel’s visit “reinforces the need for strong, joint efforts to combat transnational cybercrime, including increased efforts to hold high-level scam bosses accountable and for improved information sharing regarding complicit officials and scam compound owners.”\nThe U.S. also “fully supports efforts to ensure justice for victims and criminal accountability for online scam operators, those who enable or protect them, and those complicit in related human trafficking crimes,” it added.\nCambodia will host an international conference on combating cyber scams in September, Hun Manet said in a post on his Telegram social media account.\nShare this:\nShare on Facebook (Opens in new window)\nFacebook\nShare on X (Opens in new window)\nX\nShare on Reddit (Opens in new window)\nReddit\nPrint (Opens in new window)\nPrint\nMost PopularMost PopularClaim Jumper steakhouse in Buena Park closesClaim Jumper steakhouse in Buena Park closesIrvine police find suspected pipe bomb in car at Chevron station; 2 men arrestedIrvine police find suspected pipe bomb in car at Chevron station; 2 men arrestedDiane Keaton’s former Laguna Beach flip lists for $10.3MDiane Keaton’s former Laguna Beach flip lists for $10.3MLast chance to buy Disneyland tickets before price increaseLast chance to buy Disneyland tickets before price increaseAllegations Anaheim mayor made racist remarks, sexual orientation comments to be reviewed, city confirmsAllegations Anaheim mayor made racist remarks, sexual orientation comments to be reviewed, city confirmsBest of Orange County 2026: Best barbecue restaurantBest of Orange County 2026: Best barbecue restaurantFiery crash along 91 in Corona kills 1 womanFiery crash along 91 in Corona kills 1 womanFormer child actor Jonathan Taylor Thomas arrested after allegedly kicking LA deputy in stomachFormer child actor Jonathan Taylor Thomas arrested after allegedly kicking LA deputy in stomachBest of Orange County 2026: Readers pick the top waterfront restaurantsBest of Orange County 2026: Readers pick the top waterfront restaurantsEx-Inland woman seeking divorce doubted husband would kill her. Minutes later, she was deadEx-Inland woman seeking divorce doubted husband would kill her. Minutes later, she was dead\nMore in World News\nPolice say gunman shouted antisemitic threat before opening fire outside Canadian synagogue\nChinese auto companies race ahead on EV technology, achieving 5-minute ultrafast charging\nA (permanent) seat at the table of power: African leaders press UN for Security Council reform\nXi says 2 giant pandas from China will arrive in the coming days at Atlanta’s zoo\n2026\nJuly\n22","content_cn":"NewsWorld 新闻\nFBI局长在柬埔寨就加强安全合作打击网络诈骗犯罪举行会谈\n柬埔寨声称今年已加快打击诈骗活动；美国大使馆声明称，这些活动在当地蔓延“是美国政府的首要任务”。分享：\n在Facebook上分享（在新窗口中打开）\nFacebook\n在X上分享（在新窗口中打开）\nX\n在Reddit上分享（在新窗口中打开）\nReddit\n打印（在新窗口中打开）\n打印\n柬埔寨国家通讯社（AKP）提供的照片中，美国联邦调查局局长卡什·帕特尔（左）于2026年7月22日星期四在柬埔寨金边的办公室会见柬埔寨首相洪玛奈。（AKP via AP）\n美联社 发布于：2026年7月22日上午11:18 PDT | 更新于：2026年7月22日上午11:22 PDT正在准备您的Trinity Audio播放器……作者：SOPHENG CHEANG\n柬埔寨金边（美联社）——联邦调查局局长卡什·帕特尔周三与柬埔寨首相洪玛奈举行会谈，讨论共同打击国际网络诈骗活动的努力；这些诈骗活动每年使受害者损失数十亿美元。\n联合国毒品和犯罪问题办公室周二发布的一份报告估计，仅亚太地区在2025年因诈骗造成的损失就高达1140亿美元。报告警告说，即使当局对主要位于缅甸、老挝和柬埔寨边境地区的大型诈骗中心展开突袭，犯罪分子也通过转移到世界其他地区或缩小运营规模来应对。\n帕特尔从邻国泰国抵达柬埔寨。本周，泰国与美国共同主办了为期两天的诈骗中心峰会，近20个国家的代表和数百名执法官员出席了峰会。\n美国最高执法机构负责人会见了泰国总理阿努廷·查恩维拉库尔，就共同合作打击诈骗中心及其他跨国犯罪举行会谈，例如人口贩运和毒品贩运，尤其是通过信息共享和情报交流开展合作。\n柬埔寨国家通讯社（AKP）提供的照片中，美国联邦调查局局长卡什·帕特尔（左）于2026年7月22日星期四在柬埔寨金边的办公室会见柬埔寨首相洪玛奈。（AKP via AP）\n东南亚的网络犯罪蓬勃发展，当地执法力量薄弱，尤其是在柬埔寨和缅甸。诈骗中心还与人口贩运有关。数千名受雇从事“恋爱”诈骗和加密货币诈骗、诱骗受害者的外国人，通常是通过虚假工作机会招募，然后被迫在近乎奴役的条件下工作。\n柬埔寨声称今年已加快打击诈骗活动；美国大使馆声明称，这些活动在当地蔓延“是美国政府的首要任务”。声明说，华盛顿赞赏洪玛奈遏制这一问题的承诺，并正与柬埔寨政府密切协调，共同应对这一问题。\n相关文章\n警方称，枪手在安大略省犹太教堂外开枪前高喊反犹太主义威胁\n中国汽车企业在电动汽车技术竞赛中领先，实现5分钟超快充电\n在权力餐桌上获得一席（永久席位）：非洲领导人敦促联合国改革安理会\n习近平称中国两只大熊猫将在未来几天抵达亚特兰大动物园\n利奥教皇前往卢尔德：了解这一最著名天主教朝圣地之一\n大使馆表示，帕特尔的访问“再次强调了对……的需要\n开展强有力的联合行动打击跨国网络犯罪，包括加大力度追究诈骗团伙高层头目的责任，并改善有关涉案官员和诈骗园区所有者的信息共享。”\n美国还补充说，“完全支持为确保受害者获得正义，并追究网络诈骗操作者、为其提供便利或保护的人，以及在相关人口贩运犯罪中共谋者的刑事责任而开展的努力。”\n洪玛奈在其Telegram社交媒体账号的一篇帖子中表示，柬埔寨将于9月主办一场打击网络诈骗的国际会议。\n分享：\n分享到Facebook（在新窗口中打开）\nFacebook\n分享到X（在新窗口中打开）\nX\n分享到Reddit（在新窗口中打开）\nReddit\n打印（在新窗口中打开）\n打印\n最受欢迎最受欢迎\n布埃纳帕克的Claim Jumper牛排馆关闭\n布埃纳帕克的Claim Jumper牛排馆关闭\n欧文警方在雪佛龙加油站一辆汽车内发现疑似管状炸弹；两名男子被捕\n欧文警方在雪佛龙加油站一辆汽车内发现疑似管状炸弹；两名男子被捕\n黛安·基顿位于拉古纳海滩的前翻修房挂牌价1030万美元\n黛安·基顿位于拉古纳海滩的前翻修房挂牌价1030万美元\n涨价前购买迪士尼乐园门票的最后机会\n涨价前购买迪士尼乐园门票的最后机会\n市政府确认，将审查关于阿纳海姆市长发表种族主义言论及涉及性取向评论的指控\n市政府确认，将审查关于阿纳海姆市长发表种族主义言论及涉及性取向评论的指控\n2026年橙县最佳：最佳烧烤餐厅\n2026年橙县最佳：最佳烧烤餐厅\n科罗纳91号公路沿线惨烈车祸致1名女性死亡\n科罗纳91号公路沿线惨烈车祸致1名女性死亡\n前童星乔纳森·泰勒·托马斯因涉嫌踢打洛杉矶警员腹部被捕\n前童星乔纳森·泰勒·托马斯因涉嫌踢打洛杉矶警员腹部被捕\n2026年橙县最佳：读者评选的顶级滨水餐厅\n2026年橙县最佳：读者评选的顶级滨水餐厅\n一名寻求离婚的内陆帝国女子曾怀疑丈夫会杀害她。几分钟后，她死了\n一名寻求离婚的内陆帝国女子曾怀疑丈夫会杀害她。几分钟后，她死了\n更多世界新闻\n警方称，枪手在加拿大犹太教堂外开枪前高喊反犹太主义威胁\n中国汽车企业在电动汽车技术上竞相领先，实现5分钟超快速充电\n一张（永久的）权力餐桌席位：非洲领导人敦促联合国改革安理会\n习近平表示，来自中国的两只大熊猫将在未来几天抵达亚特兰大动物园\n2026年\n7月\n22日","publish_source":"ocregister.com","publish_time":"2026-07-22T18:18:41+00:00"}},{"id":"news-221","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美国国土安全部的成立历程及其开支情况","title_en":"How the Department of Homeland Security was created and what we spend on it – Orange County Register","summary_cn":"文章回顾了2001年9·11袭击后美国国土安全部（DHS）的成立过程：布什总统于袭击后11天设立该部门，2002年《国土安全法》通过后正式成为内阁级部门，2003年3月1日运作。文章还介绍了DHS徽章象征意义及其开支：2025年DHS支出1153亿美元，约占联邦总支出7.04万亿美元的1.6%，2027年总统提议预算超1180亿美元。","summary_en":"The article reviews how the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) was created after the Sept. 11, 2001 attacks: President Bush established it 11 days later, and it became a Cabinet-level department after the Homeland Security Act of 2002, opening March 1, 2003. It also covers the DHS seal's symbolism and spending: DHS spent $115.3 billion in 2025, about 1.6% of $7.04 trillion in federal spending, with a proposed 2027 budget exceeding $118 billion.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T13:42:54.114044+00:00","url":"https://www.ocregister.com/2026/09/11/how-the-department-of-homeland-security-was-created-and-what-we-spend-on-it","content":"NewsNewsNews Based on facts, either observed and verified directly by the reporter, or reported and verified from knowledgeable sources.\nHow the Department of Homeland Security was created and what we spend on it\nYou might be surprised by what percentage of federal spending the DHS receivesShare this:\nShare on Facebook (Opens in new window)\nFacebook\nShare on X (Opens in new window)\nX\nShare on Reddit (Opens in new window)\nReddit\nPrint (Opens in new window)\nPrint\nTwin towers wreckage\nBy Kurt Snibbe | ksnibbe@scng.com | Orange County RegisterPUBLISHED: September 11, 2026 at 2:04 PM PDT Getting your Trinity Audio player ready...Striving for our security\nEleven days after the Sept. 11, 2001, attack on America, President George W. Bush established the U.S. Department of Homeland Security. The agency was created to streamline governmental entities in the fight against terrorism in all its forms.\nThe attack resulted in one of the largest government reorganizations in U.S. history. Here’s a look at how the government reacted to the attack and created the Department of Homeland Security:\nCreating DHS\nSept. 11\nAmerica attacked\nDepartment of Defense begins combat air patrols over U.S. cities\nDepartment of Transportation grounds all U.S. private aircraft\nFEMA activates Federal Response Plan\nU.S. Customs goes to Level 1 alert at all border ports of entry\nHealth and Human Services activates the first National Disaster Medical System\nNuclear Regulatory Commission elevates all nuclear power plants to the highest level of security\nFEMA deploys National Urban Search and Rescue Response team\nFEMA deploys U.S. Army Corps of Engineers to assist debris removal\nSept. 12\nFederal Aviation Administration allows limited reopening of the nation’s commercial airspace\nSept. 13\nDepartments of Justice and Treasury deploy marshals, border patrol and customs officials to provide a larger police presence at reopening airports\nSept. 14\nPresident proclaims a national emergency\nPresident orders ready reserves of armed forces to active duty\nFBI releases list of 19 suspected terrorists\nSept. 17\nAttorney general directs the establishment of 94 anti-terrorism task forces\nSept. 18\nPresident signs authorization for Use of Military Force bill\nPresident authorizes additional disaster funding for New York\nSept. 20\nPresident addresses Congress, announces creation of the Office of Homeland Security and appointment of Gov. Tom Ridge, R-Pennsylvania, as director\nLater\nWith the passage of the Homeland Security Act by Congress in November 2002, the Department of Homeland Security formally came into being as a Cabinet-level department to further coordinate and unify national homeland security efforts, opening its doors on March 1, 2003.\nSymbolism of the seal\nThe eagle’s wings break through the inner circle into the outer ring to suggest that the Department of Homeland Security will break through traditional bureaucracy and perform government functions differently. The eagle’s talon on the left holds an olive branch with 13 leaves and 13 seeds while the eagle’s talon on the right grasps 13 arrows.Centered is a shield divided into three sections containing elements that represent the American homeland – air, land and sea. The top element, a dark blue sky, contains 22 stars representing the original 22 entities that have come together to form the department. The left shield element contains white mountains behind a green plain. The right shield element contains four wave shapes representing the oceans.\nWhat we spend\nIn 2025, the Department of Homeland Security spent $115.3 billion. That was about 1.6% of the $7.04 trillion in overall federal spending. The top three federal departments are Department of Health and Human Services (26.8%), the Social Security Administration (23.4%) and the Department of Defense (13.5%).\nIn 2027, the president proposed a DHS budget of more than $118 billion, which is a 5.7% increase from 2026. The DHS budget was $89.2 billion in 2024 and $80.86 billion in 2022.\nDHS is the third largest agency in the federal government in terms of civilian personnel. Its appropriations bill is the only one of the 12 annual measures that funds a single agency exclusively and in its entirety.\nSome major things in 2027\nThe president’s administration is seeking a 5,364-position staffing reduction for the TSA, the budget for which was further offset by a legislative proposal to provide TSA an additional $1.68 billion in budget authority by providing it the full resources of the Aviation Security Passenger Fee (previous similar proposals similar by this and previous administrations have been unsuccessful).\nA $1.46 billion increase for U.S. Coast Guard.\nA few of the changes DHS has made to protect the U.S.:\nBefore 9/11\nOsama bin Laden summoned operatives to discuss using commercial aircraft as weapons and list potential targets in the United States.\nNow\nThe private sector and the Department of Homeland Security rely on an information-sharing security strategy to identify individuals with the goal of preventing terrorists from entering the country.\nBefore 9/11\nThe hijackers began to obtain passports and visas to travel to the U.S.\nNow\nFederal agencies have a better capacity to vet individuals applying for visas. The Visa Security Program is now operational at 41 posts in 28 countries.\nApril 2001\nThe hijackers began arriving in the U.S. on tourist visas with cash and travelers checks acquired in the Middle East.\nToday\nThe Terrorist Screening Center, the National Counterterrorism Center and other federal entities analyze travel-related data to find patterns of suspected terrorists. The databases have helped intercept suspicious terrorists as well as cargo.\n2001The hijackers enrolled in flight schools and took surveillance flights.\nNow\n“Fusion” centers throughout the country serve as focal points at the state and local level to share threat- and vulnerability-related information.\nThe Nationwide Suspicious Activity Reporting Initiative and the National Crime Information Center have watch lists for law enforcement officers to use.\nThis year\nNew CT scanners at airports are being installed. As of July, the TSA had deployed a total of 1,162 across 296 airports throughout the country. The new scanners are able to produce 3D images of each scanned bag, and TSA officers can rotate X-ray images to more clearly see the contents of passengers’ carry-on luggage.\nNote: The new scanners can severely damage undeveloped photographic film.\nSources: The Department of Homeland Security; various agencies; Whitehouse.gov, Congressional Research Service\nShare this:\nShare on Facebook (Opens in new window)\nFacebook\nShare on X (Opens in new window)\nX\nShare on Reddit (Opens in new window)\nReddit\nPrint (Opens in new window)\nPrint\nMost PopularMost PopularBest of Orange County 2026: Best bakeryBest of Orange County 2026: Best bakerySouth OC Cars and Coffee co-founder was driving the McLaren in fiery Irvine crashSouth OC Cars and Coffee co-founder was driving the McLaren in fiery Irvine crashFrat where Cal State Fullerton student apparently went before death booted for alleged hazingFrat where Cal State Fullerton student apparently went before death booted for alleged hazingUber ordered to pay $40 million to parents of passenger killed on OC highwayUber ordered to pay $40 million to parents of passenger killed on OC highwaySan Clemente declares emergency for beaches, allots $1 million for North Beach sand fillSan Clemente declares emergency for beaches, allots $1 million for North Beach sand fillBest of Orange County 2026: See all the winnersBest of Orange County 2026: See all the winners‘She died doing what she loved’: Friends mourn KNBC aerial reporter Eliana Moreno‘She died doing what she loved’: Friends mourn KNBC aerial reporter Eliana Moreno5 places to find very, very good musubi in Orange County5 places to find very, very good musubi in Orange CountyCosta Mesa approves plan to replace Fairview Developmental Center with 2,300 new homesCosta Mesa approves plan to replace Fairview Developmental Center with 2,300 new homesBest of Orange County 2026: Best breakfast restaurantBest of Orange County 2026: Best breakfast restaurant\nMore in News\nDollywood’s billionaire owners plot future without Dolly Parton\nEarly voting begins in midterm elections that could flip control of Congress\nFor Latter-day Saints, the retention decline is more than a people problem\nAttorney General Blanche’s appearances at political events blur Justice Department boundaries\n2026\nSeptember\n11","content_cn":"新闻新闻新闻 基于事实，这些事实要么由记者直接观察并核实，要么由知情人士报告并核实。\n国土安全部是如何成立的，以及我们在其上的支出\n你可能会惊讶于国土安全部获得的联邦支出比例分享此内容：\n分享到 Facebook（在新窗口中打开）\nFacebook\n分享到 X（在新窗口中打开）\nX\n分享到 Reddit（在新窗口中打开）\nReddit\n打印（在新窗口中打开）\n打印\n双子塔残骸\n作者：Kurt Snibbe | ksnibbe@scng.com | 《橙县纪事报》发表于：2026年9月11日下午2:04（太平洋夏令时）正在准备您的 Trinity Audio 播放器……\n为我们的安全而努力\n2001年9月11日袭击美国后11天，乔治·W·布什总统成立了美国国土安全部。该机构的设立旨在精简政府实体，以打击各种形式的恐怖主义。\n这次袭击导致美国历史上最大规模的政府重组之一。以下是政府如何应对袭击并成立国土安全部的介绍：\n创建国土安全部\n9月11日\n美国遭到袭击\n国防部开始在美国各城市上空进行战斗空中巡逻\n交通部令所有美国私人飞机停飞\n联邦紧急事务管理局启动《联邦响应计划》\n美国海关将所有边境入境口岸提升至一级警戒\n卫生与公众服务部启动首个国家灾难医疗系统\n核管理委员会将所有核电站提升至最高安全级别\n联邦紧急事务管理局部署国家城市搜救响应队\n联邦紧急事务管理局部署美国陆军工程兵团协助清除废墟\n9月12日\n联邦航空管理局允许全国商业空域有限度重新开放\n9月13日\n司法部和财政部部署法警、边境巡逻人员及海关官员，在重新开放的机场提供更大规模的警力\n9月14日\n总统宣布国家进入紧急状态\n总统下令将武装部队的预备役部队调入现役\n联邦调查局公布19名恐怖主义嫌疑人的名单\n9月17日\n司法部长指示成立94个反恐特别工作组\n9月18日\n总统签署《授权使用军事力量法案》\n总统批准为纽约提供额外灾害资金\n9月20日\n总统向国会发表讲话，宣布成立国土安全办公室，并任命宾夕法尼亚州共和党州长汤姆·里奇为主任\n后来\n随着国会于2002年11月通过《国土安全法》，国土安全部正式成立，成为内阁级部门，以进一步协调和统一国家国土安全工作，并于2003年3月1日开始办公。\n徽章的象征意义\n鹰的双翼穿过内圈进入外环，寓意国土安全部将突破传统官僚体系，以不同方式履行政府职能。鹰左侧的利爪握着一根橄榄枝，上有13片叶子和13颗种子；鹰右侧的利爪抓着13支箭。中央是一面分为三个部分的盾牌，其中的元素代表美国本土——空中、陆地和海洋。顶部元素是一片深蓝色天空，其中有22颗星，代表最初联合组成该部门的22个实体。盾牌左侧的元素是在绿色平原后方的白色山脉。盾牌右侧的元素包含四种波浪形状，代表\n海洋。\n我们的支出\n2025年，国土安全部支出了1153亿美元。这约占联邦政府7.04万亿美元总体支出的1.6%。联邦政府规模最大的三个部门是卫生与公众服务部（26.8%）、社会保障局（23.4%）和国防部（13.5%）。\n2027年，总统提议为国土安全部安排超过1180亿美元的预算，较2026年增加5.7%。国土安全部2024年的预算为892亿美元，2022年为808.6亿美元。\n按文职人员数量计算，国土安全部是联邦政府第三大机构。其拨款法案是12项年度拨款措施中唯一一项专门且完整地为单一机构提供资金的法案。\n2027年的一些重大事项\n总统政府正寻求削减运输安全管理局（TSA）5,364个职位的人员编制；与此同时，一项立法提案拟通过向TSA提供航空安全乘客费的全部资源，额外授予其16.8亿美元的预算权限，从而进一步抵消其预算（本届及历届政府此前提出的类似方案均未成功）。\n为美国海岸警卫队增加14.6亿美元。\n国土安全部为保护美国所作的一些改变：\n9/11之前\n奥萨马·本·拉登召集行动人员，讨论利用商业客机作为武器，并列出美国境内的潜在目标。\n现在\n私营部门和国土安全部依靠信息共享安全战略来识别个人，旨在防止恐怖分子进入该国。\n9/11之前\n劫机者开始获取护照和签证，以便前往美国。\n现在\n联邦机构有更强的能力审查签证申请者。签证安全项目目前已在28个国家的41个驻点投入运行。\n2001年4月\n劫机者持旅游签证，携带在中东取得的现金和旅行支票，开始抵达美国。\n如今\n恐怖分子筛查中心、国家反恐中心和其他联邦机构分析与旅行有关的数据，以寻找疑似恐怖分子的模式。这些数据库帮助拦截了可疑恐怖分子以及货物。\n2001年劫机者进入飞行学校，并进行侦察飞行。\n现在\n全国各地的“融合”中心在州和地方层面充当焦点，分享与威胁和脆弱性相关的信息。\n全国可疑活动报告倡议和国家犯罪信息中心设有供执法人员使用的监视名单。\n今年\n机场正在安装新的CT扫描仪。截至7月，TSA已在全国296座机场部署了总计1,162台。新扫描仪能够生成每件被扫描行李的3D图像，TSA官员可以旋转X射线图像，以更清楚地查看乘客随身行李的内容。\n注意：新扫描仪可能严重损坏未冲洗的摄影胶片。\n来源：国土安全部；各机构；Whitehouse.gov；国会研究处\n分享：\n分享到Facebook（在新窗口中打开）\nFacebook\n分享到X（在新窗口中打开）\nX\n分享到Reddit（在新窗口中打开）\nReddit\n打印（在新窗口中打开）\n打印\n最受欢迎最受欢迎橙县最佳2026：最佳面包店橙县最佳2026：最佳面包店South OC Cars and Coffee联合创始人在尔湾火灾车祸中驾驶迈凯伦\n在尔湾起火车祸中驾驶迈凯伦的\n加州州立大学富勒顿分校学生去世前据称加入的兄弟会因涉嫌欺凌新生被除名\n加州州立大学富勒顿分校学生去世前据称加入的兄弟会因涉嫌欺凌新生被除名\nUber被责令向在橙县高速公路上遇难乘客的父母赔偿4000万美元\nUber被责令向在橙县高速公路上遇难乘客的父母赔偿4000万美元\n圣克莱门特宣布海滩进入紧急状态，为北滩填沙拨款100万美元\n圣克莱门特宣布海滩进入紧急状态，为北滩填沙拨款100万美元\n2026年橙县最佳：查看所有获奖者\n2026年橙县最佳：查看所有获奖者\n“她是在做自己热爱的事情时离世的”：朋友悼念KNBC空中记者埃莉安娜·莫雷诺\n“她是在做自己热爱的事情时离世的”：朋友悼念KNBC空中记者埃莉安娜·莫雷诺\n在橙县找到非常、非常美味饭团的5个地方\n在橙县找到非常、非常美味饭团的5个地方\n科斯塔梅萨批准以2300套新住宅取代费尔维尤发展中心的计划\n科斯塔梅萨批准以2300套新住宅取代费尔维尤发展中心的计划\n2026年橙县最佳：最佳早餐餐厅\n2026年橙县最佳：最佳早餐餐厅\n新闻更多内容\n多莉·帕顿的亿万富翁老板们谋划没有多莉·帕顿的未来\n中期选举开始提前投票，国会控制权可能易手\n对后期圣徒来说，留存率下降不仅仅是人口问题\n司法部长布兰奇出席政治活动，模糊了司法部的界限\n2026年\n9月\n11日","publish_source":"ocregister.com","publish_time":"2026-09-11T21:04:32+00:00"}},{"id":"news-3403","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"FBI悬赏2.5万美元征集波特兰和温哥华投票箱袭击案线索","title_en":"FBI offers $25,000 for information about ballot box attacks in Portland, Vancouver - OPB","summary_cn":"OPB于2024年11月13日报道，美国联邦调查局（FBI）悬赏2.5万美元征集有关波特兰和华盛顿州温哥华投票箱纵火袭击案的线索。FBI西雅图办事处负责人Greg Austin表示，调查人员正在寻找一辆深色2003或2004款沃尔沃S-60轿车，嫌疑人使用假车牌。调查由FBI西雅图和波特兰办事处联合主导，嫌疑人被描述为30至40岁白人男性，有焊接经验。FBI呼吁公众通过热线或邮箱提供线索。","summary_en":"OPB reported on Nov 13, 2024 that the FBI is offering $25,000 for information leading to a conviction in ballot box arson attacks in Portland and Vancouver, Washington. FBI Seattle special agent in charge Greg Austin said investigators are seeking a dark-colored 2003 or 2004 Volvo S-60 with a fake license plate. The investigation is jointly led by FBI field offices in Seattle and Portland. The suspect is described as a white male aged 30-40 with welding experience. The FBI urged the public to submit tips via hotline or email.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:41:24.929158+00:00","url":"https://www.opb.org/article/2024/11/13/fbi-offers-reward-information-ballot-box-attacks-portland-vancouver","content":"About OPBWatchListenNewslettersOPB and KMHD events OPB en EspañolHelp centerFBI offers $25,000 for information about ballot box attacks in Portland, VancouverBy Troy Brynelson (OPB)Nov. 13, 2024 9:54 p.m.Investigators hope someone will come forward about the suspect’s vehicle, a dark-colored VolvoA ballot drop box in at the Fishers Landing Transit Center in Vancouver was set ablaze early Monday morning, Oct. 28, 2024, destroying hundreds of ballots.Erik Neumann / OPBFederal law enforcement officials are still searching for the man they believe set fire to ballot boxes in Portland and Washington and are offering $25,000 for any information leading to a conviction.THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorFederal investigators said Wednesday that they continue to investigate the arson attacks, which struck two ballot boxes in Vancouver, Washington; and a ballot box in Portland. One of the Vancouver attacks torched hundreds of ballots.But federal investigators offered few updates on the case. They emphasized during a press conference that they hope someone will come forward with information about the suspect’s vehicle: a 2003 or 2004 Volvo S-60 sedan, dark-colored with a light-colored interior. The Volvo is missing the logo on the front grill.“No detail is too small, no tip is too minor,” said Greg Austin, special agent in charge of the FBI’s Seattle offices. “If it relates to a Volvo matching our description, we want to hear about it.”Austin said investigators believe that the driver of the Volvo used a fake license plate during the attacks and said technology to read the license plates haven’t helped. They also aren’t sure if the car is stolen or belongs to the suspect.THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a Sponsor“The public should know that it may have had a real license plate at other times,” Austin said.A screenshot from C-TRAN security camera footage obtained by OPB shows the attack on a ballot box in Vancouver, Wash., Oct. 28, 2024. Timestamps show the suspect spent about 30 seconds attaching an incendiary device to the ballot box, which burst as they fled.Courtesy of C-TRANThe investigation is being jointly led by FBI field offices in Seattle and Portland. Federal officials said they had no information about whether the suspect could strike again. They also declined to comment on reporting elsewhere that suggested the devices had the message “Free Gaza” written on them.“The public understandably wants to know what the ideology and the motive is behind these attacks,” Austin told reporters. “We have not been able to determine them, but we are certainly exploring all angles in an attempt to determine what the motive was.”Investigators believe the suspect is a white male between 30 and 40 years old with thinning hair.Although investigators declined to discuss any details about the devices used in the attacks, they believe the suspect has experience welding and metal working.People can send tips to a free tipline, 1-800-CALL-FBI, the email address tips@fbi.gov or visit a local field office.Federal law enforcement officials are still searching for the man they believe set fire to ballot boxes in Portland and Washington and are offering $25,000 for any information leading to a conviction.Courtesy of the Department of JusticeTHANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorTHANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a Sponsor📨 Daily news in your inboxSign up today for OPB’s “First Look” – your daily guide to the most important news and culture stories from around the Northwest.EmailPlease leave this field blankSign upTags: Election, Crime","content_cn":"关于 OPB收听新闻通讯OPB 和 KMHD 活动 OPB 西班牙语帮助中心 FBI 悬赏25,000美元征集波特兰、温哥华投票箱袭击案线索\n作者：Troy Brynelson（OPB）\n2024年11月13日晚上9:54\n调查人员希望有人能提供嫌疑人车辆的线索——一辆深色沃尔沃。\n2024年10月28日星期一清晨，温哥华菲舍斯兰丁交通中心的一个投票箱被纵火，数百张选票被烧毁。\nErik Neumann / OPB\n联邦执法官员仍在搜捕他们认为在波特兰和华盛顿州纵火焚烧投票箱的男子，并悬赏25,000美元，征集任何能促成定罪的信息。\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n联邦调查人员周三表示，他们仍在调查这起纵火案：温哥华（华盛顿州）的两个投票箱和波特兰的一个投票箱遭到袭击。其中一起温哥华袭击烧毁了数百张选票。\n但联邦调查人员没有提供多少案件进展。他们在新闻发布会上强调，希望有人能提供嫌疑人车辆的信息：一辆2003年或2004年的沃尔沃S-60轿车，深色车身、浅色内饰。该车前格栅上的徽标缺失。\n“任何细节都不算太小，任何线索都不算太微不足道，”负责FBI西雅图办公室的特工格雷格·奥斯汀说。“如果它与我们描述的沃尔沃有关，我们希望听到相关信息。”\n奥斯汀说，调查人员认为这辆沃尔沃的司机在袭击时使用了假车牌，并表示车牌读取技术没有提供帮助。他们也不确定这辆车是被盗的，还是属于嫌疑人。\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n“公众应当知道，它在其他时间可能使用过真实车牌，”奥斯汀说。\nOPB获得的C-TRAN监控摄像画面截图显示了2024年10月28日发生在华盛顿州温哥华的投票箱袭击。时间戳显示，嫌疑人花约30秒将燃烧装置连接到投票箱上；他们逃离时投票箱爆裂。\nC-TRAN提供\n调查由FBI西雅图和波特兰外勤办公室联合牵头。联邦官员表示，他们没有信息表明嫌疑人可能再次发动袭击。他们还拒绝评论其他媒体关于装置上写有“Free Gaza”（解放加沙）字样的报道。\n“公众理所当然地想知道这些袭击背后的意识形态和动机，”奥斯汀对记者说。“我们还无法确定，但我们当然正在探索所有角度，试图确定动机。”\n调查人员认为，嫌疑人为30至40岁的白人男性，头发稀疏。\n尽管调查人员拒绝讨论袭击中所用装置的具体细节，但他们认为嫌疑人有焊接和金属加工经验。\n公众可以拨打免费举报热线1-800-CALL-FBI，发送电子邮件至tips@fbi.gov，或前往当地外勤办公室提供线索。\n联邦执法官员仍在搜捕他们认为在波特兰和华盛顿州纵火焚烧投票箱的男子，并悬赏25,000美元，征集任何能促成定罪的信息。\n美国司法部提供\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n📨 每日新闻发送到你的收件箱\n立即注册OPB的“First Look”——你的每日指南，了解西北地区最重要的新闻和文化故事。\n电子邮件\n请将此字段留空\n注册\n标签：选举、犯罪","publish_source":"opb.org","publish_time":"2024-11-13T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-3358","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"特朗普提名长期盟友卡什·帕特尔接替现任FBI局长","title_en":"Trump picks longtime ally Kash Patel to replace current FBI director - OPB","summary_cn":"OPB于2024年12月1日报道，当选总统特朗普提名亲密盟友、前国家安全助理卡什·帕特尔接替克里斯托弗·雷担任美国联邦调查局（FBI）局长。帕特尔曾作为国会助理调查2016年俄罗斯干预选举案，后进入特朗普国家安全委员会和五角大楼。他承诺在“第一天”关闭FBI总部并将员工分散至全国。FBI局长任期10年，需参议院确认。FBI发表声明称局长雷将继续专注于FBI的男女员工。","summary_en":"OPB reported on Dec 1, 2024 that President-elect Donald Trump nominated close ally and former national security aide Kash Patel to replace Christopher Wray as FBI director. Patel gained national attention as a congressional aide investigating Russian interference in the 2016 election, later serving on Trump's National Security Council and at the Pentagon. He pledged to shutter FBI headquarters 'on day one' and disperse employees nationwide. The FBI director serves a 10-year term and requires Senate confirmation. The FBI issued a statement saying Director Wray remains focused on the bureau's workforce.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:37:07.599770+00:00","url":"https://www.opb.org/article/2024/12/01/trump-nominates-kash-patel-to-serve-as-fbi-director","content":"About OPBWatchListenNewslettersOPB and KMHD events OPB en EspañolHelp centerTrump picks longtime ally Kash Patel to replace current FBI directorBy Carrie Johnson (NPR)Dec. 1, 2024 4:40 p.m.Kash Patel speaks before then-Republican presidential nominee, former President Donald Trump, at a campaign rally at the Findlay Toyota Arena, on Oct. 13, 2024, in Prescott Valley, Ariz.Ross D. FranklinPresident-elect Donald Trump intends to install Kash Patel, a close ally and former national security aide who has berated the Justice Department and the news media, to replace Christopher Wray as the director of the Federal Bureau of Investigation.THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorTrump wrote in a post on social media Saturday that Patel is a “brilliant lawyer, investigator, and ‘America First’ fighter who has spent his career exposing corruption, defending Justice, and protecting the American People.”Patel came to national attention as a congressional aide investigating the feds who were probing Russian interference in the 2016 election, before he pivoted into roles in Trump’s National Security Council and Pentagon. He’s a regular on right-wing podcasts, where he has issued threats to prosecute political adversaries. Patel also pledged to shutter the FBI headquarters “on day one” and to disperse employees there across the country.THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a Sponsor“We’re absolutely dead serious,” Patel told podcaster Steve Bannon after the November election.Patel, 44, is a former Justice Department prosecutor turned fierce critic of that agency. He wrote a book promising to hollow out the DOJ and the FBI by cleaning house and sweeping out their senior ranks. Patel also said he wants to declassify reams of government secrets, and to wrest security clearances away from people who investigated Trump.The FBI director serves a 10-year term in office, across multiple presidential administrations, in an effort to shield the bureau from partisan political pressure. The job requires Senate confirmation.In response to the announcement, the FBI issued a statement: “Every day, the men and women of the FBI continue to work to protect Americans from a growing array of threats. Director Wray’s focus remains on the men and women of the FBI, the people we do the work with, and the people we do the work for.”Trump appointed Wray in 2017 after firing predecessor Jim Comey. Wray has signaled he wants to serve out the remainder of his term. But his relationship with Trump has been a tense one.Near the end of the first Trump administration, then-President Trump attempted to put Patel in a senior role at the Central Intelligence Agency, but senior leaders at the CIA and the Justice Department blocked the move.THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorTHANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a Sponsor📨 Daily news in your inboxSign up today for OPB’s “First Look” – your daily guide to the most important news and culture stories from around the Northwest.EmailPlease leave this field blankSign upTags: Politics, News, Home Page Top Stories","content_cn":"关于 OPB观看收听新闻通讯OPB 和 KMHD 活动西班牙语 OPB帮助中心\n特朗普选择长期盟友 Kash Patel 接替现任 FBI 局长\n作者：Carrie Johnson（NPR）\n2024年12月1日下午4:40\nKash Patel 于2024年10月13日在亚利桑那州普雷斯科特谷的芬德利丰田体育馆举行的一场竞选集会上，在时任共和党总统候选人、前总统 Donald Trump 之前发表讲话。\nRoss D. Franklin\n当选总统 Donald Trump 打算任命 Kash Patel——一名亲密盟友和前国家安全助理，他曾严厉抨击司法部和新闻媒体——接替 Christopher Wray，担任联邦调查局局长。\n感谢我们的赞助商：\n成为赞助商\n特朗普周六在社交媒体发文称，Patel 是一名“才华横溢的律师、调查员和‘美国优先’斗士，他的职业生涯一直致力于揭露腐败、捍卫正义并保护美国人民。”\nPatel 最初因担任国会助理、调查正在调查2016年大选中俄罗斯干预问题的联邦人员而受到全国关注，之后转任特朗普国家安全委员会和五角大楼的职务。他经常出现在右翼播客中，并曾在节目上威胁要起诉政治对手。Patel 还承诺在“第一天”关闭 FBI 总部，并将那里的员工分散到全国各地。\n感谢我们的赞助商：\n成为赞助商\n“我们绝对是认真的，”Patel 在11月大选后对播客主持人 Steve Bannon 表示。\n44岁的 Patel 曾是司法部检察官，后来成为该机构的激烈批评者。他写了一本书，承诺通过全面整顿、清除高层人员来掏空司法部和 FBI。Patel 还表示，他希望解密大量政府机密，并撤销调查过特朗普的人士的安全许可。\nFBI 局长任期为10年，跨越多届总统政府，旨在保护该机构免受党派政治压力影响。这一职位需要参议院确认。\n针对这项宣布，FBI 发表声明称：“每天，FBI 的男男女女都继续努力保护美国人免受日益增多的威胁。Wray 局长的重点仍是 FBI 的男男女女、与我们一起开展工作的人，以及我们为之开展工作的人。”\n特朗普在2017年解雇前任 Jim Comey 后任命 Wray。Wray 已表示希望履行完剩余任期。但他与特朗普的关系一直紧张。\n在第一届特朗普政府末期，时任总统特朗普试图让 Patel 在中央情报局担任高级职务，但 CIA 和司法部的高级领导人阻止了这一任命。\n感谢我们的赞助商：\n成为赞助商\n感谢我们的赞助商：\n成为赞助商\n📨 每日新闻直达您的收件箱\n立即注册 OPB 的“First Look”——您了解西北地区最重要新闻和文化报道的每日指南。\n电子邮箱\n请将此字段留空\n注册\n标签：政治、新闻、首页头条报道","publish_source":"opb.org","publish_time":"2024-12-01T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-3322","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS (deep pilot)","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"FBI局长雷将在拜登政府任期结束时辞职","title_en":"FBI Director Wray to resign at end of Biden administration - OPB","summary_cn":"OPB于2024年12月11日报道，美国联邦调查局（FBI）局长克里斯托弗·雷宣布将在拜登政府任期结束、即下个月辞职，其10年任期还剩两年多。雷在FBI市政厅会议上对员工表示，此举是为了避免将FBI拖入政治纷争。当选总统特朗普已表示将提名卡什·帕特尔接替雷。这是FBI领导人第二次在特朗普政府相关情况下提前离任。FBI拥有超过3.5万名员工，局长是唯一政治任命官员。","summary_en":"OPB reported on Dec 11, 2024 that FBI Director Christopher Wray announced he would resign at the end of the Biden administration next month, with more than two years remaining in his 10-year term. Wray told employees at an FBI town hall that this was the best way to avoid dragging the bureau deeper into political fray. President-elect Trump had already said he would nominate Kash Patel to replace Wray. It is the second time an FBI leader has left early in connection with the Trump administration. The FBI employs over 35,000 people, and its director is the only political appointee.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-25T11:34:42.765918+00:00","url":"https://www.opb.org/article/2024/12/11/fbi-director-wray-to-resign-at-end-of-biden-administration","content":"About OPBWatchListenNewslettersOPB and KMHD events OPB en EspañolHelp centerFBI Director Wray to resign at end of Biden administrationBy Carrie Johnson (NPR) and Ryan Lucas (NPR)Dec. 11, 2024 7:44 p.m.FBI Director Christopher Wray speaks during an Election Threats Task Force meeting at the Justice Department in September 2024.Andrew HarnikFBI Director Christopher Wray on Wednesday announced he would resign from the bureau at the end of the Biden administration next month, with more than two years remaining on his term in office.THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a Sponsor“My goal is to keep the focus on our mission — the indispensable work you’re doing on behalf of the American people every day,” he told employees at an FBI town hall, according to an excerpt the FBI shared with reporters. “In my view, this is the best way to avoid dragging the Bureau deeper into the fray, while reinforcing the values and principles that are so important to how we do our work.”President-elect Donald Trump has frequently expressed his displeasure with federal law enforcement, and with Wray in particular, and had already said he would nominate Kash Patel, a close ally and former national security aide, to replace Wray.THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorTrump made the FBI a frequent target during his first term in the White House. He nominated Wray to head the FBI, which Wray has led since 2017. But Trump’s relationship with Wray grew tense as FBI agents helped investigate Russian interference in the 2016 election, and then worked with a special counsel to prosecute Trump for hoarding classified documents and attempting to cling to power in 2020.It’s the second time an FBI leader has left in connection to the Trump administration before the director’s 10-year-term had expired. In 2017, Trump’s Justice Department leaders dismissed James Comey and Trump replaced him with Wray, a longtime conservative and member of the Federalist Society.After Trump’s election to a second term in the White House, Wray had initially signaled he intended to remain on the job. “The director is continuing to oversee the day to day operations of the FBI and is actively planning with his team to lead the FBI into next year and beyond,” an FBI official said in November after the election.The FBI employs more than 35,000 people who work to investigate federal crimes, prevent terrorist attacks and analyze intelligence materials. Its leader is the only political appointee at the FBI. Congress tried to insulate the agency from political winds by giving the FBI director 10 years in office, to extend beyond the tenure of any one president.But some of Trump’s conservative advisers have suggested trying to overhaul the FBI to make its director accountable to more junior officials inside the Justice Department with more political control. The FBI’s headquarters building continues to bear the name of J. Edgar Hoover, its longest-serving leader, whose tenure is now remembered for overreach and personal vendettas. THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorTHANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a Sponsor📨 Daily news in your inboxSign up today for OPB’s “First Look” – your daily guide to the most important news and culture stories from around the Northwest.EmailPlease leave this field blankSign upTags: Politics, World","content_cn":"关于 OPB观看收听新闻简报OPB 和 KMHD 活动 OPB 西班牙语帮助中心\nFBI局长雷将于拜登政府任期结束时辞职\n作者：凯丽·约翰逊（NPR）和瑞安·卢卡斯（NPR）\n2024年12月11日晚上7:44\nFBI局长克里斯托弗·雷于2024年9月在司法部举行的一次选举威胁特别工作组会议上发言。\n安德鲁·哈尼克\n联邦调查局（FBI）局长克里斯托弗·雷周三宣布，他将在下个月拜登政府任期结束时从该局辞职，而其任期尚余两年多。\n感谢我们的赞助商：\n成为赞助商\n据FBI向记者分享的一段摘录，他在一次FBI全员大会上对员工表示：“我的目标是让重点始终放在我们的使命上——你们每天代表美国人民所从事的不可或缺的工作。在我看来，这是避免让该局进一步卷入纷争、同时强化对我们工作方式至关重要的价值观和原则的最佳方式。”\n当选总统唐纳德·特朗普经常表达对联邦执法部门，尤其是对雷的不满，并且已表示将提名亲密盟友、前国家安全助理卡什·帕特尔接替雷。\n感谢我们的赞助商：\n成为赞助商\n特朗普在其第一个白宫任期内经常将FBI作为攻击目标。他提名雷领导FBI，雷自2017年以来一直领导该局。但随着FBI特工协助调查俄罗斯干预2016年选举，随后又与特别检察官合作，以囤积机密文件和企图在2020年继续掌权为由起诉特朗普，特朗普与雷的关系变得紧张。\n这是第二次FBI领导人因特朗普政府相关情况在局长10年任期届满前离任。2017年，特朗普政府的司法部领导人解雇了詹姆斯·科米，特朗普以长期保守派人士、联邦党人协会成员雷取代了他。\n特朗普当选重返白宫开启第二个任期后，雷起初曾表示打算留任。选举后的11月，一名FBI官员表示：“局长正继续监督FBI的日常运作，并积极与其团队规划，在明年及以后继续领导FBI。”\nFBI雇用超过35,000人，他们负责调查联邦犯罪、预防恐怖袭击和分析情报材料。其领导人是FBI唯一的政治任命官员。国会试图通过给予FBI局长10年任期，使其任期超出任何一位总统的任期，来让该机构免受政治风向影响。\n但特朗普的一些保守派顾问建议尝试改革FBI，使其局长向司法部内部拥有更多政治控制权的较低级别官员负责。FBI总部大楼仍以其任期最长的领导人J. 埃德加·胡佛命名；胡佛的任期如今因越权和个人报复而为人所铭记。\n感谢我们的赞助商：\n成为赞助商\n感谢我们的赞助商：\n成为赞助商\n📨 每日新闻直达收件箱\n立即注册OPB的“First Look”——你每日获取西北地区最重要新闻和文化故事的指南。\n电子邮件\n请将此字段留空\n注册\n标签：政治、世界","publish_source":"opb.org","publish_time":"2024-12-11T00:00:00"}},{"id":"news-3227","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"参议院确认克里斯蒂·诺姆出任特朗普的国土安全部长","title_en":"Senate confirms Kristi Noem as Trump's Secretary of Homeland Security - OPB","summary_cn":"美国参议院以59票对34票确认南达科他州州长克里斯蒂·诺姆出任国土安全部长，她成为特朗普内阁中第四位获确认的成员。诺姆将负责移民与边境事务、海岸警卫队、反恐、航空安全、网络安全以及联邦应急管理局。她在确认听证会上称南部边境是“国土安全头号威胁”，并承诺关闭CBP One应用程序，支持终止出生公民权及削减拜登时期移民假释项目。","summary_en":"The U.S. Senate confirmed South Dakota Gov. Kristi Noem as Secretary of Homeland Security in a 59-34 vote, making her the fourth Trump cabinet member confirmed. Noem will oversee immigration and border issues, the Coast Guard, counterterrorism, aviation security, cybersecurity and FEMA. At her confirmation hearing, she called the southern border 'the number one threat to our homeland security,' vowed to shut down the CBP One app, and backed ending birthright citizenship and slashing a Biden-era migrant parole program.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:10:46.854764+00:00","url":"https://www.opb.org/article/2025/01/25/senate-confirms-kristi-noem-as-trump-s-secretary-of-homeland-security","content":"About OPBWatchListenNewslettersOPB and KMHD events OPB en EspañolHelp centerPoliticsSenate confirms Kristi Noem as Trump's Secretary of Homeland SecurityBy Juliana Kim (NPR)Jan. 25, 2025 5:47 p.m.Secretary of the Department of Homeland Security nominee Kristi Noem arrives for the inauguration of U.S. President-elect Donald Trump in the U.S. Capitol Rotunda on Jan. 20, 2025 in Washington, DC.Kenny Holston-Pool / Getty ImagesThe Senate confirmed South Dakota Gov. Kristi Noem on Saturday to serve as the Secretary of Homeland Security, putting her in charge of executing one of President Trump’s biggest priorities in his second term: cracking down on immigration.THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorNoem, a Republican, won confirmation in a 59-34 vote. She becomes the fourth person confirmed in Trump’s cabinet, following Secretary of State Marco Rubio, CIA Director John Ratcliffe, and Pete Hegseth, who was narrowly appointed Secretary of Defense on Friday night after Vice President J.D. Vance cast a tie-breaking vote. As Secretary of Homeland Security, Noem will oversee immigration and border-related issues; the Coast Guard; counterterrorism; aviation security; cybersecurity; and the Federal Emergency Management Agency — which Trump recently proposed should be completely overhauled or possibly, eliminated.Noem was South Dakota’s first female governor and led the state for two terms. She gained national prominence for her stances against mask and lockdown mandates during the COVID-19 pandemic. Trump also previously praised Noem for sending National Guard troops to the U.S.-Mexico border. THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorNoem has been a longtime ally of Trump, with a history of backing his immigration policies and proposals. During her confirmation hearing, when asked why she wanted to lead Homeland Security, Noem said she asked for the job “because I knew it was the president’s number one priority.” She added, “I knew that it would be needed to have someone in the position, that would do what the president promised the American people and would be strong enough to do it.”Noem described the southern border as “the number one threat to our homeland security.”She vowed to make several changes in the department, including shutting down the CPB One App, which asylum seekers use to set appointments. (Trump has falsely said the app is used for smuggling migrants.) Noem also supports Trump’s plans to end birthright citizenship and slash a program that was used by the Biden administration to temporarily allow more than a half-million migrants fleeing Cuba, Haiti, Nicaragua, and Venezuela, to live and work in the U.S. if they have a financial sponsor and pass background checks. Trump issued an executive order terminating the program in his first day in office.What remains to be seen is how her role will align with that of Tom Homan, who was picked to served as the White House’s Border Czar. His post did not require Senate confirmation.In her confirmation hearing, Noem said she does not expect responsibilities to be taken away from the Secretary of Homeland Security or the department. THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorTHANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a Sponsor📨 Daily news in your inboxSign up today for OPB’s “First Look” – your daily guide to the most important news and culture stories from around the Northwest.EmailPlease leave this field blankSign upTags: Politics, News","content_cn":"关于 OPBWatchListenNewslettersOPB 和 KMHD 活动 OPB 西班牙语帮助中心政治参议院确认克里斯蒂·诺姆为特朗普的国土安全部长朱莉安娜·金（NPR）2025年1月25日下午5:47国土安全部部长提名人克里斯蒂·诺姆于2025年1月20日抵达华盛顿特区美国国会大厦圆形大厅，参加美国当选总统唐纳德·特朗普的就职典礼。Kenny Holston-Pool / Getty Images参议院周六确认南达科他州州长克里斯蒂·诺姆担任国土安全部长，使她负责执行特朗普总统第二任期内最大的优先事项之一：严厉打击移民。感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商诺姆是共和党人，以59比34的投票结果获得确认。她成为特朗普内阁中第四位获确认的人，此前获确认的包括国务卿马可·卢比奥、中央情报局局长约翰·拉特克利夫，以及周五晚间在副总统J.D.万斯投出破局票后以微弱优势获任命的国防部长皮特·赫格塞思。作为国土安全部长，诺姆将负责移民和边境事务；海岸警卫队；反恐；航空安全；网络安全；以及联邦紧急事务管理局——特朗普最近提议对该机构进行彻底改造，甚至可能予以取消。诺姆是南达科他州首位女性州长，并领导该州两个任期。新冠疫情期间，她因反对口罩和封控强制令的立场而获得全国知名度。特朗普此前也称赞诺姆向美墨边境派遣国民警卫队。感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商诺姆长期以来一直是特朗普的盟友，历来支持他的移民政策和提案。在确认听证会上，当被问及为何想领导国土安全部时，诺姆说她要求这份工作“因为我知道这是总统的首要任务”。她补充说：“我知道这个职位需要有人来担任，去做总统向美国人民承诺的事情，而且要足够强硬，能够做到。”诺姆将南部边境称为“对我们国土安全的头号威胁”。她承诺对该部门进行多项改革，包括关闭 CPB One 应用程序，寻求庇护者使用该应用预约。（特朗普曾错误地说该应用被用于偷运移民。）诺姆还支持特朗普结束出生公民权的计划，以及削减拜登政府使用的一项计划；该计划曾允许超过50万名逃离古巴、海地、尼加拉瓜和委内瑞拉的移民在有经济担保人并通过背景审查的情况下，暂时在美国居住和工作。特朗普在上任第一天发布行政命令终止了该计划。她的角色将如何与被选为白宫“边境事务主管”的汤姆·霍曼的职责协调，仍有待观察。霍曼的职位不需要参议院确认。在确认听证会上，诺姆表示，她预计国土安全部长或该部门的职责不会被剥夺。感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商📨 每日新闻发送到你的收件箱立即注册 OPB 的“First Look”——你每日了解西北地区最重要新闻和文化故事的指南。电子邮件请将此字段留空注册标签：政治、新闻","publish_source":"opb.org","publish_time":"2025-01-25T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2976","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"俄勒冈州议员请求FBI局长协助侦破1989年谋杀案","title_en":"Oregon lawmakers ask FBI director for help solving 1989 murder - OPB","summary_cn":"俄勒冈州众议院两党领袖致信FBI局长卡什·帕特尔，请求联邦调查局介入调查1989年俄勒冈州惩教署长迈克尔·弗兰克被谋杀一案。信中称案件悬而未决是对受害者家属和俄勒冈州的严重不公，需要FBI主导新的公正调查。FBI发言人拒绝置评。1991年被定罪的弗兰克·盖布尔后因证人翻供等新证据被联邦法院释放，案件至今未破。","summary_en":"Leaders of the Oregon House of Representatives sent a bipartisan letter to FBI Director Kash Patel requesting the bureau's help investigating the 1989 murder of Oregon Department of Corrections Director Michael Francke, who was found dead outside the agency's Salem headquarters. The lawmakers said the lack of resolution is a grave injustice and that a new, impartial FBI-led investigation is necessary. An FBI spokesperson declined to comment. Frank Gable, convicted in 1991, was later released after a federal court cited witness recantations and other new evidence; the case remains unsolved.","tag":"security","founddate":"2026-09-24T22:37:29.491475+00:00","url":"https://www.opb.org/article/2025/04/15/oregon-lawmakers-ask-fbis-patel-for-help-solving-1989-murder","content":"About OPBWatchListenNewslettersOPB and KMHD events OPB en EspañolHelp centerCrimeOregon lawmakers ask FBI director for help solving 1989 murderBy Conrad Wilson (OPB)April 15, 2025 7:42 p.m.On Jan. 17, 1989, the former Director of the Oregon Department of Corrections, Michael Francke, was found dead outside the agency’s headquarters Oregon lawmakers on Monday asked FBI Director Kash Patel to investigate the 1989 murder of the state’s top prison official. Michael Francke, the former Director of the Oregon Department of Corrections, was found dead outside the agency’s headquarters in Salem on Jan. 17, 1989. Some three decades later, a federal judge released the person convicted of killing Francke, citing new “evidence of witness recantations.”THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorKash Patel, President Donald Trump's new Director of the FBI, speaks during a swearing-in ceremony, Friday, Feb. 21, 2025, in the Indian Treaty Room at the Eisenhower Executive Office Building on the White House campus in Washington. (AP Photo/Mark Schiefelbein)Mark Schiefelbein / APLeaders of the Oregon House of Representatives asked Patel in a letter for the agency’s help to solve the case.“The lack of resolution of this case is a grave injustice of the Francke family and the State of Oregon,” Democratic House Majority Leader Ben Bowman and Republican House Minority Leader Christine Drazen wrote. “A new, impartial investigation, led by the FBI, is necessary for justice to be served.”THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorThe bipartisan request for assistance states they are unaware of any active investigation from any law enforcement agency.“Thirty-six years later, we still do not know who killed Michael Francke or why they killed him,” Bowman and Drazen state. “The people of Oregon have a compelling interest in knowing the answers to these questions.”A spokesperson for the FBI declined to comment. Typically, federal law enforcement only gets involved in cases where there is either a federal law broken, or a formal request for assistance from local or state officials. Murder is most often a crime dealt with under state law.In 1991, Frank Gable was wrongfully convicted for Francke’s murder, despite no physical evidence connecting him to the crime. A federal magistrate granted Gable’s petition in 2019, which challenged his imprisonment. A federal appeals court ultimately agreed. “What we now know, and the jury did not, is that the testimony of the State’s main witness was irreversibly tainted by coercive investigative techniques, and that another man gave compelling confessions on multiple occasions,” a federal appeals court concluded Feb. 22, 2023.In May 2023, U.S. Magistrate Judge John Acosta granted Gable his release, ruled he could not be retried for Francke’s murder and expunged the case from Gable’s record.The Oregon Department of Justice has offered $2 million to settle a wrongful conviction lawsuit Gable has filed against the state.THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorTHANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a Sponsor📨 Daily news in your inboxSign up today for OPB’s “First Look” – your daily guide to the most important news and culture stories from around the Northwest.EmailPlease leave this field blankSign upTags: Oregon Legislature, Crime","content_cn":"关于 OPBWatchListenNewslettersOPB 和 KMHD 活动 OPB en Español帮助中心犯罪俄勒冈州立法者请求 FBI 局长协助侦破 1989 年谋杀案作者：Conrad Wilson（OPB）2025年4月15日晚上7:42。1989年1月17日，俄勒冈州惩教署前署长 Michael Francke 被发现死于该机构总部外。俄勒冈州立法者周一请求 FBI 局长 Kash Patel 调查该州最高监狱官员于 1989 年遭谋杀一案。俄勒冈州惩教署前署长 Michael Francke 于1989年1月17日被发现死于该机构位于塞勒姆的总部外。约三十年后，一名联邦法官以新的“证人翻供证据”为由释放了被判杀害 Francke 的人。感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商。总统 Donald Trump 新任命的 FBI 局长 Kash Patel 在宣誓就职仪式上讲话，2025年2月21日，星期五，地点是华盛顿白宫园区艾森豪威尔行政办公楼内的印第安条约厅。（美联社照片/Mark Schiefelbein）Mark Schiefelbein / AP。俄勒冈州众议院领导人在一封信中请求 Patel 让该机构协助侦破此案。“此案迟迟未能解决，是对 Francke 家族和俄勒冈州的严重不公，”民主党众议院多数党领袖 Ben Bowman 和共和党众议院少数党领袖 Christine Drazen 写道。“必须由 FBI 领导开展一项新的、公正的调查，才能实现正义。”感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商。这项两党援助请求称，他们不知道有任何执法机构正在对此案进行积极调查。“36 年后，我们仍然不知道是谁杀害了 Michael Francke，或他们为何杀害他，”Bowman 和 Drazen 表示。“俄勒冈州人民迫切希望知道这些问题的答案。”一名 FBI 发言人拒绝置评。通常，联邦执法部门只会介入涉及违反联邦法律，或收到地方或州官员正式援助请求的案件。谋杀通常是根据州法律处理的犯罪。1991年，Frank Gable 被错误判定犯有谋杀 Francke 罪，尽管没有任何物证将他与该犯罪联系起来。一名联邦治安法官于2019年批准了 Gable 对其监禁提出异议的申请。联邦上诉法院最终表示同意。“我们现在知道、而陪审团当时并不知道的是，州方主要证人的证词已被强制性调查手段不可逆转地污染，而且另一名男子曾多次作出令人信服的供认，”一家联邦上诉法院于2023年2月22日裁定。2023年5月，美国治安法官 John Acosta 批准释放 Gable，裁定他不能因谋杀 Francke 再次受审，并从 Gable 的记录中撤销该案。俄勒冈州司法部已提出支付 200 万美元，以和解 Gable 针对该州提起的错误定罪诉讼。感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商。感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商。📨 每日新闻直达您的收件箱。立即注册 OPB 的“First Look”——您了解西北地区最重要新闻与文化报道的每日指南。电子邮件。请将此字段留空。注册。标签：俄勒冈州立法机构、犯罪","publish_source":"opb.org","publish_time":"2025-04-15T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2842","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"律师请求法院阻止国土安全部将移民遣送至南苏丹","title_en":"Attorneys ask court to block alleged DHS move to deport migrants to South Sudan - OPB","summary_cn":"移民律师请求马萨诸塞州联邦法官阻止特朗普政府将移民遣送至第三国南苏丹，并敦促法官下令让已被遣送者返回。来自缅甸、越南等国的无合法身份移民收到遣返通知，至少一名缅甸籍人士已被送往南苏丹。律师称此举违反禁止未经正当程序遣送第三国的司法命令。国土安全部未立即回应。此前该部门已将200多名委内瑞拉移民遣送至萨尔瓦多，并曾试图送往利比亚。","summary_en":"Immigration attorneys asked a Massachusetts federal judge to block an alleged Trump administration move to deport migrants to South Sudan, a third country, and to order the return of anyone already removed. Migrants from Myanmar, Vietnam and elsewhere received deportation notices; at least one Myanmar national was reportedly already sent to South Sudan. Lawyers argued the move violates a judicial order barring third-country deportations without due process. The Department of Homeland Security did not immediately respond. DHS previously deported over 200 Venezuelans to El Salvador and sought to send migrants to Libya.","tag":"geopolitics","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:43:39.491057+00:00","url":"https://www.opb.org/article/2025/05/20/attorneys-seek-to-block-trump-administration-from-deporting-migrants-to-south-sudan","content":"About OPBWatchListenNewslettersOPB and KMHD events OPB en EspañolHelp centerImmigrationAttorneys ask court to block alleged DHS move to deport migrants to South SudanBy Scott Neuman (NPR)May 21, 2025 12:10 a.m.A United States Air Force Boeing C-17 used for deportation flights is pictured at Biggs Army Airfield in Fort Bliss, El Paso, Texas on February 13, 2025.Justin HamelImmigration attorneys have asked a Massachusetts federal judge to block an alleged Trump administration move to deport migrants to a third country, in this case South Sudan.THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorThey also urged federal Judge Brian Murphy to immediately order the return of any migrants who may have already been deported to these “third countries,” or places they’re not from originally, according to a court filing on Tuesday.Migrants from Myanmar, Vietnam and other countries who do not have legal status to remain in the U.S. received notices on Monday that they would be deported, their lawyers said.When lawyers representing detainees sought to contact them on Tuesday, officials at the Port Isabel Detention Center in Texas said that at least one person, originally from Myanmar, had already been removed to South Sudan, according to a declaration from their attorney.THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a Sponsor“Plaintiffs ask this Court for an immediate order ordering the immediate return of any class members removed to South Sudan,” according to documents filed in the U.S. District Court in Massachusetts. The attorneys also asked the government not to deport migrants to any country which isn’t their country of origin unless they get proper written notice and enough time to contest their deportation.They argued that the Trump administration’s actions to deport their clients also violate the judicial order barring deportations to such third countries without adequate due process.The Department of Homeland Security didn’t immediately respond to a request for comment.The Trump administration has been negotiating with other countries willing to take in people deported from the U.S.The Department of Homeland Security has already deported more than 200 Venezuelan migrants to El Salvador. It argued that some of the men were members of the Tren de Aragua gang and could be deported using the Alien Enemies Act, an obscure wartime law that allows for quick removals. The Trump administration has also paid El Salvador $6 million to house them.Earlier in May, the administration had sought to send migrants to Libya, despite concerns from human rights groups about the violence there and the country’s notorious human rights record.South Sudan endured a long civil war that killed more than 50,000 people until a fragile peace halted the fighting in 2018. Even so, political instability persists and clashes between rebels and the government continue. Many, including the United Nations, worry about the prospect of the country tipping into civil war again.THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorTHANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a Sponsor📨 Daily news in your inboxSign up today for OPB’s “First Look” – your daily guide to the most important news and culture stories from around the Northwest.EmailPlease leave this field blankSign upTags: National Security, Courts, Immigration","content_cn":"关于 OPB收听新闻通讯OPB 和 KMHD 活动 OPB 西班牙语帮助中心移民律师请求法院阻止据称 DHS 将移民驱逐至南苏丹的行动\nScott Neuman（NPR）撰写\n2025年5月21日凌晨12:10\n2025年2月13日，美国空军一架用于驱逐航班的波音 C-17 运输机停在得克萨斯州埃尔帕索的布利斯堡大兵营机场。\nJustin Hamel\n移民律师请求马萨诸塞州一名联邦法官阻止特朗普政府据称将移民驱逐到第三国的行动，本案中的第三国是南苏丹。\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n据周二提交的法庭文件，他们还敦促联邦法官 Brian Murphy 立即下令，让任何可能已经被驱逐到这些“第三国”（即他们原本并非来自的国家或地区）的移民返回。\n他们的律师说，来自缅甸、越南和其他国家、没有合法身份留在美国的移民周一收到通知称他们将被驱逐。\n据其律师的一份声明，当代表被拘留者的律师周二试图联系他们时，得克萨斯州伊莎贝尔港拘留中心的官员说，至少有一人原籍缅甸，已经被遣送至南苏丹。\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n“原告请求本法院立即下令，让任何被遣送至南苏丹的集体成员立即返回，”马萨诸塞州美国地区法院提交的文件称。律师还请求政府不要将移民驱逐到任何非其原籍国的国家，除非政府向他们发出适当的书面通知，并给予足够时间对驱逐提出异议。\n他们辩称，特朗普政府驱逐其当事人的行为也违反了司法命令；该命令禁止在没有充分正当程序的情况下将移民驱逐到此类第三国。\n国土安全部没有立即回应置评请求。\n特朗普政府一直在与其他愿意接收从美国驱逐人员的国家进行谈判。\n国土安全部已经将200多名委内瑞拉移民驱逐到萨尔瓦多。该部门称，其中一些人是“阿拉瓜火车”帮派成员，可以依据《敌国人法》将其驱逐；这是一部鲜为人知的战时法律，允许快速遣返。特朗普政府还向萨尔瓦多支付了600万美元，用于安置他们。\n5月早些时候，尽管人权组织担忧利比亚的暴力局势及其臭名昭著的人权记录，政府仍寻求将移民送往利比亚。\n南苏丹经历了漫长的内战，造成5万多人死亡，直到脆弱的和平在2018年停止了战斗。即便如此，政治不稳定仍在持续，叛军与政府之间的冲突也未停止。包括联合国在内的许多人担心，该国可能再次陷入内战。\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n📨 每日新闻送达您的收件箱\n立即注册 OPB 的“First Look”——您每日了解西北地区最重要新闻和文化故事的指南。\n电子邮件\n请将此字段留空\n注册\n标签：国家安全、法院、移民","publish_source":"opb.org","publish_time":"2025-05-20T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2364","ktid":1000110,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1000110","source":"Google News RSS discovery","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"前FBI局长詹姆斯·科米因2020年证词被刑事起诉","title_en":"Ex-FBI Director James Comey indicted on criminal charges stemming from 2020 testimony - OPB","summary_cn":"美国司法部对前FBI局长詹姆斯·科米提出指控，涉及他向参议院司法委员会作证时的一项虚假陈述罪和一项妨碍司法罪。此前特朗普总统要求检察官加快对其批评者的调查。弗吉尼亚东区联邦检察官曾对证据表示怀疑，随后被撤换。科米长期与特朗普不和，曾将特朗普比作黑帮头目。其女儿莫琳·科米今年被司法部解雇后已提起诉讼。","summary_en":"The Justice Department has charged former FBI Director James Comey with one count of making false statements and one count of obstruction of justice related to his testimony before the Senate Judiciary Committee nearly five years ago. The move followed President Trump's demands that prosecutors speed up an investigation targeting a prominent critic. The top federal prosecutor in Northern Virginia had cast doubt on the evidence before being forced out and replaced. Comey, long at odds with Trump, once likened the president to a mob boss. His daughter Maurene Comey, fired by DOJ this year, has sued.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-24T21:40:39.785051+00:00","url":"https://www.opb.org/article/2025/09/25/ex-fbi-director-james-comey-indicted-on-criminal-charges-stemming-from-2020-testimony","content":"About OPBWatchListenNewslettersOPB and KMHD events OPB en EspañolHelp centerEx-FBI Director James Comey indicted on criminal charges stemming from 2020 testimonyBy Carrie Johnson (NPR)Sept. 26, 2025 1:26 a.m.00:00 / Former Federal Bureau of Investigation Director James Comey leaves the Rayburn House Office Building after testifying to two House committees on Dec. 7, 2018, in Washington, D.C.Chip SomodevillaTHANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorThe Justice Department has leveled charges against former FBI Director James Comey, after President Trump demanded prosecutors speed up their pace in an investigation targeting one of his most prominent critics.Comey faces one count of making false statements and one count of obstruction of justice in connection with his testimony to the Senate Judiciary Committee nearly five years ago. The move comes days after the top federal prosecutor in Northern Virginia cast doubt on the evidence, only to be forced out by the president and replaced with one of Trump’s former defense attorneys.THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a Sponsor“No one is above the law.,” Attorney General Pam Bondi said in a post on X. “Today’s indictment reflects this Department of Justice’s commitment to holding those who abuse positions of power accountable for misleading the American people.”Trump has long railed against Comey, blaming the former FBI leader for the appointment of a special prosecutor who probed contacts between Russia and Trump’s 2016 campaign through most of the president’s first term in office.The charges against Comey mark the latest escalation in a years-long war of words between Trump and Comey, who famously likened the president to a mob boss.This year the Secret Service interrogated the former FBI director after he posted a photo of seashells in the shape of the numbers “86 47.” Comey later removed the post, saying he thought it was a restaurant-themed joke about removing Trump, the 47th president. But Trump’s son and cabinet members interpreted the message as an assassination threat. “Eighty-six” is a slang term that means “get rid of,” and Trump is the 47th (and 45th) president of the United States.Comey once served as second in command at the Justice Department, under President George W. Bush. But he started his career as a federal prosecutor, working to some acclaim in the same office in Virginia that later moved to seek his indictment.DOJ fired his daughter, Maurene Comey, from a prominent job as an assistant U.S. attorney in Manhattan earlier this year, only hours after she said she had been asked to lead an important public corruption case.This month, Maurene Comey sued current Justice Department leaders, arguing she was fired without cause and in violation of the Constitution and federal statutes. Her lawsuit said she thought she was dismissed because of “her father’s protected speech, or because of her perceived political affiliation and beliefs, or both.”THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorTHANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a Sponsor📨 Daily news in your inboxSign up today for OPB’s “First Look” – your daily guide to the most important news and culture stories from around the Northwest.EmailPlease leave this field blankSign upTags: Politics, News, National, Home Page Top Stories","content_cn":"关于 OPBWatchListenNewslettersOPB 和 KMHD 活动 OPB en Español帮助中心\n前 FBI 局长詹姆斯·科米因 2020 年证词相关刑事指控被起诉\n作者：Carrie Johnson（NPR）\n2025年9月26日凌晨1:26\n00:00 / \n前联邦调查局局长詹姆斯·科米于 2018年12月7日在华盛顿特区向众议院两个委员会作证后离开雷伯恩众议院办公楼。\nChip Somodevilla\n感谢我们的赞助商：\n成为赞助商\n在特朗普总统要求检察官加快针对其最知名批评者之一的调查进度后，司法部对前 FBI 局长詹姆斯·科米提出指控。\n\n科米因近五年前在参议院司法委员会作证而面临一项作虚假陈述罪和一项妨碍司法公正罪指控。此举发生在数日前：北弗吉尼亚州最高联邦检察官对证据表示怀疑，随后被总统迫使离职，并由特朗普的一名前辩护律师取代。\n\n感谢我们的赞助商：\n成为赞助商\n\n“没有人凌驾于法律之上。”司法部长帕姆·邦迪在 X 上发文称，“今天的起诉书体现了本司法部致力于追究那些滥用权力职位、误导美国民众者责任的承诺。”\n\n特朗普长期抨击科米，指责这位前 FBI 领导人促成任命特别检察官；该特别检察官在总统第一个任期的大部分时间里，调查了俄罗斯与特朗普 2016 年竞选团队之间的联系。\n\n对科米的指控标志着特朗普与科米长达数年的口水战最新升级；科米曾著名地将总统比作黑帮老大。\n\n今年，特勤局在这位前 FBI 局长发布一张排列成数字“86 47”的贝壳照片后对其进行了问询。科米后来删除了该帖子，称他以为那是一个以餐厅为主题、关于移除第 47 任总统特朗普的玩笑。但特朗普的儿子和内阁成员将该信息解读为暗杀威胁。“Eighty-six”是意为“除掉”的俚语，而特朗普是美国第 47 任（也是第 45 任）总统。\n\n科米曾在乔治·W·布什总统任内担任司法部二把手。但他的职业生涯始于联邦检察官，并在弗吉尼亚州同一办公室工作且颇获赞誉；该办公室后来决定寻求对他的起诉。\n\n今年早些时候，司法部解雇了他的女儿莫琳·科米，她曾在曼哈顿担任助理联邦检察官这一重要职位；解雇发生在她表示被要求领导一宗重要公共腐败案件后仅数小时。\n\n本月，莫琳·科米起诉现任司法部领导人，称自己被无故解雇，违反了宪法和联邦法规。她的诉讼称，她认为自己被解雇是因为“其父亲受保护的言论，或因为人们认为她具有某种政治归属和信念，或两者兼有。”\n\n感谢我们的赞助商：\n成为赞助商\n感谢我们的赞助商：\n成为赞助商\n📨 每日新闻发送至您的收件箱\n立即注册 OPB 的“First Look”——您每日了解西北地区最重要新闻和文化故事的指南。\n电子邮件\n请将此字段留空\n注册\n标签：政治、新闻、全国、主页头条新闻","publish_source":"opb.org","publish_time":"2025-09-25T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-2276","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"美国国土安全部寻求在波特兰购买新设施","title_en":"US Department of Homeland Security looking to purchase new facilities in Portland - OPB","summary_cn":"美国国土安全部长克里斯蒂·诺姆在白宫内阁会议上表示，正考虑在波特兰购买新设施，但未说明建筑类型或用途。市长基思·威尔逊办公室称未收到联邦政府购地意向。此前诺姆周二访问了南波特兰的移民和海关执法局大楼，并称州长和市长“不诚实”，威胁若联邦官员安全无改善将“加倍”行动。","summary_en":"U.S. Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem said at a White House Cabinet meeting that she is looking to purchase new facilities in Portland, without specifying type or use. Mayor Keith Wilson's office said it received no indication of federal intent to buy property. The news followed Noem's Tuesday visit to the South Portland ICE building, where she called the governor and mayor dishonest and threatened to double down.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T16:18:53.073770+00:00","url":"https://www.opb.org/article/2025/10/09/dhs-purchase-new-facilities-portland","content":"About OPBWatchListenNewslettersOPB and KMHD events OPB en EspañolHelp centerPoliticsUS Department of Homeland Security looking to purchase new facilities in PortlandBy Alex Zielinski (OPB)Oct. 9, 2025 9:02 p.m.At a meeting of the Cabinet at the White House on Thursday, Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem didn’t specify what kind of buildings she wanted to buy.U.S. Department of Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem stands on the roof of the U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement building in South Portland on Tuesday, Oct. 7, 2025.Kristyna Wentz-Graff / OPBThe U.S. Department of Homeland Security is looking to buy more property in Portland. THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorAt a meeting of the Cabinet at the White House on Thursday, Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem said she is “looking at new facilities to purchase” in Portland. She didn’t specify what kind of buildings, or for what use. A spokesperson from Mayor Keith Wilson’s office said they received “no indication” from the federal government that it intends to buy property in the city. This news followed Noem’s Tuesday visit to Portland, where she visited the U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement building in South Portland, which has been the focus of months of protests opposing President Donald Trump’s immigration policies. Noem met with Wilson and Gov. Tina Kotek, among other leaders. At the Cabinet meeting, she called the governor and the mayor “disingenuous and dishonest people,” and that she intended to “double down” if they didn’t improve security for federal officers working at the ICE facility. THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorRelated: Portland weighs taking over lease at ICE facilityWilson has previously told DHS that the city’s police force has been responsive to any safety concerns outside the ICE facility, and has instead raised concerns about federal officers’ heavy-handed response to peaceful protests. A spokesperson for the mayor said Thursday that Wilson “urges federal partners to engage constructively and to refrain from rhetoric that misrepresents our city’s leadership and values.”Noem and Wilson did discuss properties during their meeting. ICE currently leases the South Macadam Avenue building from a private property owner. At the Tuesday meeting, an aide for Noem asked Wilson about whether the city would want to take over ICE’s lease, to potentially relocate the ICE office. In a press statement, Wilson wrote that he “expressed openness” to either taking over the lease or purchasing the facility.Related: 9th Circuit Court skeptical of order blocking Trump’s troop deployment to PortlandThe city has separately questioned whether ICE should operate out of the South Portland building. Last month, city officials notified the building’s owner that ICE may be violating its land use permit with the city by potentially holding detainees for too long.Spokespeople for DHS and the General Services Administration, which oversees federal property, did not immediately respond to OPB’s request for comment. Noem’s announcement came as a federal appeals court weighed overturning a court order blocking Trump from deploying members of the Oregon National Guard to Portland. The court has yet to make a ruling. THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorTHANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a Sponsor📨 Daily news in your inboxSign up today for OPB’s “First Look” – your daily guide to the most important news and culture stories from around the Northwest.EmailPlease leave this field blankSign upTags: Politics, Portland, Immigration, Trump, Protests","content_cn":"关于OPB\n观看\n收听\n新闻通讯\nOPB和KMHD活动\nOPB西班牙语\n帮助中心\n政治\n\n美国国土安全部寻求在波特兰购买新设施\n作者：Alex Zielinski（OPB）\n2025年10月9日晚上9:02\n\n周四在白宫举行的内阁会议上，国土安全部长Kristi Noem没有具体说明她想购买哪种建筑。\n\n美国国土安全部部长Kristi Noem于2025年10月7日星期二站在位于南波特兰的美国移民与海关执法局大楼屋顶上。\nKristyna Wentz-Graff / OPB\n\n美国国土安全部正寻求在波特兰购买更多房产。\n\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n\n周四在白宫举行的内阁会议上，国土安全部长Kristi Noem表示，她正在“寻找要购买的新设施”，地点在波特兰。她没有具体说明要购买哪种建筑，也没有说明用途。市长Keith Wilson办公室的一名发言人表示，他们“没有得到任何迹象”表明联邦政府打算在该市购买房产。\n\n这一消息发布前，Noem于周二访问了波特兰，并视察了位于南波特兰的美国移民与海关执法局大楼。数月来，该大楼一直是抗议活动的焦点，抗议者反对总统Donald Trump的移民政策。Noem还会见了Wilson和州长Tina Kotek等领导人。在内阁会议上，她称州长和市长是“虚伪且不诚实的人”，并表示如果他们不改善在ICE设施工作的联邦官员的安全，她打算“加倍施压”。\n\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n\n相关阅读：波特兰考虑接管ICE设施租约\n\nWilson此前曾告知国土安全部，市警察部门一直在回应ICE设施外的任何安全问题；相反，他对联邦官员对和平抗议采取的强硬回应表示担忧。市长的一名发言人周四表示，Wilson“敦促联邦合作伙伴进行建设性接触，并避免使用歪曲我市领导层和价值观的言辞”。\n\nNoem和Wilson在会面期间确实讨论了房产问题。ICE目前从私人业主处租用南麦卡丹大道上的大楼。在周二的会议上，Noem的一名助手询问Wilson，市政府是否愿意接管ICE的租约，以便重新安置ICE办公室。Wilson在一份新闻声明中写道，他“表示愿意”接管租约或购买该设施。\n\n相关阅读：第九巡回法院对阻止特朗普向波特兰部署部队的命令持怀疑态度\n\n市政府还单独质疑ICE是否应在南波特兰大楼办公。上个月，市政府官员通知大楼业主，ICE可能违反了与市政府签订的土地使用许可证，因为其可能让被拘留者在此停留过久。\n\n国土安全部和负责管理联邦房产的总务管理局发言人没有立即回应OPB的置评请求。Noem发表声明之际，一家联邦上诉法院正在考虑是否推翻一项阻止特朗普向波特兰部署俄勒冈州国民警卫队成员的法院命令。该法院尚未作出裁决。\n\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n\n📨 每日新闻送达您的收件箱\n立即注册OPB的“First Look”——您每日了解西北地区最重要新闻和文化故事的指南。\n\n电子邮件\n请将此字段留空\n注册\n\n标签：政治、波特兰、移民、特朗普、抗议","publish_source":"opb.org","publish_time":"2025-10-09T00:00:00+00:00"}},{"id":"news-1946","ktid":1001140,"fields":{"id":"","kt_id":"1001140","source":"Google News RSS","create_time":"","update_time":"","title_cn":"联邦法官将裁定国土安全部是否必须协助被拘留者会见律师","title_en":"Federal judge to rule whether Homeland Security must help detainees meet attorneys - OPB","summary_cn":"俄勒冈州尤金市一名联邦法官将裁定美国国土安全部是否必须协助被移民官员拘留者在被转移前会见律师。律师和倡导者作证称，ICE官员常以当事人拒绝为由或迅速转移人员，阻碍其获得法律咨询。原告为波特兰法律非营利组织CLEAR Clinic和农场工人倡导组织PCUN，要求法官命令ICE提供通知、时间和空间以保障律师会见权。司法部律师辩称转移至塔科马拘留中心不违法。","summary_en":"A federal judge in Eugene, Oregon, will soon rule whether the U.S. Department of Homeland Security must help people detained by immigration officers meet with attorneys before transfer. Attorneys and advocates testified that ICE officers often block access to legal advice by claiming detainees declined or quickly moving them. Plaintiffs CLEAR Clinic and PCUN seek an order requiring ICE to provide notice, time and space for counsel access. Justice Department attorneys argue transfers to the Tacoma facility do not violate rights.","tag":"policy","founddate":"2026-09-18T15:51:43.946780+00:00","url":"https://www.opb.org/article/2025/12/18/federal-judge-to-rule-whether-homeland-security-must-help-detainees-meet-attorneys","content":"About OPBWatchListenNewslettersOPB and KMHD events OPB en EspañolHelp centerFederal judge to rule whether Homeland Security must help detainees meet attorneysBy Troy Brynelson (OPB)Dec. 18, 2025 11:39 p.m.One lawyer told the court about ICE taking a person through the Eugene facility’s backdoor when she arrived.A federal judge in Eugene will soon rule whether the U.S. Department of Homeland Security must help people detained by immigration officers meet with attorneys before they are transferred.Attorneys and advocates testified Thursday about times they have failed to connect with a detained person for a bevy of reasons, like officers with U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement saying a person declined to speak with an attorney or quickly moving them out of the area. THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorOne lawyer told the court about ICE taking a person through the Eugene facility’s back door when she arrived.Federal officers confront protesters at the U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement facility, Portland, Ore., Oct. 4, 2025.Eden McCall / OPBThose actions, the attorneys contend, can block detainees from accessing legal advice.“You have attorneys who are literally at the doors of these facilities offering free legal services at no cost to the government,” said Tess Hellgren, of Innovation Law Lab, the law firm leading the lawsuit.The plaintiffs suing the Trump administration are the CLEAR Clinic, a legal nonprofit based in Portland, and the farmworker advocacy group Pineros y Campesinos Unidos del Noroeste – or PCUN. THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorIn court filings, the plaintiffs ask U.S. District Court Judge Ann Aiken to mandate ICE to give “notice, time and space to provide access to counsel.”Aiken ended the two-hour hearing by saying she will soon issue an order, but declined to specify a deadline. Attorneys are seeking class-action status for the case.Attorneys with the U.S. Department of Justice argue they are not violating anyone’s rights by moving detainees to the Northwest ICE Processing Center in Tacoma, Washington, where there is a 12-hour window for attorneys to meet with people detained there.“On their way to the NWIPC, detainees stop at one of ICE’s three field offices in Oregon, located in Portland, Eugene and Medford, respectively,” government attorneys wrote in court filings. “These small and limited facilities are temporary stopovers used for ministerial tasks.”The lawsuit, which was filed Oct. 16, comes as immigration arrests have ramped up across the state in October and November. A U.S. Customs and Border Protection official recently wrote on social media that there were more than 560 immigration detentions in October.Since then, attorneys with Innovation Law Lab argued, immigration detentions in Oregon “soared 1,400% over prior months.” Headlines have shown dozens of arrests in Portland and across the state, including one morning last week, when witnesses say immigration officers shot pepper balls at onlookers.THANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a SponsorTHANKS TO OUR SPONSOR:Become a Sponsor📨 Daily news in your inboxSign up today for OPB’s “First Look” – your daily guide to the most important news and culture stories from around the Northwest.EmailPlease leave this field blankSign upTags: ICE, Department Of Homeland Security, Immigration, Immigrants, Immigration Reform","content_cn":"关于OPB观看收听新闻简报OPB和KMHD活动OPB西班牙语帮助中心\n联邦法官将裁定国土安全部是否必须帮助被拘留者与律师会面\nTroy Brynelson（OPB）报道\n2025年12月18日晚上11:39\n一名律师告诉法庭，她抵达尤金设施时，ICE从后门带走了一名当事人。\n尤金的一名联邦法官很快将裁定，美国国土安全部是否必须帮助被移民官员拘留的人在被转移前与律师会面。\n周四，律师和倡导者作证说，他们曾因各种原因无法与被拘留者取得联系，例如美国移民与海关执法局官员称某人拒绝与律师交谈，或迅速将其带离该区域。\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n一名律师告诉法庭，她抵达尤金设施时，ICE从后门带走了一名当事人。\n联邦官员在美国移民与海关执法局设施外与抗议者对峙，俄勒冈州波特兰，2025年10月4日。\nEden McCall / OPB\n律师们认为，这些行为可能阻碍被拘留者获得法律建议。\n“有些律师就在这些设施门口，为人们提供免费法律服务，政府无需承担任何费用。”领导这起诉讼的Innovation Law Lab律师事务所的Tess Hellgren说。\n起诉特朗普政府的原告是总部位于波特兰的法律非营利组织CLEAR Clinic，以及农场工人权益倡导团体Pineros y Campesinos Unidos del Noroeste（简称PCUN）。\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n原告在法庭文件中请求美国地区法院法官Ann Aiken强制ICE提供“通知、时间和空间，以便获得律师协助”。\nAiken在为时两小时的听证会结束时表示，她很快会发布命令，但拒绝说明期限。律师们正在为本案申请集体诉讼地位。\n美国司法部的律师辩称，他们将被拘留者转移到华盛顿州塔科马的西北ICE处理中心并未侵犯任何人的权利，那里为律师与被拘留者会面提供12小时窗口。\n政府律师在法庭文件中写道：“前往NWIPC途中，被拘留者会在ICE位于俄勒冈州波特兰、尤金和梅德福的三个外勤办公室之一停留。这些规模小且条件有限的设施只是用于办理行政事务的临时中转站。”\n这起于10月16日提起的诉讼发生之际，10月和11月全州移民逮捕行动不断增加。美国海关与边境保护局一名官员最近在社交媒体上写道，10月有超过560起移民拘留。\nInnovation Law Lab的律师随后辩称，俄勒冈州的移民拘留数量“较前几个月激增1,400%”。新闻标题显示，波特兰及全州各地发生了数十起逮捕事件，包括上周某个早晨，目击者称移民官员向围观者发射了胡椒球。\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n感谢我们的赞助商：成为赞助商\n📨 每日新闻发送到您的收件箱\n立即注册OPB的“First Look”——为您每日梳理西北地区最重要的新闻和文化故事。\n电子邮件\n请将此字段留空\n注册\n标签：ICE、国土安全部、移民、移民人士、移民改革","publish_source":"opb.org","publish_time":"2025-12-18T00:00:00+00:00"}}];
